/* Generated from content/case-studies.json and content/case-study-stories.json. */
window.CASE_STUDIES = {"onespace":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","kind":"ux","headline":"Finding a class file should be straightforward.","summary":"OneSpace is a college communication app I designed for the Microsoft Design Challenge. It gives every class one home for its conversations, files and notices.","role":"Research, app structure, wireframes and screen design","status":"Design challenge concept","context":"Microsoft Design Challenge · Education communication","facts":[{"value":"127","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"5+","label":"one-to-one interviews"},{"value":"Mobile","label":"primary prototype"}],"problem":"During the shift to online classes, students were being asked to follow different teachers across different communication tools. My starting question was whether bringing communication into one place could make collaboration easier. The more useful question became: how does a student find the right resource when they need it?","process":["I began with more than five one-to-one interviews and a virtual discussion with students from different colleges, then used a ten-question survey answered by 127 people. The interviews exposed an adoption mismatch: teachers chose tools they found comfortable, while students inherited the work of keeping up with all of them.","The survey complicated my original assumption. The deck reports that more than half of respondents did not mind using multiple apps, while about 14% found it frustrating. Interest in an all-in-one tool was therefore a signal to explore, rather than proof that every communication tool needed replacing. I translated the findings into a student persona, four problem areas, information architecture, wireframes, and high-fidelity screens."],"decisions":[{"title":"Organise around a class, then its conversations","choice":"The concept uses spaces and categorised channels, with calls accessible from the chat context.","reason":"A class provides a recognisable place to return to; students should not have to remember which external app a teacher chose.","tradeoff":"This would require institutional participation. A cleaner student interface alone cannot solve fragmented adoption."},{"title":"Give files and announcements their own home","choice":"I separated a unified file store and an announcements centre from the conversation stream.","reason":"These are the items students need to retrieve later. Their usefulness should not depend on scrolling back through chat.","tradeoff":"Separating destinations adds navigation; useful defaults and clear links back to the class would be essential."},{"title":"Treat access as part of the product scope","choice":"The prototype explores language support and preferences alongside the core communication screens.","reason":"The interviews describe uneven comfort with technology. Familiar structures and understandable language matter as much as consolidating features.","tradeoff":"The deck also explores watch support and parental controls. Today I would defer those until the core retrieval journey had been tested."}],"outcome":"I designed spaces for classes, channels for conversation, and separate places for files and announcements. The prototype covers the wider communication flow, but those two destinations are the part I would carry into a first release. The project stopped at a prototype; usability testing was the next step.","reflection":"The survey made me question my original all-in-one idea. Being willing to try a new app did not mean students needed every tool replaced. I would start with finding a file and catching an announcement, then test whether those tasks are enough to justify another app.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask students to retrieve an assignment, identify the latest announcement, and join the correct class; compare task success and recovery with their current workflow.","Proposed scope: pilot one class before adding institution-wide migration, parental controls, or a second device. Include teacher setup effort in the evaluation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Process, problem statement, interviews, survey and problem identification."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 11–17","detail":"Information architecture, wireframes, final screens and the proposed usability study."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p05.webp","caption":"The original survey: a useful counterpoint to my initial assumption about app overload.","alt":"Original OneSpace survey slide with response charts.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","caption":"Files and announcements become explicit destinations instead of getting buried in chat.","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/onespace.webp","readingMinutes":8,"pdfPage":3,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Finding a class file [[should be straightforward.]]","scene":"Picture a student getting ready for an online class. The conversation is in one app, the link in another, and the assignment somewhere further back. Before learning can begin, there is a search to finish.","question":"Would putting everything into one app actually solve the problem?","insight":"More than half of the survey respondents were [[fine with using multiple apps]]. That changed the question: the stronger opportunity was making class resources easier to retrieve.","pivot":"I organised the concept around a dependable place to return to: the class, its conversations, and the resources attached to it.","takeaway":"A useful home for information matters more than an all-in-one promise.","journey":[{"title":"Find the class","detail":"A recognisable space becomes the starting point."},{"title":"Find the context","detail":"Channels keep the conversation attached to its class."},{"title":"Retrieve what matters","detail":"Files and announcements have their own destinations."}],"decisionLeads":["First, give the student somewhere to return.","Then, design for the moment after a message is sent.","Finally, decide how much the first release should do."],"annotations":[{"title":"Files outlive the chat","detail":"A dedicated file store supports later retrieval."},{"title":"Announcements deserve a signal","detail":"Important class information gets a destination of its own."},{"title":"Preferences stay close","detail":"The prototype explores personal settings alongside the core flow."}],"evidenceTag":"Original survey · 127 responses","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Original research persona","label":"Vivek · student","context":"An engineering student attending online classes across tools chosen by different teachers.","need":"Retrieve class resources when they are needed.","friction":"Resources scattered across apps, limited phone storage, and difficulty asking for help.","implication":"Give the class and its resources a predictable home."}},"theme":{"layout":"collaboration","mode":"light","paper":"#f7fbff","ink":"#173447","dim":"#53697d","line":"#cbdfe9","accent":"#087e88","wash":"#dcf3f4","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Class confusion","process":"What the research changed","decisions":"Designing a dependable home","outcome":"The communication concept","reflection":"The next research question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep class resources easy to find.","resultHeading":"A class, with a place for its resources."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens.","caption":"The original screens give files and announcements destinations beyond the conversation stream.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","focus":[{"title":"Retrieve","detail":"Files have a searchable destination."},{"title":"Notice","detail":"Announcements have a separate reading surface."},{"title":"Personalise","detail":"Preferences remain close to the core experience."}],"width":1600,"height":900,"details":[{"decision":1,"x":95,"y":190,"width":840,"height":610,"caption":"Files and announcements each have a dedicated destination in the original prototype.","alt":"Detail of the original OneSpace file store and announcements screens."}]},"v2":{"title":"Finding a class file should be [[straightforward]].","hook":"OneSpace is a college communication app I designed for the Microsoft Design Challenge. It gives every class one home for its conversations, files and notices.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Research, app structure, wireframes and screen design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"For","value":"Microsoft Design Challenge"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Phone app, with a smartwatch companion"},{"label":"Methods","value":"Interviews, group discussion, survey, persona, paper wireframes"},{"label":"Status","value":"Design concept. Not built or tested with users"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original deck (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original deck","summary":["The problem: when college classes moved online, every teacher picked their own apps. Students had to keep up with all of them, and files and notices got lost in between.","What I did: I interviewed students and teachers, surveyed 127 people, and designed a phone app where each class has one home.","What I learned: most people (58%) did not mind using several apps. What cost them time was hunting for things. So the design is built around finding a file and catching a notice quickly.","Where it stands: a full set of screens for phone and watch. It was a design entry, so it has not been built or tested with users."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/lead.webp","alt":"Five OneSpace phone screens side by side: spaces, channels, a class chat, files and announcements","caption":"The heart of the app, left to right: your spaces, the channels inside one, a class conversation, that class's files, and its announcements.","width":2464,"height":962},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","heading":"Every teacher picked a different app","blocks":[{"p":"The challenge asked for a way to improve collaboration in India. That is a wide brief, so I picked one part of it: how colleges communicate. Classes had moved online, and each teacher was using whichever apps they were comfortable with."},{"p":"For a student, that meant one class on WhatsApp, another on email and a third on Google Classroom. Keeping up was tiring. Finding something from last week was worse."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/before-after.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing five steps to find a timetable today with three steps in OneSpace","caption":"The problem in one picture. Today a student has to remember which app each teacher uses. In OneSpace the path is the same for every class.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"aside":{"title":"A limit to be clear about.","detail":"This was a design challenge entry. I had no college to try it in and no way to build it, so everything after the research is a design proposal."}}]},{"id":"plan","label":"My plan","heading":"Four stages, from listening to finished screens","blocks":[{"p":"I split the work into four stages and did not start drawing screens until the third. The first two were about understanding the problem well enough to describe it in one sentence."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/plan.webp","alt":"Diagram of four stages: discover, define, develop and deliver, with the work done in each","caption":"What I did in each stage. The problem statement was written after the research, not before it.","width":1600,"height":640}]}]},{"id":"research","label":"Research","heading":"I asked people before I designed anything","blocks":[{"p":"I ran more than five one-to-one interviews with people of different ages and professions. I also held a group discussion over video with students from several colleges. Then I sent out a ten-question survey, and 127 people answered."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/interview-quotes.webp","alt":"Slide from the original deck with three interview quotes, two from students and one from a teacher","caption":"Three things I heard. Read the teacher's quote on the right first: students were inheriting whichever apps each teacher found easiest.","width":1400,"height":1048}]},{"p":"The survey showed how scattered things were. Ten different apps were being used for the same job, and most people used several of them every day."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/chart-apps.webp","alt":"Bar chart of the apps used for institute communication, led by Google Meet at 89 percent and WhatsApp at 76 percent","caption":"Apps used for official communication. Google Meet, WhatsApp, email and Google Classroom were each used by more than half of the 127 people.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Then came the surprise. I expected people to hate using so many apps. Most did not."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/chart-answers.webp","alt":"Three small bar charts: time spent in these apps each day, how using several apps feels, and whether people would use one combined app","caption":"The middle chart changed my direction: 58.3% said using several apps was fine and only 14.2% found it frustrating.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"So the real problem was smaller than I thought. People could live with many apps. What they could not do easily was find the file or the notice they needed. That became the thing to design for."},{"aside":{"title":"What I chose not to assume.","detail":"More than half said \"Yes, I might\" use one combined app. I did not treat that as proof people needed one. Being willing to try something is not the same as needing it."}}]},{"id":"persona","label":"Who it is for","nav":"Who it is for","heading":"One student to keep in mind","blocks":[{"p":"To keep the interviews in view while designing, I wrote a persona: one made-up student who stands for the people I spoke to. Whenever I was unsure about a screen, I asked whether it would help him."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/persona.webp","alt":"Persona card for Vivek, a 20-year-old engineering student in Goa, with his situation, wants and frustrations","caption":"Vivek's phone is short on storage, which is why the app has a Lite mode. He finds it awkward to ask for help, which is why the screens use plain labels.","width":1600,"height":700}]}]},{"id":"features","label":"From problems to features","nav":"Problems to features","heading":"Four problems became six features","blocks":[{"p":"I boiled the research down to four problems. For each one I asked what would fix it, and that gave me six features. Parents came up in the interviews as a third group, so one feature is for them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/problems-features.webp","alt":"Diagram linking four problems to six features, each tagged with the problem it answers","caption":"Each feature on the right is tagged with the problem it answers. Nothing went into the app without a problem behind it, except the parental controls.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"Before settling on these, I drew a mind map to explore other answers. That map is also where I decided to design for the phone first and add a watch app later."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p08.webp","alt":"Mind map from the original deck linking problems to solutions and features","caption":"The mind map from my deck. It runs from the problems, through possible solutions, out to specific features.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Structure","heading":"A map of the app before any screens","blocks":[{"p":"Next I drew a map of everything the app would hold and how the parts connect. After logging in there are six areas: Spaces, Channels, Chat, Announcements, Profile and My Files. Admins get a few extra options in each."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p11.webp","alt":"Map of the OneSpace app showing six areas under login, each with its own actions","caption":"The app map from my deck. Each column is one area, with what you can do there listed below it.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The map also let me check the three things students do most. Each one should start from the class and take only a few taps."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/task-flows.webp","alt":"Diagram of three tasks, find a file, catch an announcement and join a class call, each as a short path of screens","caption":"Three everyday tasks. Every one begins the same way: open your spaces, then pick the class.","width":1600,"height":580}]}]},{"id":"sketches","label":"Sketches","heading":"On paper first","blocks":[{"p":"I sketched every screen on paper before opening any design tool. Paper is quick to change, so I could try a layout, see what was wrong with it and redraw it in minutes."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p12.webp","alt":"Photographed paper wireframes for the OneSpace phone and watch screens","caption":"Paper sketches for spaces, channels, chat, announcements, files, profile, calls and the watch.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"design","label":"The design","nav":"A home for each class","heading":"A home for every class","blocks":[{"p":"A student thinks \"my database class\", not \"the app my database teacher uses\". So the app starts with spaces. A space can be your college, a study group, or just you. Inside a space, channels are grouped the way a timetable is."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-spaces.webp","alt":"OneSpace screen listing the user's spaces as tiles with unread counts","caption":"Spaces. One tile for each group you belong to, with its unread count on the corner.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-channels.webp","alt":"OneSpace screen listing channels grouped under Classes, Labs, Hangout and Fun","caption":"Channels inside a space, grouped as Classes, Labs, Hangout and Fun.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-chat.webp","alt":"OneSpace chat screen for a class channel with a shared file and an announcement card","caption":"A class conversation, with a shared timetable and an exam notice.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"three"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/annotated-channels.webp","alt":"The channels screen with five numbered notes explaining its parts","caption":"The channels screen, part by part.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/annotated-chat.webp","alt":"The class chat screen with five numbered notes explaining its parts","caption":"The class conversation, part by part. Calls start from the header, so there is no need to open a separate meeting app.","width":1600,"height":1000}]}]},{"id":"finding","label":"The design","nav":"Files and notices","heading":"Files and notices get their own place","blocks":[{"p":"In a chat, last week's timetable is buried under everything said since. So files and announcements each have their own screen. The chat only points to them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-files.webp","alt":"OneSpace files screen with All and Starred tabs, sort and filter","caption":"Files. Tabs for All and Starred, with sort and filter, and the date each file was shared.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-announcements.webp","alt":"OneSpace announcements screen with General and Mentions tabs","caption":"Announcements. General and Mentions tabs. Upcoming items show how long is left.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-profile.webp","alt":"OneSpace profile and settings screen","caption":"Profile and settings, including status, themes and preferences.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"three"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Two more screens mean a little more moving around. For this to be quicker than scrolling a chat, the default sort order and the links back to the class have to be right."}}]},{"id":"everyone","label":"The design","nav":"For everyone","heading":"Made for people who find new apps hard","blocks":[{"p":"One teacher told me younger people pick up new apps quickly while she has to struggle. That stayed with me. The app had to be simple and readable first."},{"list":["A Lite mode for phones that are short on storage.","Language support. The profile is shown here in Hindi and in French.","An accessibility setting, reminders, and adding important dates to the calendar."]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-hindi.webp","alt":"OneSpace profile screen in Hindi","caption":"The profile in Hindi.","width":560,"height":1127,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-french.webp","alt":"OneSpace profile screen in French","caption":"The same screen in French.","width":560,"height":1127,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/watch.webp","alt":"Four OneSpace smartwatch screens: home, a call, spaces and a chat","caption":"The watch app: home, a call, your spaces and a chat.","width":1100,"height":1115,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"I also designed screens for parents and for calls."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-parental.webp","alt":"OneSpace parental control screen with a usage ring and links to notes, assignments, tests and attendance","caption":"For parents: time spent, notes from teachers, assignments, tests and attendance.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-audio.webp","alt":"OneSpace audio call screen listing speakers and guests","caption":"An audio call, with speakers and guests listed apart.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/screen-video.webp","alt":"OneSpace video call screen with a shared screen above a grid of participants","caption":"A video call: the shared screen on top, everyone else below.","width":560,"height":1071,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"three"}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","nav":"What comes next","heading":"What I would do before building it","blocks":[{"p":"The design is complete, but it has not been in front of users yet. These are the next three steps I would take."},{"steps":[{"title":"Test three tasks.","detail":"Find last week's assignment, find the latest announcement and join the right class. Time each one against how students do it today."},{"title":"Start with one class.","detail":"Try it with a single class before a whole college, and count how long it takes the teacher to set up."},{"title":"Launch smaller.","detail":"The first version would be the class, its files and its announcements. Parental controls, calls and the watch app can wait until those work."}]},{"p":"My deck also lists longer-term ideas: desktop apps, usability studies with more kinds of users, and trying it outside education."}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Let the research change the plan","blocks":[{"p":"I started with an answer: one app for everything. The survey told me that was not what people needed most. The useful part of this project is that I listened, and the design got more focused because of it."},{"p":"If I did it again, I would decide what goes in the first version straight from the research, and keep everything else for later."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","headline":"A clearer first step towards professional support."}},"umatter":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","kind":"ux","headline":"In our survey, the first thing in the way of therapy was privacy, not price.","summary":"uMatter is a phone app concept for finding a mental-health professional and booking a session. A team of three designed it in a 14-hour design challenge, and I led the team.","role":"Team lead. UX research and design","status":"Team design challenge concept","context":"Team Elementors · Pixel Perfect UI/UX Challenge","facts":[{"value":"14h","label":"challenge window"},{"value":"65","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"3","label":"team members"}],"problem":"The challenge brief focused on making access to mental-health support more efficient. I led the UX research and design contribution for Team Elementors, working with Pratik Manjrekar on UI design and Nidhi S Kamath on UI and graphic design. Our task was to turn a broad and sensitive brief into a coherent mobile journey within fourteen hours.","process":["We used a shortened Double Diamond process to divide research, structure, and interface work. Our ten-question survey received 65 responses. The deck reports that almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous, and almost 89% had never visited a therapist. Those responses guided the concept toward privacy expectations and an approachable first step.","We supplemented the survey with article research, assembled a persona, mapped the flow, and progressed from wireframes to high-fidelity screens. The sample was exploratory: the deck does not document recruitment, a representative population, or a controlled evaluation. I treat its percentages as directional responses from this sample."],"decisions":[{"title":"Build a path to support, rather than promising diagnosis","choice":"The concept connects expert discovery, an expert profile, appointment selection, and payment.","reason":"Breaking access into visible steps gives a person a clearer idea of what happens after they decide to seek help.","tradeoff":"Although the challenge brief used the word diagnosis, these screens do not demonstrate clinical diagnosis or treatment effectiveness."},{"title":"Put trust information before commitment","choice":"Expert detail and review-based trust cues sit before calendar booking and payment in the documented journey.","reason":"A person needs to understand who they are approaching before being asked to commit time or money.","tradeoff":"Ratings alone do not establish professional competence. Credential verification would need its own operational process."},{"title":"Explore lower-pressure ways to engage","choice":"The team added awareness content, stories, and lighter activities alongside the booking journey.","reason":"Not every visitor is ready to book immediately. The concept offers a way to explore the topic before taking that step.","tradeoff":"More destinations can dilute the primary route to professional support; I would test whether they help or distract."}],"outcome":"We completed the mobile concept within the fourteen-hour challenge, from onboarding and expert profiles to appointment selection and payment. I led the team and the UX research and design. Pratik Manjrekar contributed UI design; Nidhi S Kamath contributed UI and graphic design. Our final artifact was a prototype, not a clinical service.","reflection":"The survey raised a question we could not resolve in the challenge: how does someone stay anonymous while booking an accountable professional service? I would work through what is shared at each step before adding more features to the concept.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: test whether participants understand what is private, what a provider can see, and whether the product offers booking or diagnosis.","Proposed iteration: add clear provider credentials, consent and cancellation information, then evaluate the booking journey with appropriate professional input."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Named team roles, fourteen-hour constraint, survey size and reported responses."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–18","detail":"Persona, solution framing, user flow, wireframes and final booking screens."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p02.webp","caption":"The original team slide preserves who contributed what.","alt":"Team Elementors slide naming Srinidhi Bhat, Pratik Manjrekar and Nidhi S Kamath.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/umatter.webp","readingMinutes":11,"pdfPage":6,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A clearer first step [[towards professional support.]]","scene":"Imagine being ready to explore professional support, but unsure who will see your details or what the first step involves. Every unclear commitment can make it easier to postpone the search.","question":"What should someone understand before being asked to book?","insight":"In our 65-response survey, almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous. [[Privacy expectations]] had to shape the journey before calendar and payment screens.","pivot":"Within a fourteen-hour challenge, I led the UX contribution toward a visible sequence: understand the provider, choose a time, then commit.","takeaway":"Trust begins with knowing what the next step asks of you.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start with providers and support information."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"See the expert’s details before committing."},{"title":"Book","detail":"Move through calendar selection and payment."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the service boundary clear.","Earn confidence before asking for commitment.","Leave room for someone who is still exploring."],"annotations":[{"title":"Context before commitment","detail":"Expert details sit before the calendar and payment."},{"title":"A visible sequence","detail":"The concept separates discovery, consideration, and booking."},{"title":"A team outcome","detail":"I led UX research/design; Pratik and Nidhi contributed UI and graphic design."}],"evidenceTag":"Original research · 65 responses","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Original team research persona","label":"Shritija · student seeking support","context":"The original deck describes a medical student balancing studies with a need for professional support.","need":"Understand a provider and the first commitment before booking.","friction":"Limited time and a lack of support around seeking help.","implication":"Put provider information and privacy expectations before the appointment."}},"theme":{"layout":"care","mode":"light","paper":"#faf9ff","ink":"#242444","dim":"#62627c","line":"#dedced","accent":"#5356a8","wash":"#eeedfb","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before asking for help","process":"Listening to the sample","decisions":"Designing for consideration","outcome":"The booking journey","reflection":"What trust needs next"},"decisionHeading":"Help people decide who to book.","resultHeading":"From considering support to choosing a time."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens.","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"v2":{"title":"In our survey, the first thing in the way of therapy was [[privacy]], not price.","hook":"uMatter is a phone app concept for finding a mental-health professional and booking a session. A team of three designed it in a 14-hour design challenge, and I led the team.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Team lead. UX research and design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Three people. Pratik Manjrekar on UI design, Nidhi S Kamath on UI and graphic design"},{"label":"For","value":"Pixel Perfect UI/UX Challenge, as Team Elementors"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Phone app"},{"label":"Methods","value":"Survey, article reading, persona, ideation board, user flow, pencil wireframes"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"Status","value":"Design concept. Not built, not tested with users, and not a clinical service"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original deck (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original deck","summary":["The problem: the brief asked how to make mental-health diagnosis cheaper and easier to reach. In our survey of 65 people, 89.2% had never been to a therapist, and privacy was the difficulty they named most.","What I did: I led the team and its UX research and design. We ran the survey, wrote a persona, mapped the app and drew a path from sign-in to a paid booking. My two teammates worked on the UI and graphic design.","The key idea: show who the expert is, when they are free and what it costs before asking for any money. Two lighter routes sit beside booking for people who are not ready yet.","Where it stands: a set of phone screens made in 14 hours. It was never built or tested, and it makes no claim about diagnosis or treatment. One question is still open: how to stay anonymous while booking and paying."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/lead.webp","alt":"Six uMatter phone screens side by side: an onboarding screen, home, the experts list, an expert's page, the booking calendar and the confirmation screen","caption":"The path through the app, left to right: the privacy promise, home, the list of experts, one expert's page, booking a slot, and the price before payment. Photos and names in the mock-ups were placeholders and are blurred.","width":2400,"height":1066},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","heading":"The brief asked about diagnosis. People were stuck before that.","blocks":[{"p":"The challenge brief was titled \"Mental health app - Diagnosis\". It said that finding out what is wrong through ordinary check-ups costs a lot of money and time, and it ended with a question."},{"quote":{"text":"How can we make the process of diagnosis more efficient and accessible?","by":"The challenge brief"}},{"p":"Our survey and our screens ended up one step earlier than diagnosis. Most of the people we asked had never been to a therapist at all. So the work became about reaching a professional in the first place: finding one, trusting them enough to book, and knowing the cost."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/before-after.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing five unanswered questions a person faces today with four steps in the uMatter app","caption":"The problem in one picture. The top row is what people told us got in the way. The bottom row is the path our screens offer.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"aside":{"title":"A limit to be clear about.","detail":"We were three designers with 14 hours. The screens show how someone might find an expert and book a session. They do not diagnose anything, and nothing here says whether any treatment works."}}]},{"id":"plan","label":"Our plan","heading":"Fourteen hours, so we cut the process down","blocks":[{"p":"We used a shortened version of the Double Diamond, a common design process with four stages: discover, define, develop and deliver. Planning the work this way let us size the work and split it between three people. I led the research and the UX design, which means how the app is organised and how someone moves through it. My two teammates worked on the UI, the look of the screens, and the graphics."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/plan.webp","alt":"Diagram of four stages, discover, define, develop and deliver, with the work the team did in each","caption":"What we did in each stage. Look at the first card: a survey was the only research with people that fit in the time.","width":1600,"height":680}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A survey is quick to send and quick to read, which is why we chose it. It cannot tell you why someone answered the way they did. Interviews would have, and we had no time for them."}}]},{"id":"research","label":"Research","heading":"65 people answered, and most had never seen a therapist","blocks":[{"p":"We wrote a ten-question survey about people's experience of therapy and therapists. 65 people answered it. We also read three published articles on why people avoid therapy."},{"stats":[{"value":"65","label":"people answered the survey"},{"value":"89.2%","label":"had never visited a therapist"},{"value":"52.3%","label":"wanted professional help online while staying anonymous"},{"value":"14 of 43","label":"named privacy as a difficulty, more than any other answer"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/survey-anonymity.webp","alt":"Pie chart from the original survey: 52.3 percent yes, 26.2 percent no and 21.5 percent maybe to staying anonymous while getting professional help online","caption":"One chart straight from our survey form. The blue slice is the 52.3% who wanted professional help online while staying anonymous.","width":1200,"height":584}]},{"p":"I redrew all the answers as bars so they are easier to compare."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/chart-core.webp","alt":"Five small bar charts of survey answers on visiting a therapist, considering one, mental health issues, anonymity and willingness to pay","caption":"The five questions everyone answered. Start with the top left: 89.2% had never visited a therapist. Then the bottom right: only 30.8% gave a clear yes to paying.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The brief talked about cost, in money and in time. The people who answered put privacy ahead of both."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/chart-barriers.webp","alt":"Three bar charts: difficulties in reaching a therapist led by privacy, changes that would help led by acceptance and encouragement, and preferred ways to talk led by chat","caption":"Three follow-up questions. Privacy leads the first chart with 14 people, ahead of cost with 9. Chat leads the third with 24.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"Two more things stood out. The change people wanted most was acceptance and encouragement, named by 18 of 50, with awareness next at 15. A booking screen cannot give anyone those. And chat was the way most people felt comfortable talking, picked by 24 of 47."},{"aside":{"title":"How far these numbers go.","detail":"This was an informal survey, and I have no record of how the 65 people were reached. The numbers describe that group only. They do not measure how common mental-health problems are, and I do not set them beside public health figures."}},{"aside":{"title":"A correction to our own deck.","detail":"Our slide called the 52.3% \"almost half\". It is just over half. This page uses the exact figures from the charts."}}]},{"id":"persona","label":"Who it is for","heading":"Three groups, and one student to keep in mind","blocks":[{"p":"We named three groups the app could serve: students and teenagers, doctors and psychiatrists, and people living with anxiety, stress or depression. To keep one of them in view while designing, we wrote a persona. A persona is a made-up person who stands for a group of users."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/persona.webp","alt":"Persona card for Shritija, a 19-year-old medical student, with what keeps her going, what she wants and what gets in the way","caption":"Read the right-hand card first. No support at home and little free time echo difficulties people named in the survey.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"We named doctors and psychiatrists as a group, but every screen we drew is for the person looking for help. The expert's side of the app was not designed."}]},{"id":"features","label":"From problems to features","nav":"Problems to features","heading":"A list of problems became six features","blocks":[{"p":"I wrote every problem we had found on one note. Beside it I listed possible answers, and we starred some of them. Feature ideas went in the circles around the two lists."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/ideation-board.webp","alt":"Ideation board with a pink note listing problems, a yellow note listing solutions with stars, and purple circles of feature ideas","caption":"Our ideation board. My problems list is on the left. Possible solutions are in the middle, some of them starred. The circles on the right are feature ideas.","width":1500,"height":1249}]},{"p":"From the board we settled on six features, and on a phone app, because the phone is the device people use most."},{"list":["Easy, affordable access to genuine mental-health professionals and resources.","A safe, secure and anonymous way to talk to them.","Content that builds wellbeing and awareness.","Help offered when someone needs it.","Ratings, feedback and reviews, so trust in an expert can build.","Community events and forums about mental health."]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/research-to-design.webp","alt":"Diagram pairing six things heard in research with what the screens offer in answer, the last one marked as not designed","caption":"What we heard on the left, what the screens offer on the right. The last row is a gap I can see now: people preferred chat, and we never drew a chat session.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"Some ideas stayed on the board: an emergency helpline, sessions at home, end-to-end encryption, and a filter for therapists by speciality. Fourteen hours covered the booking path. None of these reached the screens we finished."}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Structure","heading":"One path from home to a booked session","blocks":[{"p":"Before the screens, we drew a user flow. That is a diagram of every screen in the app and the routes between them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p12.webp","alt":"User flow slide from the original deck, with coloured circles for login, home, search, community, profile and the booking steps","caption":"The user flow from our deck. Yellow is the booking path, red is community, green is profile and blue is search.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/structure-map.webp","alt":"Redrawn app map with four areas after login, home, search, community and profile, and the booking path as a row of steps","caption":"The same flow, redrawn so it can be read. The bottom row is the booking path, which ends back at home.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"In the flow, date, time and mode are three separate steps. On the final screen they sit together on one page, so a booking takes fewer screens. The cost is a longer page to scroll."}]},{"id":"sketches","label":"Sketches","heading":"Pencil first","blocks":[{"p":"We sketched the main screens in pencil before the finished designs. These are wireframes: rough drawings that settle where things go before any colour or type is chosen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/wireframes.webp","alt":"Six photographed pencil wireframes: a splash screen, two login screens, an appointment summary, home and a booking calendar","caption":"All six sketches from our deck. The bottom row holds the three that matter most: the appointment summary, home, and the booking calendar.","width":1500,"height":956}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/sketch-to-screen.webp","alt":"Three pencil sketches each shown beside the finished screen it became: home, booking and confirmation","caption":"Sketch on the left of each pair, finished screen on the right. The order of things on each screen was settled on paper and did not change.","width":1600,"height":1020}]}]},{"id":"privacy","label":"The design","nav":"A promise of privacy","heading":"The first screens make a promise about privacy","blocks":[{"p":"The app opens with four short screens. The second one answers the survey directly. It reads \"Anonymous, safe and secure\", and under that, no more being embarrassed to speak about how you feel."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-onboard-talk.webp","alt":"uMatter opening screen reading Talk it out, talk to experts about how you feel","caption":"The first screen says what the app is for: talking to experts about how you feel.","width":520,"height":1122},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-onboard-anonymous.webp","alt":"uMatter onboarding screen reading Anonymous, safe and secure","caption":"The second makes the privacy promise in three words.","width":520,"height":1116},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-onboard-happiness.webp","alt":"uMatter onboarding screen reading Spread happiness","caption":"The third invites people to encourage others.","width":520,"height":1116}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"Then comes sign-in. Most apps ask for an account here. This one also lets you in as a guest."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/closer-signin.webp","alt":"The uMatter sign-in screen with four numbered notes on its options, including sign in as a guest","caption":"The sign-in screen, part by part. Note 2 is the one that matters: a way in without an account.","width":1300,"height":1100}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A promise on an opening screen is easy to make. The design does not yet show how it is kept: what a guest can do, what is stored, and what an expert gets to see."}}]},{"id":"ways-in","label":"The design","nav":"Three ways in","heading":"Home offers three ways in, and only one is booking","blocks":[{"p":"Not everyone who opens the app is ready to book. So home has three buttons: Inspiration, Talk to an expert, and Cheer me up."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-home.webp","alt":"uMatter home screen with three buttons, Inspiration, Talk to an expert and Cheer me up, above a video feed","caption":"Home. Three buttons under the greeting, then a feed of videos.","width":520,"height":1126},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-inspiration.webp","alt":"uMatter Inspirations screen with a row of stories and cards holding users' written stories","caption":"Inspiration. Stories written by other users, and a plus button to add your own.","width":520,"height":1126},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-cheer.webp","alt":"uMatter Cheer me up screen showing a short chat that tells a joke","caption":"Cheer me up. A short chat that asks how you are and offers a joke.","width":520,"height":1127}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"Inspiration speaks to the change people wanted most: acceptance, encouragement and awareness. Reading that someone else has been through the same thing is a start. Cheer me up is light relief and nothing more. It is not support, and it is not therapy."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Two extra destinations can pull attention away from the route to a professional. I would test whether they help people take the next step or keep them from it."}}]},{"id":"booking","label":"The design","nav":"Booking","heading":"Booking shows who, when and how much before it asks for money","blocks":[{"p":"Talk to an expert opens a list of experts. Picking one opens their page. Only after that do you choose a slot, and only after that do you see a way to pay."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-experts.webp","alt":"uMatter experts screen listing experts as cards, each with a client count and a star rating","caption":"The experts list. Each card shows a client count and a star rating.","width":520,"height":1124},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-doctor.webp","alt":"uMatter expert page with a rating, a description, an intro video, a Book an appointment button and reviews","caption":"One expert's page. The button to book sits below the description and the video.","width":520,"height":1126},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/app-booking.webp","alt":"uMatter booking screen with a month calendar, six time slots, an online or offline switch and a Confirm button","caption":"Booking. A date, a time slot, and online or offline, then Confirm.","width":520,"height":1448}],"layout":"three"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/closer-doctor.webp","alt":"The expert page with five numbered notes on the rating, description, intro video, booking button and reviews","caption":"The expert's page, part by part. Everything that helps you judge the person comes above the button to book.","width":1300,"height":1100}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/closer-confirmation.webp","alt":"The confirmation screen with four numbered notes on the close button, the chosen slot, the price and the payment options","caption":"The confirmation screen, part by part. The total sits above the payment options, so nobody picks a way to pay before knowing the price.","width":1300,"height":1100}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Stars and a client count are quick to read, but they do not show that someone is qualified. A real service would have to check each expert's credentials, and that is a process, not a screen."}}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","nav":"What comes next","heading":"The question we could not answer in 14 hours","blocks":[{"p":"People asked for privacy. A booking needs a name somewhere: for the payment, for the expert, or for a visit in person. We drew both and did not decide how they fit together."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/sharing.webp","alt":"Diagram of five steps from guest sign-in to the session, showing what each asks the user to share, with payment and the session marked as open questions","caption":"What each step asks of you. The first three ask for little. The last two are where anonymity runs out, and where the design stops.","width":1600,"height":640}]},{"p":"These are the steps I would take before adding any feature."},{"steps":[{"title":"Decide what is shared at each step.","detail":"Then say it on the screen, in plain words, before the person commits."},{"title":"Design the session itself.","detail":"Start with chat, since that is what most people in the survey were comfortable with."},{"title":"Show how experts are checked.","detail":"Credentials, consent and how to cancel, worked out with mental-health professionals."},{"title":"Test what people understand.","detail":"Can they tell what is private, what the expert sees, and that this app books a session and does not diagnose?"}]},{"p":"Our deck also lists longer-term ideas: free webinars and question-and-answer sessions at organisations, funding through NGOs and crowdfunding, reaching more kinds of users, and applying for government recognition."}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Anonymous and accountable pull against each other","blocks":[{"p":"The survey gave us a clear ask, and we answered it with a sentence on an opening screen and a guest button. That was the easy half. The hard half is the booking, where a real person pays a real professional. If I did this again I would map what is shared at each step before drawing a single screen."},{"p":"I would also read my own numbers twice. \"Almost half\" and \"just over half\" are different claims, and only one of them was true."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","headline":"Making the first budget less of a form."}},"mucoin":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","kind":"ux","headline":"Setting up a budget should give something back along the way.","summary":"MuCoin is a personal-finance app concept, and this is its setup: five steps that take a person from their income to a first budget they have checked themselves.","role":"Flow, screen design, prototype and written rationale","status":"Prototype & design rationale","context":"Personal finance · Progressive onboarding","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"onboarding stages"},{"value":"3","label":"review groups"},{"value":"Prototype","label":"linked in source"}],"problem":"A useful financial picture depends on personal information that takes effort to recall and can feel uncomfortable to share. I structured MuCoin around a sequence that asks for income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, savings goals, and a final review. The design challenge was to make that effort understandable and correctable.","process":["The original write-up explains the flow screen by screen, including privacy links, expense summaries, editable goals, and review behaviour. It also defines proposed KPIs for each stage. The design centres on repeatable entry, useful summaries and a review that remains editable.","The source mentions a small user subset when discussing the label Saving Goals, but gives no sample size, protocol, or measured comparison. I treat that as informal input. The numerical completion and drop-off figures in the appendix are targets, not results."],"decisions":[{"title":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information","choice":"The income stage starts with amount and source, while fixed and variable expenses use similar entry patterns.","reason":"A familiar pattern lets the person concentrate on their finances instead of relearning the interface at every step.","tradeoff":"A short form still needs room for irregular income, uncertain estimates, and multiple sources; simplicity cannot mean forcing inaccurate answers."},{"title":"Show a useful picture before onboarding ends","choice":"The expense stages return a chart, summary table, total, and relationship to income.","reason":"Summaries create opportunities to notice a missing entry or an implausible total while the information is still fresh.","tradeoff":"A chart is only as good as its inputs. It should communicate an estimate when the person is unsure, rather than imply financial precision."},{"title":"Make confirmation an editable checkpoint","choice":"The review groups income, expenses, and goals into three collapsible, editable sections.","reason":"Being able to inspect and correct information gives the person control over what they submit.","tradeoff":"The old rationale claims review ensures complete task success. That is a hypothesis to test; a confirmation screen cannot guarantee it."}],"outcome":"I designed a five-stage setup flow for income, expenses, savings goals and review. The entry patterns repeat, the summaries give something back along the way, and the final review stays editable. The accompanying KPI figures describe targets for a future test, not measured completion or drop-off.","reflection":"A useful setup should leave someone with a financial picture they understand. I would pay close attention to corrections: noticing and fixing an amount can be a better outcome than finishing the form quickly with the wrong numbers.","nextSteps":["Proposed measures: completion per stage, time to a usable summary, errors noticed during review, and understanding of what happens to entered information.","Proposed study: compare a short path with optional detail against the original sequence. Include irregular income and skipped estimates rather than testing only ideal inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 1–5","detail":"Project purpose and screen-by-screen design rationale."},{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 6–11","detail":"KPI definitions and aspirational targets; no measured results reported."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding.","width":1120,"height":1585}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/fTSm9Chs0awMrjMUN2OWSa/MuCoin?node-id=1-2&starting-point-node-id=191%3A955&t=z97O9d2NsQAgujdk-1","cover":"assets/work/mucoin.webp","readingMinutes":11,"pdfPage":9,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Making the first budget [[less of a form.]]","scene":"Picture someone setting up a finance app. They are recalling income, estimating expenses, and deciding how much personal information to share—all before seeing whether the product is useful.","question":"How can the setup give something back before it is finished?","insight":"The flow already contained a useful idea: [[show the emerging financial picture]] while a person enters it. Each summary can become a chance to understand and correct the information.","pivot":"I structured the onboarding as a repeatable sequence with visible progress, immediate summaries, and an editable checkpoint.","takeaway":"A good onboarding flow helps someone trust the information they just entered.","journey":[{"title":"Enter","detail":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information."},{"title":"See","detail":"Return a summary while the context is fresh."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Make the complete picture editable before submission."}],"decisionLeads":["Start with one manageable question.","Give the input a visible consequence.","Let review become an act of control."],"annotations":[{"title":"Five connected stages","detail":"Income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, goals, and review."},{"title":"A measurement plan","detail":"The original percentages are targets for evaluation, not achieved results."}],"evidenceTag":"Original flow rationale · five stages","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person setting up a budget","context":"Preparing a first picture of income, spending, and savings.","need":"Enter information accurately and understand what it adds up to.","friction":"Recall takes effort; mistakes are easy to make before the full picture is visible.","implication":"Give input an immediate summary and an editable review."}},"theme":{"layout":"ledger","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f8ff","ink":"#142d57","dim":"#536782","line":"#d0dded","accent":"#205bb7","wash":"#e1edff","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"The cost of setup","process":"A picture worth reviewing","decisions":"Five stages, one pattern","outcome":"The proposed onboarding","reflection":"Measuring understanding"},"decisionHeading":"Make each onboarding step useful.","resultHeading":"A financial picture that can be corrected."},"visual":{"presentation":"document","src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding.","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","width":1120,"height":1585},"v2":{"title":"Setting up a budget should [[give something back]] along the way.","hook":"MuCoin is a personal-finance app concept, and this is its setup: five steps that take a person from their income to a first budget they have checked themselves.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Flow, screen design, prototype and written rationale"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"For","value":"A design assignment set by a company"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Setup flow for a personal-finance app, as a clickable Figma prototype"},{"label":"Methods","value":"Double Diamond process, three usability rules (Hick, Miller, Jakob), a plan of 18 measures"},{"label":"Status","value":"Concept. Not built or tested. Every number on this page is a target, not a result"}],"links":[{"label":"Open the Figma prototype","url":"https://www.figma.com/proto/fTSm9Chs0awMrjMUN2OWSa/MuCoin?node-id=1-2&starting-point-node-id=191%3A955&t=z97O9d2NsQAgujdk-1"},{"label":"Read the written rationale (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The prototype and the written rationale","summary":["The problem: a budgeting app has to ask what you earn and spend before it can help. Those numbers are private and hard to remember, and a long form gives nothing back until the end.","What I did: I designed a five-step setup (income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, saving goals, review), made it a clickable prototype and wrote down the reason for every screen.","The key idea: ask for a little, then show what it adds up to. Each expense step ends with a chart and a total, and the last step lets the person fix anything.","Where it stands: a concept. It has not been built or tested. I set targets for 18 measures, and none of them has been measured."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/lead.webp","alt":"Five schematic screens in a row: income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, saving goals and review, each under a progress bar","caption":"The whole setup, left to right: income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, saving goals and review. These are schematics I drew from my rationale. The real screens are in the Figma prototype.","width":1600,"height":800},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","heading":"A budgeting app asks its hardest questions first","blocks":[{"p":"A budgeting app can only help once it knows what you earn and what you spend. So the first thing it does is ask. Income is a private number. Expenses are hard to remember, and there are many of them."},{"p":"If setup is one long form, the person does all of that work before they see anything useful. I wanted the setup itself to give something back along the way."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/before-after.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing one long setup form, which shows a result only at the end, with MuCoin's five steps that show results as they go","caption":"The idea in one picture. In the top row the first useful view comes after all the work. In MuCoin each expense step ends with a chart and a total. This is an illustration, not a measured comparison.","width":1600,"height":660}]},{"aside":{"title":"What you are looking at.","detail":"MuCoin is a concept. It has not been built or tested with users. The real screens are in the Figma prototype linked on this page. The pictures here are diagrams I drew from my written rationale, so they show what each screen holds and why. They do not show its final look."}}]},{"id":"approach","label":"My approach","heading":"I wrote down a reason for every screen","blocks":[{"p":"This was a solo project. I followed the Double Diamond, a design process with four stages: discover, define, develop and deliver. Here is what I have to show for each stage, including what has not happened yet."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/plan.webp","alt":"Diagram of four stages, discover, define, develop and deliver, with what exists for each and two items marked as not done","caption":"Read the last card first. The flow was designed and prototyped, and the measures were planned. No test has been run.","width":1600,"height":650}]},{"p":"For each screen I leaned on a well-known usability rule. These rules are short summaries of how people tend to behave. Each one gave me a reason to keep a screen small or familiar."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/laws.webp","alt":"Three cards explaining Hick's Law, Miller's Law and Jakob's Law in plain words, each with where it is used in MuCoin","caption":"Each rule is at the top of its card in plain words. The green box says where it shows up in the flow.","width":1600,"height":630}]},{"p":"I wrote all of this up as an 11-page rationale. One page covers the name and the process, four pages go through the flow screen by screen, and six pages define how each screen would be measured."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p03.webp","alt":"A page of the written rationale covering the expense summary and the variable expenses screen","caption":"A page from the flow section. It explains the expense summary and why the variable-expense screen copies the fixed-expense one.","width":1120,"height":1585},{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p09.webp","alt":"A page of the written rationale listing measures for the goals screen, each with a definition, goal, measurement and target","caption":"A page from the measures section. Every measure has a definition, a goal, a way to count it and a target.","width":1120,"height":1585}],"layout":"two"}]},{"id":"income","label":"The design","nav":"Step 1: income","heading":"Income gets two fields and nothing more","blocks":[{"p":"Income is the first step, and it is a private number. I did not want the screen to scare anyone off. So it asks for two things only: the amount and where it comes from."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/screen-income.webp","alt":"Schematic of the income step with five numbered notes: progress bar, amount with currency, source dropdown, add another source, and help and privacy links","caption":"The income step, part by part. Look at 5: the help and privacy links are on this step and stay on every step after it.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Two fields cannot describe every kind of income. Someone who is paid irregularly, or who is unsure of the exact figure, still needs a way to answer honestly. A short form should not force a wrong number."}}]},{"id":"expenses","label":"The design","nav":"Steps 2 and 3: expenses","heading":"Expenses go in one at a time, then add up on screen","blocks":[{"p":"Fixed expenses stay the same each month. Variable expenses change. Listing all of them in detail is where I expected people to give up, because nobody holds a whole month of spending in their head."},{"p":"So the step starts empty, with one prompt. Each expense goes through a short form: the details, a category and an optional comment."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/expense-loop.webp","alt":"Three schematic states of the expense step: an empty screen with a prompt, a short form, and a summary with a pie chart, a table and a total","caption":"Three states of the expense step, left to right. The form in the middle is the only thing a person has to learn.","width":1600,"height":1030}]},{"p":"Once an expense is entered, the person sees a summary. This is the part that gives something back: real numbers about their own money, before setup is finished."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/screen-summary.webp","alt":"Schematic of the expense summary with four numbered notes: pie chart, summary table, total in bold, and total as a share of income","caption":"The summary, part by part. Look at 4: the total is shown as a share of income, which ties this step back to the first one.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"p":"The variable-expense step uses the same three states. By then the pattern is familiar, so I expected it to go faster. That is a guess I have not tested."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A chart is only as good as what was typed in. If someone is guessing, the summary should read as an estimate and not as a precise account."}}]},{"id":"goals","label":"The design","nav":"Step 4: saving goals","heading":"“Financial goals” became “saving goals”","blocks":[{"p":"This step was first called Financial goals. In informal research with a small group of users, Saving goals appealed more, so I renamed it. My write-up does not say how many people that was, so I treat it as a hint and not as a finding."},{"p":"The step opens with a question: how much are you looking to save? Under it are suggested amounts, shown as shares of the person's income. After that they can set a goal of their own or pick a suggested one."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/goals-flow.webp","alt":"Flow diagram of the goals step: savings prompt, suggested amounts, set a goal, the goal form, and a celebration animation","caption":"The goals step in order. The celebration at the end is there so that setting one goal makes people want to set another.","width":1600,"height":640}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/screen-goal.webp","alt":"Schematic of the goal form with five numbered notes: goal name, target amount, amount already saved, reminder, and icon","caption":"The goal form, part by part. It looks like the expense form on purpose, so it is already familiar.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The icons and the celebration make this the longest step. I set its time target at 3 to 5 minutes, against 1 to 2 minutes for an expense step. Whether that extra time helps people or loses them is something only a test can show."}}]},{"id":"review","label":"The design","nav":"Step 5: review","heading":"The last step is a check, and everything can still be changed","blocks":[{"p":"The final step shows everything the person entered, grouped into three sections: Income, Expenses and Goals. Each section opens and closes, and each entry can be edited. Editing goes back to a form the person already knows."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/screen-review.webp","alt":"Schematic of the review step with five numbered notes: full progress bar, three sections, open and close, edit links, and confirm","caption":"The review, part by part. Look at 4: an edit returns to the same form the entry came from.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"p":"I wanted this step to do two jobs. It lets people catch mistakes while the numbers are fresh. It also gives setup a clear end, so the person feels finished and in control of what they shared."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"In my rationale I wrote that this screen would ensure a 100% completion rate. No screen can promise that. A review also adds one more screen before the person gets into the app, so it has to earn its place by catching real errors."}}]},{"id":"measures","label":"Measures","nav":"How I would measure it","heading":"Eighteen measures, each with a target","blocks":[{"p":"A KPI, or key performance indicator, is a number you track to see whether something is working. I defined 18 of them across the five steps. The two expense steps share the same four. For each one I wrote what it counts, how to count it and the number I would aim for."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/kpi-targets.webp","alt":"Chart of fourteen percentage targets on a scale from 0 to 100 percent, grouped by income, expenses, saving goals and review","caption":"The fourteen targets that are percentages, on one scale. Dark bars are zones where higher is better. Pale bars are drop-off, where lower is better. These are aims, not results.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"Here is the full list, including the four that are times or counts."},{"table":{"head":["Step","Measure","What it counts","Target"],"rows":[["Income","First-attempt completion","People who finish the step without going back to fix or fill in something","85% or more"],["Income","Drop-off","People who leave the step unfinished","Under 10%"],["Income","Task completion","People who fill in every required field","95 to 100%"],["Expenses","Drop-off","People who leave before finishing the required fields","Under 10%"],["Expenses","Time to complete","Average time from opening the step to finishing it","1 to 2 minutes"],["Expenses","Input accuracy","People whose entries are correct and complete on the first try","90% or more"],["Expenses","Optional fields used","People who use an optional field or add a category of their own","60% or more"],["Saving goals","Form completion","People who start the goal form and submit it","90% or more"],["Saving goals","Time on goal setting","Average time from opening the step to finishing the goals","3 to 5 minutes"],["Saving goals","Goal tools used","People who adjust or customise a goal","70% or more"],["Saving goals","Goals section completed","People who reach the step and submit a goal","95 to 99%"],["Saving goals","Drop-off","People who start a goal and do not finish it","Under 10%"],["Saving goals","Celebration shown","People who finish a goal and see the animation","90% or more"],["Saving goals","Goals revisited","People who come back to review or change a goal within 30 days","40% or more"],["Review","Time on review","Average time on the review screen before submitting","1 to 3 minutes"],["Review","Edits after review","Changes made after reaching the review screen","1 or fewer per person, on average"],["Review","Edit and collapse used","People who use click-to-edit or open and close a section","75% or more"],["Review","Errors found","People who spot at least one error and fix it","80% or more"]]}},{"aside":{"title":"Targets, not results.","detail":"None of these numbers has been measured. My rationale does not say where each target came from, so they are starting points for a test and nothing more. The Expenses rows apply to the fixed step and again to the variable step."}}]},{"id":"questions","label":"Looking back","nav":"What I would question","heading":"Three targets I would rewrite","blocks":[{"p":"Reading the targets again, some of them do not hold up. I would fix these before running any test."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/kpi-questions.webp","alt":"Three cards, each pairing something written in the rationale with what the author thinks of it now","caption":"Each card shows what I wrote in pink and what I think now in green. The middle card matters most: a corrected number is a good outcome, not a failure.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"A smaller one sits beside these. I set a target for how often the celebration plays after a goal is set. It plays every time, so that number would only tell me whether the animation works. It says nothing about how people feel."},{"p":"The measure I care about most now is corrections. Someone who notices a wrong amount and fixes it has a better budget than someone who finished fast with the wrong numbers."}]},{"id":"name","label":"Name and look","nav":"Name and look","heading":"The name means small steps","blocks":[{"p":"Mu is the Greek letter μ. It is often used to mean micro, and in statistics it stands for the mean, or average. Both fit a budgeting app, where small amounts and averages matter. Despite the word coin, MuCoin has nothing to do with cryptocurrency."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/name.webp","alt":"The MuCoin logo, a gold coin stamped with the Greek letter mu beside the name, with three notes on what the name means","caption":"The logo and the three ideas behind the name. Look at the coin: it carries the letter μ.","width":1600,"height":770}]},{"p":"For colour I wanted a mix of formal and casual. Money is a heavy subject, and the look was meant to make it feel a little lighter."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","nav":"What comes next","heading":"What I would do before building it","blocks":[{"p":"The flow is designed and the measures are written. The next job is to find out whether any of it is true."},{"steps":[{"title":"Test it with real people.","detail":"Watch each step: who finishes, how long it takes to reach a useful summary, and which errors get caught at review."},{"title":"Compare it with a shorter path.","detail":"Try a short version with optional detail against this five-step sequence, and see which one people complete."},{"title":"Include the awkward cases.","detail":"Irregular income, rough estimates and skipped answers, as well as tidy inputs."},{"title":"Ask about privacy.","detail":"Check that people understand what happens to the numbers they enter."}]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A target is a guess until it is tested","blocks":[{"p":"The flow rests on one idea I still believe in: give people something back while they are still entering their numbers. A summary after each step is a reason to keep going and a chance to fix a mistake."},{"p":"What I would change is how I wrote about it. I stated some targets as if they were outcomes. Next time I would write each one as a question, run a small test, and only then put numbers on the page."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","headline":"An SEO panel you can actually scan."}},"gmd":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","kind":"ux","headline":"An SEO panel you can read at a glance.","summary":"I compared GetMetaData, a Chrome extension that lists the details search engines read on a web page, with three rival tools, then redesigned its panel so the data is easier to find and read.","role":"Competitive review, information structure, wireframes and interface design","status":"Redesign prototype","context":"Chrome extension · SEO inspection","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"competitors reviewed"},{"value":"1","label":"shared comparison site"},{"value":"2","label":"persona perspectives"}],"problem":"GetMetaData exposes a page’s SEO elements, but a dense readout makes it difficult to see what needs attention. The original review describes small text, weak information architecture, and a repeated-output bug. My task was to improve inspection without stripping away the useful detail or export capability.","process":["I compared SEO META in 1 CLICK, All in One SEO Analyzer, and Detailed SEO Extension alongside GetMetaData. I used my own portfolio as the common test page and looked at functionality, usability, SEO information, and documentation. This kept the content context consistent, although one website could not represent every page structure.","I then used two persona perspectives—an SEO engineer and a small-business/content-marketing user—to frame different reading needs, before creating wireframes and a high-fidelity prototype. The deck does not document interviews validating those personas, so I use them as design lenses rather than research findings."],"decisions":[{"title":"Retain information depth, improve the route through it","choice":"The redesign explores a clearer hierarchy for metadata, headings, images, structured data, and links.","reason":"An inspector should help a person locate relevant evidence before asking them to interpret a full page of metrics.","tradeoff":"A more segmented interface can make cross-category comparison harder. Testing should include jumping between related issues."},{"title":"Compare tools in a controlled content context","choice":"I reviewed the competing extensions against the same portfolio page.","reason":"Using one common page makes differences in presentation easier to discuss without changing the underlying website at every comparison.","tradeoff":"The review is a historical snapshot on one site, not a current market ranking or a large performance benchmark."},{"title":"Separate interface redesign from a functional repair","choice":"The review records duplicated output as a bug, while the deliverable remains an interface prototype.","reason":"Making data easier to read would not fix incorrect or repeated extraction. The two problems need separate acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"The prototype can demonstrate hierarchy and navigation; it cannot prove the extraction bug was resolved."}],"outcome":"I completed the competitor review, wireframes and high-fidelity prototype. The redesign groups the extension’s information into clearer destinations while retaining its depth and export workflow. I recorded the repeated-output bug separately; an interface prototype could not establish whether extraction had been fixed.","reflection":"The next test should use real inspection tasks: find a missing description, check headings, compare related issues and export the findings. That would tell me more than asking whether the new panel looks cleaner.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a novice and an experienced practitioner to locate a missing title, inspect image descriptions, and export their findings.","Proposed acceptance criteria: correct extraction without duplication, clear reading order, and useful handling of a page with unusually long or missing values."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 3–9","detail":"Problem, comparison method, historical tool observations and comparison mapping."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–14","detail":"Persona perspectives, wireframes and final prototype."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p08.webp","caption":"The original review separates useful capabilities from readability and data-quality problems.","alt":"Original GetMetaData strengths and weaknesses slide.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/P86VsJJUoQDMzxsUFCnRHo/3MMaven-Project?node-id=8-2&starting-point-node-id=8%3A2&t=K4zjIkNOs7p8WSiR-1","cover":"assets/work/gmd.webp","readingMinutes":10,"pdfPage":12,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"An SEO panel [[you can actually scan.]]","scene":"Imagine opening an SEO extension to answer one question about a page. The tool has plenty of data, but finding the relevant item becomes another inspection task.","question":"What should stay visible when an inspector becomes easier to read?","insight":"The historical review found [[useful depth and export]] alongside small text, weak organisation, and duplicated output. A visual refresh needed to preserve the valuable workflow.","pivot":"I separated three concerns: finding information, interpreting it, and making sure the extraction itself is correct.","takeaway":"Information depth becomes useful when there is a clear route through it.","journey":[{"title":"Locate","detail":"Get to the relevant category."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the page evidence in context."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Keep export useful in the wider workflow."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the data a reading order.","Hold the comparison context steady.","Keep correctness on its own checklist."],"annotations":[{"title":"Grouped information","detail":"The high-fidelity prototype explores a clearer inspection hierarchy."},{"title":"A usable work surface","detail":"Readability and navigation are part of the tool’s utility."},{"title":"A separate acceptance criterion","detail":"The repeated-output bug needs an product behaviour fix beyond this prototype."}],"evidenceTag":"Historical review · three competitors","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Original persona framing · not interview-validated","label":"SEO specialist & small-business content owner","context":"Two personas in the original deck frame expert inspection and a less technical reading need.","need":"Find and understand relevant page information.","friction":"Dense lists make scanning difficult; unfamiliar terms make the same display harder for a novice.","implication":"Preserve depth while creating clearer groups and entry points."}},"theme":{"layout":"inspector","mode":"light","paper":"#f6faff","ink":"#203146","dim":"#516984","line":"#cbdbee","accent":"#286cad","wash":"#e4effc","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A dense readout","process":"Two ways of reading","decisions":"Making inspection clearer","outcome":"The redesigned extension","reflection":"Testing the hierarchy"},"decisionHeading":"Help different users scan the same metadata.","resultHeading":"A more structured inspection surface."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design.","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"v2":{"title":"An SEO panel you can [[read at a glance]].","hook":"I compared GetMetaData, a Chrome extension that lists the details search engines read on a web page, with three rival tools, then redesigned its panel so the data is easier to find and read.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Competitive review, information structure, wireframes and interface design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"For","value":"A design assignment set by a company"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Chrome extension"},{"label":"Methods","value":"Review of four tools on one test page, a positioning map, two personas, paper wireframes, a Figma prototype"},{"label":"Status","value":"Design prototype. Not tested with users, and no built version to show"}],"links":[{"label":"Open the prototype in Figma","url":"https://www.figma.com/proto/P86VsJJUoQDMzxsUFCnRHo/3MMaven-Project?node-id=8-2&starting-point-node-id=8%3A2&t=K4zjIkNOs7p8WSiR-1"},{"label":"View the original deck (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"Try the prototype","summary":["The problem: GetMetaData shows a lot of useful SEO data, but in very small text with no clear grouping. It is hard to see what needs attention.","What I did: I reviewed it beside three competing extensions on the same web page, sketched a new layout on paper, and designed a prototype with a side menu of eight sections.","The key idea: keep the depth and the export, and give the data a reading order. A separate bug that shows the data twice needs a code fix, so I kept it out of the design work.","Where it stands: my deck shows two finished screens, Overview and Headings, and there is a Figma prototype. It has not been tested with users."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/lead.webp","alt":"Two screens of the redesigned GetMetaData panel side by side: the Overview and the Headings view","caption":"The two finished screens. On the left, the Overview, with a pass or fail chip beside each value. On the right, Headings, with a count for each level above the heading tree.","width":2200,"height":960},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","heading":"Useful data, in text too small to read","blocks":[{"p":"SEO is short for search engine optimisation: the work of helping a web page show up in search results. Much of it comes down to details most visitors never see, such as the page title, its description, its headings, the text behind its images and its links."},{"p":"GetMetaData is a Chrome extension that pulls those details out of a page and lists them. The brief was to design an interface for it that helps people extract and analyse five kinds of data: meta tags, header tags, image tags, structured data and links."},{"p":"When I used the extension, the data was all there. Reading it was the hard part. The text was very small, the information was poorly organised, and the look was dated."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/before-after.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing the extension as reviewed, one dense readout in small text shown twice, with the redesign, a side menu and one readable panel","caption":"The problem in one picture. On the left, everything arrives at once in small text. On the right, the same data is split into sections you can read. This is a sketch from my notes, not a screenshot of the old extension.","width":1600,"height":850}]},{"aside":{"title":"Two problems, kept apart.","detail":"The extension also showed its data twice every time it opened. That is a bug in the code. A new layout cannot fix it, so I recorded it and kept it out of the design work."}}]},{"id":"review","label":"Competitive review","nav":"The review","heading":"I ran four tools on the same page","blocks":[{"p":"Before drawing anything, I wanted to know what good looked like. I picked three other SEO extensions and ran each of them, and GetMetaData itself, on one page: my own portfolio site. With the page held steady, the differences I saw came from the tools."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/method.webp","alt":"Diagram of the review method: one test page, four tools, four questions and two outputs","caption":"How the review ran. The four questions are the ones named in my deck: functionality, usability and interface, SEO metrics, and documentation.","width":1600,"height":630}]},{"p":"For each tool I wrote down what it did well and where it fell short."},{"stats":[{"value":"4","label":"tools reviewed, three competitors and GetMetaData"},{"value":"1","label":"test page shared by all four"},{"value":"23","label":"strengths written down"},{"value":"11","label":"weaknesses written down"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/competitors.webp","alt":"Four cards listing the strengths and weaknesses noted for SEO META in 1 CLICK, All in One SEO Analyzer, Detailed SEO Extension and GetMetaData","caption":"My notes for each tool, redrawn from four slides. Read the last card first: GetMetaData had as many weaknesses as strengths, and one of them was a bug.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"Lists like these are hard to compare by eye. So I sorted the same notes by question."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/matrix.webp","alt":"Table comparing the four tools on ten questions, with each cell marked as a strength, a weakness or not noted","caption":"The same notes side by side. Look at the last column: GetMetaData is red on looks and on organisation, where two of the competitors are green. It is green on export, where two of them are red.","width":1600,"height":1150}]},{"list":["All three competitors had help tooltips on every feature. I counted that as a strength each time.","Two of them ran their data down one long column, which I expected to be a pain on a large website.","GetMetaData was the only one that showed every metric at one time, and it exported easily. One competitor could not export at all. Another had swapped export for copy."]},{"aside":{"title":"A snapshot, not a ranking.","detail":"These are my notes on each tool as it was when I looked, on one page. The tools may have changed since, and one page cannot stand for every kind of website."}}]},{"id":"map","label":"Where it stood","nav":"Where it stood","heading":"The others had more features and were easier to use","blocks":[{"p":"I then placed all four tools on a simple map. Left to right is how many features a tool has. Bottom to top is how good it is to use."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/map.webp","alt":"Two-by-two map with features on one axis and ease of use on the other. Three competitors sit top right and GetMetaData sits bottom left, with a dashed arrow pointing up","caption":"The map from my deck, redrawn. The three competitors sit top right and GetMetaData sits bottom left. The dashed arrow is the aim of the redesign, not a result.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"My brief was the interface, not the feature list. So the goal was to move the tool up the map, and to keep its easy export on the way."}]},{"id":"personas","label":"Who it is for","nav":"Who it is for","heading":"An expert and a beginner read the same panel","blocks":[{"p":"An SEO tool has two very different kinds of reader. One does this for a living. The other runs a business and wants the website to be found. To keep both in mind I wrote two personas, made-up people who each stand for a type of user."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/personas.webp","alt":"Two persona cards without photos: Rutik, a 29-year-old SEO engineer, and Sneha, a 31-year-old content marketer and small business owner, each with a quote, goals, a frustration and what matters to them","caption":"Rutik wants something simple that helps him at work. Sneha is not technical and finds normal SEO tools a hassle to understand. Compare the two frustrations: one is about usefulness, the other about being understood.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"aside":{"title":"What these are not.","detail":"I wrote these personas as a design aid. They are not backed by interviews, so I treat them as a way to frame decisions and not as research findings."}}]},{"id":"features","label":"From review to design","nav":"Review to design","heading":"Each change traces back to a finding","blocks":[{"p":"I went through the weaknesses one at a time and asked what the interface could do about each. I also kept an eye on the strengths, in the competitors and in GetMetaData, so the redesign would not throw away something that worked."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/review-to-design.webp","alt":"Diagram pairing six findings from the review with what the prototype does about each. The sixth, the repeated-data bug, is marked as not answered by the design","caption":"Findings on the left, answers on the right. The last row is in red on purpose: the bug is real, but no layout can fix it.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The old tool's one clear advantage was showing every metric at one time. Splitting the data into sections gives some of that up, and comparing items in different sections takes more clicks. The Overview screen keeps the basics of a page together in one place."}}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Structure","heading":"Eight sections in one side menu","blocks":[{"p":"The side menu has eight sections. Five of them match the five kinds of data in the brief. Social, Advanced and Settings are additions."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/structure.webp","alt":"Map of the eight menu sections, showing which kind of data from the brief each one covers and whether it exists as a finished screen, a paper sketch or a menu item only","caption":"The eight sections in menu order. Solid outlines are finished screens, Images is a paper sketch, and dashed boxes are named in the menu only.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"My deck shows two of these as finished screens, Overview and Headings, and a third, Images, as a paper sketch."}]},{"id":"sketches","label":"Sketches","heading":"Three screens on paper first","blocks":[{"p":"I started on paper. A wireframe is a rough sketch of a screen that shows where things go, without colour or detail. I drew three: the overview, the headings and the images."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/wire-overview.webp","alt":"Photographed pencil sketch of the overview screen with a menu on the left and two cards on the right","caption":"Overview. The menu, a main card with two buttons, and a second card below.","width":800,"height":594},{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/wire-headings.webp","alt":"Photographed pencil sketch of the headings screen with a strip of counts above an indented list","caption":"Headings. A strip of counts above an indented list.","width":800,"height":589},{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/wire-images.webp","alt":"Photographed pencil sketch of the images screen with three thumbnails and two counts","caption":"Images. Thumbnails, then two counts.","width":800,"height":507}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"The pencil is faint in the photos, so here are the same three sketches traced cleanly."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/wireframes-traced.webp","alt":"The three paper sketches redrawn as clean line drawings: overview, headings and images","caption":"A tracing of the three sketches. Most of the final layout is already here: the menu on the left, the two buttons in the corner of the panel, the count strip above the heading tree.","width":1600,"height":790}]},{"p":"A few things changed between paper and screen. The first sketch is headed getMetaTags, and the finished screens say getMetaData. The sketched count strip stops at H5, and the finished one runs to H6. The first sketch also has a switch in the top corner that the finished Overview does not."}]},{"id":"overview","label":"The design","nav":"The Overview screen","heading":"The basics of a page, each with a verdict","blocks":[{"p":"The Overview is the first screen. It lists the page's title, description, address and canonical link, which is the address a page names as its official one. Below those come the keywords, the word count and the publisher."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/annotated-overview.webp","alt":"The Overview screen with six numbered notes explaining the menu, the Export and Copy buttons, the pass and fail chips, the missing value and the keywords","caption":"The Overview, part by part. Start with notes 3 and 4: the chips are what turn a list of values into a list of verdicts.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The chip on the right of each row carries the main idea. Someone who knows SEO can run an eye down the chips and stop at the red one. Someone who does not gets a plain signal that a row needs attention before reading any detail."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/details.webp","alt":"Close crops of the Overview panel showing the pass and fail chips and a help tooltip open beside the Title label","caption":"Two details up close. The page in the prototype is the same portfolio site I used for the review, so the 25 and the 85 are real character counts.","width":1600,"height":1090}]},{"p":"The tooltips come straight from the review. All three competitors had them, and for a reader like Sneha they are where a word like \"canonical\" gets explained."}]},{"id":"headings","label":"The design","nav":"The Headings screen","heading":"Headings shown the way they nest","blocks":[{"p":"Headings are the titles and subtitles inside a page. They are numbered from H1, the main title, down to H6. This screen answers two questions about them: how many of each level does the page have, and how do they sit under one another?"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/annotated-headings.webp","alt":"The Headings screen with five numbered notes explaining the menu, the buttons, the count strip and the heading tree","caption":"The Headings screen, part by part. The count strip answers the first question and the tree answers the second.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A tree gets long on a page with many headings. That is the same long-column problem I marked down in two competitors. The count strip at the top gives the short answer, and the tree is there when you need the detail."}}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","nav":"What comes next","heading":"What I would do before calling it done","blocks":[{"p":"The prototype shows how the panel could look and read. It does not show that people find things faster with it. That needs a test, and a few other things need doing first."},{"steps":[{"title":"Fix the bug on its own terms.","detail":"The extension should show its data once. I would check that separately, with its own pass or fail, before judging the new layout."},{"title":"Test real tasks with both kinds of reader.","detail":"Ask someone new to SEO and someone experienced to find a missing description, check the image descriptions and export what they found."},{"title":"Try awkward pages.","detail":"Use pages with very long values, missing values and a great many headings, and see where the layout breaks."},{"title":"Bring every section to the same level.","detail":"My deck shows Overview and Headings finished and Images as a sketch. All eight sections need the same care."},{"title":"Tidy one label.","detail":"The menu says Headings and the panel title says Headers. I would use one word in both places."}]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Easier to read is not the same as correct","blocks":[{"p":"The most useful thing I did here was keep two problems apart. One was how the data is laid out. The other was whether the data is right. A cleaner panel that still showed everything twice would look better and be just as wrong."},{"p":"The review also taught me to look for what a weak tool does well. GetMetaData sat lowest on my map, yet it was the only one of the four that showed every metric at once, and it exported easily. Export stayed in the redesign because of that."},{"p":"If I did it again, I would talk to a few real users before writing the personas."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","headline":"Let the work lead the portfolio."}},"steve-wilson":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","kind":"ux","headline":"A designer's portfolio site, worked out in four pages.","summary":"Steve Wilson is a made-up designer. I used him to design a portfolio site concept: a home page, a list of work, a project page and a contact form.","role":"Visual design and page structure","status":"Independent visual concept","context":"Designer portfolio · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"page types"},{"value":"Type-led","label":"visual direction"},{"value":"Concept","label":"not a client engagement"}],"problem":"A portfolio has to communicate what a designer does, offer a convincing sample of their work, and make contact straightforward. This early concept uses the fictional Steve Wilson identity to explore that sequence through a home page, work listing, project-detail template, and contact destination.","process":["The available evidence is a four-page visual artifact, including a long project-detail template. It contains sample copy, mock project names, placeholder results, and a sample client attribution. Those are layout content, not my client history or achieved results.","I organised the pages so that the introduction establishes the designer’s role, the work listing exposes project categories and tools, and the detail template reserves space for the problem, process, outcome, and lessons. No original research or testing record accompanies it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use typography to establish the first impression","choice":"The concept combines a large name treatment with a restrained dark background and a bright accent.","reason":"The visual hierarchy gives the identity a clear point of entry before visitors scan services or projects.","tradeoff":"A strong name treatment cannot replace useful positioning copy; I would make the designer’s particular value more specific."},{"title":"Separate browsing from deep reading","choice":"The work listing acts as an index, while project details have a longer narrative structure.","reason":"Visitors can skim the portfolio before deciding which story deserves more attention.","tradeoff":"A listing only helps if its labels explain the work rather than relying on unfamiliar project names."},{"title":"Give each project the same narrative spine","choice":"The template includes role, problem, process, results, takeaways, and final product.","reason":"A repeated structure helps a reviewer compare contributions across projects.","tradeoff":"In the archive, much of that structure is empty sample content. A template is useful scaffolding, not evidence of a completed case study."}],"outcome":"I designed a homepage, work index, project-detail template and contact page around the fictional Steve Wilson identity. The exercise helped me explore the difference between scanning a body of work and reading one project closely. Names, client credits and result figures in the original template are sample content.","reflection":"I would keep the clear browsing structure and rewrite the project pages around actual decisions. A well-proportioned template is useful, but the writing and evidence inside it are what make a portfolio convincing.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace the sample project with one real story, then test whether a reviewer can identify the role, central decision, and outcome in a short scan.","Proposed UI review: verify contrast, keyboard navigation, responsive reading order, and a concise contact path before implementation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, pp. 1–4","detail":"Home, work list, project-detail template and contact; sample identities and results are concept content."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p01.webp","caption":"Original home-page concept: identity, introduction, and services.","alt":"Dark Steve Wilson home-page concept with a yellow accent.","width":1600,"height":1800},{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing.","width":1600,"height":1800}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/swportfolio.webp","readingMinutes":7,"pdfPage":15,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Let the work [[lead the portfolio.]]","scene":"Picture a reviewer opening a designer’s portfolio between other applications. The first page has to establish what the designer does and make one relevant project easy to choose.","question":"What makes someone want to move from a first impression into the work?","insight":"The original concept already separates [[browsing from deep reading]]. Its project template creates room for a story, but that room still needs real decisions and evidence.","pivot":"I used the retrospective to connect the visual hierarchy to the reviewer’s sequence: orient, select, investigate, then make contact.","takeaway":"The layout earns attention; the evidence earns confidence.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"Understand the designer’s role."},{"title":"Choose","detail":"Scan a focused work index."},{"title":"Investigate","detail":"Open a project with room for contribution and process."}],"decisionLeads":["Establish a clear first impression.","Let browsing remain lightweight.","Give the detail page something real to say."],"annotations":[{"title":"A type-led opening","detail":"The name treatment establishes the visual entry point."},{"title":"A separate work index","detail":"Projects can be scanned before opening the longer story."}],"evidenceTag":"Original visual concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Prospective client reviewing a portfolio","context":"Scanning for relevant work before deciding whether to make contact.","need":"Understand the designer’s offer and inspect a suitable example.","friction":"A polished introduction alone does not explain the work.","implication":"Move quickly from identity to selected projects."}},"theme":{"layout":"portfolio","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f5f4ed","dim":"#c3c3b8","line":"#3f3f37","accent":"#f1d548","wash":"#343019","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A first impression","process":"The useful signal","decisions":"Identity into evidence","outcome":"The portfolio concept","reflection":"Adding project depth"},"decisionHeading":"Lead from the work into the case study.","resultHeading":"A strong front door, with depth still to add."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing.","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","width":1600,"height":1800},"v2":{"title":"A designer's portfolio site, worked out in [[four pages]].","hook":"Steve Wilson is a made-up designer. I used him to design a portfolio site concept: a home page, a list of work, a project page and a contact form.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Visual design and page structure"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself. No client asked for it"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop website, drawn 1920 pixels wide"},{"label":"Scope","value":"Four pages: Home, Work, Project, Contact"},{"label":"Method","value":"Layout, type and colour. No research behind it"},{"label":"Status","value":"Visual concept. Not built, not tested with users"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original pages (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original pages","summary":["The problem: a designer's site has three jobs. Say who the designer is, show a few projects, and make it easy to get in touch.","What I did: I set myself that brief and designed four desktop pages around a made-up designer called Steve Wilson.","The key idea: keep choosing and reading apart. The work page is a short numbered list. Each project then gets one long page built from the same template.","Where it stands: a visual concept. There was no research and no testing, and it was never built. The name, the client and the numbers in the mock-ups are sample text, not my record."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/lead.webp","alt":"The four pages of the concept side by side: home, the work list, the long project page and contact","caption":"The whole concept. Home and Work are two screens tall, Contact is one, and the project page (third from the left) runs to seven. The stock portrait on the home page is swapped for a grey placeholder here.","width":2400,"height":1050},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"I set the brief myself: who, what, how to reach me","blocks":[{"p":"Nobody asked for this site. I wanted to practise designing a portfolio, so I wrote the brief myself. A visitor should learn who the designer is, see a few projects and find a way to get in touch, in that order."},{"p":"I did no research for it and tested it with no one. I invented a designer, Steve Wilson, so the pages had a name and some content to hold. So this page is about the design itself: how the pages are arranged, and the type and colour choices."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/site-map.webp","alt":"Diagram of the site: a menu with three links, the Home, Work, Project and Contact pages, and a footer strip shared by all","caption":"The four pages as a map. The menu has three doors. The project page is the only one you reach from inside another page.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"aside":{"title":"What is not real.","detail":"Steve Wilson does not exist. His projects, the client named on the project page and the result figures are filler I typed to test the layout. None of it is my client history or my results."}}]},{"id":"home","label":"Home page","nav":"Home page","heading":"The name is the loudest thing on the site","blocks":[{"p":"The home page opens with a small greeting and then the name, set 250 pixels tall. Nothing else on the site is bigger than 75. I wanted a visitor to know whose site this is before reading a sentence."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/home-name.webp","alt":"Close crop of the home page: the words Hi, I am, then Steve in yellow and Wilson in white, with four yellow social icons below","caption":"The name at close range. The first name takes the yellow and the surname stays white. The four social links below use the same yellow.","width":1100,"height":902}]},{"p":"Under the name come a short About me paragraph and four services: graphic design, content writing, presentation design and logo design."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/annotated-home.webp","alt":"The full home page with seven numbered callouts, from the menu at the top to the yellow footer strip","caption":"The home page from top to bottom. Follow the numbers: each one is a separate job the page does.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/home-services.webp","alt":"Lower half of the home page: an About Me paragraph, four service tiles with yellow icons, and the yellow footer strip","caption":"The lower half. Look at the service tiles: each has a soft yellow glow, the one decorative effect on the page.","width":1400,"height":788}]},{"p":"The trade-off: a huge name says nothing about why this designer is worth hiring. If I did it again, I would put one clear line about the work next to it."}]},{"id":"system","label":"Type, colour, layout","nav":"Type and colour","heading":"Eight type sizes, three colours and a few fixed widths","blocks":[{"p":"I measured the artwork to see what system it really uses. There are eight type sizes across the four pages. Section headings are 75 pixels, body text is 35 and the menu is 30."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/type-scale.webp","alt":"Chart of the eight type sizes used, from 250 pixels for the name down to 25 pixels for form hints, with where each is used","caption":"Every type size on the site, drawn to scale. The jump from 75 to 250 is what makes the name stand out.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"The colour is just as narrow. Every page is black, with white text and one yellow. On the home page black covers 75% of the area and yellow less than 5%. Because yellow is rare, it works as a pointer: it marks the name, the page you are on and the button to press."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/palette.webp","alt":"Colour chart: black, yellow, white and one dark grey, a bar showing how much of the home page each covers, and three contrast pairs","caption":"The palette, with how much of the home page each colour covers. Yellow on black has a contrast of 12 to 1, so it stays easy to read.","width":1600,"height":1030}]},{"p":"The layout has no strict grid, meaning a fixed set of columns that everything lines up to. It leans on repeated sizes and on centring instead."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/grid.webp","alt":"Three pages with red bands drawn over them to show column widths: the work list, the service tiles on the home page and the contact form","caption":"The widths that repeat. On the work page every tile is the same size and the rows are evenly spaced. The service tiles and the contact form are centred on the page.","width":1600,"height":1370}]},{"p":"Measuring also showed two loose ends. Headings start between 130 and 167 pixels from the left edge, depending on the page. And the project names in the work list are set in a second, plainer typeface. I would fix both."}]},{"id":"work","label":"Work page","heading":"The work page is a list you can scan in seconds","blocks":[{"p":"The work page shows four projects and nothing else. Each row has a large outline number, a colour tile, the project name, the kind of work and the tool used. There are no descriptions to read. This page is for choosing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/work.webp","alt":"The work page: four numbered rows, each with a coloured tile, a project name, the type of work and the tool used","caption":"The whole work page. Four rows fit with room to spare, so nothing competes with the list.","width":1200,"height":1350,"kind":"tall"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/annotated-work.webp","alt":"One row of the work list enlarged, with five numbered callouts for the number, the tile, the name, the type of work and the tool","caption":"One row, taken apart. The eye goes from the number to the colour, then to the three lines of text.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"The four projects are sample entries. Three of them use well-known product names and logos as stand-ins. None of them is client work."},{"p":"The trade-off: a list this bare depends on its labels. A project name that nobody knows would need a line saying what it is."}]},{"id":"project","label":"Project page","heading":"Every project gets the same long page","blocks":[{"p":"Choosing a project opens the long page. It is a template, which means the same fourteen blocks in the same order for every project. A reviewer who has read one knows where to look in the next."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/template-map.webp","alt":"The full project page shown top to bottom beside a list of its fourteen blocks, grouped as who and what, answers first, then the story, and where next","caption":"The project page as a map. The summary, results and lessons come first. The story of problem, process, personas and solutions comes after, for anyone who wants it.","width":1600,"height":1640}]},{"p":"That order is the main decision on this page. A reviewer in a hurry gets the answer before the method."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/annotated-template-top.webp","alt":"Top of the project page with four numbered callouts: the outline number, the boxed project name, a grey strip of five facts and a purple cover tile","caption":"The top of the page answers the basic questions before any story starts. The values are sample text, and the sample client name has been painted out.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/template-results.webp","alt":"Project page blocks titled Overview, Results and Key Takeaways, filled with prompts and placeholder figures","caption":"The answers. The bullet points are prompts for the writer, such as whether the project shipped. The figures 123%, 123+ and $123 are placeholders, not results.","width":1300,"height":1380},{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/template-video.webp","alt":"Project page blocks titled Final Product, showing a phone outline with a play button, and Problem, with three filler bullet points","caption":"The Final product block is a slot for a video of the finished design. Below it the story begins, in lorem ipsum, the filler Latin that designers use before real text exists.","width":1300,"height":1199}],"layout":"two"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/template-more.webp","alt":"End of the project page: the heading More of my work, two project tiles and the yellow footer strip","caption":"The page ends by offering two more projects, so a visitor always has somewhere to go next.","width":1300,"height":484}]},{"p":"The weakness is plain in the pictures: the template is empty. It is scaffolding, and it proves nothing until a real project fills it."}]},{"id":"contact","label":"Contact page","heading":"Three fields and one button","blocks":[{"p":"The contact page is the shortest, a single screen. It has fields for name, email and message, and a yellow Send button. It is the only page where white takes up real space, about 18% of it, because the fields are white."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/contact.webp","alt":"The contact page: the heading Contact Me, white fields for name, email and message, and a yellow Send button","caption":"Contact. The button is the only yellow between the menu and the footer, so it is hard to miss. The grey hint text in the fields is too faint: its contrast with white is about 1.8 to 1, where 4.5 to 1 is the usual minimum.","width":1400,"height":788}]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"What it would need to become a real site","blocks":[{"p":"What exists is four flat pictures of a desktop site. To take it further, I would do these things in order."},{"steps":[{"title":"Fill the template with one real project","detail":"Then ask a reviewer to find the role, the main decision and the outcome in a quick scan."},{"title":"Design it for a phone","detail":"Every page here is drawn at one desktop width."},{"title":"Check it can be used by everyone","detail":"Contrast, keyboard use and reading order, before any build."},{"title":"Tidy the system","detail":"One typeface, one left edge and darker hint text in the form."}]},{"p":"What I took from it: a layout can earn a visitor's attention, but only real work earns their trust. I would keep the split between a list for choosing and a page for reading, and write the project pages around real decisions."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","headline":"A furniture shop organised around the room."}},"gloria-furniture":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","kind":"ux","headline":"One long page that takes a furniture shopper from looking to choosing.","summary":"Gloria Furniture is a storefront concept: a single long page that opens with photos of furniture and ends with ten products, their prices and a place to ask for help.","role":"Visual design and page structure","status":"Storefront concept","context":"E-commerce · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"featured product groups"},{"value":"Category-led","label":"browsing"},{"value":"Concept","label":"no sales results recorded"}],"problem":"Furniture is both a visual and a practical purchase. The original Gloria concept tries to support inspiration at the top of the page, category exploration in the middle, and concrete products with prices further down. The design question is how to make those modes feel like one useful shopping path.","process":["The surviving PDF is a single long storefront design, not a research report. I reviewed its hero, category imagery, quality messaging, product cards, and service links to make the underlying structure explicit. The archived dollar prices, warranty copy, and contact details are sample storefront content, not verified business facts.","This is a retrospective reading of the design. The available artifact does not establish customer interviews, a checkout implementation, or commercial performance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with inspiration, then offer more specific choices","choice":"The hero leads into visual categories before the featured Home Decor and Furniture groups.","reason":"A shopper can begin with a broad interest and progressively narrow to a product, instead of meeting a full catalogue immediately.","tradeoff":"The path would need descriptive category labels and accessible images; atmosphere alone does not tell a shopper where to go."},{"title":"Make product cards comparable","choice":"The featured groups repeat name, price, image, Add to Cart, and Buy Now treatments.","reason":"A stable card structure supports scanning across products and makes purchase actions predictable.","tradeoff":"Two equally prominent purchase actions can compete. Today I would test a primary product-detail path before offering immediate purchase everywhere."},{"title":"Keep practical reassurance near the shopping journey","choice":"The concept includes a quality band and footer links for stores, warranty claims, and custom requests.","reason":"Furniture decisions often require service and fulfilment information as well as appealing photography.","tradeoff":"Those claims need real policies and product information. The visual concept alone cannot establish quality or a working support service."}],"outcome":"I designed a storefront landing page that moves from room inspiration into categories and comparable product cards. Prices and service information sit alongside the imagery so browsing has a practical direction. The artifact is a visual concept; it does not include a working checkout.","reflection":"I would next add the details someone needs before buying furniture: dimensions, materials, stock and delivery coverage. Those are more useful at this stage than another promotional section.","nextSteps":["Proposed task: find a product within a budget, compare two options, and explain delivery expectations. Record missing information as well as completion.","Proposed iteration: build a product-detail page and a narrow-screen catalogue, using real product data and one clearly prioritised action."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Full storefront: hero, category browsing, quality messages, featured groups and service footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original hierarchy moves from an aspirational hero to category-led browsing.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture hero and category layout.","width":1400,"height":1969},{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups.","width":1400,"height":1969}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/gloriafurniture.webp","readingMinutes":6,"pdfPage":18,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A furniture shop [[organised around the room.]]","scene":"Imagine finding a piece of furniture you like, then asking whether it fits the room, the budget, and the practicalities of buying it. A storefront has to support both kinds of attention.","question":"How does visual browsing become a useful buying decision?","insight":"The concept moves from [[inspiration to categories to comparable products]]. That sequence is the useful foundation; dimensions and fulfilment would make the next step stronger.","pivot":"I organised the retrospective around how the page narrows a shopper’s options, then identified the information still needed to support a purchase.","takeaway":"A beautiful product card should help someone decide what to do next.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start from an image or a category."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Use a repeated card pattern to scan options."},{"title":"Decide","detail":"Bring practical product and delivery information into the next step."}],"decisionLeads":["Give inspiration a direction.","Make comparison predictable.","Connect reassurance to real information."],"annotations":[{"title":"Category-led discovery","detail":"The upper page offers ways into the catalogue."},{"title":"Repeated product cards","detail":"The lower page carries names, prices, and actions in a consistent pattern."}],"evidenceTag":"Original storefront concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Furniture shopper making a shortlist","context":"Browsing room inspiration and comparing categories.","need":"Find a relevant collection and enough detail to continue.","friction":"A visual catalogue can leave practical questions unanswered.","implication":"Connect inspiration to categories and a next product decision."}},"theme":{"layout":"catalogue","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf8","ink":"#2b2320","dim":"#79615a","line":"#e8d9d1","accent":"#bc273a","wash":"#f9e2e2","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A room in mind","process":"Inspiration needs a route","decisions":"From room to product","outcome":"The storefront concept","reflection":"Beyond the first screen"},"decisionHeading":"Help shoppers compare a collection.","resultHeading":"A catalogue organised around the room."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups.","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"v2":{"title":"One long page that takes a furniture shopper from [[looking to choosing]].","hook":"Gloria Furniture is a storefront concept: a single long page that opens with photos of furniture and ends with ten products, their prices and a place to ask for help.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Visual design and page structure"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself. No client asked for it"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop web page, drawn 1920 pixels wide"},{"label":"Scope","value":"One long landing page in five bands"},{"label":"Method","value":"Layout, type and colour. No research behind it"},{"label":"Status","value":"Visual concept. No shop behind it, not built, not tested"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original page (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original page","summary":["The problem: furniture is a visual purchase and a practical one. A shop's front page has to inspire first, then give names and prices.","What I did: I set myself that brief and designed one long desktop page in five bands: a hero, categories, quality promises, two rows of products and a footer.","The key idea: narrow the choice step by step. Photos first, then five categories, then ten products on one repeated card so they are easy to compare.","Where it stands: a visual concept. There was no research and no testing, and there is no cart or checkout. The prices, warranty and contact details are illustrative."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/lead.webp","alt":"The first screen of the Gloria Furniture page, with a headline, a Buy Now button and six photos, beside a small view of the whole long page","caption":"The first screen at full size, with the whole page beside it for scale. The page is five screens long. The contact details in its footer are painted out.","width":2400,"height":1230},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"I set the brief: one page, from looking to choosing","blocks":[{"p":"No shop asked for this page. I set the brief myself, to practise designing a storefront. Furniture is a visual purchase and a practical one. A shopper wants to be inspired first, and then wants names and prices. I wanted one page that follows that order."},{"p":"There was no research behind it and nobody tested it. So this page is about the design: how the long page is cut into bands, what each band looks like up close, and what the type, colour and layout really measure."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/page-map.webp","alt":"The whole Gloria page shown top to bottom beside a list of its eight parts, grouped into five steps: look, browse, trust, choose and ask","caption":"The page as a map. Read the five coloured bars from top to bottom: each one is a step in the shopper's path.","width":1600,"height":1640}]},{"aside":{"title":"What is not real.","detail":"The prices, the three-year warranty and the contact details on the page are illustrative. None of the buttons work. There is no cart and no checkout."}}]},{"id":"hero","label":"First screen","heading":"The first screen makes one offer","blocks":[{"p":"The first screen has one headline, one button and six photos. The headline is 88 pixels tall, the largest type on the page, split into a red line and a black line. The photos sit in four narrow columns at different heights."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/annotated-hero.webp","alt":"The first screen with six numbered callouts: logo, menu, headline, Buy Now button, photo collage and the dividing line","caption":"The first screen, taken apart. The red button is the only solid red shape here, so the eye lands on it after the headline.","width":1600,"height":1230}]},{"p":"The trade-off: the button says Buy Now before a single product has been shown. Looking back, a label that promises browsing would suit this spot better."}]},{"id":"browse","label":"Browse and trust","nav":"Categories and quality","heading":"Five ways in, then four reasons to trust","blocks":[{"p":"The second screen offers five categories: Furniture, Lighting, Re-Styling, Decor and Art & More. Each is a tall photo with a red label. Every other photo sits lower, which keeps the row from looking like a table."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/categories.webp","alt":"The We are Gloria band: five tall photos labelled Furniture, Lighting, Re-Styling, Decor and Art and More, with the second and fourth set lower","caption":"The five categories. The photo does the selling and the small red label does the naming.","width":1500,"height":829}]},{"p":"The third screen is about trust. Four icon tiles carry four promises: 3 Year Warranty, Top Quality Material, All QC Passed and Best-in-class."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/quality.webp","alt":"The Symbol of Quality band: four pale pink tiles with red icons, labelled 3 Year Warranty, Top Quality Material, All QC Passed and Best-in-class","caption":"The quality band comes before any price, so the reassurance is read first. The promises are sample text.","width":1500,"height":720}]},{"p":"The trade-off: promises like these need real policies behind them. A concept can show where reassurance belongs on a page. It cannot make the claims true."}]},{"id":"products","label":"Products","heading":"Ten products on one card design","blocks":[{"p":"The last band before the footer holds two rows of five products, Home Decor and Furniture. All ten use the same card: photo, name, price, three colour dots and two buttons. Because the card never changes, the eye can run along a row and compare."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/home-decor.webp","alt":"The Home Decor row: five product cards for a stool, three vases and a table lamp, each with a price and two buttons","caption":"The Home Decor row. Run your eye along the prices: they sit in the same place on every card.","width":1600,"height":584}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/annotated-card.webp","alt":"One product card enlarged with six numbered callouts: photo, name, price, colour dots, Add to Cart and Buy Now","caption":"One card, taken apart. The order on the card is the order of a decision: what it is, what it costs, which colour, then what to do.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/furniture.webp","alt":"The Furniture row: five product cards for a couch, a chair, drawers, a coffee table and another chair, with a Visit Shop button below","caption":"The Furniture row uses the same card for much higher prices. Visit Shop, bottom right, is the only way on to the full shop.","width":1600,"height":692}]},{"p":"The weak point is the pair of buttons. Add to Cart and Buy Now sit side by side at the same size on every card, so neither is clearly the main one."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/buttons.webp","alt":"Bar chart counting the buttons on the page: Buy Now 11 times, Add to Cart 10 times, Visit Shop once","caption":"Every button on the page, counted. Twenty-one of the 22 ask the shopper to buy at once.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"Today I would give each card one main action that opens a product page, and test that before offering instant purchase everywhere."}]},{"id":"system","label":"Type, colour, layout","nav":"Type and colour","heading":"Type, colour and layout, measured","blocks":[{"p":"I measured the artwork to see how tidy the system is. It uses ten type sizes. Two pairs are almost the same (88 and 85, 36 and 35), so fewer sizes would do the same job."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/type-scale.webp","alt":"Chart of the ten type sizes on the page, from 88 pixels for the headline down to 15 pixels for the card buttons, with where each is used","caption":"Every type size on the page, drawn to scale. Compare the top row with the bottom one: the card buttons are set at 15 pixels.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"p":"Colour is simpler. Half the page is white, about a tenth is one pure red, and four pale pinks sit behind the header, cards and tiles. The red is strong enough to lead the eye. But white text on it has a contrast of 4 to 1, just under the 4.5 to 1 usually asked of small text."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/palette.webp","alt":"Colour chart: red, white, four pale pinks and black, a bar showing how much of the page each covers, and four contrast pairs","caption":"The palette, with how much of the page each colour covers. Look at the last row: the pale grey Featured tag is the hardest thing on the page to read.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"p":"The three main rows each use their own column widths. So the edges of the category photos, the quality tiles and the product cards do not line up down the page."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/grid.webp","alt":"Three bands of the page with teal column guides drawn over them: five category photos, four quality tiles and five product cards","caption":"The columns in each row. One shared set of columns would make the long page feel calmer.","width":1600,"height":1650}]}]},{"id":"footer","label":"Footer","heading":"The footer answers the practical questions","blocks":[{"p":"The page ends in a solid red footer. It names the shop's city, Panjim in Goa, and lists five service links: Our Stores, Warranty Claim, Custom Requests, Bulk Orders and Open a Franchise. The copyright line is dated 2022."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/footer.webp","alt":"The red footer with the Gloria Furniture logo, a Panjim, Goa address and five service links, above a pale pink copyright bar","caption":"The left two thirds of the footer. The right-hand column holds a placeholder email and phone numbers, which I have left out.","width":1400,"height":578}]},{"p":"Buying furniture takes service as well as photos, so the links for stores, warranty and custom orders sit where people look for help."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"What it would need before anyone could buy","blocks":[{"p":"What exists is one flat picture of a desktop page. To take it further, I would do these things in order."},{"steps":[{"title":"Design a product page","detail":"Dimensions, materials, stock and delivery area. Those are the facts someone needs before buying furniture."},{"title":"Give each card one main action","detail":"Then test it: ask someone to find a product within a budget, compare two options and explain the delivery terms."},{"title":"Design it for a phone","detail":"The page is drawn at one desktop width."},{"title":"Put real content in","detail":"Real products, prices and policies in place of the samples."},{"title":"Tidy the system","detail":"Fewer type sizes, one set of columns and stronger contrast for small text."}]},{"p":"What I took from it: a good-looking product card still has to help someone decide what to do next."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","headline":"Choosing a boat before making an enquiry."}},"fun-cruises":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Choose a boat before you enquire.","summary":"Fun Cruises Goa is a landing-page concept for a yacht-hire business. I designed one long page that shows each boat's seats and starting rate before a visitor has to ask.","role":"Page structure, fleet browsing and visual design","status":"Website redesign concept","context":"Travel & leisure · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"fleet filter dimensions"},{"value":"Capacity","label":"visible on listings"},{"value":"Enquiry","label":"core next step"}],"problem":"A visitor choosing a boat needs to know whether it fits their group and budget before making an enquiry. The Fun Cruises Goa artifact contains the building blocks for that decision: a prominent booking action, a description of the experience, fleet filters, and listings with seating capacity and starting hourly prices.","process":["I reviewed the original long-page design as a visitor journey from initial interest to a suitable fleet option. The available PDF shows the landing and fleet-discovery surface. It does not include completed reservation, payment, or availability states, so I describe the scope as discovery and enquiry.","The rates, capacities, address, and response-time copy visible in the old concept are historical design content. This case study uses their placement to explain hierarchy, rather than presenting them as current offers."],"decisions":[{"title":"Bring decision criteria into the fleet list","choice":"The design offers seating, price-order, and boat-type controls, with capacity and starting rate on individual listings.","reason":"A visitor should be able to rule out unsuitable options without opening every boat’s detail page.","tradeoff":"Filters depend on consistent inventory data. Empty results and combinations that return no suitable boat would need explicit handling."},{"title":"Support both ready-to-book and exploratory visitors","choice":"The concept has a hero booking action and an enquiry route alongside fleet details.","reason":"Some visitors know what they want; others need clarification before committing. The page makes both next steps available.","tradeoff":"Booking, getting a quote, and learning more can become competing calls to action. I would clarify which action each listing should lead to."},{"title":"Use imagery with practical content","choice":"Fleet photographs sit beside capacity, starting price, and short descriptions.","reason":"The images convey the experience while the text helps establish whether the option is suitable.","tradeoff":"A starting hourly price does not communicate the full cost. Minimum duration, inclusions, fees, and availability would need to be explained next."}],"outcome":"I redesigned the landing and fleet-browsing page around boat type, capacity and starting rate. The imagery introduces the experience, while the listings give visitors something concrete to compare before enquiring. Availability and a completed reservation flow are outside the delivered concept.","reflection":"An enquiry should not look like a confirmed booking. I would make that distinction explicit, then add the inclusions, accessibility details and cancellation terms needed to discuss a trip. Prices in the original design are historical concept content.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor planning for a group to choose a suitable option, explain the expected cost, and identify what remains to confirm.","Proposed extension: add a boat-detail page with availability, inclusions, accessibility information, cancellation terms, and a clear enquiry handoff."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Hero, quote action, seating/price/type filters, fleet cards and contact footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_01.webp","caption":"The upper page connects the experience to a visible booking action and fleet filters.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises Goa hero, introduction and fleet filters.","width":1400,"height":2166},{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content.","width":1400,"height":2166}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/funcruises.webp","readingMinutes":7,"pdfPage":21,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Choosing a boat [[before making an enquiry.]]","scene":"Picture planning an outing with a group. Before an enquiry, the practical questions arrive: how many people fit, what does it cost, and which type of boat makes sense?","question":"Which questions can the fleet list answer before a visitor opens every option?","insight":"The strongest part of the concept is its [[comparable fleet information]]: seating, starting rate, and boat type accompany the photographs.","pivot":"I treated the landing page as a route into a suitable enquiry, with discovery criteria that narrow the choices.","takeaway":"Good discovery makes the next conversation more informed.","journey":[{"title":"Narrow","detail":"Use capacity, price, and type to find suitable options."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Read practical information alongside the experience."},{"title":"Enquire","detail":"Confirm availability, inclusions, and the complete cost."}],"decisionLeads":["Answer the comparison questions early.","Support different levels of booking readiness.","Explain what the starting rate leaves to confirm."],"annotations":[{"title":"Decision criteria near the fleet","detail":"Filters appear before the visitor scans individual boats."},{"title":"Imagery with practical content","detail":"Listings pair the experience with capacity and price information."}],"evidenceTag":"Original fleet-discovery concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Group organiser considering a boat","context":"Comparing options for a group occasion in Goa.","need":"Identify a suitable boat before making an enquiry.","friction":"Capacity, starting price, and suitability must be read together.","implication":"Keep the practical comparison close to each listing."}},"theme":{"layout":"voyage","mode":"light","paper":"#fffcf6","ink":"#203c40","dim":"#5a7174","line":"#d6ded7","accent":"#866117","wash":"#faedcb","bodyFont":"Lato","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Planning for a group","process":"The practical shortlist","decisions":"Designing the enquiry","outcome":"The fleet experience","reflection":"Questions before booking"},"decisionHeading":"Help a group compare the boats.","resultHeading":"A clearer route to a suitable enquiry."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content.","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","width":1400,"height":2166},"v2":{"title":"Choose a boat [[before you enquire]].","hook":"Fun Cruises Goa is a landing-page concept for a yacht-hire business. I designed one long page that shows each boat's seats and starting rate before a visitor has to ask.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Page structure, fleet browsing and visual design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2022"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself: redesign the home page of a yacht-hire business in Goa"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop web page, designed 1920 px wide"},{"label":"Scope","value":"One landing page. Finding a boat and starting an enquiry, with no booking flow"},{"label":"Status","value":"Design concept. Not built, tested or launched"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original page design (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original design","summary":["The problem: someone planning a boat trip for a group has two questions before they ask about a boat. Will everyone fit, and roughly what will it cost?","What I did: I designed one long landing page with a hero, an About block, a fleet list with three filters, and a footer.","The key idea: put the seats and a starting rate on every boat card, so a visitor can rule boats out without opening each one.","Where it stands: a 2022 concept with no research behind it. It stops at the enquiry. There is no booking flow, and the prices in the mock-up are old sample content."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/lead.webp","alt":"The whole Fun Cruises Goa page shown in three columns: hero and About, the fleet filters and first boat card, and the second boat card with the footer","caption":"The whole page, read left to right: the hero and About block, the fleet filters with the first boat, then the second boat and the footer. Contact details are covered and guests' faces are blurred.","width":2400,"height":848},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"I set myself a brief: help a group pick a boat","blocks":[{"p":"This was a brief I set myself. I did no interviews and no testing, so this page is about the design and nothing else."},{"p":"I pictured someone organising a day out on the water for a group. Before they send an enquiry they have a few plain questions. I made answering those questions the job of the page. They are my assumption, not a research finding."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/questions.webp","alt":"Diagram pairing four visitor questions with the parts of the page that answer them","caption":"The four questions and where each is answered. The first three are all answered in the fleet list, which is why that part of the page got the most care.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"aside":{"title":"What this concept leaves out.","detail":"It covers finding a boat and starting an enquiry. Checking dates, booking and paying were not designed. The hourly rates in the mock-up are sample content from 2022. They are not current offers."}}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Page structure","heading":"One long page in seven bands","blocks":[{"p":"The whole concept is a single page, 1920 px wide and 5940 px tall. It has seven bands. The fleet is the largest at about 45% of the page, because that is where a visitor compares boats."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/page-map.webp","alt":"Scaled picture of the full page beside a list of its seven bands and the share of the page each one takes","caption":"The page from top to bottom, drawn to scale. Compare the thin navigation band with the fleet band, which is larger than the hero and About put together.","width":1600,"height":1370}]}]},{"id":"hero","label":"The top of the page","heading":"The first screen shows a boat and a way to book it","blocks":[{"p":"The hero, the large opening area of a page, does two things. It says what the business offers in one line, and it puts a boat in front of the visitor straight away. That featured boat already carries its seats and starting rate, so the pattern used later in the fleet list starts here."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/annotated-hero.webp","alt":"The navigation and hero with seven numbered markers and a note for each","caption":"The first screen with its seven parts marked. Look at 4 and 7: one is for a visitor who is ready to book, the other for one who wants to look first.","width":1600,"height":1290}]},{"p":"The navigation holds seven links in one cream bar. Online Booking sits apart as a green button, so the quickest way in is always in the same corner. The cost is that Book Now and Online Booking sit close together and seem to do the same thing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/nav.webp","alt":"The navigation bar: logo, seven orange links and a green Online Booking button","caption":"The navigation on its own. Home is bold to mark the current page.","width":1600,"height":102}]},{"p":"Below the hero, the About block is for visitors who are not ready to choose. Its button says Get a quote, not Book Now. Some people know what they want. Others need a question answered first, and this is their way in."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/about.webp","alt":"The About block: a collage of six tall photos on the left, a heading, a paragraph and a Get a quote button on the right","caption":"The About block. The button is an outline, quieter than the filled Book Now above it. I have blurred the photos that show guests.","width":1600,"height":828}]}]},{"id":"fleet","label":"The fleet list","heading":"Every boat card shows seats and a starting rate","blocks":[{"p":"This is the centre of the page. Three filters sit above the list: seating, price order and boat type. They match the first three questions in my brief."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/filters.webp","alt":"The fleet heading above three outlined filter buttons for seating, pricing and type","caption":"The three filters, each with an icon and its current setting in brackets. They are drawn closed. I did not design the open menus.","width":1600,"height":276}]},{"p":"Each boat gets a wide card. Photos take most of it. One strip underneath holds what a visitor needs to compare: the name, a starting hourly rate, the type and the number of seats. They can rule a boat out without opening it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/annotated-card.webp","alt":"One fleet card with six numbered markers: the large photo, the gallery photos, the boat name, the starting rate, the description line and the Know more button","caption":"One fleet card. Follow the numbers to see the reading order: photo first, then name, rate and seats, then the button.","width":1600,"height":1410}]},{"p":"Two trade-offs come with this. Filters only work if every boat has the same details recorded, and I did not design what happens when no boat matches. A starting rate is also not the full cost. Minimum hours, what is included and any extra fees would still need explaining."}]},{"id":"type-colour","label":"Type and colour","heading":"Large names, and orange for anything you can press","blocks":[{"p":"I measured the type on the page. The headline is 75 px and running text is 25 px. Boat names are 70 px, almost as large as the headline, so the fleet reads as a list of names first."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/type.webp","alt":"Seven samples of text cut from the page, each with its measured size and where it is used","caption":"Real samples from the page, all at the same scale. Compare the first two rows: a boat name is nearly as big as the page headline.","width":1600,"height":1010}]},{"p":"Looking at it now, the page uses five type styles: a wide sans for the headline, a condensed sans for headings, a light sans for filters, a round sans for links and buttons, and a serif for running text. That is a lot for one page, and it is the first thing I would tidy."},{"p":"Colour has a simpler job. Orange marks what can be pressed: links, filter outlines, buttons and the line under each boat name. Cream sits behind as a quiet background. Green is kept for the hero shape, the Online Booking button and the bottom bar."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/colour.webp","alt":"Eight colour swatches with their hex values and where each is used on the page","caption":"The eight colours on the page. Three of them are shades of orange, used from strongest on links to palest on the Know more buttons.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"enquiry","label":"The enquiry path","heading":"Three routes lead towards an enquiry, and the page stops there","blocks":[{"p":"A visitor can move towards an enquiry in three ways, depending on how sure they are. Each route ends in a button. What opens after the button was not designed, so the diagram shows those screens as dashed boxes."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/enquiry-path.webp","alt":"Flow diagram of three routes: hero to Book Now, About to Get a quote, and fleet filters to a card to Know more, each ending in a screen that was not designed","caption":"The three routes. The red dashed boxes on the right are where this concept ends.","width":1600,"height":660}]},{"p":"The footer repeats the main links and adds two pages a visitor would look for before paying: Terms & Conditions and Refund Policy."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/footer.webp","alt":"The dark footer with the logo and a short description, seven links, an orange bar with two policy links and a copyright line dated 2022","caption":"The footer. I have left out the contact column that sat on the right.","width":1600,"height":717}]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"An enquiry should not look like a confirmed booking","blocks":[{"p":"That is what I would fix first. The page uses Book Now, Online Booking, Get a quote and Know more, and it does not say which of them reserves a boat and which only asks a question."},{"list":["Design the boat page that Know more points to, with availability, what is included, accessibility details and cancellation terms.","Design the open filter menus and the screen shown when no boat matches.","Test it. Ask someone planning for a group to choose a boat, say what they expect to pay, and say what they would still need to confirm.","Correct the copy. The mock-up spells yacht as \"Yatch\" in several places, and I have left the artwork as it was."]},{"p":"None of this was built or tested, so there are no results to report."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","headline":"Meet the chapter. Know how to take part."}},"bni-website":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Meet the chapter. Know how to take part.","summary":"A one-page website concept for BNI Victory, a business-networking chapter in Goa. The page moves from what the chapter is for, to who is in it, to how to ask for an invitation.","role":"Information hierarchy and website visual design","status":"Chapter website concept","context":"BNI Victory Goa · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"People-led","label":"credibility"},{"value":"Visit","label":"primary invitation"},{"value":"Concept","label":"sample member content"}],"problem":"A person considering a business network needs to understand its purpose, who participates, and how to take a first step. The BNI Victory Goa design brings those questions into a local chapter website with an invitation, organisation overview, leadership section, member directory, and contact form.","process":["The available PDF is a long-page visual concept. I reviewed the sequence from the hero invitation through purpose and people to contact. The artifact does not contain an interview report, launch evidence, or a membership funnel.","The member cards repeat placeholder identities and informal sample biographies. The overview also contains network-wide statistics. Neither is evidence of chapter growth or my project impact, so those details are not promoted as results here."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the visitor a concrete first step","choice":"The hero uses a Get Invited action instead of making the organisation overview the only starting point.","reason":"An invitation turns interest in networking into an understandable next action.","tradeoff":"The next screen would need to explain meeting expectations and eligibility; a button label alone does not establish that service."},{"title":"Make the organisation visible through its people","choice":"Leadership and member-card patterns occupy distinct sections.","reason":"The directory can help a visitor understand the kinds of businesses represented and who is responsible for the chapter.","tradeoff":"Repeated mock content weakens credibility. Production would require member permission, verified biographies, and a reliable update process."},{"title":"Keep purpose and contact in the same journey","choice":"The page connects the chapter overview and mission to a form and local contact details.","reason":"A visitor can move from understanding the organisation to asking a practical question without searching elsewhere.","tradeoff":"The long single-page structure would benefit from a tighter summary and explicit links to meeting details."}],"outcome":"I designed a chapter-site concept that brings the organisation’s purpose, leadership, members and invitation into one sequence. Reusable member patterns give the page a structure that could grow with the chapter. The archived design contains sample biographies and is not a record of actual member results.","reflection":"Before expanding the directory, I would confirm the local chapter information, member permissions and visitor process. The people and meeting details need to be accurate for the invitation to be useful.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace all sample biographies with verified chapter content and explain the visitor-meeting process before the invitation action.","Proposed evaluation: ask a prospective visitor what the chapter does, whether it is relevant to their business, and what will happen after they request an invitation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Invitation, organisation overview, mission, leader/member layouts and contact; repeated cards are placeholders."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview.","width":1400,"height":2411}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/bniwebsite.webp","readingMinutes":6,"pdfPage":24,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Meet the chapter. [[Know how to take part.]]","scene":"Imagine considering a business network for the first time. You want to understand who participates, why they meet, and whether you could take a first look without already knowing someone.","question":"What turns an organisation overview into an approachable first visit?","insight":"The chapter concept gives the visitor [[a purpose, people, and an invitation]]. Those pieces need accurate local content to become credible.","pivot":"I connected the page structure to a prospective member’s sequence of questions, keeping the invitation visible alongside the chapter story.","takeaway":"Community credibility comes from real people and a clear next step.","journey":[{"title":"Understand","detail":"Learn what the chapter is for."},{"title":"Recognise","detail":"Meet the people and kinds of businesses represented."},{"title":"Visit","detail":"Find out how an invitation works."}],"decisionLeads":["Make a first visit imaginable.","Give responsibility a human face.","Let useful local information carry the invitation."],"annotations":[{"title":"A concrete invitation","detail":"The hero offers a first step into the organisation."},{"title":"A purpose-led introduction","detail":"The overview and mission explain why the chapter exists."}],"evidenceTag":"Original chapter-site concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Business owner exploring a local chapter","context":"Considering whether the network is relevant and how to participate.","need":"Understand local people, purpose, and the next step.","friction":"Global organisation information does not answer local membership questions.","implication":"Bring the chapter’s people and participation path forward."}},"theme":{"layout":"chapter","mode":"light","paper":"#fffefe","ink":"#32262a","dim":"#76636a","line":"#ead6dc","accent":"#b7213c","wash":"#fae5e9","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A local membership question","process":"What a chapter must explain","decisions":"Designing participation","outcome":"The chapter concept","reflection":"Content before scale"},"decisionHeading":"Connect the chapter’s purpose to joining.","resultHeading":"A membership story with a next step."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview.","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","width":1400,"height":2411},"v2":{"title":"Meet the chapter. [[Know how to take part.]]","hook":"A one-page website concept for BNI Victory, a business-networking chapter in Goa. The page moves from what the chapter is for, to who is in it, to how to ask for an invitation.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Information hierarchy and website visual design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2023"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself: a website for a local business-networking chapter"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop web page, designed 1920 px wide"},{"label":"Scope","value":"One long page: invitation, purpose, people and contact. Sample content in the people cards"},{"label":"Status","value":"Design concept. Not built, tested or launched"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original page design (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original design","summary":["The problem: a business owner hearing about a networking chapter wants to know what it is for, who is in it, and how to take a first step.","What I did: I designed one long page with an invitation at the top, the chapter's purpose, two kinds of people cards and a contact form.","The key idea: let people carry the page. Leaders and members take up 46% of its length.","Where it stands: a 2023 concept with no research behind it. The people cards hold sample content, and the worldwide BNI numbers in the About text are not results of this project."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/lead.webp","alt":"The whole BNI Victory page shown in four columns: hero and About, mission and leaders, leaders and members, contact form and footer","caption":"The whole page, read left to right in four columns. Faces, names and contact details are blurred or covered, here and in every picture below.","width":2400,"height":789},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"I set myself a brief: make a first visit feel possible","blocks":[{"p":"This was a brief I set myself. I did no interviews and no testing, so this page is about the design and nothing else."},{"p":"I pictured a business owner who has just heard of the chapter. I assumed they would ask three things, in this order, and I put the page in the same order. The questions are my assumption, not a research finding."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/questions.webp","alt":"Diagram pairing three visitor questions with the sections of the page that answer them","caption":"Three questions and the sections that answer them: purpose first, then people, then the invitation.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"aside":{"title":"Two limits to be clear about.","detail":"The About text quotes BNI's own worldwide numbers. They describe the organisation, not anything this project achieved. The people cards repeat the same sample person twelve times, so they show a layout and not a real chapter's members."}}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Page structure","heading":"One page in nine bands, and nearly half of it is people","blocks":[{"p":"The concept is a single page, 1920 px wide and 9919 px tall. The four links in the navigation match its main parts. The two people sections are the largest. Together they fill 46% of the page, more than purpose and contact combined."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/page-map.webp","alt":"Scaled picture of the full page beside a list of its nine bands and the share of the page each one takes","caption":"The page from top to bottom, drawn to scale. Compare Our Leaders at 28% with the hero at 8%.","width":1600,"height":1370}]}]},{"id":"hero","label":"The first screen","heading":"The first screen makes one offer: get invited","blocks":[{"p":"The hero, the large opening area of a page, has one button. It says Get Invited, not Join. An invitation to visit is a smaller step than signing up, so it is an easier first thing to ask for."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/annotated-hero.webp","alt":"The navigation, hero and contact strip with seven numbered markers and a note for each","caption":"The first screen with its seven parts marked. Look at 4: it is the only button, so there is no doubt about what to press.","width":1600,"height":1340}]},{"p":"The trade-off is that the button promises something the concept does not show. The next screen would have to explain what a visit involves and who can come."}]},{"id":"purpose","label":"Purpose","heading":"Purpose comes before people","blocks":[{"p":"Two sections explain what the chapter is for. About Us sets a paragraph beside a mosaic of portraits. Our Mission is a single card, half photo and half red, with the mission in large white type."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/about.webp","alt":"The About Us section: a red ribbon heading, a justified paragraph on the left and a blurred mosaic of portrait photos on the right","caption":"About Us. The mosaic fades into red towards the right edge. The numbers in the paragraph are BNI's worldwide figures, not results of this project.","width":1600,"height":958},{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/mission.webp","alt":"The Our Mission card: a photo of a speaker at a lectern on the left blending into a red panel with the mission text on the right","caption":"Our Mission. The photo blends into the red panel with no hard edge between them.","width":1600,"height":735}],"layout":"one"},{"p":"Every section opens with the same red ribbon, set on a background made of the logo repeated in very pale grey. Someone scrolling quickly can still see where each section starts."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/ribbon.webp","alt":"A red ribbon with the words About Us at the left edge of the page, over a pale pattern of repeated BNI logos","caption":"The ribbon and the logo pattern behind it. The same pair marks About Us, Our Leaders, Team Members and Contact Us.","width":1600,"height":218}]}]},{"id":"people","label":"People","heading":"Two kinds of card for two kinds of member","blocks":[{"p":"Leaders and members get different cards. A leader card is tall and has room for a short biography. A member card is compact and swaps the biography for two buttons, one for email and one for phone. I have redrawn both here without the photos or sample details."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/cards.webp","alt":"Two redrawn cards side by side with numbered notes: a tall leader card with a biography and a shorter member card with email and phone buttons","caption":"The two card types, redrawn. Compare item 3 on each: leaders are introduced, members can be contacted.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/leaders-row.webp","alt":"The Our Leaders ribbon above a row of three tall white cards, with portraits and text blurred","caption":"One row of leader cards from the mock-up, with the sample content blurred.","width":1600,"height":1217},{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/members-row.webp","alt":"The Team Members ribbon above a row of three shorter grey cards with a curved red lower edge, with portraits and text blurred","caption":"One row of member cards. The curved red edge holds the social icons.","width":1600,"height":792}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"The trade-off is upkeep. A directory like this needs each member's permission, checked biographies and someone to keep it up to date. Repeated sample content, as in my mock-up, would weaken a real page."}]},{"id":"type-colour","label":"Type and colour","heading":"One main typeface and one red","blocks":[{"p":"I measured the type on the page. Nearly all of it is one round sans in a few weights. Sizes run from 82.5 px for the contact heading down to 25 px for links, with a few smaller labels."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/type.webp","alt":"Eight samples of text cut from the page, each with its measured size and where it is used","caption":"Real samples from the page, all at the same scale. The ribbons, the hero headline and the mission heading are close in size, between 60 and 70 px.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"Colour is just as narrow. One red marks ribbons, buttons, the mission card and the rings round portraits. Orange appears in two small places. Everything else is white, grey or near-black."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/colour.webp","alt":"Six colour swatches with their hex values and where each is used on the page","caption":"The six colours on the page. Red is the only strong one.","width":1600,"height":860}]}]},{"id":"contact","label":"The way in","heading":"Three ways to reach the chapter, each one step long","blocks":[{"p":"A visitor can reach the chapter from the hero, from the navigation or from the footer. Each route ends at a button or a link. What comes after it was not designed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/enquiry-path.webp","alt":"Flow diagram of three routes: hero to Get Invited, the Contact Us link to the form, and the footer to three membership links, each ending in a step that was not designed","caption":"The three routes. The dashed boxes on the right are where this concept ends.","width":1600,"height":680}]},{"p":"The contact form is the only route with a second step on the page. It asks for a name, an email and a message."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/annotated-contact.webp","alt":"The Contact Us section with six numbered markers: the ribbon, the logo, the heading, three form fields, the button and three social icons","caption":"The contact section. Compare 3 and 5: the heading is the largest text on the page, and the button that sends the form has the smallest text in the section.","width":1600,"height":1290}]},{"p":"The footer repeats the four section links and adds three named for the wider organisation: BNI Global, Become a Member and Find a Chapter."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/footer.webp","alt":"The dark footer with the chapter logo and a short description, a Website column of seven links and a Contact column with its details covered","caption":"The footer. I have covered the contact details in the right-hand column.","width":1600,"height":576}]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"The invitation needs real people and a clear next step","blocks":[{"p":"A page about a community is only as believable as the people on it. Before growing the directory I would confirm the chapter's details, its members' permission and how a visit works."},{"list":["Replace the sample cards with real, checked content for each member.","Explain how a visitor meeting works before the Get Invited button, and design the screen that follows it.","Shorten the page with a tighter summary near the top and direct links to meeting details.","Test it. Ask a possible visitor what the chapter does, whether it suits their business, and what they expect to happen after asking for an invitation."]},{"p":"None of this was built or tested, so there are no results to report."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","headline":"The train search comes first."}},"irctc":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","kind":"ux","headline":"The train search comes first.","summary":"A redesign concept for the IRCTC rail-booking homepage. I set the brief myself: make searching for a train the first thing the page asks of you.","role":"Page structure, hierarchy and visual design","status":"Independent redesign concept","context":"Travel booking · No organisational affiliation","facts":[{"value":"Search-first","label":"page hierarchy"},{"value":"5","label":"core journey inputs"},{"value":"Concept","label":"homepage scope"}],"problem":"A railway homepage serves many audiences, but searching for a train is a clear primary task. My independent IRCTC concept places the booking form prominently while retaining entry points for PNR status, vacancies, concessions, and related travel services. The challenge is hierarchy: helping the common task stay visible without erasing important exceptions.","process":["The source is one full homepage design, not a documented end-to-end booking redesign. I evaluated the visible grouping of From, To, Date, Class, and Quota, the surrounding service links, and the separation of holiday content below the search area.","There is no original usability study or live implementation in the provided material. Dates, phone numbers, and offers in the mockup are sample or historical interface content, not current travel guidance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the core journey fields together","choice":"The concept groups origin, destination, travel date, class, and quota in a single booking panel with a visible Search action.","reason":"The person can form a complete query in one recognisable task area.","tradeoff":"The retained panel still contains many specialised options. Today I would test how to reveal less common settings without hiding essential access needs."},{"title":"Distinguish a current journey from trip inspiration","choice":"Related services and curated holidays appear beneath the primary search region.","reason":"A person trying to book a known route should not have to read promotional content first.","tradeoff":"The holiday cards remain text-heavy. Visual separation helps hierarchy but does not by itself solve page density."},{"title":"Retain explicit entry points for adjacent rail tasks","choice":"PNR status and chart/vacancy shortcuts remain accessible near the booking area.","reason":"People returning to manage an existing journey have a different task from people searching for a new one.","tradeoff":"The homepage only establishes entry points. It does not demonstrate those destination flows or their error handling."}],"outcome":"I created an independent homepage concept with a clear train-search area and grouped entry points for secondary rail services. The work concentrates on the starting task. Search results, changing availability and the rest of the booking flow were not part of this concept.","reflection":"I would take the same hierarchy into a difficult journey: no seats available, a changed fare or an unsuitable route. A quieter homepage only helps if the following screens make those decisions understandable too.","nextSteps":["Proposed test: find a train, check an existing journey, and locate a concession option; compare discovery and mistakes without assuming that a shorter visual scan means a successful booking.","Proposed extension: add no-results, changing availability, route correction, keyboard navigation, and narrow-screen form states."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Independent homepage mockup showing journey fields, rail-task shortcuts, related services and holidays."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts.","width":1400,"height":1969}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/irctc.webp","readingMinutes":7,"pdfPage":27,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"The train search [[comes first.]]","scene":"Picture arriving at a railway homepage with a route and a date in mind. Other services matter, but the immediate task is to form a complete train search.","question":"How should a dense service homepage protect its primary task?","insight":"The concept gives the search [[one recognisable task area]], while retaining adjacent rail services and placing trip inspiration further down.","pivot":"I focused the redesign on hierarchy: group the journey inputs, preserve useful shortcuts, and make the difference between booking and browsing legible.","takeaway":"Hierarchy should make the common task clear without erasing important exceptions.","journey":[{"title":"Form the query","detail":"Keep route, date, class, and quota together."},{"title":"Search","detail":"Give the completed query one clear action."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Carry the task into results and recovery states next."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the primary inputs together.","Let inspiration follow the immediate task.","Preserve a route for people managing an existing journey."],"annotations":[{"title":"A central task area","detail":"The booking panel groups the core journey inputs."},{"title":"Adjacent rail tasks","detail":"PNR and vacancy entry points remain nearby."}],"evidenceTag":"Independent homepage concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Traveller starting a rail search","context":"Arriving with a route and a possible date in mind.","need":"Start a train search without losing the primary task.","friction":"Many services and promotions compete for attention.","implication":"Give journey inputs first priority."}},"theme":{"layout":"departure","mode":"light","paper":"#f8faff","ink":"#162c50","dim":"#536785","line":"#d0dced","accent":"#253a96","wash":"#e5eafa","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A journey to begin","process":"The task worth protecting","decisions":"Clearing the search path","outcome":"The homepage concept","reflection":"Testing the first search"},"decisionHeading":"Keep train booking at the centre.","resultHeading":"Search leads. Other services follow."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts.","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"v2":{"title":"The train search [[comes first]].","hook":"A redesign concept for the IRCTC rail-booking homepage. I set the brief myself: make searching for a train the first thing the page asks of you.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Page structure, hierarchy and visual design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself. Not commissioned by IRCTC and not connected to it"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop web. One homepage, drawn 1,920 px wide"},{"label":"Scope","value":"Homepage only. No results, payment or ticket screens"},{"label":"Status","value":"Visual concept. Not built and not tested with users"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original design (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original design","summary":["The problem I set myself: a rail-booking homepage has to offer dozens of services, yet it should be obvious where to search for a train.","What I did: I designed one long homepage in six bands. The train search fills the first screen and everything else sits below it.","The key idea: rank tasks by position. Booking comes first, checking a trip you already have sits on its edge, and other services and holidays follow.","Where it stands: a single desktop mock-up. It was not commissioned, built or tested, and it stops at the Search button."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/lead.webp","alt":"The whole IRCTC homepage concept shown in three columns: the top with the train search and services, the middle with five holiday cards, and the end with the social strip and footer","caption":"The whole page, cut into three columns so it fits. Read it left to right: the search and services, then the holiday cards, then the footer.","width":2400,"height":1016},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"Nobody asked for this, so I wrote the brief","blocks":[{"p":"I chose this page as practice because it is a hard ordering problem. One homepage has to carry train booking, trip status, flights, hotels, buses, catering, holidays and a long list of rules and help pages."},{"p":"My rule was simple. Someone who arrives knowing their route and date should be able to start a search without reading anything else. Everything else stays on the page, further down."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/hero.webp","alt":"First screen of the concept with the header, a booking card on the left and a train photograph with the words Welcome to Indian Railways on the right","caption":"The first screen. The booking card takes the left half. The photograph and the welcome line take the right, so nothing else asks for a click.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"What this is not.","detail":"There was no client and no research. I did not study how people use the real site, so I make no claim that this version is faster or better. The emblems, logos, photographs and holiday text in the mock-up are placeholder content and belong to their owners."}}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Structure","heading":"One long page in six bands","blocks":[{"p":"The design is a single page, 1,920 px wide and 5,400 px long. I split it into six bands. The header and the search together are 1,080 px tall, the height of a full HD screen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/page-map.webp","alt":"Map of the page as six numbered bands with their heights: header, hero and train search, other services, curated holidays, social strip and footer","caption":"The six bands and how much of the page each one takes. Look at band 4: the holiday cards are 41% of the page length, more than twice the search.","width":1600,"height":1200}]},{"p":"That is the trade-off in this layout. The search is first, but most of the scrolling is promotion. Moving the holidays lower fixes the order. It does not make the page any shorter."}]},{"id":"hierarchy","label":"Task hierarchy","nav":"Task hierarchy","heading":"Position does the ranking","blocks":[{"p":"I sorted what the page offers into five kinds of task and gave each one a place. The more common I judged a task to be, the higher it sits. That was my own judgement. I had no usage data."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/task-hierarchy.webp","alt":"Diagram ranking five tasks by where they sit on the page: book a train, check an existing trip, use another travel service, browse holiday ideas, and look up everything else","caption":"The five tasks in order. The dashed line is the bottom of the first screen. Only booking and checking an existing trip sit above it.","width":1600,"height":1130}]},{"p":"Type size does not follow the same order, and that is a weakness. The two section headings further down are 60 px, larger than Book Tickets at 40 px. The card wins on position and colour, and loses on size."}]},{"id":"search","label":"The search card","heading":"Five fields and one orange button","blocks":[{"p":"The booking card holds the whole search: From, To, Date, Class and Quota, then Search. From and To share a box, so the route reads as one thing. Class and Quota open with a value already in place."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/annotated-booking.webp","alt":"The booking card with six numbered callouts covering the status tabs, the route box, the date, the class and quota menus, the six checkboxes and the Search button","caption":"Six things to notice on the card. Start at 6: Search is the only orange button on the whole page.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The card still carries six less common options as small checkboxes, such as the disability concession and flexible dates. I left them in view because some of them are access needs. The cost is a busier card. A way to tuck the rest away, without hiding those, would need testing."},{"p":"People who already hold a ticket have a different job. For them, PNR Status (a PNR is the booking reference on a rail ticket) and Charts/Vacancy sit as two tabs on the card's top edge. Track Train is the first item in the menu."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/annotated-header.webp","alt":"The page header with six numbered callouts: helpline and email, text-size buttons, language menu, the four menu items, login and register, and the two emblems","caption":"The header in two rows. The grey bar holds help and the text-size buttons. The white row holds the menu and the login.","width":1600,"height":760}]}]},{"id":"below","label":"Below the search","heading":"Everything else waits below the first screen","blocks":[{"p":"Under the search, ten other services appear as a grid of icons with short labels, from Flights and Hotels to Charter Train and Gallery. Icons keep this band light, like a menu."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/services.webp","alt":"Services band with the heading Find a suitable service for you here and ten navy icons in two rows of five","caption":"Ten shortcuts in two rows of five. All ten are the same size, so none of them competes with the search above.","width":1600,"height":917}]},{"p":"Holidays come after that. Each of the five cards has a photo, an orange title, a paragraph and a Read more link. Someone booking a known route never has to read them."},{"layout":"wide-narrow","figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/holidays.webp","alt":"Special Curated Holidays band with five navy cards in two rows, each with a photograph, an orange title and a paragraph","caption":"The five holiday cards. They start two screens down the page.","width":1500,"height":1571},{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/holiday-card.webp","alt":"One holiday card enlarged, with a photograph of a train at night, an orange title and a long justified paragraph in white","caption":"One card up close. The paragraph is long, bold and justified, which makes it heavy to read. The cards are still the densest part of the page.","width":760,"height":1113}]}]},{"id":"style","label":"Type and colour","heading":"Navy for structure, orange for one action","blocks":[{"p":"Navy carries almost everything: headings, icons, pills and the holiday cards. Orange is saved for the Search button and the card titles. The footer uses a darker indigo, so it reads as the end of the page."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/type-colour.webp","alt":"Chart of eight text samples from 85 px to 15 px cut from the design, five colour swatches with their values, and three contrast measurements","caption":"Type samples cut from the design at full size, with the colours and three contrast measurements. Read the contrast column: two of the three fall short.","width":1600,"height":1250}]},{"p":"Measuring the mock-up shows two weaknesses. There are 13 text sizes between 12 and 85 px, more than a page like this needs. And the white Search label on orange has a contrast of 2.7 to 1, below the 3 to 1 that accessibility guidelines ask for large text. The grey 15 px option labels are low too, at 3.3 to 1."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/footer.webp","alt":"End of the page: a social strip shading from orange to green with eight network icons, a dark indigo footer with 19 link groups, and a row of payment logos","caption":"The end of the page. A social strip in flag colours, then 19 link groups, each with an arrow to open it. The copyright line and payment logos are placeholder content.","width":1600,"height":842}]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"What it would take to go further","blocks":[{"p":"This is one screen of a much longer journey. A calm homepage proves little until the screens after it handle the hard cases: no seats left, a changed fare, a route with no suitable train."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/scope.webp","alt":"Diagram of a booking journey in five steps where only the first, the homepage and train search, is marked as designed, with four missing homepage states listed below","caption":"Where the concept stops. Only the first box was designed. The rest is drawn to show what a full booking redesign would have to cover.","width":1600,"height":610}]},{"list":["Draw the homepage's own missing states: an empty or wrong field, the station list and the open date picker.","Design a phone-width version. The page exists at desktop width only.","Test three tasks with travellers: find a train, check an existing trip and find a concession option.","Raise the contrast of the Search label and the small grey text, and cut down the number of type sizes."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","heading":"Order is the cheapest tool, and it is not enough","blocks":[{"p":"Putting the search first cost nothing and did most of the work. But position alone left gaps that only show up when you measure: headings lower down that are bigger than the main task, and an action colour that is hard to read."},{"p":"The lesson I take from it is to settle the type scale and check contrast before drawing the first card."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","headline":"A photography site that leaves room for the photographs."}},"nityananda":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","kind":"ux","headline":"A photography site that leaves room for the photographs.","summary":"A one-page portfolio concept for a photographer. Three words run the whole site: About, Work and Contact.","role":"Page structure, navigation and visual design","status":"Independent portfolio concept","context":"Photography · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"work categories"},{"value":"3","label":"primary destinations"},{"value":"Image-led","label":"visual hierarchy"}],"problem":"A photography portfolio needs to show the work before asking visitors to read a long introduction. The Nitya Nanda concept explores a restrained structure with About, Work, and Contact destinations, an image-led opening, and separate professional and freelance collections.","process":["The available source is a single long visual concept. I traced how the page introduces the photographer, groups the work, and leads to the contact form. The listed shoots, locations, and credentials are illustrative portfolio content; the artifact does not substantiate them as real commissions.","This retrospective draws design observations from the page itself. No original audience research, client engagement record, or booking measurements accompany the concept."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the navigation vocabulary small","choice":"The page repeats About, Work, and Contact around an image-led presentation.","reason":"These are familiar questions for someone considering a creative professional and make the portfolio easy to orient within.","tradeoff":"Repeated navigation should help orientation rather than consume space. Today I would test a single persistent version on smaller screens."},{"title":"Separate two ways of reading the portfolio","choice":"Professional and freelance work have their own grouped image collections with captions.","reason":"The distinction gives visitors a route based on the kind of commission they are considering.","tradeoff":"The grouping is a design assumption. Real enquiries might be better served by subject, service, or photographic style instead."},{"title":"Give the visual journey a practical endpoint","choice":"A short contact form follows the portfolio, with additional work and consultation links.","reason":"Someone convinced by the images should have a direct route to starting a conversation.","tradeoff":"The concept stops at submission. A complete service needs clear expectations, error messages, and confirmation of what happens next."}],"outcome":"I designed a restrained portfolio with professional and freelance collections, a small navigation system and a contact destination. The layout lets visitors browse images before deciding to make an enquiry. The biography and photography credentials in the concept are illustrative.","reflection":"I would spend the next pass on selecting images, writing useful captions and checking loading performance. The interface has already done enough when it helps someone understand the work and find a way to get in touch.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask visitors choosing a photographer to identify a relevant body of work and start an enquiry; observe which category language they expect.","Proposed implementation: responsive image sizes, descriptive alternatives, keyboard access, and a complete form confirmation and error flow."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Photographer introduction, professional/freelance galleries, contact form and illustrative project captions."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout.","width":1400,"height":3557}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/nityananda.webp","readingMinutes":6,"pdfPage":30,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A photography site [[that leaves room for the photographs.]]","scene":"Imagine choosing a photographer. You want to see relevant work, get a feel for the person behind it, and know how to begin an enquiry.","question":"How much interface does an image-led portfolio actually need?","insight":"The concept uses [[three familiar destinations]] and two work groupings to keep the route through the photography understandable.","pivot":"I treated restraint as a structural choice: let the images carry the first impression, then give the visitor a practical endpoint.","takeaway":"A quiet interface still needs to give clear directions.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Let images establish the first impression."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Choose a relevant collection."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Start an enquiry with clear expectations."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep orientation familiar.","Group the work around a useful distinction.","Give the visual journey a practical endpoint."],"annotations":[{"title":"Images carry the story","detail":"The concept keeps the interface around a large body of work."},{"title":"Contact closes the sequence","detail":"The enquiry form gives interest somewhere to go."}],"evidenceTag":"Original photography-portfolio concept","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person considering a photographer","context":"Looking for a relevant style and a way to discuss a shoot.","need":"See a coherent body of work and begin an enquiry.","friction":"A broad gallery can make relevance hard to judge.","implication":"Use collections and a clear contact route."}},"theme":{"layout":"gallery","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f2eee8","dim":"#bcb5ac","line":"#3d3832","accent":"#d9c6a9","wash":"#342e26","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Looking for a style","process":"Letting the work speak","decisions":"A portfolio with space","outcome":"The photography concept","reflection":"Making relevance clearer"},"decisionHeading":"Let visitors browse the photographs.","resultHeading":"A body of work before a sales pitch."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout.","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","width":1400,"height":3557},"v2":{"title":"A photography site that [[leaves room for the photographs]].","hook":"A one-page portfolio concept for a photographer. Three words run the whole site: About, Work and Contact.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Page structure, navigation and visual design"},{"label":"Team","value":"Solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"2021–22"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself. Not a client commission"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Desktop web. One long page, drawn 1,920 px wide"},{"label":"Content","value":"Sample photographs, shoot names and award badges. Not real credentials"},{"label":"Status","value":"Visual concept. Not built and not tested with users"}],"links":[{"label":"View the original design (PDF)","url":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf"}],"linksHeading":"The original design","summary":["The brief I set myself: design a photographer's portfolio where the interface stays quiet and the pictures do the talking.","What I did: one long page with four screen-height sections (opening, About, Work, Contact) and a footer, tied together by a three-word tab bar.","The key idea: no brand colour. The page is black, white and grey, so all the colour on screen comes from a photograph.","Where it stands: a single desktop mock-up filled with sample content. It was not built or tested."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/lead.webp","alt":"The whole portfolio page in two columns: the dark opening screen and white About section on the left, the black Work and Contact sections and white footer on the right","caption":"The whole page, cut in two so it fits. The left column is the top half and the right column carries on from it. Photos that show faces, and the award badges, are blurred.","width":2400,"height":1750},"sections":[{"id":"brief","label":"The brief","heading":"A brief I set myself: keep the interface quiet","blocks":[{"p":"This is a concept, not client work. I set myself the job of designing a portfolio where a visitor sees pictures first, gets a feel for the person second and can get in touch third."},{"p":"I had no research to lean on, so every decision here is design judgement. The page is my answer to one question: how little interface does a picture-led site need?"},{"aside":{"title":"Sample content.","detail":"The photographs, shoot names, places, years and award badges are there to fill the layout. They are not a record of real commissions or prizes."}}]},{"id":"structure","label":"Structure","heading":"Four screens and a footer on one page","blocks":[{"p":"The whole site is one page, 1,920 px wide and 4,878 px long. The first four sections are each close to one full HD screen tall, between 1,030 and 1,135 px. The backgrounds run dark, white, black, then a white card on black."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/page-map.webp","alt":"Map of the page as five numbered parts with their heights and background colours: opening screen, About, Work, Contact and footer","caption":"The five parts and their heights. The small squares show the backgrounds: About is the one fully white screen, set between two dark ones.","width":1600,"height":1270}]},{"p":"One page keeps the visit to a single scroll. The cost is room. The Work section holds eight cards, and there is no gallery behind any of them."}]},{"id":"opening","label":"The opening screen","nav":"Opening screen","heading":"The first screen is a picture and three words","blocks":[{"p":"There is no menu bar across the top. The three links sit in a list on the left in 80 px type, so they work as the headline and the menu at once. The rest of the screen belongs to a portrait and a film strip of photos."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/annotated-hero.webp","alt":"The dark opening screen with four numbered callouts: the logo, the three large links, the portrait with its face blurred, and the film strip of photos","caption":"Four things to notice on the opening screen. Start at 2: the menu is the largest type on it.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"navigation","label":"Navigation","heading":"Three words, shown four times","blocks":[{"p":"After the opening list, the same three words come back as a tab bar above each section. The section you are in gets a thin box around its name. The bar takes its colours from the section it sits on."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/nav-states.webp","alt":"The list of About, Work and Contact from the opening screen beside the three tab bars, each with a different word boxed","caption":"The opening list on the left and the three tab bars on the right. Only the box moves.","width":1600,"height":770}]},{"p":"These are the questions I would expect from someone choosing a photographer: who is this, what have they shot, how do I reach them. The trade-off is space. Repeating the bar three times takes room that could go to pictures, and on a small screen I would test one bar fixed to the top."}]},{"id":"about","label":"About","heading":"Even the biography has pictures beside it","blocks":[{"p":"The About section has a cut-out portrait on the left, three short paragraphs in the middle and three photos stacked on the right. A row of four award badges sits under the text. The badges are sample content, so they are left out of the crop below."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/about.webp","alt":"The biography in three justified paragraphs with three stacked photographs to its right: clasped hands, a figure in a cave mouth, and a cat with a dog","caption":"The text column and the photo stack. Look at the second paragraph: justified text leaves uneven gaps between words. Left-aligned text would read more evenly.","width":1400,"height":895}]}]},{"id":"work","label":"Work","heading":"Two rows give two ways into the work","blocks":[{"p":"The Work section splits the pictures into Professional Work and Freelance Work, four cards each. The idea is that a visitor can head straight for the kind of job they have in mind."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/annotated-work.webp","alt":"The Work section with five numbered callouts: the tab bar, the underlined group heading, the row of four cards, the second group, and three blurred photos","caption":"The Work section with five callouts. The two groups use one pattern, so neither looks more important.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Each card carries its caption inside the photo: a title in 25 px serif, then the place and year in 15 px italics, over a dark fade."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/professional-row.webp","alt":"The Professional Work heading above four photo cards showing a racing car, a backpack, a figure in a market lane and a pizza, each with a caption","caption":"The Professional Work row at full size. The captions are sample text.","width":1600,"height":327}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/work-card.webp","alt":"One photo card enlarged, showing a pizza with a two-line caption in white serif type along the bottom","caption":"One card enlarged. The fade behind the caption is gentle, so on a bright photo like this the title is harder to read than on the darker cards.","width":1000,"height":629}]},{"p":"The split into professional and freelance is my assumption. Real visitors might look by subject or by style, and that would need asking."}]},{"id":"contact","label":"Contact","heading":"The scroll ends at a short form","blocks":[{"p":"The last screen is a white card on black with three fields: name, email and message. A black Submit button and three social icons finish the card."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/contact.webp","alt":"The Contact section: the tab bar with Contact boxed above a white card holding the logo, three form fields, a black Submit button and three social icons","caption":"The contact card. The logo takes the left half, so the form stays short and narrow.","width":1600,"height":851}]},{"p":"Below it, the footer repeats the logo and adds three service links and three pill buttons. The concept stops at the Submit button. There is no error message, no confirmation and no note on what happens next."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/footer.webp","alt":"The white footer with the logo on the left, three service links in the middle and three pill buttons on the right","caption":"The footer. Placeholder contact details are removed from this crop, and the dark credit bar under it is left out.","width":1600,"height":402}]}]},{"id":"style","label":"Type and colour","heading":"Black, white and four typefaces","blocks":[{"p":"There is no brand colour. The page is black, white and two greys, so the only colour on screen comes from the photographs."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/type-colour.webp","alt":"Chart of text samples grouped under four typefaces, four colour swatches from black to light grey, and two contrast measurements","caption":"Text samples cut from the design, grouped by typeface, with the colours beside them. Count the groups on the left: four typefaces on one page.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"Type is where I was less strict. A thin display serif does the navigation, an old-style serif does the work captions, a light sans-serif does the reading and a plain sans-serif does the footer. Two would have been calmer. The small grey copyright line also measures 2.3 to 1 against white, which is too faint."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What comes next","heading":"What it would take to go further","blocks":[{"list":["Build it with real photographs at sensible file sizes, with text descriptions and keyboard access.","Design the phone layout. Four cards across and 80 px links will not fit a narrow screen as drawn.","Add the missing form states: errors, a confirmation and what happens next.","Ask people choosing a photographer which grouping they expect to find.","Cut the typefaces to two and darken the small grey text."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","heading":"A quiet interface still has to give directions","blocks":[{"p":"Taking the colour away was the easy part. What holds the page together is the tab bar: three words, always in one order, always showing where you are."},{"p":"The part I under-did was type. With so little else on the page, every extra typeface shows."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","headline":"Change a token. See what it changes."}},"arkitype":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","kind":"product","headline":"A design system built from one file, so nothing has to be copied by hand.","summary":"Arkitype is a web tool I designed and built on my own. You decide the colour, type, spacing, shape and motion. It turns those decisions into 90 working components and 17 export formats.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Public beta","context":"Design systems · Working product","facts":[{"value":"90","label":"components in the system registry"},{"value":"8","label":"decision areas"},{"value":"Shared","label":"token-to-export model"}],"problem":"A palette, a component library, and a handoff document can each look convincing while disagreeing with one another. I approached Arkitype as a connected workspace: decisions about colour, type, space, shape, and motion should remain visible in the components and artifacts they produce.","process":["I framed the workspace around eight connected design areas, rather than a compulsory wizard. A designer should be able to revisit a foundation while considering a component and still understand what depends on that decision.","The UX goal is continuity: foundations, component states, previews, and handoff should feel like parts of one system. The current 90-component library is product scope; it does not establish that a new user understands the workspace."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use dependencies to guide, rather than enforce a sequence","choice":"Foundations, assets, components, and preview remain accessible, with relationships expressed as Built from and Lands in links.","reason":"The navigation reflects iterative design work instead of pretending every system starts and ends in the same order.","tradeoff":"An open workspace gives less automatic direction. First-use guidance must help a person find a useful starting point."},{"title":"Keep design changes consistent across surfaces","choice":"The preview, component workspace, and handoff reflect the same design decisions.","reason":"A change should carry through to the places a team actually uses, rather than depend on repeated manual updates.","tradeoff":"A designer needs to know which properties carry through and which need review before handoff."},{"title":"Let people inspect appearance and behaviour","choice":"The studio distinguishes controlled component specimens from interactive previews.","reason":"A design system needs comparable states and realistic behaviour. One view cannot do both equally well.","tradeoff":"Interactive examples add state and complexity; the underlying specimen must remain predictable for documentation and export."}],"outcome":"I shaped Arkitype around the relationship between foundations, component states, previews and handoff. The beta brings those surfaces into one workspace, with a documented library of 90 components. The design question I still want to test is whether a new user can follow a change through the whole system.","reflection":"I would ask a designer to change a foundation, find the affected component states and prepare a handoff. If they need a second explanation at every step, the workspace is still making them carry too much of the system in their head.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a designer to start from one brand colour, adjust a component, and produce a usable handoff. Record confusion, corrections, and output mismatches.","Proposed measure: time to the first coherent system and the number of inconsistencies discovered after export, with a defined task and baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and lib/systemAreas.ts","detail":"Eight-area navigation, dependency graph, specimen/interactive distinction, beta scope and export pipeline."},{"label":"lib/componentLanes.ts and docs/GETTING_STARTED.md","detail":"Component registry and the documented first-use journey. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://arkitype.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/arkitype.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":11,"pdfPage":3,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Change a token. [[See what it changes.]]","scene":"Imagine changing a brand colour and then checking every place it appears: components, documentation, previews, and the handoff. The change is small; keeping all its consequences aligned is the real work.","question":"What if the different outputs all came from the same decision?","insight":"A system needs [[relationships between decisions]], not just an inventory of components. Arkitype’s shared model connects foundations to the things a team can inspect and use.","pivot":"I organised the workspace around dependencies, letting designers revisit decisions while keeping their consequences visible.","takeaway":"System quality lives in what stays connected when something changes.","journey":[{"title":"Define","detail":"Set colour, type, space, shape, and motion."},{"title":"Bind","detail":"Connect component behaviour to system tokens."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"See the decisions in a real preview."},{"title":"Ship","detail":"Generate artifacts from the same model."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the work move in more than one direction.","Make one change travel through the system.","Show both the specimen and the behaviour."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working system","detail":"The archived capture introduces the actual product, rather than a speculative redesign."},{"title":"Follow a change","detail":"The palette and contrast inspector keep a foundation choice beside the information needed to evaluate it."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · shared model","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer shaping a shared system","context":"Revisiting foundations and component decisions during an evolving project.","need":"Understand how one decision affects the rest of the system.","friction":"Disconnected screens and handoff artifacts can drift.","implication":"Show relationships, states, and consequences together."}},"theme":{"layout":"system","mode":"light","paper":"#e8e8eb","ink":"#24242a","dim":"#5c5c68","line":"#bcbcc6","accent":"#4e3cad","wash":"#e1def2","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A system in motion","process":"Relationships over inventory","decisions":"The workspace contract","outcome":"From decisions to handoff","reflection":"The first-use question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep design changes connected.","resultHeading":"One system, visible in several places."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/arkitype-workspace.jpg","alt":"Arkitype workspace showing foundation palettes, navigation and contrast inspector.","caption":"Foundations in context: the palette, navigation and contrast inspector share one workspace.","label":"Product interface","source":"Arkitype/docs/images/workspace.jpg","focus":[{"title":"Navigate the system","detail":"Foundations and components remain within reach."},{"title":"See the consequence","detail":"Editable palettes stay beside the result."},{"title":"Inspect the decision","detail":"Contrast information sits in the same workspace."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":0,"y":60,"width":550,"height":360,"caption":"Foundations and components stay available in the workspace navigation.","alt":"Detail of the original Arkitype navigation beside the foundation palette."}]},"v2":{"title":"A design system built from [[one file]], so nothing has to be copied by hand.","hook":"Arkitype is a web tool I designed and built on my own. You decide the colour, type, spacing, shape and motion. It turns those decisions into 90 working components and 17 export formats.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July to October 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web. The editor needs a desktop screen"},{"label":"Status","value":"Public beta, version 0.2.0-beta"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Zustand, Tailwind, Supabase"}],"links":[{"label":"Open Arkitype","url":"https://arkitype.srinidhibhat.com"},{"label":"Read the manual","url":"https://arkitype.srinidhibhat.com/docs"},{"label":"Figma plugin","url":"https://www.figma.com/community/plugin/1658818555967908857/arkitype-figma-sync-beta"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: a design system usually lives in four places: the design file, a token file, a documentation site and the code. Each is edited by hand, so they slowly stop matching.","What I built: a web tool with eight areas of decisions, 90 components that read those decisions, and 17 export formats compiled from one file.","What changed on the way: I removed the numbered steps, rebuilt the templates after measuring them, and reversed my own rule against written documentation.","Where it stands: a public beta, version 0.2.0-beta. I have no usage numbers to share yet."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/workspace-colour.webp","alt":"The Arkitype workspace on the Colour area, showing six colour ramps of ten shades each and a contrast panel on the right","caption":"The Colour area of a sample system called Northwind. Each colour family is a ten-shade ramp generated from one starting colour.","width":1600,"height":900},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A design system usually lives in four copies","blocks":[{"p":"A design system is the shared set of colours, type, spacing and components a product is built from. It usually lives in four places: the design file, a file of tokens, a documentation site and the code. A token is a named value, such as a colour or a spacing size, that design and code both refer to."},{"p":"Each copy is edited by hand, and nothing tells you when two of them stop matching. I wanted one file that holds the decisions, with everything else produced from it."},{"p":"I designed it for three groups: independent designers with no team behind them, developers who read the system and take its exports, and agencies who build systems for clients."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/dev-mode.webp","alt":"Arkitype in Dev mode: the button component on the left, greyed-out settings in the middle and a list of token names on the right","caption":"Dev mode, made for developers. Nothing can be edited. The right column lists each value with the token name used in code.","width":1600,"height":1179}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I have no interview notes or usage data for these groups, so this page does not quote any."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Eight areas of decisions, then one place to ship","blocks":[{"p":"You start with a name and one brand colour. From there the product is eight areas: five foundations (colour, typography, spacing, shape and motion), then logos, components and a preview."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/areas-map.webp","alt":"Diagram of the eight areas in four groups, Foundations, Parts, Material and Proof, with Ship drawn separately as not an area","caption":"The eight areas. Read the arrows as \"feeds into\", not \"do this next\".","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"Each foundation is a short set of rules, not a long list of values."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/type-scale.webp","alt":"Typography area showing the same sentence set at five sizes, each with size, line height and tracking fields","caption":"Typography. One base size and one ratio set every step.","width":1400,"height":943},{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/spacing-scale.webp","alt":"Spacing area showing ten spacing steps as bars of growing length","caption":"Spacing. Ten steps on a 4px base, drawn as bars.","width":1400,"height":786},{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/shape-elevation.webp","alt":"Shape area showing corner radius steps, opacity steps and shadow levels in light and dark","caption":"Shape and elevation. Shadows appear in light and dark together.","width":1400,"height":943}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"The 90 components come in four groups: 26 controls, 28 display pieces, 16 for navigation and 20 larger patterns. Each opens in a studio where every setting points at a token."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/studio-annotated.webp","alt":"The component studio showing a button, with six numbered notes explaining the preview, the templates button, the variants, the states and the settings panel","caption":"The studio with a button open. Start at note 5: the background is a pointer to a role, not a typed colour.","width":1600,"height":1280}]},{"p":"Preview draws a sample product from those components, as a desktop app, a phone app or a marketing page."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/preview-desktop.webp","alt":"Preview area showing a finance dashboard with a sidebar, four number cards and a table, drawn from the system","caption":"Preview as a desktop finance app, drawn from the library in the file's own colours.","width":1500,"height":1189},{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/preview-phone-dark.webp","alt":"Preview area showing a phone banking screen in dark mode","caption":"The same system as a phone app in dark mode.","width":1500,"height":1044}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"Ship then turns the system into files, written documentation, a public page or a named release."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/ship-export.webp","alt":"The Ship space on Export, with a generated Tailwind config in the middle and seventeen format buttons on the right","caption":"Ship, on Export. The file shown is generated from the sample system's 249 tokens. The buttons on the right are the 17 formats.","width":1600,"height":687}]}]},{"id":"areas","label":"Decision 1","nav":"No numbered steps","heading":"Areas you can open in any order, not eight numbered steps","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a list of areas with no numbers. A tick marks an area I have called settled. If something it depends on changes later, the tick becomes a warning."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/rail-status.webp","alt":"The list of areas with six numbered notes explaining group headings, the settled tick, the warning triangle, the tally and the built-from links","caption":"Notes 2 and 3 are the point: the product tells you where your last edit landed.","width":1600,"height":950}]},{"p":"In the build: the first version was a wizard, with steps numbered 01 to 08 and a progress bar. That described a process nobody follows. You pick colours, build a button, find the colours cannot carry a disabled state, and go back."},{"p":"So I deleted the step order. When an area is settled, the file stores a fingerprint of everything that area depends on. When the fingerprint stops matching, the area asks for another look."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A list with no numbers gives a first-time user less of a push than \"Step 1 of 8\". Group headings, status marks and a short tour have to do that job."}}]},{"id":"source","label":"Decision 2","nav":"One colour, everywhere","heading":"One colour choice updates every component and every export","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: type a new brand colour and the system follows, in light and dark."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/colour-change.webp","alt":"Before and after strips of the same card: with a blue brand colour, then with an orange one typed into a single field","caption":"The same card before and after one edit. Only the field at note 1 was changed.","width":1600,"height":1020}]},{"p":"In the build: no component holds a typed-in value. A button's background points at a semantic role, a name for a job such as \"primary action\". The role points at one shade of a colour ramp."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/token-flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of four linked tiers, primitive, semantic role, component token and component property, feeding the canvas, the exports, the styleguide and the Figma file","caption":"The chain behind that edit. A value passes through four named links before it reaches the screen.","width":1600,"height":790}]},{"p":"A ramp is ten shades. I ruled out stepping the lightness evenly, because the steps then look uneven and differ from hue to hue. Each shade is worked out to hit a fixed brightness target."},{"p":"The chain is also drawn as a map. My first version drew every link as a thin line, and with hundreds of them it could not be read. Now there is one ribbon for each pair of groups, thicker when it carries more links."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/variables-map.webp","alt":"The variables map with four columns of tokens and one role selected, its links drawn in purple to every component that uses it","caption":"The map with one role held. The panel on the right lists the 53 places that depend on it.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The exports are compiled from this chain, so a Tailwind file, a Figma variable and the published page cannot disagree about a value."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/export-formats.webp","alt":"Diagram of the 17 export formats in five groups, Figma, Tokens, Frameworks, Guardrails and Handoff, plus one hosted page","caption":"All 17 formats, in the product's own five groups.","width":1600,"height":830}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"An export is a snapshot. After an edit the files have to be exported again, and nothing yet checks whether the code using them has moved away from the system."}}]},{"id":"templates","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Templates change shape only","heading":"Templates change the shape and never the colour","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: five styles modelled on well-known design systems (Material 3, Apple HIG, IBM Carbon, Atlassian and Fluent 2). Pick one and every component takes on its corners, sizes and spacing. Your colours stay."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/templates-picker.webp","alt":"The template picker with six cards, the file's own style and five templates, each showing a small button","caption":"The picker: the file's own style and five templates, each drawn in this file's colours.","width":1430,"height":998}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/button-material.webp","alt":"The button variants with the Material 3 template: fully rounded pill shapes","caption":"Material 3. Pill corners on a 40px control.","width":1600,"height":433},{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/button-carbon.webp","alt":"The button variants with the IBM Carbon template: square corners","caption":"IBM Carbon. Square corners. The colours have not moved.","width":1600,"height":433},{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/button-apple.webp","alt":"The button variants with the Apple HIG template: soft rounded corners","caption":"Apple HIG. 12px corners on a compact 34px control.","width":1600,"height":433}],"layout":"one"},{"p":"In the build: the first version described a template with eight settings. I measured it across all 90 components. The Fluent template and the default looked identical on 41 of them."},{"p":"I rebuilt it with 23 settings, such as control height, focus ring and icon weight. A check now fails the build if any two templates differ on fewer than 14 of them. A template never overrides a setting the designer chose."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"A template that brings its own colours. The cost of getting this right was a rebuild: the eight-setting version was thrown away."}}]},{"id":"docs","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Written and generated pages","heading":"Written pages sit beside generated ones, never instead of them","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a documentation editor inside Ship. Every page starts with a block the system generates, such as a component's live preview. Around it you write your own text, images and code. The generated block can be moved but not deleted."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/docs-editor.webp","alt":"The documentation editor in split view: blocks being written on the left and the finished page on the right","caption":"Writing on the left, the finished page on the right. The first block, \"Generated by the system\", cannot be removed.","width":1500,"height":1517}]},{"p":"In the build: my first rule was that a published page would show only what the system generated, so it could never go out of date. That was too strict. A generated page can say which token a button uses. It cannot say that filled buttons are kept for the save bar."},{"p":"I kept the guarantee with a narrower rule, and in the same change moved Ship out of the list of areas. Exporting is something you do with a system, not a decision about one."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/published-button.webp","alt":"A published styleguide page for the Button component in dark mode, with navigation on the left and live states and variants in the middle","caption":"A published component page in dark mode. Anyone with the link can read it without an account.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Publishing freezes a copy. Later edits stay private until someone republishes, so the public page can be behind the file."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One file in the middle, and checks around it","blocks":[{"p":"The whole design lives in one file. The canvas, the exports, the published page and the Figma plugin all read from it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: the design file, token compiler and canvas in the browser; exports, published styleguide and Figma plugin compiled from the file; Supabase, database rules and Stripe on the server","caption":"What runs where: the editor on the left, everything compiled from the file in the middle, the server on the right.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript"],["Design file","Zustand, one store that every screen and export reads"],["Components","CSS variables, so a token change shows without redrawing"],["Accounts and saving","Supabase (Postgres), with access rules in the database"],["Billing","Stripe, optional"],["Checks","Vitest and GitHub Actions"]]}},{"p":"Every push runs type checking, the test suite and nine smaller checks. One walks all 90 components and confirms that 948 declared token links match what each component draws."},{"stats":[{"value":"223","label":"commits, July to October 2026"},{"value":"150","label":"test files in the project"},{"value":"1,171","label":"tests passing at the last full run I recorded"},{"value":"9","label":"database migrations, each run against a real Postgres in the tests"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of the build in four months with commit counts: July 53, August 40, September 123, October 7, and the main milestones in each","caption":"The build by month. In September the library reached 90 components and the product opened to the public.","width":1600,"height":770}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were not on the canvas."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Saving","A readiness audit found that moving an unopened file could overwrite its contents, and that very large shared edits were cut short. Both are fixed and covered by tests, one of which pushes 1,000 changes through."],["Database rules","No test had ever run the rules for who can read a file. The tests now run every migration against a real Postgres. That found four bugs on the first run."],["Figma plugin","It is released separately from the web app. One component went out as a grey placeholder because the plugin had no drawing for it. A check now fails when that would happen."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Contrast is checked as you work, and I audited my own interface","blocks":[{"p":"A contrast ratio says how far apart two colours are in brightness. Text usually needs at least 4.5 to 1. Arkitype measures every text and background pairing in the file, in light and dark, while you work."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/arkitype/contrast-audit.webp","alt":"The contrast audit screen with six numbered notes explaining the counts, the backgrounds tested, the AA and AAA scores, the rows and the fix button","caption":"The audit for the sample system. Look at note 4: the stricter level is not fully met, and the screen says so.","width":1600,"height":1350}]},{"p":"Two details mattered. A see-through colour is measured as the blend that appears on screen, not the colour that is stored. Decorative lines are reported but do not count as failures, so the real ones are not buried."},{"p":"Then I turned the same idea on the tool itself. Arkitype argues for systems over one-off values, and its own interface was not following that."},{"stats":[{"value":"28","label":"font sizes in use"},{"value":"12","label":"corner radii"},{"value":"155","label":"different panel styles"},{"value":"326","label":"hand-made buttons, with 282 different style strings"},{"value":"90","label":"icon-only buttons with no name for a screen reader"}]},{"p":"The cause was a missing layer: a colour system, and no system for sizes. I added one scale, a shared kit of eight parts, and a check that fails if the count of one-off styles goes up. Making the shared icon button require a label is what surfaced the 90 unnamed controls."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"Contrast checks and live previews are not an accessibility audit of the 90 components, and I do not claim one. The interface targets WCAG 2.2 AA. A full screen-reader pass is still to be done."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"A public beta, with no usage numbers to share yet","blocks":[{"p":"Arkitype is live as a public beta, version 0.2.0-beta. It opened to anyone in September 2026, when the shared password came off. The free plan needs no card."},{"p":"I launched it on Product Hunt as a public beta."},{"p":"I have no user counts, testimonials or revenue to report. These are also not there yet:"},{"list":["The editor needs a desktop-sized window. Only the manual and published pages work on phones.","No usability sessions have been run with the three groups it is designed for.","Real payments, two people editing at once and restoring a backup still need a rehearsal on a staging copy."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Read the rest of the code, then test with people","blocks":[{"list":["Read the interface code line by line. The pre-launch audit only scanned it, about 87,000 lines, and five bugs later turned up there that a scan could not see.","Run the staging rehearsals, a screen-reader pass and the first usability sessions.","Planned and not built: a check that compares a live website with the system, a view of what a token change would break, and publishing under a client's own brand."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Put the rule where it cannot be skipped","blocks":[{"p":"The most useful fixes were small rules placed where they could not be skipped. Requiring a label on one shared button found 90 problems in an afternoon."},{"p":"I learned to measure before trusting my eye: the templates looked different to me until the numbers said 41 of 90 were the same. And twice I had to undo my own rule, the numbered steps and the ban on written documentation. Both were protecting something real, and both were too wide."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","headline":"Room to sketch before committing to a design."}},"applecider":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","kind":"product","headline":"A wireframe tool that looks like a sketch and works like a real canvas","summary":"AppleCider is a web tool I designed and built on my own for the first hour of a design, when the idea is still changing. It draws like a pen sketch, keeps your work in your own browser, and turns rough screens into a clickable flow or starter code.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Design tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"250","label":"documented library components"},{"value":"3","label":"sketch styles"},{"value":"Local","label":"browser-owned files"}],"problem":"Early design conversations can become arguments about polish before the underlying idea is settled. I framed AppleCider around an intentionally unfinished visual language: the speed and social permission of a sketch, with the editing capabilities of a digital workspace.","process":["I separated the experience into a welcoming introduction, a files workspace, and the editing canvas. This gives each moment a clear task: understand the tool, find the work, then explore an idea.","The design loop is sketch, revise, demonstrate, and hand off. Components, undo, snapping, links, and export matter because they let someone change their mind without starting again. Formal user validation remains a next step."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the unfinished quality deliberate","choice":"Wobbly strokes, a sketch-like type treatment, and three visual styles preserve a low-fidelity feel.","reason":"The interface should invite changes to the idea before encouraging arguments about pixel-level finish.","tradeoff":"A sketch aesthetic can also reduce legibility. Labels, focus states, and interactions still need the clarity of a serious tool."},{"title":"Keep the work portable","choice":"The files surface stores documents locally and supports a plain project-file export, alongside image and vector outputs.","reason":"A person should be able to keep and move their work without an account or a proprietary cloud workflow.","tradeoff":"Browser storage is device-specific and can be cleared. File ownership needs an understandable backup and export path."},{"title":"Carry structure into the next artifact","choice":"The handoff offers editable output and a written specification, alongside the clickable prototype.","reason":"The transition from a rough screen to a working conversation should preserve the decisions already made.","tradeoff":"Generated structure is a starting point. Placeholder copy and sketch styling should not be presented as finished product requirements."}],"outcome":"I used an intentionally rough visual language across the canvas, files workspace and prototype flow. The product includes 250 sketch components and editable handoff formats, so an early idea can move forward without pretending to be a finished interface.","reflection":"The roughness needs to make revision easier, not make the tool harder to use. I would test whether someone can change a screen, demonstrate its flow and hand it over without losing the decisions they already made.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: give participants an early product idea, ask them to revise it after feedback, and then explain the exported prototype to someone else.","Proposed measure: time to the first understandable screen, effort to revise its structure, and corrections needed after export."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and docs/architecture.md","detail":"Visual intent, component library, three surfaces, local file model and storage trade-off."},{"label":"src/io/export.ts and src/store/store.ts","detail":"Export and document-state behaviour supporting the documented workflow. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://applecider.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/applecider.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":14,"pdfPage":6,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Room to sketch [[before committing to a design.]]","scene":"Picture an early design review drifting into a discussion about colour and spacing. The underlying idea is still changing, but the polished screen already feels too finished to challenge.","question":"Can a deliberately unfinished interface still have useful structure?","insight":"[[Low visual fidelity can preserve room to change]]. Clickable connections and portable outputs let a rough idea move into the next design conversation.","pivot":"I kept the sketch-like visual language and gave it the capabilities of a working design tool: move, revise, link, and hand off.","takeaway":"Commit to the structure only as fast as the idea earns it.","journey":[{"title":"Sketch","detail":"Get an idea onto a movable canvas."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Change the structure while it still feels disposable."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Link the screens into a prototype."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Export the structure for the next conversation."}],"decisionLeads":["Make unfinished feel intentional.","Give the person ownership of the work.","Carry the structure into the next artifact."],"annotations":[{"title":"The sketch is the point","detail":"The visual language makes the stage of the work clear."},{"title":"Structure underneath","detail":"Editable components and exports give the rough screen a useful next step."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · sketch and handoff workflow","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer discussing an early idea","context":"Exploring structure before investing in final visual polish.","need":"Make an idea concrete while keeping it easy to change.","friction":"A finished-looking mockup can make tentative decisions feel settled.","implication":"Keep the drawing rough and the interactions usable."}},"theme":{"layout":"sketchbook","mode":"light","paper":"#fffdf8","ink":"#2f2b25","dim":"#6c6256","line":"#d7cdbd","accent":"#a33a20","wash":"#f8e5d7","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"An idea still in pencil","process":"Keep it changeable","decisions":"Rough on purpose","outcome":"A sketch you can discuss","reflection":"What I would try with designers"},"decisionHeading":"Keep early ideas easy to revise.","resultHeading":"Rough enough to revise. Real enough to discuss."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/applecider.webp","alt":"AppleCider — archived product interface","caption":"The archived homepage demonstrates the intentionally rough visual language. These are the project’s own wireframe examples.","label":"Original wireframe examples","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"A wireframe tool that [[looks like a sketch]] and works like a real canvas","hook":"AppleCider is a web tool I designed and built on my own for the first hour of a design, when the idea is still changing. It draws like a pen sketch, keeps your work in your own browser, and turns rough screens into a clickable flow or starter code.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself: make the first hour of a design faster"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"September to October 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web. Runs in the browser with no account. The editor needs a desktop screen"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 18, TypeScript, Vite, Zustand, Immer"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live, version 0.1.0. Not tested with users. No usage numbers"}],"links":[{"label":"Open AppleCider","url":"https://applecider.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: at the start of a design, a polished mock-up gets people arguing about colour and corners, while a paper sketch cannot be moved, shared or reused.","What I built: a browser tool that draws in a hand-drawn style on a real canvas, with 250 parts, auto layout, layout grids, links between screens and export to images, a clickable review page or code.","The key idea: keep the look rough, make every box real underneath, and never turn the roughness into fake decisions when the work moves on.","Where it stands: live as version 0.1.0. I have not tested it with users and I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/editor-board.webp","alt":"The AppleCider editor with three hand-drawn phone screens for a shop app on the canvas, the parts library on the left and the style settings on the right","caption":"The editor with a sample board I built for this page from three of the library's ready-made phone screens. All names, prices and text inside the screens are the library's placeholder content.","width":2400,"height":1500},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"The first hour of a design needs a sketch you can move","blocks":[{"p":"The first hour of a design is when you decide what a screen is for. It is also when the costly mistakes happen, while everyone still thinks they agree."},{"p":"The usual tools pull in two directions. A paper sketch is fast, and nobody mistakes it for finished work. But you cannot move a block, send the file or reuse the header on the next screen. A polished mock-up fixes all of that, and then people argue about colour and corner sizes on a layout that should still be easy to throw away."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing a paper sketch, AppleCider and a polished mock-up, with the strengths and weaknesses of each","caption":"The gap I wanted to close: the look of the sketch on the left with the abilities of the tool on the right.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"So I set myself one goal: shorten the time from \"I have an idea\" to \"here is the screen\". A wireframe, a rough plan of a screen made of boxes and labels, should keep the honesty of a sketch and still be a real file."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/landing-hero.webp","alt":"The AppleCider front page with the headline Wireframes at the speed of thought, two buttons and a sketched dashboard and phone beside it","caption":"The front page says the promise in one line. The sketched dashboard and phone are drawn live by the tool itself, not pasted in as pictures.","width":1600,"height":632}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"No interviews or user tests sit behind this goal. It is my own reasoning about early design work, and this page does not claim anything more."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Three places in one web page, and no account","blocks":[{"p":"AppleCider is one web page with three places in it: a front page, a drive for your designs and the editor. Your designs are never sent to a server. They are saved in your own browser and can be saved out as a plain file."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/surfaces.webp","alt":"Diagram of the three places, front page, drive and editor, with three shared foundations below: saved in the browser, a file you own and any static host","caption":"How the parts connect. Look at the bottom row: no account, no upload, and the whole thing can sit on any plain web host.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/files-drive.webp","alt":"The drive showing three designs as live thumbnails, one starred, with All files, Recent, Starred and Trash in the sidebar","caption":"The drive. Each card draws the real document, so a thumbnail can never be out of date. The star and the trash count come from the designs I made for this page.","width":1600,"height":550}]},{"p":"The editor is where the work happens. I kept the canvas clear: tools live in the top bar, panels on either side, and only the zoom control floats."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/annotated-editor.webp","alt":"The editor with eight numbered notes on the top bar, the rail of panel icons, the parts library, the frame labels, the inspector and the zoom control","caption":"The editor part by part. Note 4 matters most: one rail switches between seven panels, so the left side never fights over one row of tabs as the tool grows.","width":1600,"height":1440}]}]},{"id":"rough","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Rough on purpose","heading":"It looks unfinished on purpose, and every box still says what it is","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: wobbly lines, a handwriting-style font called Comic Neue and crossed-out picture boxes. One switch changes the whole board between three styles: Sketch, Wire (clean grey lines) and Mono (monospace type and hard shadows)."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/style-sketch.webp","alt":"A phone product screen drawn in the Sketch style with wobbly lines and handwriting-style type","caption":"Sketch. The lines wobble, so nobody reads it as final.","width":872,"height":1794},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/style-wire.webp","alt":"The same product screen in the Wire style with straight grey lines and a plain sans-serif font","caption":"Wire. The same screen in clean grey, for a tidier review.","width":872,"height":1794},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/style-mono.webp","alt":"The same product screen in the Mono style with monospace type and black buttons","caption":"Mono. Monospace type and hard edges. Nothing else on the board changed.","width":872,"height":1794}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"In the build: the wobble is one SVG filter laid over the whole canvas, with a slider for how strong it is. I ruled out redrawing every shape with a sketch library. One filter is cheaper, and it bends text and lines together the way a real pen does."},{"p":"The style switch changes one class on the canvas. The parts themselves never know which style is on, which is why all three stay consistent."},{"p":"Rough is not the same as vague, though. If every part is the same thin rectangle, people stop reading the drawing and only read the labels. So each of the 250 parts takes one role, and the role sets its line, fill and corner."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/roles.webp","alt":"Diagram of ten visual roles: structure, surface, raised, field, control, bar, bare, media, sticky note and annotation, each with a small sample","caption":"The visual rules, redrawn. Compare Field and Control: a thin tinted well means \"type here\", a thick rounded block means \"click here\", and you can tell them apart from across a room.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A sketch style can hurt legibility, and the three fonts wrap text differently. A built-in check measures every part in all three styles for boxes that spill out, clipped text or parts that draw nothing. I recorded the library passing all 250 in all three."}}]},{"id":"speed","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Fast from a blank page","heading":"From a blank page to a laid-out screen in a few keystrokes","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: drop a frame from 26 device sizes, press Alt and a number to split it into columns, then fill the columns from the library. Each column is real auto layout, a container that lines up and spaces its contents for you."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/split-1-frame.webp","alt":"An empty desktop frame drawn inside a monitor outline on the canvas","caption":"Step 1: an empty desktop frame, drawn with its monitor stand.","width":1600,"height":1246},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/split-2-columns.webp","alt":"The same desktop frame split into three equal columns, all three selected","caption":"Step 2: one shortcut splits it into three columns.","width":1600,"height":1246},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/split-3-filled.webp","alt":"The three columns holding a sign-in form, a revenue card with a line chart, and a task column","caption":"Step 3: parts dropped into the columns line up on their own.","width":1600,"height":1246}],"layout":"three"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/palette.webp","alt":"The insert search box with the word pricing typed and three results: pricing card, pricing page and pricing table","caption":"Press the slash key anywhere, type a few letters and press Enter. The part lands where the pointer was.","width":1400,"height":560}]},{"p":"In the build: once auto layout exists, the stored position of a part inside it is wrong, because the browser decides where it goes. I could have rebuilt the browser's layout rules in my own code. I ruled that out and ask the browser instead. Selection, snapping, hit-testing and export all measure the live page."},{"p":"Dragging needed the same idea. When you drag a part out of a column, it is lifted out at the exact spot where it sat on screen. When you drop it into another column, the tool finds the nearest gap and shows a line where it will land. The whole drag is one undo step."},{"p":"Layout grids follow the same thinking. A grid only draws guides and snaps. It never owns a part, so you can change the grid under finished work, or delete it, without losing anything."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/annotated-grid.webp","alt":"The dashboard screen with a layout grid shown as tinted regions and columns, and the grid settings in the inspector, with eight numbered notes","caption":"A grid on a sample dashboard from the library. Look at notes 6 and 7: any region can split again, and each part takes a share of the space.","width":1600,"height":1660}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/grid-tree.webp","alt":"Diagram of a stored tree of regions, frame, header, body, sidebar and content, beside the boxes it produces on the frame","caption":"What the grid stores and what you see. A sidebar beside an eight-column content area is three choices, not a pile of throwaway boxes.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Measuring the page costs time on every read. At wireframe scale, hundreds of parts, I have not seen it matter, but I have not measured large boards. Tests with 50 and 100 screens are on the list."}}]},{"id":"flow","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Screens that click","heading":"Screens link together, so a rough idea can be clicked through","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: select any part, choose which screen it opens, then press Present. Click an empty spot and every link on the screen lights up, so a reviewer knows what to try."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/flow-panel.webp","alt":"The prototype flow panel listing each screen with its incoming and outgoing links, next to the three phone screens","caption":"The flow panel lists every link as text: the home screen's picture opens the detail screen, and the detail screen has two links out. A missing destination can be fixed from here.","width":1600,"height":1273}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/present-hotspots.webp","alt":"Present mode showing the home screen with the first picture highlighted in blue as a link","caption":"Present mode. I clicked an empty spot, and the one link on this screen lit up in blue.","width":900,"height":1920,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/present-detail.webp","alt":"Present mode showing the detail screen after clicking the picture","caption":"Clicking the picture opens the detail screen. The bar at the top shows where you are: 2 of 3.","width":900,"height":1920,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"In the build: links are stored on the parts themselves, so they travel with the file. They also survive export. The clickable HTML review page and the generated code both keep them, with one small click handler instead of a full router."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A link can only open another screen. Going back, overlays, transitions and links to other websites are not built yet."}}]},{"id":"handoff","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Code that does not invent","heading":"The code export keeps the structure and refuses to invent the rest","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one shortcut turns the board into a small React project, plain HTML and CSS, or a written brief for a developer. Retyping a wireframe into code is slow, dull work, and this skips it."},{"p":"The hard part was knowing what not to carry over. A wireframe is vague on purpose. Its greys are placeholders and its thick lines are thick because they are drawn. Turning those into real colour and border choices would launder guesses into decisions nobody made."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/codegen.webp","alt":"Diagram of the export: the wireframe becomes a plain description of the screens, which is printed as React and Tailwind, HTML and CSS, or a build spec, with a list of what is left behind and what is carried across","caption":"One description of the screens, printed three ways. The red box is the point: what the export leaves out on purpose.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/code-react.webp","alt":"The export code window on the React tab showing the generated checkout screen, with a file list on the left","caption":"The checkout screen as React. Look at line 8: the phone status bar comes out as a note to skip it, not as fake code. The card titles became headings and the panels became sections.","width":1600,"height":1086}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/annotated-spec.webp","alt":"The build spec tab of the export window with six numbered notes on the counts, the output tabs, the scope switch, the rules and the list of repeated parts","caption":"The written brief. Notes 4 and 5 carry the main idea: it tells the reader which parts are decided and which are still open.","width":1600,"height":1500}]},{"p":"In the build: all the thinking happens in one step that works out what each box is. A button becomes a real button, a table gets real header cells, and heading levels follow text size. Parts that repeat are found by their shape, ignoring their words, and lifted out once. The three outputs are then simple printers, so a new one is a single file."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Charts, maps and date pickers come out as labelled gaps, not working parts. Choosing a chart library is a decision, and the export leaves it to you."}}]},{"id":"second","label":"Second pass","nav":"Second pass","heading":"A second pass, checked against what people expect from Balsamiq","blocks":[{"p":"Five weeks after the first version I compared AppleCider with Balsamiq, a long-standing wireframing tool. I read its documentation and the themes in public reviews on G2 and Capterra. This was desk research, not research with my own users, and reviews are individual opinions, not test results."},{"p":"The gaps it showed were about working over time, not about drawing. I built these and wrote down the limit of each:"},{"table":{"head":["What people expect","What I added","Its limit"],"rows":[["Reuse common parts","Save a part as a component, insert copies, push changes to them, or detach one","Only inside one document. Pushing a change replaces the copies, it does not merge edits"],["Give feedback","Comments on a part or the whole document, with replies, resolve and filters","Stored in the file. Shared by sending the file, not live"],["Try alternatives","Named versions with saved and current previews, restore, download, or a separate copy","The last 30 per document, kept in this browser"],["Find things on a big board","Search screen names and text, then jump to the result","First 100 matches"],["Share without an account","A clickable HTML review page, which can be printed to PDF","View only. No editing or comments in it"],["Export every screen","All visible screens as a ZIP of SVG files","Some design apps cannot open this kind of SVG"],["Use real images","Upload pictures straight into an image box","Up to 10 MB each"]]}},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/comments.webp","alt":"The review comments panel with one comment attached to the revenue chart on a sample dashboard","caption":"A comment pinned to one part of the dashboard. I wrote this sample note for the page; the author field takes any name.","width":1600,"height":944},{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/versions.webp","alt":"The versions panel with a saved version called Starter layout and small previews of the saved and current design","caption":"A saved version beside the current design, after I switched the board to the Wire style.","width":1600,"height":944}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"The same pass made saving safer. Each save now remembers which file it belongs to at the moment of the edit, so a quick switch between designs cannot write to the wrong one. Failed saves can be retried, and imported files are checked and rejected if they are broken. This is also when I added the first automated tests."}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One document, drawn by the browser and measured back","blocks":[{"p":"Every part on the canvas, from a frame to a button, has the same shape in the document. So undo, copy and paste, and export are each written once. Every change goes through one function, which is what makes undo and saving reliable."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: one store and its single path for changes; the renderer, browser layout and measuring; and the browser database, images and review page, and code","caption":"What runs where. The green boxes are the two ideas the rest depends on: one store, and measuring the real page.","width":1600,"height":830}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Interface","React 18 and TypeScript, built with Vite"],["Document and undo","Zustand with Immer. Unchanged parts are shared between undo steps, so a long history stays small"],["Saving","The browser's own database (IndexedDB), with a .cider.json file to keep or send"],["Everything else","Written in the project: canvas, snapping, icons, charts, export, code output and the ZIP writer"],["Hosting","A static site that any plain web host can serve. My hosting setup adds a strict security policy: no third-party scripts and no analytics"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"5","label":"runtime libraries in total"},{"value":"250","label":"parts in 15 groups, 20 of them full screens"},{"value":"26","label":"device frames, from a watch to an ultrawide monitor"},{"value":"25","label":"regression test cases, recorded passing on 27 October 2026"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/applecider/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of four commits: three on 21 September 2026 for the first version, hosting and layout grids, and one on 27 October for the second pass","caption":"The build in four commits. The first one holds the whole first version, so the history shows little of the process before it.","width":1600,"height":600}]},{"p":"The checks that run on every push are the type checker and the production build. The regression tests cover save races, retries, undo, copy and paste, link remapping and broken imports. They run by hand. A separate set tests the browser database wrapper."},{"p":"The hardest problems were the ones where what you export has to match what you see:"},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What I did"],"rows":[["Phone corners","In the first version the square screen poked out through the rounded phone outline. It was the most visible bug. Now each device clips its screen to its own corner radius."],["Fonts in exports","Web fonts do not travel inside an exported image, so a PNG would show a default font on another computer. The export embeds the fonts inside the file."],["PNG export in Chrome","Chrome refuses to turn an SVG into a PNG when the SVG comes from one kind of temporary link. The export loads it a different way."],["Text that did not fit","Text boxes had a fixed width of 200px by default. Four short labels in one order tracker overflowed it by 368px. Making text fit its content by default fixed 18 parts at once."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no usage numbers and no user tests yet","blocks":[{"p":"AppleCider is live at version 0.1.0 and carries the MIT licence. It needs no account, has no analytics and keeps designs in the browser, so I have no usage numbers. Nobody has tested it in a study."},{"p":"For this page I ran the built version and made the sample boards shown here. These are not done yet:"},{"list":["Editing on phones and tablets.","Working on one board with other people at the same time, or syncing between devices.","Richer links: back, overlays, transitions and outside links.","Importing Balsamiq files.","Speed tests on large boards, and working fully offline."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Find out if it really is faster, then add people","blocks":[{"list":["Test the one number the tool is built for. Give people an early product idea, time them to a screen someone else can understand, ask them to change it after feedback, and then have them explain the export to a colleague.","Measure large boards with 50 and 100 screens before claiming the canvas stays fast.","Then the bigger pieces: shared editing without a server, parts that can be reused across projects, and code output for Vue, Svelte and SwiftUI from the same description."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Rough on the surface, strict underneath","blocks":[{"p":"The roughness only works if the structure under it is precise. The sketch look gives people permission to change things. The roles, the grid and the measured layout make sure that what they draw still means something."},{"p":"The export taught me the same lesson from the other side. The most useful thing it does is refuse: it leaves the greys, the drawn lines and the dummy text behind and says so. And comparing my tool with an established one showed me that drawing was never the weak spot. Keeping, sharing and coming back to the work was."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","headline":"A trip plan that leaves room for people."}},"planit":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","kind":"product","headline":"A group trip planner that catches the flight that lands too late.","summary":"PlanIt is a web app I designed and built on my own. It plans trips where people arrive, attend and leave at different times, in different time zones, and it reads the plan back to say what cannot work.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Group planning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Shared","label":"plan across multiple views"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"board-to-time action"},{"value":"Local","label":"no account required"}],"problem":"A group trip is not a single ordered checklist. Different people arrive at different times, attend parallel activities, and move between places. I approached PlanIt as several useful views of one plan, with a board for thinking and a time model for checking whether that plan can work.","process":["I distinguished tentative ideas from scheduled commitments. The canvas supports exploration, while itinerary, timeline, map, and people views help the group understand the consequences of a plan.","The key interaction is the deliberate move from arrangement to schedule. Pins protect an existing commitment; loose ideas can stay undecided. The UX needs to explain what Resolve changes and make conflicts understandable."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the board a clear but limited grammar","choice":"Day frames, ordering, and connectors convey meaning; loose cards can remain uncommitted ideas.","reason":"A planning surface needs room for uncertainty instead of assigning a date and time to everything immediately.","tradeoff":"Spatial meaning must be taught. A frame or connector that looks decorative can otherwise change a plan unexpectedly."},{"title":"Make scheduling an intentional action","choice":"Tidy turns the existing itinerary into a board; Resolve reads the board back into the schedule.","reason":"A person should know when arrangement becomes a commitment. Automatic rearrangement would undermine the value of a free board.","tradeoff":"Two explicit directions add a concept to learn, so the product needs visible feedback about what changed and why."},{"title":"Protect decisions the planner has already made","choice":"Pinned times win, untouched loose cards opt out, and resolution only moves times forward when constraints require it.","reason":"Automation should respect existing intent and explain an impossible plan rather than silently rewrite it.","tradeoff":"A conservative resolver may leave conflicts unresolved. The interface must help the person decide what to relax."}],"outcome":"I designed the board, itinerary, timeline, map and people views as different ways of working with the same trip. Scheduling is deliberate, pinned commitments stay protected, and conflicts remain visible. That behaviour is the part of PlanIt I would explain first to a new group.","reflection":"I would try the planner with a late arrival, two parallel activities and a booking that cannot move. The useful test is whether people can predict what Resolve will change and spot a conflict before it becomes a problem on the trip.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: plan a trip with parallel sessions, a pinned departure, and travellers in two zones; ask participants to predict Resolve before using it.","Proposed measure: unexpected schedule changes, correctly identified conflicts, and confidence explaining the final plan."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Planning views, board grammar, sharing model and scheduling constraints."},{"label":"src/core/resolve.ts and src/core/store.ts","detail":"Priority of pins, opt-in cards and forward-only resolution. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://planit.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/planit.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":9,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A trip plan [[that leaves room for people.]]","scene":"Imagine one person at a conference session, another arriving later, and a shared dinner across town. Put it all into one simple list and the useful differences disappear.","question":"When should a loose idea become a scheduled commitment?","insight":"A board needs room for uncertainty. [[Scheduling should be an explicit decision]], with existing commitments protected.","pivot":"I separated thinking on the board from committing to the schedule, with clear UX rules for what automation is allowed to change.","takeaway":"Useful automation makes its changes predictable.","journey":[{"title":"Arrange","detail":"Use frames and connectors to express intent."},{"title":"Protect","detail":"Keep pinned times as commitments."},{"title":"Resolve","detail":"Translate the arrangement into a schedule deliberately."},{"title":"Explain","detail":"Make remaining conflicts visible to the planner."}],"decisionLeads":["Let an idea stay an idea.","Make the moment of commitment visible.","Give automation a boundary it cannot cross."],"annotations":[{"title":"Several views, one plan","detail":"The working product carries the same trip across planning surfaces."},{"title":"An explicit contract","detail":"Pins, opt-in cards, and forward-only changes define the resolver."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · scheduling rules","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person coordinating a group trip","context":"Combining people with different arrivals, activities, and commitments.","need":"Turn possibilities into a plan while protecting confirmed times.","friction":"A single list hides overlaps and treats ideas as promises.","implication":"Separate tentative cards from explicit scheduling."}},"theme":{"layout":"planner","mode":"dark","paper":"#0d0f17","ink":"#edeff7","dim":"#a8b0c6","line":"#343e56","accent":"#ffb094","wash":"#35251f","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Instrument Sans","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"People with different plans","process":"Ideas are not commitments","decisions":"The scheduling contract","outcome":"The shared plan","reflection":"Where understanding can break"},"decisionHeading":"Separate ideas from confirmed plans.","resultHeading":"One plan, with room for different people."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/planit.webp","alt":"PlanIt — archived product interface","caption":"The archived PlanIt interface. Ideas, scheduled commitments and location are treated as different views of a shared trip.","label":"Planning workspace","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"v2":{"title":"A group trip planner that catches the [[flight that lands too late]].","hook":"PlanIt is a web app I designed and built on my own. It plans trips where people arrive, attend and leave at different times, in different time zones, and it reads the plan back to say what cannot work.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"September 2026"},{"label":"Why","value":"My own brief: group trips where one shared list stops working"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web. Runs entirely in the browser, with a phone layout"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Leaflet, lz-string"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live. No usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Open PlanIt","url":"https://planit.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: on a group trip, people land, attend and leave at different times. One shared list hides who is where, and it cannot tell you when a plan is impossible.","What I built: one plan with eight views, a whiteboard that turns into a schedule only when asked, and a checker that flags journeys that do not fit.","The key idea: automation should be predictable. Resolve only moves cards that opted in, only pushes them later, and never moves a pinned time.","Where it stands: live, with no accounts and no server. The UX India group used it to plan their trip. I have no usage numbers beyond that."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/planit/timeline-lead.webp","alt":"PlanIt timeline for Saturday 26 September of the sample trip: a filter panel on the left and one lane per traveller, with four Track B sessions outlined in red","caption":"The timeline on one day of the sample trip. Each lane is one person. The red outlines mark a session that one of its four travellers cannot reach in time. Names are blurred.","width":1600,"height":700},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"One list cannot show eight different days","blocks":[{"p":"On a group trip, people rarely do the same thing at the same time. One flies in a day early. Two go to a different track. Someone leaves before the farewell dinner. Most planners still treat the trip as one list that everyone follows."},{"p":"I set myself the brief of planning a trip like that properly. PlanIt opens with a worked example: eight people meet in Bengaluru for a conference, from six cities and three time zones."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/problem.webp","alt":"Chart of eight sample travellers as bars across 22 September to 1 October, coloured by conference track, with the Track A, Track B and workshop days shaded","caption":"The sample trip as eight bars. Look at how few days everyone is there at once, and at the two travellers who land on the morning Track B begins.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"p":"Two faults are built into the example on purpose, so the checker has something to find. The worse one looks fine on paper. A flight lands at 09:15 and the session starts at 09:30. But the airport is 33 km from the venue, in morning traffic."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/late-arrival.webp","alt":"Time bar diagram: a flight lands at 09:15, 15 free minutes, sessions at 09:30, and below it a drive of about 1 hour 35 minutes that ends near 10:50","caption":"The fault in one picture. The schedule leaves 15 minutes. The app estimates the drive at about an hour and 35 minutes, so the plan is short by about 80.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"The library also holds a second trip, copied from a real group's planning board. Where the board was blank or had a question mark, the item is marked tentative instead of guessed."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"There were no interviews or surveys behind this. The problem comes from the kind of trip I wanted to plan, so this page quotes no users."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Eight views of the same plan, in the order a planner works","blocks":[{"p":"A trip holds people, places and a plan. The plan is made of blocks: a flight, a session, a lunch, a transfer. Eight views show the same blocks in different shapes, and every one of them can add, move and remove blocks."},{"p":"The first time a trip opens, a short tour explains the order that works. It starts with people, not with the calendar, because everything else hangs off who is coming."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/trip.webp","alt":"Trip view: the whole trip as a grid of day cards from 21 September to 1 October, each listing its first few blocks and the people present","caption":"Trip view. Each card is a day. It is the view for spotting the empty evening or moving something to another day.","width":1600,"height":720},{"src":"assets/cases/planit/tour-flow.webp","alt":"First-run tour card titled The order that works, listing five steps: add who is coming, sketch it on the board, fix what cannot happen, fill the empty evenings, hand it out","caption":"The tour's second card. Five steps, each tied to a view.","width":544,"height":478}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/day.webp","alt":"Day view for Saturday 26 September: hours down the side and a column per traveller, with flights at night and four red-outlined sessions from 09:30","caption":"Day view. Hours down the side, a column per person. It answers \"where do I need to be, and when?\"","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/planit/agenda.webp","alt":"Agenda view for Saturday 26 September: each block written as a line of text with its times, type, place and a note, and the journey between two stops","caption":"Agenda view. The plan as plain sentences, with the journeys spelled out. This is the version that prints and that a screen reader reads.","width":1600,"height":1000}],"layout":"two"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/ideas.webp","alt":"Ideas view: a column of activities not yet scheduled, each showing who is interested, beside day columns that count evening items and invite a drop","caption":"Ideas view. Things people want to do, with interest counted. Drop one on a day and it looks for a free evening slot.","width":1600,"height":720}]}]},{"id":"people","label":"People first","nav":"People first","heading":"Each person keeps their own arrival, their own window and their own clock","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a lane per person on the timeline, and a card per person that holds where they fly from, when they arrive and leave, their hotel, what they like and how full their days are. A stretch before someone arrives is shaded and labelled with the day they land, so an empty lane is never a mystery."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/people.webp","alt":"People view: eight traveller cards showing origin city, track, arrival and departure flights, hotel, interests and a daily load bar","caption":"People view. Every field here feeds the checker: arrival, departure, the window someone can be there, diet and interests.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/planit/clock-menu.webp","alt":"The Show times in menu: item local time, trip time, my device, and one entry per traveller's home clock with its zone","caption":"The clock menu. Times can show in each place's own zone, in trip time, or as any one traveller sees them from home.","width":560,"height":660}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"In the build: every moment is stored in UTC, the world reference time. A block also records the zone it physically happens in. The screen then shows it in whichever zone you pick. This handles daylight saving, India's half-hour offset and Nepal's quarter-hour one."},{"p":"One rule took a bug to find. The board groups cards into days, and at first it read the day in whatever clock was on screen. Switching to a London traveller's clock moved every overnight flight to the wrong day. Now the board always reads days in the trip's own zone, because it is one shared board."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A person takes longer to add than a name in a list. I kept it because each field earns its place: the checker uses all of them."}}]},{"id":"board","label":"The board","nav":"The board","heading":"The board stays a whiteboard until you press Resolve","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a board you can pan and zoom, with cards you place by hand. It is deliberately not a calendar. Nothing on it touches the clock until you ask."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/annotated-board.webp","alt":"The board zoomed into Saturday and Sunday frames, with six numbered notes on the Times and Resolve buttons, a day frame, gap chips, parallel columns and the day ribbon","caption":"The sample trip laid out with Tidy. Look at note 4: the gap chips say what Resolve would do before you press it.","width":1600,"height":1300}]},{"p":"Position gets its meaning from frames and connectors. I kept this grammar small on purpose, so a card's place on the board always means one thing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/board-grammar.webp","alt":"Six small panels: inside a day frame, top to bottom, side by side, outside any frame with a dashed edge, a travel connector and a then connector","caption":"The board's grammar. These panels are drawn to explain the rules, not captured from the app.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"In the build: the board stores positions and the calendar stores times. Only one piece of code turns one into the other. That is what lets a card sit somewhere meaningless without breaking the plan."},{"p":"Two buttons cross between them. Tidy lays the existing plan out on the board, a frame per day. Resolve reads the board back and hands out times. I ruled out a board that rearranges itself, because a board that moves under your hands is not a board. Resolve follows four rules, so its changes are predictable."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/resolve-rules.webp","alt":"Diagram of Tidy and Resolve as two directions between the plan and the board, then four rules for Resolve, each with a small before and after","caption":"Resolve's four rules. The small before and after cards are an illustration, not a capture.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"A test checks the most important property: on a freshly tidied board, Resolve changes nothing. In the app it says so in plain words: \"Everything already sits where the board says it should.\""},{"p":"One default taught me how far automation can reach. Leaving ten minutes between back-to-back cards rewrote thirty cards of a schedule that was already fine. So the default gap is zero: lunch can end as the afternoon session begins."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/board-flows.webp","alt":"The whole board at 75 percent zoom, with day frames in a row and coloured lines that bundle travellers moving from one card to another","caption":"People flows on. The lines are worked out from who is on each card and the clock, not from anything drawn. Four people making the same hop share one line.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Spatial meaning has to be learned. A frame that looks decorative can change the plan, and two buttons are one more idea to hold. The view description and the tour explain both, and each button explains itself on hover."}}]},{"id":"checker","label":"The checker","nav":"The checker","heading":"Warnings come in three strengths, so people keep reading them","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a list of issues, sorted by how serious they are. On the sample trip there is 1 blocking issue, 2 risky ones and 41 to consider, such as free evenings that match someone's interests or long days with no meal."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/annotated-issues.webp","alt":"The timeline with the issues panel open, with six numbered notes on the issue levels, the blocking issue, a tight transfer, the clock menu, journey ribbons and red-outlined blocks","caption":"The checker beside the timeline. Compare notes 2 and 3: one is impossible, the other is two minutes short, and they are not given the same weight.","width":1600,"height":1240}]},{"p":"A journey can fail in three ways, and each needs a different fix. So each gets its own verdict."},{"table":{"head":["Verdict","What it means","Example from the app"],"rows":[["Tight","The cab is slow. Leave earlier or allow more time.","A hotel to a restaurant, 2.5 km, two minutes short"],["Needs a flight","Nothing on the ground closes the gap. A flight would, and none is booked.","Delhi to Bengaluru with eight hours and no booking"],["Impossible","Even the quickest route does not fit. Move something.","Bengaluru to Goa, 560 km, in three hours"]]}},{"p":"I held the line between them to a real margin. Calling a two-minute shortfall impossible teaches people to ignore the warnings that are. Repeated findings are also rolled up per person per day, so the real problems stay visible."},{"p":"In the build: travel time starts from a road route when the routing service answers. A traffic curve for the time of day, set per city, goes on top, so 09:15 in Delhi is not 09:15 in Goa. Airport check-in and walking time are added separately. The screen says which number came from the router and which from the model."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/map-offline.webp","alt":"Map view for Saturday 26 September with no map tiles: seven numbered stops joined by dashed straight lines, and a stop list noting that routing is unavailable","caption":"The map with the network off. The tiles are blank, the routes fall back to dashed straight lines, and the panel says \"Routing unavailable\".","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/planit/inspector.webp","alt":"Details panel for a Track B session, with the blocking issue at the top explaining the 33 km drive and the 15 free minutes","caption":"Select a flagged block and the reason is the first thing in its details.","width":368,"height":440}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"Travel times are modelled estimates, not live traffic. I captured these screens offline, so the drive time shown is the app's own model."}}]},{"id":"sharing","label":"Sharing","nav":"Sharing","heading":"A plan can be shared without a server or an account","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a share dialog with a link, a calendar file and a backup. A link can be read-only or an editable copy, and it can carry one person's days instead of the whole trip."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/share.webp","alt":"Share this plan dialog: read only or editable copy, a choice of whose itinerary to send, a link with a Copy button and its length, calendar export with reminders, and other formats","caption":"The share dialog. For the sample trip the link is 9,934 characters long, because the whole plan travels inside it.","width":607,"height":738,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: the plan is compressed into the part of the link after the \"#\". Browsers never send that part to a server, so a link is private to whoever holds it and needs nothing to sign in to. Calendar files write times in UTC, so Google, Outlook and Apple Calendar each show them in the reader's own zone."},{"p":"Trips are saved in the browser, one entry per trip plus a small index. A hosted app that stores data in the browser must never lose it when a new version ships. So the index is treated as a cache: if it cannot be read, it is rebuilt from the trips. Older storage names are still searched, and a save that fails says so and offers a backup."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"With no accounts, a trip lives in one browser on one device. Moving it means a link or a JSON backup. I ruled out accounts and sync because they need a server, and this app has none by design."}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Keyboard, contrast and colour were built in, not added later","blocks":[{"p":"Drag and drop works from the keyboard, using the same logic as the mouse. Space picks a block up, the arrow keys move it, Enter drops it and Escape puts it back. Each step is announced with the new time. This meets two WCAG rules: everything works from a keyboard, and dragging always has a simpler alternative."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/tooltip.webp","alt":"The board toolbar with a tooltip under Resolve to timeline that gives its name, its shortcut and one line on what it does","caption":"Every control explains itself: a name, its shortcut and what it is for. The tip also appears on keyboard focus, and Escape closes it.","width":960,"height":400}]},{"p":"I measured contrast for every visible piece of text against the background it is actually painted on. Both themes have zero failures at the AA level. The lowest ratio is 4.99 to 1, and about two thirds of the text passes the stricter AAA level. A higher-contrast setting raises that to 91%."},{"p":"Colour is never the only signal. Every block has an icon as well as a colour. A tentative block is hatched, dashed and labelled. The app also follows the system settings for reduced motion, reduced transparency, more contrast and Windows high contrast."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/day-light.webp","alt":"Day view in the light theme for Saturday 26 September, with hatched tentative blocks and red-outlined sessions","caption":"The light theme. Look at the sunrise trip: it is tentative, so it is hatched and dashed, not just a paler colour.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/planit/phone-agenda.webp","alt":"Phone layout of the agenda for Saturday 26 September, with a tentative label, a clash label and a tab bar of views at the bottom","caption":"On a phone, the views move to a tab bar at the bottom.","width":780,"height":1458,"kind":"tall"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are my own measurements with my own audit script. They are not a study with people who use a screen reader, and I do not claim one."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"No server: a tested core, eight views and the browser's own storage","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: views, one store and a core with no screen code; browser storage, share link and calendar files; and three optional online services with their offline fallbacks","caption":"How PlanIt is put together. Everything on the right is optional. With the network off, each part falls back to something that still works.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","React 19, TypeScript, Vite"],["Map","Leaflet, with OpenStreetMap tiles"],["Share links","lz-string compression"],["Routes and places","A public routing service and OpenStreetMap search, both optional"],["Hosting","Four static files. Any static host works"]]}},{"p":"The core has no screen code in it, so it is tested on its own. The tests cover time zone maths, the traffic model, calendar output, share links, storage recovery, the board's maths and Resolve. The project has a single check that runs type checking, the tests and a production build."},{"stats":[{"value":"9","label":"commits"},{"value":"2","label":"days, 10 and 11 September 2026"},{"value":"8","label":"views of one plan"},{"value":"0","label":"servers, accounts or databases"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/planit/commits.webp","alt":"Timeline of nine commits on 10 and 11 September 2026, from the first planner to a second built-in trip","caption":"The project history. The board that resolves to the timeline arrived on the first evening.","width":1600,"height":660}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were small rules that broke big things."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Overnight flights","The board read days in the clock on screen, so switching clocks moved every overnight flight by a day. Days are now always read in the trip's zone."],["Default slack","Ten minutes between cards rewrote thirty cards of a good schedule. The default is now zero."],["Fast drags","Drag tracking started a moment too late, so a quick gesture could be lost. It now starts the instant the pointer goes down."],["Text in blocks","Blocks have a fixed height, and long text painted over the line below. Each block now measures first and drops the place, then the clock, then the title, down to just an icon."],["Dialogs","A style name used by both old and new dialogs made every dialog show at once. The new panels use their own name."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no accounts and no usage numbers","blocks":[{"p":"PlanIt is live and free to use. With no accounts and no server, I have no count of users or trips to report. These are also still open:"},{"list":["I have no feedback from a group using it to share here.","Travel times are estimates from a model, not live traffic.","A trip lives in one browser. Moving it needs a link or a backup file."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Find out whether people can predict what Resolve will do","blocks":[{"p":"The test I would run next:"},{"list":["Give people a trip with parallel sessions, a pinned departure and travellers in two time zones.","Ask them to say what Resolve will change before they press it.","Count the changes that surprised them, the conflicts they found on their own, and how well they can explain the final plan."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Useful automation is modest and easy to predict","blocks":[{"p":"Each time automation reached too far, it cost trust. Ten minutes of invented slack rewrote thirty cards. A board that rearranged itself would have stopped being a board. Resolve became useful when I limited what it may touch: cards that opted in, only later, never a pin."},{"p":"Warnings work the same way. If every shortfall is \"impossible\", people stop reading. Giving each kind of failure its own name and its own fix kept the one real problem easy to see among 44."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","headline":"What is happening around this place?"}},"earthlog":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","kind":"product","headline":"A globe that tells you what happened here, and how sure it is.","summary":"EarthLog is a web app I designed and built on my own. Click anywhere on Earth and it shows the quakes, NASA events, weather, night sky and landmark disasters around that point, each with its source, its age and its limits.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Data exploration · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"directions of exploration"},{"value":"Place-led","label":"event context"},{"value":"Labelled","label":"models and estimates"}],"problem":"A place can have recent earthquakes, old incident records, current weather, and predicted sky events. Bringing them onto one map makes exploration easier, but can also make very different information look equally current and reliable.","process":["I framed two ways into exploration: start from a place, or follow an interesting event to its location. Both routes should arrive at a readable local picture, rather than another layer to decode.","Freshness and uncertainty are part of the information hierarchy. An observation, an older open record, and a forecast need different labels so the map does not make them look equally current or certain."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with a place or a question","choice":"People can search for a place, select the globe, or follow an event shortcut.","reason":"Someone may be curious about the latest earthquake without knowing where it happened.","tradeoff":"Every route needs to lead back to the same place context."},{"title":"Show when the information was observed","choice":"The documented event model uses observation recency instead of treating every open incident as current.","reason":"A source’s administrative status can outlive the actual event. The label should communicate what is known.","tradeoff":"Freshness rules remain approximations; source links and timestamps must stay visible."},{"title":"Explain the limits beside the result","choice":"Eclipse paths, station passes, and aurora layers carry model limitations in the documented product.","reason":"A compelling map should not appear more precise than the calculation behind it.","tradeoff":"Caveats can overwhelm the visual story, so they belong beside the relevant result rather than only in documentation."}],"outcome":"I brought place search and event discovery together on the globe. A visitor can start with a location or an event, then inspect dates, sources and the relevant model limits. I kept that context close to the result rather than making the globe’s appearance carry the explanation.","reflection":"I would ask someone to explain one result in their own words: what happened, when it was observed and where the information came from. Finding an event is only part of the task.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask visitors to distinguish a recent observation from a modelled forecast and locate the source behind each result.","Proposed measure: correct interpretation of freshness and uncertainty, alongside success finding a relevant local event."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What it answers and Honesty notes","detail":"Bidirectional exploration, source freshness and model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://earthlog.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":12,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"What is happening [[around this place?]]","scene":"Imagine looking at a place on a globe and wanting more than a pin: the latest quake, tonight’s sky, or the weather around it. Those answers arrive from different sources with different levels of certainty.","question":"What is happening here—and how current is the information?","insight":"An incident can remain open after the last observation. [[A status label is not enough]] to explain whether something is happening now.","pivot":"I kept dates, sources, and model limits alongside the result, so visitors can judge the information while exploring.","takeaway":"Make the answer easy to find and easy to question.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Start with a place or an interesting event."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Read the local context across sources."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"See when the information was observed and how it was derived."}],"decisionLeads":["Offer a question as well as a place.","Make freshness more than a status label.","Keep uncertainty beside the answer."],"annotations":[{"title":"A place-led dossier","detail":"The capture shows how the globe connects to local information."},{"title":"An explanation with boundaries","detail":"Model limits remain part of the result."}],"evidenceTag":"Based on the documented freshness rules and source labels.","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"People exploring a place or following an event","context":"Following an event or asking what is happening near a location.","need":"Find local context and understand how current it is.","friction":"Different observations and forecasts can appear equally certain on a map.","implication":"Keep timestamps, sources, and uncertainty beside the result."}},"theme":{"layout":"atlas","mode":"dark","paper":"#06080b","ink":"#ece9e3","dim":"#b6bac1","line":"#454b55","accent":"#f2a93b","wash":"#33271a","bodyFont":"Public Sans","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Start with a place","process":"Keep the date beside the answer","decisions":"Reading an uncertain world","outcome":"The local picture","reflection":"What the map implies"},"decisionHeading":"Three choices behind the exploration","resultHeading":"One place to explore several kinds of information"},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","alt":"Earthlog — archived product interface","caption":"The actual exploration surface: globe and event entry points on the left; place, weather, sky and history in the inspector.","label":"Globe and place inspector","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Enter through a place","detail":"Search and globe selection start from a location."},{"title":"Enter through an event","detail":"Event shortcuts reverse the route into the same place context."},{"title":"Read in context","detail":"The inspector separates place, weather, sky and history."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":14,"y":488,"width":500,"height":192,"caption":"Event shortcuts let a visitor start with a question before choosing a place.","alt":"Original Earthlog event shortcuts for earthquakes, eclipses, the space station and other events."}]},"v2":{"title":"A globe that tells you [[what happened here]], and how sure it is.","hook":"EarthLog is a web app I designed and built on my own. Click anywhere on Earth and it shows the quakes, NASA events, weather, night sky and landmark disasters around that point, each with its source, its age and its limits.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"August 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"My own project, no client"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on desktop and phone"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript, MapLibre GL JS 5, Open Props"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live. No usage numbers, no user testing and no claim of scientific validation"}],"links":[{"label":"Open EarthLog","url":"https://earthlog.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: a measured quake, an old NASA record, a forecast and a worked-out eclipse all look the same as dots on a map. They are not equally fresh or equally sure.","What I built: a globe in the browser that answers from a place or from a question, with each answer showing its source, its age and how far to trust it.","The key idea: an open record is not an event happening now. I grade NASA events by when they were last seen, and put each model’s error next to its answer.","Where it stands: live on the web, built over three days in August 2026. I have no usage numbers, and there are no automated tests yet."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/lead-panels.webp","alt":"Three EarthLog panels side by side: the Place tab for a point near Naples, The Log filtered to deep history, and the record for the total solar eclipse of 2 August 2027","caption":"One point near Naples, three views: the Place tab, The Log showing landmark events, and the next total eclipse. Captured offline. The live feeds are empty and the globe is not shown.","width":2400,"height":1308},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A dot on a map hides how old and how sure it is","blocks":[{"p":"I wanted one place to ask what the planet is doing at any spot: the weather now, this week’s quakes, this century’s disasters and what is overhead tonight. I also wanted the reverse question. Where was the last big quake? Where does the next total eclipse land?"},{"p":"The data is free and public. The trouble is that it comes in different kinds. Some is measured by instruments. Some is a record nobody has closed. Some is a forecast, some is worked out from orbits, and some is a plan that may slip."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/sureness.webp","alt":"Table of six kinds of answer in EarthLog, from measured earthquakes to planned rocket launches, with how fresh each is and where it can mislead","caption":"The six kinds of answer, side by side. Read the right-hand column: each one can mislead in its own way.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"p":"Draw all of that as the same coloured dot and the map looks more certain than it is. So the design problem was to make each answer easy to find and easy to question."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did not interview anyone or test EarthLog with users. The people I designed for are an assumption: someone curious about a place, or following an event they heard about."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Start from a place or from a question, and land on the same readout","blocks":[{"p":"A globe fills the screen. As you zoom in, it unrolls into a flat map. A readout panel on the right has four tabs: Place, Weather, Sky and The Log."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/annotated-home.webp","alt":"The EarthLog start screen with six numbered notes: search, the Where is chips, the globe area, the four tabs, Layers and the status line","caption":"The first screen before anything is chosen. Note 3 is honest about my capture: offline, the globe could not load.","width":1600,"height":1340}]},{"p":"You can pick a point by clicking the globe, typing a name or pasting coordinates. Or you can press one of ten “Where is…” chips, which run the question the other way round."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/two-ways.webp","alt":"Diagram: three ways to start from a place on the left, ten question chips on the right, both arrows meeting at one readout with four tabs","caption":"Both routes end in the same readout, so there is one thing to learn.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"The Place tab is a short report on that point, always in the same order. The things that change by the hour come first. The deep past and the night sky come last."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/annotated-place.webp","alt":"The Place tab for a point near Naples with five numbered notes on the place header, weather, next eclipse, NASA events and recent quakes","caption":"The top half of the Place tab. Note 5 is a flaw my offline capture exposed, and I return to it in the last sections.","width":1600,"height":1240}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/place-record.webp","alt":"Lower half of the Place tab: two past eruptions near Naples, then a table of sun, daylight, moon, planets and the next meteor shower","caption":"The lower half. Past eruptions come with a distance and a compass direction from the chosen point. The sky lines are worked out in the browser, so they still appear offline.","width":880,"height":1352},{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/sky-tonight.webp","alt":"The Sky tab listing six planets with their height, direction, time and brightness, each with a bar for its height in the sky","caption":"The Sky tab. Each planet gets a height, a direction, a time and a plain word for how bright it is, such as “unmistakable” or “faint but naked-eye”.","width":880,"height":1330}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"The Log is the whole board in one list, newest first, with filters by kind. A deep-history filter lists the 48 landmark events that ship with the app."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/log-history.webp","alt":"The Log filtered to deep history: a list of past disasters from the 2022 Hunga Tonga eruption back through the 2004 Indian Ocean earthquake","caption":"The Log on its deep-history filter. These come from a curated list bundled with the app, so they show offline too.","width":880,"height":1736,"kind":"tall"}]}]},{"id":"backwards","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Ask it backwards","heading":"Ten chips that answer “where” as well as “what is here”","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a row of chips under the globe, such as “the last big quake” and “the next total eclipse”. Each one finds the answer, flies the globe there and opens the record."},{"p":"Someone may be curious about the latest earthquake without knowing where it happened. Making them search for a place first would ask a question they cannot answer."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/record-vesuvius.webp","alt":"Record for the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 CE, with its date, scale, position and distance from the chosen point","caption":"A deep-history record. The “something extraordinary” chip opens one of the 48 at random and flies the globe to it.","width":880,"height":848},{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/phone-eclipse.webp","alt":"EarthLog on a phone showing the total solar eclipse record for 2 August 2027","caption":"The next total eclipse chip, on a phone. Its answer comes from the bundled catalogue, so it works offline.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"In the build: each chip is a small query over data the app holds or can fetch. Some need no network. The next total eclipse and something extraordinary come from lists bundled with the app, and the planets are worked out in the browser. The rest wait on live feeds."},{"p":"The chips and the place search had to arrive at the same record view. Every record has a Back button that returns to the tab you came from."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A chip promises an answer. When its feed is down it can only say so, and nothing on the chip tells you in advance which ones need the network."}}]},{"id":"open","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Open is not now","heading":"An open NASA record is not an event happening now","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: each NASA event is labelled active, ongoing or ended, with when it was last seen. The status line at the bottom counts only the active ones."},{"p":"In the build: NASA’s EONET feed keeps an event open until someone closes it by hand. It holds over 2,000 open wildfire records, most of them long out, and volcanoes whose last satellite fix is a year old. Counting “open” would have made the planet look on fire."},{"p":"So I grade every event by its most recent observation, not by its status."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/open-vs-active.webp","alt":"Decision diagram: a NASA record is ended if closed, active if still open and seen within 30 days, ongoing if open but not seen for longer","caption":"The rule as a diagram. The ages in the labels are examples. My offline captures could not show live events, so this is drawn rather than shown on a screen.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The record view says the same in words. For an ongoing event it gives the date it was last seen and adds that an open record is not proof it is still going."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Thirty days is my cut-off, not NASA’s. A slow eruption last seen 31 days ago drops to ongoing, and a fire put out yesterday can still read as active until NASA closes it."}}]},{"id":"limits","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Limits beside the answer","heading":"Each answer carries its own error, right where you read it","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: under an eclipse, a space station pass or a forecast, a short note says how far to trust it, in plain words."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/annotated-eclipse.webp","alt":"An eclipse record for 1 June 2030 with five numbered notes: the focused Back button, catalogue facts, the answer for this place, the data table and the accuracy note","caption":"Look at notes 3 and 5. The answer for this point and the limit of the drawing sit one above the other.","width":1600,"height":1200}]},{"p":"In the build: I chose simple models that run in the browser with no server. That makes them fast and free, and less exact. So the notes had to be specific."},{"table":{"head":["Answer","How it is worked out","How far to trust it"],"rows":[["Eclipse paths","Dates and widths from NASA’s Five Millennium Canon. Each path is a dozen-odd points, smoothed into a curve.","About ±50 km. Enough to see if it crosses your region, not to choose a viewing spot."],["Space station passes","A Kepler + J2 orbit model, simpler than SGP4, the usual standard.","Drifts about half a minute a day from its starting data."],["Planets","JPL’s approximate orbital elements.","A few arcminutes. Fine for “is it up”, not for a narrow telescope view."],["Aurora","A model from the current geomagnetic index.","Real aurora is patchy and moves fast."],["Weather","Open-Meteo’s forecast, interpolated to the exact point.","The weather near there, not a station reading."],["Local time","Sun time from longitude, until the real time zone arrives.","Can be an hour or more off the clock on the wall."],["Quiet areas","USGS earthquake coverage.","Much denser in the United States. A quiet map can mean few instruments."]]}},{"p":"A space station pass is only called visible when the station is in sunlight, above your horizon, and your own sky is dark. Far north or south of its orbit, the app explains that the station can never climb high, instead of just saying there are no passes."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/sky-limits.webp","alt":"Foot of the Sky tab: a launch manifest message, space weather still loading, and a boxed note on the accuracy of eclipses, planets and station passes","caption":"The foot of the Sky tab, where the limits are repeated in one place. Offline, the launch list and space weather could not load.","width":880,"height":940}]},{"aside":{"title":"What this is not.","detail":"No scientist has checked these results, and EarthLog makes no claim of scientific accuracy. The notes are there so you can judge each answer yourself, with a link to the original source where one exists."}}]},{"id":"less","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Less on the globe","heading":"Draw the next three eclipses, not all 22","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the globe draws the next three eclipse paths and dims everything except what is open in the panel. Layers lets you show the next 8, or all 22 in the catalogue."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/layers.webp","alt":"The Layers menu with eight map layers, a choice of next 3, next 8 or all 22 eclipse tracks, a time window and a scan radius","caption":"Layers, opened from the keyboard: the yellow ring marks where focus is. Next 3 is the default. Captured offline.","width":536,"height":1166,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: twenty-two eclipse tracks drawn at once was the problem this rebuild set out to fix. The globe could not be read. Each track is now smoothed into a curve and drawn as the band of the shadow, not a thin line. The eclipse that matters to the chosen place stays on screen even when it is not one of the next three."},{"p":"The camera had the same kind of problem. Choosing a place fired one move straight away and a second a moment later, when the place’s outline arrived. The two raced, and if the map was still loading the move was lost. Now each choice makes exactly one camera move, which waits until the map exists."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Someone looking for an eclipse ten years out has to open Layers or the Sky tab. I accepted that in exchange for a globe you can read."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Plain files in the browser, and a globe that needed care","blocks":[{"p":"EarthLog has no framework, no build step and no accounts. Every source is a free public feed that needs no key, so the whole app is a folder of files."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: eight public sources, then the browser with source modules, local astronomy, bundled catalogues and app state, then the globe, readout panel and status line","caption":"What runs where. Everything in the middle column runs in your browser.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Globe and map","MapLibre GL JS 5 in globe mode, with CARTO’s dark map and Esri satellite imagery"],["Look","Open Props tokens plus my own colour tokens; Fraunces, Public Sans and IBM Plex Mono"],["Astronomy","My own code for the sun, moon, planets, twilight and the line between day and night"],["Orbits","My own Kepler + J2 model for the space station’s track and passes"],["Hosting","Netlify, serving the files as they are"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"5","label":"commits, 19 to 21 August 2026"},{"value":"16","label":"JavaScript files, about 5,300 lines"},{"value":"8","label":"kinds of public source, none needing a key"},{"value":"0","label":"automated tests so far"}]},{"p":"The first commit is already a rebuild. The earlier version is not in the history, so I cannot show a before and after."},{"p":"The hardest problems were in the globe, not the panel."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Colour from space","The dark map paints water lighter than land, so from orbit the Earth read as a flat grey ball. I flipped land and sea with zoom ramps, and hand back to the map’s own colours at street level."],["Atmosphere","The haze that gives the globe its edge painted a street map solid black. The library did not update it while zooming, so I set the values myself on every zoom step."],["Size","The globe’s width on screen is 256 pixels times 2 to the power of the zoom. I work the starting zoom out from that, so the sphere fills the space beside the panel."],["Phones","When the web fonts arrived they changed the header’s height, and the globe stayed sized for the first guess. It now re-fits once the fonts settle."],["A missing map","If the map style has not arrived after 12 seconds, a bare globe takes its place so the data layers still draw."]]}},{"aside":{"title":"What is missing.","detail":"There are no automated tests and no checks that run on each change. The astronomy and orbit maths have not been compared against published tables in code."}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Dark, but every word clears the AA contrast level","blocks":[{"p":"Dark designs often fade small text into the background. I set a floor instead. Contrast ratio measures how far apart two colours are in brightness, and body text needs at least 4.5 to 1 under WCAG AA, the common accessibility standard."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/contrast.webp","alt":"Bar chart of six text colours against the dark panel: 15.9, 9.9, 7.4, 5.6 and 4.6 to 1, all above the 4.5 line, and a 2.2 colour used only for rules","caption":"Each text colour against the panel. The faintest one used for words sits just above the line. The 2.2 colour is kept for dividing lines only.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"list":["Keyboard: / jumps to search, 1 to 4 switch tabs, Esc leaves a record, and the arrow keys move through tabs and search results. Two skip links lead past the globe.","The tabs and the search box follow the standard ARIA patterns. ARIA is a set of labels that tell a screen reader what a control is and what state it is in.","A live region reads out one short sentence when the view changes, rather than the whole panel.","Touch targets are 44 pixels on touch screens. The app follows the system settings for reduced motion and for higher contrast.","When a record opens, focus moves to its Back button, so a keyboard user starts at the top."]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/phone-home.webp","alt":"EarthLog on a phone with nothing chosen: search under the title, one row of chips and the readout as a sheet below","caption":"On a phone, the readout becomes a sheet under the globe.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/phone-place.webp","alt":"EarthLog on a phone with a point near Naples chosen, showing the place header and the weather unavailable message","caption":"The chips stay in one row that scrolls sideways. Captured offline.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"The AA claim is mine, from building to it. No outside audit or screen-reader session has been done."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no usage numbers, and a gap my offline screens exposed","blocks":[{"p":"EarthLog is live at earthlog.srinidhibhat.com. I have no visitor numbers or feedback to share."},{"p":"I took every screen on this page with no network, so the globe, the map tiles and the live feeds could not load. That turned out to be a useful test. Some failures are handled well. The weather card and the space station section both say plainly that their source could not be reached."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/fail-weather.webp","alt":"Weather tab saying the forecast service did not answer for this point and will retry","caption":"Weather, offline. It names the problem and says it will retry.","width":880,"height":748},{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/fail-iss.webp","alt":"A No answer message saying the element set for the space station is not reachable right now","caption":"The space station chip, offline. A clear “no answer”.","width":880,"height":588}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"Others are not. A failed quake request reads as a quiet week: “The ground here has been quiet.” The status line counts 0 quakes. The Log says “updated just now” over an empty list, and one chip shows a raw browser error."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/fail-log.webp","alt":"The Log showing 0 events on the board, updated just now, and a message that nothing matches the filter","caption":"The Log, offline. “Updated just now” over nothing reads as a calm planet.","width":880,"height":876},{"src":"assets/cases/earthlog/fail-quake.webp","alt":"A No answer message reading That lookup failed: Failed to fetch","caption":"The last big quake chip, offline. The browser’s own error leaks through.","width":880,"height":588}],"layout":"two"}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Tell a failed feed apart from a calm planet, then test with people","blocks":[{"list":["Give every feed its own failed state, so “no quakes” and “could not reach USGS” never look the same, in the panel, the status line or The Log.","Fix the moon icon. My captures showed a 4% crescent drawn as an almost full disc.","Add tests for the astronomy and orbit maths, checked against published tables.","A first test with people: ask them to explain one result in their own words. What happened, when was it seen, and where did the information come from? Finding an event is only half the task."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"An empty list has to know why it is empty","blocks":[{"p":"Most of the design work was not the globe. It was deciding how sure each answer is, and saying so in plain words right beside it."},{"p":"The offline screens showed me where I stopped short. I wrote careful notes for every model, then let a failed request tell people the ground had been quiet. Honesty about a model is not enough if the app is not also honest about its own connection."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","headline":"Learning chess on a different board."}},"hexchess":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","kind":"product","headline":"Hexagonal chess you learn by making the moves.","summary":"HexChess is a web app I designed and built on my own. It teaches Gliński’s hexagonal chess in six one-move lessons, then lets you play the computer, a friend on the same device, or a friend by link.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Learning & play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"91","label":"board cells"},{"value":"6","label":"guided piece lessons"},{"value":"Offline","label":"after first successful load"}],"problem":"A hexagonal board changes movement patterns enough to make familiar chess knowledge unreliable. I designed HexChess around learning by trying: clear targets, feedback on moves, and a route into untimed practice instead of expecting a beginner to absorb a rules document first.","process":["I connected a six-part introduction to the first practice game. A learner should encounter a rule on the board, try a move, and carry that understanding into play.","I treated interruption and sharing as parts of the experience. A saved game needs a clear return path, and a correspondence link needs to explain that the other person replies with an updated game rather than joins a live room."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make movement teachable on the board","choice":"Lessons give a specific target, neutral markers, retries, and progress based on actual practice.","reason":"A learner can connect a rule to a move instead of memorising abstract directions.","tradeoff":"Move previews and legal moves serve different purposes; the interface must explain when inspection ignores king safety."},{"title":"Keep assistance appropriate to the context","choice":"Hints and takebacks belong to computer practice; human games remove assistance and pause controls.","reason":"Learning support should not silently change the rules of a friendly competitive game.","tradeoff":"Assistance rules need to be clear to both players; the interface is not evidence of tournament-grade enforcement."},{"title":"Describe sharing accurately","choice":"Correspondence links carry move history and require an updated reply after a turn.","reason":"People should understand whether they are entering a synchronized room or receiving a game snapshot.","tradeoff":"Long histories create long URLs, and a shared snapshot is readable by anyone given the link."}],"outcome":"I connected the 91-cell board to six guided lessons, practice, saved sessions and correspondence sharing. Legal-move feedback helps explain the unfamiliar geometry where the move happens. I have not measured whether the lessons improve learning.","reflection":"I would follow a new player from a lesson into their first game. A completed tutorial is less useful evidence than seeing them recognise a move, recover from a mistake and continue without an explanation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a new player complete a piece lesson and use the same movement unaided in practice.","Proposed checks: reload during a game, resume offline, and exchange a correspondence reply while confirming both players understand the snapshot model."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Learning, board interactions, saves, offline support, sharing boundaries and existing test scope. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hexchess.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":15,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Learning chess [[on a different board.]]","scene":"Imagine recognising the pieces but no longer trusting your instinct for where they can move. A rules page can explain the geometry; a first move makes it tangible.","question":"How can the board become part of the teaching?","insight":"[[Practice makes a rule concrete]]. Six guided piece lessons lead into an untimed first game with move highlights.","pivot":"I brought explanation into the interaction: a target, an attempt, feedback, and a recoverable retry.","takeaway":"Teach the next useful action, then give someone room to try it.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Give the lesson one clear target."},{"title":"Try","detail":"Let the learner select and move."},{"title":"Recover","detail":"Explain a wrong attempt without ending the lesson."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Transfer the movement into beginner practice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the rule visible where it is used.","Keep practice help appropriate to the game.","Describe what a shared game really is."],"annotations":[{"title":"The board stays primary","detail":"The playing room keeps the central interaction prominent."},{"title":"Progress survives interruption","detail":"Local saves and offline support belong to the game experience."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · six guided lessons","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Chess player learning a different board","context":"Recognising familiar pieces in an unfamiliar hexagonal geometry.","need":"Understand a movement rule and try it safely.","friction":"Abstract directions do not automatically translate into a playable move.","implication":"Teach on the board, then open guided practice."}},"theme":{"layout":"lesson","mode":"light","paper":"#f6f6f3","ink":"#32363b","dim":"#62696e","line":"#d2d5d6","accent":"#46584c","wash":"#e1e7db","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Libre Caslon Display","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar piece, a new board","process":"Learning through a move","decisions":"From hint to understanding","outcome":"The first game","reflection":"What practice would reveal"},"decisionHeading":"Teach the board through the next move.","resultHeading":"From the first lesson into play."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","alt":"HexChess — archived product interface","caption":"The original playing room: a 91-cell board, legal-move guidance and game controls.","label":"Playing interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Understand the board","detail":"The playing surface carries the movement explanation."},{"title":"Try a move","detail":"Guidance stays in the context of play."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Practice and a saved game support a return to the board."}],"width":1265,"height":949},"v2":{"title":"Hexagonal chess you learn by [[making the moves]].","hook":"HexChess is a web app I designed and built on my own. It teaches Gliński’s hexagonal chess in six one-move lessons, then lets you play the computer, a friend on the same device, or a friend by link.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"October 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"People who already play chess and want to try the hexagonal version. This is my assumption, not research"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web app for phone and desktop. It installs to the home screen and plays offline after the first visit"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 19, TypeScript, Vite, a Web Worker and a service worker. Vitest for tests"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live and free. No accounts and no analytics, so no usage numbers. Not tested with learners"}],"links":[{"label":"Play HexChess","url":"https://hexchess.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: on a hexagonal board the pieces keep their names but not their moves. A rook has six directions, a knight has twelve jumps, and each side has three bishops.","What I built: a web app with a rules guide, six one-move lessons, a computer opponent at three levels, pass-and-play and games sent by link. It works offline.","The key idea: teach on the board. A lesson asks for one move to one outlined cell, and in a real game you can tap any enemy piece to see where it reaches.","Where it stands: live and free, with no accounts. I have not watched anyone learn with it, so I cannot say the lessons work."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/lead.webp","alt":"HexChess on a laptop mid-game, the knight lesson on a phone, and a game in progress on a phone, all on a grey hexagonal board","caption":"The playing room on a laptop, the knight lesson on a phone, and a game against the computer on a phone. All three use the default Classic board.","width":2400,"height":1200},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","nav":"The problem","heading":"On this board, chess instincts point the wrong way","blocks":[{"p":"Gliński’s hexagonal chess is played on 91 six-sided cells. It has the same six pieces as ordinary chess, and most of them move differently."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/compare.webp","alt":"Table comparing standard chess with Gliński’s hexagonal chess for the board, rook, bishop, knight, king and stalemate","caption":"Read the right column. Every row is a habit a chess player has to unlearn, down to how a stalemate is scored.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"A player who knows chess brings the wrong instincts. They reach for a diagonal that is not there, or miss a knight jump they have never seen. A rules page can describe the new directions. It cannot make them feel normal."},{"p":"Even the board needs explaining. Cells are named by a letter and a number, like squares, but the letters mark slanted columns, called files, and there are three shades instead of two."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/board-map.webp","alt":"Map of all 91 hexagonal cells with their names, in three shades of grey, with the centre cell f6 outlined and the file letters a to l along the bottom","caption":"Every cell and its name. Find f6 in the middle, then follow file f up to f11. The j is missing on purpose.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"aside":{"title":"Who it is for.","detail":"I designed for someone who already plays chess and wants to try this version. That is my assumption. I did no interviews or survey."}}]},{"id":"approach","label":"My approach","nav":"My approach","heading":"Learn by moving a piece, then play straight away","blocks":[{"p":"I laid out a route with four stops. Each is optional. The playing room opens first, with links to the lessons and the rules beside the board."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/journey.webp","alt":"Flow of four steps: read the basics, six lessons, an untimed first game, then a choice of opponent","caption":"The route. The lessons in the middle are the part I spent the most care on.","width":1600,"height":640}]},{"p":"The rules guide is for reading. It explains a first game in three steps, has a piece explorer, and keeps the finer rules, such as en passant, promotion and endings, in panels you open when you want them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/learn-knight.webp","alt":"The rules guide on the knight: a practice board with twelve dots around a knight, and a short explanation beside it","caption":"The piece explorer on the knight. The dots are all twelve of its jumps from the centre of an empty board.","width":1600,"height":930}]},{"p":"The lessons are for doing. The next four sections are the decisions that make that work, each with what it meant in the build."}]},{"id":"lessons","label":"Decision 1","nav":"One move per lesson","heading":"Each lesson asks for one move to one outlined cell","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: six lessons, one per piece. Each puts a single white piece on an empty board, outlines one target, and asks you to select the piece and then move it there."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/annotated-lesson.webp","alt":"The rook lesson with six numbered notes on the lesson tabs, the outlined target, the move dots, the two-step checklist, the feedback line and the locked Next lesson button","caption":"The rook lesson just after a real rook move to the wrong cell. Look at note 5: the reply corrects me, and the lesson carries on.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The dots matter as much as the target. They show the whole shape of a piece’s reach, so a learner sees six rook lines or twelve knight jumps at once."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/board-rook.webp","alt":"Lesson board with a rook in the centre and dots along six straight lines","caption":"Rook: six straight lines through cell edges.","width":900,"height":954},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/board-bishop.webp","alt":"Lesson board with a bishop in the centre and dots along six diagonals","caption":"Bishop: six diagonals through cell corners. Every dot is on the same shade as the bishop.","width":900,"height":954},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/board-knight.webp","alt":"Lesson board with a knight in the centre and twelve dots around it","caption":"Knight: twelve jumps. Pieces in between would not block it.","width":900,"height":954}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"In the build: each lesson runs on its own small position, apart from any game. Every tap goes through one function that returns the next message. I wrote a reply for each kind of mistake, so a wrong tap explains itself instead of ending the lesson."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/feedback.webp","alt":"Five rows pairing an action in the rook lesson with the exact message the app shows for it","caption":"Every reply in lesson 1. The two orange rows are the mistakes. Neither one resets the board.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"A lesson is ticked only when you make its move. Skipping does not count. Tests check every lesson’s target, selecting before moving, other legal moves, retries and the pawn capture."},{"p":"One of the six commits went only to this: clearer lesson targets and simpler beginner rules."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A lesson board shows how a piece moves, not whether the move is safe. King safety waits for the king lesson and the rules guide, so the first lessons stay about shape."}}]},{"id":"preview","label":"Decision 2","nav":"See the enemy’s reach","heading":"Tap an enemy piece to see where it can go","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: in a game, tapping one of your pieces shows filled dots where it can legally move. Tapping one of the opponent’s pieces shows hollow rings where it could go next. Nothing is moved or queued."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/annotated-preview.webp","alt":"A game board with a black bishop selected for preview, hollow rings on the cells it reaches, a dashed ring on a white pawn, and the white king’s cell in red, with six numbered notes","caption":"The computer’s bishop has just given check. Tapping it shows why: note 4 is the king it attacks.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"p":"On an unfamiliar board, the threat is the hard thing to see. This lets a player check a piece’s reach before moving, without leaving the game for the rules."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/select-queen.webp","alt":"The same game with the white queen selected, filled dots on its legal moves and a ring around a black pawn it can capture","caption":"My own queen selected, for comparison. Filled dots are its legal moves, and the ringed pawn is one it can take.","width":1600,"height":1711,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: the preview follows each piece’s movement rules and respects pieces in the way, but it does not test king safety. The status line says so every time."},{"p":"Two smaller rules are covered by tests. If a piece of yours is selected and you tap an enemy piece it can capture, the capture wins over the preview. And en passant shows only for the side whose turn it is."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A ring can sit on a cell the piece could not really reach, because moving there would expose its own king. That is why the preview is labelled as a preview."}}]},{"id":"help","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Help only in practice","heading":"Hints and takebacks belong to practice against the computer","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: against the computer you get Hint, Undo and Pause. In a game with a person, on one device or by link, all three are gone. Flipping the board, drawing arrows, offering a draw and resigning stay."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/hint-board.webp","alt":"A game against the computer with a pawn highlighted by a hint and a message at the bottom reading Try pawn f5 to g5","caption":"A hint against the computer. It selects the piece, marks the cell, and names the move at the bottom.","width":1600,"height":1980,"kind":"tall"},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/new-game.webp","alt":"The new game dialog with Computer, Pass and play and Play by link, a choice of side, a computer level menu and six time options","caption":"Starting a game: who to play, which side, the computer’s level and the clock.","width":960,"height":1608,"kind":"tall"}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build, hints, takebacks and pausing each have a one-line rule: computer games only. Tests check that a human game cannot use them. Every move, from you, the computer, a premove or a link, passes the same legality check."},{"p":"The computer searches for its move in a Web Worker, a background thread, so the board never freezes while it thinks. Its three levels differ in how far ahead it looks and how long it may think: about a fifth of a second, two thirds of a second, or 1.8 seconds. The beginner level picks at random from up to five moves close to its best."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not claim.","detail":"The levels are not ratings. I have not measured their strength against players or other programs, and the app shows no Elo number."}}]},{"id":"links","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Games by link","heading":"A game sent by link is a snapshot, and the app says so","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Play by link needs no account. You make a move and send a link. Your friend opens it, plays one move and sends a new link back. There is no live room and no clock."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/link-flow.webp","alt":"Flow of a link game in five steps, with a list of links the app turns away and a list of what a link cannot do","caption":"One round of a link game. The red box is what the app refuses. The white box is what it cannot promise.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/share-dialog.webp","alt":"The share dialog with three numbered steps, a note about snapshots, the game link with a copy button, and a warning about identity and engine help","caption":"The share dialog. The warning at the bottom is part of the design. The address is from the local copy I captured it on.","width":960,"height":1392,"kind":"tall"},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/link-recipient.webp","alt":"The game opened from the link as Black, with the board turned so Black is at the bottom and the side panel showing Black to move","caption":"The same game opened as Black. The board turns to Black’s side, and the cell names stay the same.","width":1600,"height":1237}],"layout":"two"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/annotated-link.webp","alt":"A link game while waiting for Black, with a premove in blue, an orange arrow, the status line and the side panel, with six numbered notes","caption":"Back on my side while I wait. Note 1 is a premove: it is checked again when the reply arrives, and played only if still legal.","width":1600,"height":1090}]},{"p":"In the build: a link holds the game’s moves, not its board. Opening one replays every move through the rules. If this device already knows the game, the reply must keep the saved moves and add at most one. Anything else is turned away with the message “This link is invalid or conflicts with your saved game. Your board is unchanged.”"},{"p":"A premove lives only on the device and never goes into a link. When the reply arrives it is checked against the new position, including pins, captures and promotion, before it is played."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"With no server there is no identity. Anyone with the link can read the game, and nothing stops a player using a chess engine on the side. The app calls link games friendly play and does not claim more."}}]},{"id":"screens","label":"On every screen","nav":"Phone and desktop","heading":"On a phone, the board takes the width","blocks":[{"p":"Starting a game turns on zen mode, which hides everything except the players, the board and the controls. Z or Esc brings the full room back, and the app remembers that choice."},{"p":"On a phone the board runs almost edge to edge. Move history and the game menu open only when asked for. Controls are at least 44 pixels tall."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/phone-room.webp","alt":"A phone screen in zen mode with two player cards, the board across the full width, a status line and a row of controls","caption":"A game on a phone. Players, board, status, one row of controls.","width":900,"height":1948},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/phone-history.webp","alt":"The move history panel open over the board on a phone, listing the first three turns","caption":"Move history opens over the board. Picking a move shows that position, and the clock keeps running.","width":900,"height":1948},{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/phone-menu.webp","alt":"The game menu on a phone with eight buttons: new game, rules, tutorial, preferences, share, pause, draw and resign","caption":"The game menu holds the rules, the lessons, preferences, sharing and game actions.","width":900,"height":1948}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"The fifth commit was for phones: a larger board, and a piece that follows your finger while you drag it."},{"p":"The board comes in four palettes and a dark mode. The second commit added Classic, a soft grey board, and it is now the default. Garden, Slate and Walnut remain."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/palettes.webp","alt":"The starting position shown five times: Classic grey, Garden green, Slate blue, Walnut brown, and Classic in dark mode","caption":"The same opening position in all four palettes, and Classic in dark mode. The three shades stay distinct in each.","width":2000,"height":530}]}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"No server, so the rules have to hold up on their own","blocks":[{"p":"HexChess is a set of static files. The rules, the computer, saved games and links all run in the browser."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: the React interface and one legal-move gate; the rules engine, session and computer opponent; and local storage, game links and the service worker","caption":"What runs where. Start at the legal-move gate: everything that changes a game passes through it.","width":1600,"height":960}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","React 19, TypeScript, Vite"],["Board","SVG, drawn from each cell’s two hex coordinates"],["Computer","A search that looks a few moves ahead, in a Web Worker"],["Offline","A service worker that saves the app on the first visit"],["Checks","Vitest, run by GitHub Actions on every push and before every deploy"],["Hosting","Vercel, as static files"]]}},{"p":"The board is built from coordinates, not drawn by hand. Each cell has two numbers, and its name, its shade and every move direction come from them. The board map near the top of this page was drawn from the same formula."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/link-anatomy.webp","alt":"A game link and, below it, the decoded record: version, game id, sender, settings, the list of moves, draw offer and result, with notes on four fields","caption":"What a link carries after one move. Only the moves travel, so the receiving app can check every one of them.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"stats":[{"value":"6","label":"commits, all on 9 October 2026"},{"value":"10","label":"test files, run before every deploy"},{"value":"90,000","label":"characters, the longest link the app accepts"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/commits.webp","alt":"Timeline of six commits on one day, from the first full version to the recorded live deployment","caption":"The six commits. After the first working version, four passes refined the board, the lessons, the notation and the phone layout.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The hardest parts were not the board."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What made it hard, and what I did"],"rows":[["Rules","Hexagonal geometry has many edge cases: pins, en passant, discovered check, promotion on 11 edge cells and the ¾ stalemate score. Tests cover each, plus the starting armies and the bishops’ shades."],["Links","A link can be old, edited or your own. Replies must keep the saved moves and add at most one, and a draw needs an offer and an acceptance. Tests try conflicting links, made-up results and impossible clock values."],["Premoves","A queued move can turn illegal after the reply. It is checked again against the new position. Tests cover captures, blocked cells, stale queues, promotion and link replies."],["Offline and clocks","A game has to survive reloads and updates. A new version waits until open tabs close, and clocks count real time through background tabs. Tests cover offline loading, cache updates and damaged saves."]]}},{"p":"Beyond the tests, I checked the desktop and phone layouts, finishing the lessons, computer replies, link round trips, reloads and offline play in a browser."}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"The board can be played from the keyboard","blocks":[{"p":"Press Tab to reach the board, the arrow keys to move between cells, and Enter or Space to select and move. Each cell has a name a screen reader can read out, such as “d1, white Knight, selected”."},{"p":"Drawing arrows, normally a right-drag, also has a Draw tool for touch and keyboard. A reduced-motion setting on the device turns off animation. The move list writes out each piece and both cells instead of chess shorthand, and has a short guide to reading it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hexchess/move-list.webp","alt":"The side panel of a game against the computer: both clocks, then a move list where each turn shows a piece name and the from and to cells","caption":"The side panel. Each move names the piece and both cells in full, so no notation has to be learned first.","width":600,"height":1290,"kind":"tall"}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I have not done.","detail":"I have no record of testing with a screen-reader user or of a formal accessibility audit, so I do not claim either."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no way of knowing who plays it","blocks":[{"p":"HexChess is live at hexchess.srinidhibhat.com. The deployment was checked on 9 October 2026. It needs no account and has no analytics, so I have no usage numbers."},{"list":["No one has been observed learning with it, so there is no evidence that the lessons improve learning.","The computer levels have no measured rating.","Link games have no sign-in, so a player cannot prove who they are.","The rules guide’s video and outside links need a connection. Everything else works offline."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Watch a new player go from a lesson to a game","blocks":[{"list":["Watch someone new to the variant finish a piece lesson, then see whether they use the same move unaided in a practice game.","Check the moments most likely to confuse: reloading mid-game, picking up offline, and two people trading replies while saying what they think a link is."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A finished lesson is weak evidence","blocks":[{"p":"A ticked lesson tells me someone made one move. It does not tell me they will spot that move in a real game. The evidence I need is a new player recognising a move, recovering from a mistake and carrying on without an explanation."},{"p":"The other thing I would keep is putting limits where they apply. The preview says it ignores king safety, the share dialog says a link cannot prove who sent it, and the levels make no claim about strength."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","headline":"Follow the driver. Keep the race in view."}},"dashf1":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","kind":"product","headline":"Follow a Formula 1 race on one screen, and know which numbers are estimates.","summary":"DashF1 is a free web dashboard I designed and built on my own. It puts the timing, the track map, the car's data and the race messages of a Formula 1 session on one screen, as a replay or live.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Live data · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"driver focus across panels"},{"value":"Live / replay","label":"distinct data modes"},{"value":"Estimated","label":"free-feed track positions"}],"problem":"A race weekend produces more signals than a person can follow at once, and the available feed changes between live and replay sessions. DashF1 uses one shared driver focus across the timing tower, map, and detail views so the dashboard supports a question rather than a wall of independent charts.","process":["I organised the experience around one driver focus. Timing, track context, and detail panels should answer related questions without requiring the same selection repeatedly.","I separated live and replay expectations. When a position is estimated, the label belongs near the moving marker; missing coverage should be understandable at the moment someone tries to use it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Carry one selection through the dashboard","choice":"Choosing a driver updates the relevant map and telemetry context.","reason":"The person should not have to repeat the same selection in several panels.","tradeoff":"The relationship must remain clear when the selected driver retires or a particular data channel is unavailable."},{"title":"Let the mode determine the promise","choice":"Live mode shows supported channels; fuller replay data becomes available through a separate path.","reason":"Missing data should change the interface’s promise instead of being replaced with plausible-looking numbers.","tradeoff":"A mode change can alter familiar panels, so the dashboard needs an understandable explanation."},{"title":"Label an estimate at the point of use","choice":"The documented free-feed map derives position from mini-sector timing and gaps, and calls it estimated.","reason":"Visual continuity is useful, but it should not be mistaken for location precision the feed does not provide.","tradeoff":"Race interruptions and stale timing can challenge the estimate; smooth motion must not conceal those limits."}],"outcome":"I organised timing, track position, telemetry and replay around one selected driver. The selection carries across panels, while session mode and data coverage explain what each view can show. Estimated positions need to stay visibly different from recorded data.","reflection":"I would check what someone believes when a feed is incomplete. An empty or estimated view should be understandable without making them leave the race to read a technical explanation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a viewer to follow a battle and explain which values are observed, historical, or estimated.","Proposed checks: missing telemetry, stale updates, interrupted streams, safety-car periods, and the transition back to replay."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s on screen and Race mode","detail":"Layout, shared focus, data-source boundaries and position-estimation limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Local built F1DASH, captured 31 October 2026; historical replay, not a live session."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","alt":"DashF1 actual Monaco 2025 historical replay with timing, track, telemetry and playback controls","caption":"Actual local dashboard replaying the 2025 Monaco race from OpenF1 data. The prediction panel labels its heuristic estimates.","width":1280,"height":720}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://f1dash.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":18,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Follow the driver. [[Keep the race in view.]]","scene":"Imagine following two drivers through a race weekend. Timing, position, tyres, and telemetry compete for attention—and not every channel is available in every session.","question":"What should the dashboard do when its data coverage changes?","insight":"A smooth marker can still be an estimate. [[The mode should determine the promise]] the interface makes.","pivot":"I used one driver focus across the dashboard and separate live/replay modes that respect the available information.","takeaway":"A useful dashboard can say less when it knows less.","journey":[{"title":"Focus","detail":"Choose the driver once."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Carry that focus across panels."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Distinguish measured channels from estimated positions."}],"decisionLeads":["Carry one question across the dashboard.","Let the available data set the scope.","Make a moving estimate visibly an estimate."],"annotations":[{"title":"Shared focus","detail":"A selected driver carries across timing, track position and available detail."},{"title":"Coverage has boundaries","detail":"The product distinguishes live estimates from richer replay data."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · live and replay modes","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Race follower tracking a driver","context":"Moving between timing, track position, and available detail.","need":"Keep one driver in context while following the race.","friction":"Repeated selections and uneven data coverage disrupt the story.","implication":"Synchronise focus and explain each mode’s coverage."}},"theme":{"layout":"race","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b0e14","ink":"#eef1f6","dim":"#b3bccb","line":"#394454","accent":"#ff6a61","wash":"#3a1e24","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A driver to follow","process":"One focus across panels","decisions":"The dashboard contract","outcome":"The race in context","reflection":"Knowing what is estimated"},"decisionHeading":"Keep one driver in focus across the dashboard.","resultHeading":"Follow the driver through the available data."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","presentation":"spread","alt":"DashF1 actual Monaco 2025 historical replay with timing, track, telemetry and playback controls","caption":"Actual local dashboard replaying the 2025 Monaco race from OpenF1 data. The prediction panel labels its heuristic estimates.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Local built F1DASH, captured 31 October 2026; historical replay, not a live session.","width":1280,"height":720},"v2":{"title":"Follow a Formula 1 race on one screen, and [[know which numbers are estimates]].","hook":"DashF1 is a free web dashboard I designed and built on my own. It puts the timing, the track map, the car's data and the race messages of a Formula 1 session on one screen, as a replay or live.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July 2026"},{"label":"Why","value":"To follow a race weekend without the broadcast, with no login and no cost"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, a desktop dashboard. A Mac launcher runs it locally"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 18, TypeScript, Vite, Canvas and SVG, a Netlify Function"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live and free. No usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Open DashF1","url":"https://f1dash.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: Formula 1 race data is free, but it is split across three sources. One is locked during live sessions, one has no car positions, and one only has results.","What I built: one screen with a timing tower, a track map, car telemetry, nine tabs of race detail and a replay bar, fed from whichever source works at that moment.","The key idea: during a live race the free feed has no GPS, so the map places cars from timing lines and says so. Anything the feed does not carry is left out, not guessed.","Where it stands: live on the web and free. I have no usage numbers, and the latest work on race interruptions is not yet merged."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/screen-dashboard.webp","alt":"The DashF1 dashboard replaying the 2025 Monaco Grand Prix: timing tower on the left, track map and prediction tab in the middle, car telemetry and race control on the right, playback bar along the bottom","caption":"The whole dashboard, replaying the 2025 Monaco Grand Prix three and a half minutes in. The field is still bunched after the start. Driver photo and name blurred.","width":1280,"height":720},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"The race data is free, but no single source has all of it","blocks":[{"p":"DashF1 is for following a Formula 1 race weekend without watching the broadcast. It needs no login and no account, and it costs nothing."},{"p":"The data to do that is public, but it lives in three places. OpenF1, a public data service, has almost everything, including GPS positions and car telemetry. Telemetry is the stream of speed, gear, throttle and brake readings from each car. But OpenF1 allows a free user about 3 requests a second, and it locks free users out while a session is live."},{"p":"The official F1 live-timing feed is open during the session, but it has no GPS and no telemetry, and a web page cannot connect to it on its own. Jolpica, a results archive, adds championship standings."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram of three free data sources, OpenF1, the official F1 live-timing feed and Jolpica, each with what it has and its gap, all feeding into DashF1","caption":"Read the red lines. Each source has a different hole, so the dashboard has to know which one to use at any moment.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did no interviews or user testing for this project, and I have no usage data. This page does not quote any."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Timing on the left, the track in the middle, one car on the right","blocks":[{"p":"It opens on the most recent session that has started. From the top bar you can pick any season, Grand Prix and session. In replay, the data streams in around the point you are watching, and you can play it at up to 60 times normal speed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/annotated-dashboard.webp","alt":"The dashboard with seven numbered notes on the top bar, timing tower, track map, bottom tabs, telemetry, race control and playback bar","caption":"The seven parts. The tower and the map take the most room.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The timing tower is the running order. The panel on the right shows one car in detail. With nobody picked, that car is the leader."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/annotated-panels.webp","alt":"The timing tower and the car telemetry panel side by side, with seven numbered notes on tyres, gaps, lap times, the leader label, the speed trace, throttle and brake, and the large readouts","caption":"Note 4 is easy to miss: the panel says out loud that it is showing the leader because no one is picked.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/screen-map.webp","alt":"The track map of Monaco with the cars bunched together in team colours after the start, and sector markers on the circuit","caption":"The map. The circuit shape is rebuilt from GPS data and kept in the browser, one copy per circuit.","width":528,"height":344},{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/screen-race-control.webp","alt":"The race control panel showing session started, green light with pit exit open, and DRS disabled messages, with a team radio tab beside it","caption":"Race control and team radio. Each message appears when the replay reaches it.","width":324,"height":174}],"layout":"wide-narrow"}]},{"id":"focus","label":"Decision 1","nav":"One click to follow a driver","heading":"One click picks the driver for every panel","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: click a row in the timing tower, or a car on the map, and the whole dashboard turns to that driver. Click again to let go."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/focus.webp","alt":"Diagram of one click in the timing tower changing six panels: the track map, car telemetry, lap times, positions, all cars and the tower itself","caption":"What one click changes. Each panel shows the choice in its own way, from a bigger dot to faded lines.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"In the build: the chosen driver is held once, in one shared store, and every panel reads it from there. No panel keeps its own copy, so they cannot disagree about who you are following."},{"p":"The Lap Times chart shows the top six by default and adds your driver to them. Chips above the chart let you switch other drivers on and off by hand."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"With nobody picked, the telemetry panel follows whoever leads, so it jumps to a new car when the lead changes. Picking a driver is the way to hold it still."}}]},{"id":"modes","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Leave out what is missing","heading":"During a live race, the dashboard leaves out what the free feed does not carry","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: in replay every panel is full. During a live session the dashboard switches to race mode. That mode shows what the free live feed carries, and leaves the rest out instead of showing dashes or made-up numbers."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/two-modes.webp","alt":"Table comparing replay and race mode across track map, timing, race control, history, telemetry, DRS and the bottom bar","caption":"Look at the two red rows. Car telemetry and DRS, the opening rear wing, are not in the free live feed, so race mode does not show them.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"In the build: this was a reversal. On 22 July my live mode filled every panel. It worked out speed, gear, rpm, throttle and brake for every car from a profile of the circuit and the driver's pace, and tagged each value \"(est)\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/reversal.webp","alt":"Before and after cards: on 22 July live mode estimated every telemetry value with a small tag, on 26 July race mode left those panels out and kept one labelled estimate","caption":"Both versions are in the project history. The later one shows less, and more of what it shows is measured.","width":1600,"height":680}]},{"p":"Four days later I took it out. Race mode now shows only what the feed carries, instead of putting estimates in place of what it lacks. One thing is still estimated, the car's place on the track, and it is labelled where you read it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/race-mode.webp","alt":"Schematic of three race-mode elements: the bottom bar with a live badge and a note on what is estimated, a driver card marked estimated with speed traps marked real measurements, and the map caption","caption":"Where race mode says \"estimated\": in the bottom bar, on the driver card and under the map. A schematic redrawn from the code, because I have no capture of a live session yet.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/screen-connecting.webp","alt":"A dark card reading Connecting to live timing, explaining that the dashboard is switching to the official F1 live-timing feed and what race mode will and will not show","caption":"The card shown while race mode connects. It says what you will get before you get it. I captured it from the built app with no network, so here it waits for a feed that never arrives.","width":448,"height":340}]},{"p":"If someone has an OpenF1 key, they can type it in. It stays in their browser, and a live session then gets the full replay data."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Race mode looks emptier than replay, and familiar panels change when a session goes live. The connecting card and the bottom bar have to explain why."}}]},{"id":"estimate","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Placing cars without GPS","heading":"Cars are placed from timing lines, and the map says so","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: during a live race every car still moves around the map. A caption under the circuit says the positions are estimated from mini-sector timing, and hovering a car tells you what its position was worked out from."},{"p":"A mini-sector is one of about 20 short timing stretches in a lap. The free feed reports each car crossing each of those lines, roughly every 4 seconds, along with sector times, lap times and time gaps."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/estimation.webp","alt":"Diagram of a lap drawn as a straight strip with about 20 timing lines; a car's estimate sits after the last line it crossed and just short of the next, with the drawn dot moving forward toward it, and four steps below","caption":"The method in four steps. An illustration, not real race data.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"In the build: the circuit outline comes from GPS recorded earlier that weekend. Its points are spaced by time, not distance. So a car that moves along it at a steady rate is slow in corners and quick on straights without any extra work."},{"p":"The model first spreads the timing lines evenly between the sector boundaries. That is only a starting guess. Each crossing shows where a line really is, so the model averages the crossings over the whole field. In testing, the real lines sat up to about 5 seconds of lap time away from that first layout."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/accuracy.webp","alt":"Chart of estimate error measured in mini-sectors: typical error up to half a mini-sector, worst case about 1.2, and 83% of estimates inside the exact mini-sector","caption":"How close it gets, by my own measurement on a recorded live race feed. Good enough to see who is in a train and where a battle is. Not GPS.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These numbers come from my own replay of a recorded feed, checked against the feed's own timing. No one else has tested them, and they are not a GPS comparison."}}]},{"id":"predict","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Guesses name their method","heading":"Every prediction says it is a rule of thumb","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the Predict tab gives a safety-car risk, cars that are catching the one ahead, and battles where a pit stop could swap places. Each one names its method next to its title."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/annotated-predict.webp","alt":"The Predict tab with five numbered notes on the heuristic label, the 34% safety car risk, the reason chip, catch-ups and the undercut watch","caption":"Note 2 is the whole model for this number. Anyone can add it up.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"In the build: the safety-car risk starts at 12% for a quiet race. Points are added for a yellow flag, recent incident messages, the first two laps and a wet track, and the total stays between 5% and 95%. The Weather tab works the same way and labels its rain outlook \"extrapolated, not a forecast\"."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"None of these models is fitted to past races, so each is a rough signal. In return, every number can be traced to a reason printed on screen."}}]},{"id":"conditions","label":"Race conditions","nav":"Race conditions","heading":"The map was built for a green-flag race, so I taught it the rest","blocks":[{"p":"The first version of the position model assumed nothing interrupts the race. Every interruption either put cars in the wrong place or stopped them moving. I went through them one at a time."},{"table":{"head":["Situation","What the model now does"],"rows":[["Safety car or virtual safety car","Pace drops by more than half at once. Every car's pace is rescaled the moment it is called, not a lap later, and learning the timing lines pauses."],["Red flag, before the start, after the flag","The model's clock stops, so nothing drifts round an empty track."],["Pit stop","The car is walked down the pit lane over the time cars are seen to spend there."],["Car stopped on track","It stays on the map, frozen and ringed, until marshals have had time to clear it. A car that retires without stopping on track is removed."],["Change of leader","The gap-based backup is re-anchored to the new leader, so it keeps working."],["Blue flags and penalties","Read from race control and shown next to the driver. Checked against the race control feed of the 2026 Hungarian Grand Prix."]]}},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/pit-lane.webp","alt":"Before and after illustration on an oval track: before, three pitting cars frozen on the circuit; after, the same cars moving along the pit lane toward the exit","caption":"The pit lane fix. Before, a pitting car froze at the last line it crossed. An illustration of the change, not a capture.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"A check script drives the model through these situations on a simple test circuit. It has 16 checks: the dots never jump, never go backwards and stay close to the true position, plus checks for the safety car, red flag, lead change, pit lane and stopped cars."}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Replays run in the browser. Live timing needs a small relay.","blocks":[{"p":"Replays talk to the public data services straight from the browser. The live feed cannot be opened from a web page, so a small server opens it and passes it on."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: free sources OpenF1, Jolpica and the F1 live-timing feed; a request queue and a server relay in between; the store, lap model and drawing in the browser","caption":"What runs where. The highlighted parts are the two pieces the live race depends on.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Interface","React 18, TypeScript, Vite"],["Track map and speed trace","Canvas, drawn by hand"],["Charts","SVG, with no chart library"],["Replay data","OpenF1 and Jolpica, called from the browser"],["Live data","The F1 live-timing feed, through a relay that runs as a Netlify Function or in the local dev server"],["Checks","One script for the position model. No test framework and no automated builds"]]}},{"p":"OpenF1 allows about 3 requests a second, so every request waits in one queue and goes out at least 380 milliseconds after the last. Data is fetched in 30-second slices around the playhead, so a full race never downloads at once."},{"stats":[{"value":"8","label":"commits, 19 to 26 July 2026"},{"value":"9","label":"tabs of race detail"},{"value":"16","label":"checks on the position model"},{"value":"380 ms","label":"minimum gap between data requests"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/dashf1/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of eight commits over five days between 19 and 26 July 2026, with what each day added","caption":"The build day by day. 24 and 26 July are fixes for problems that only showed up in live races.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"The hardest problems all came from live sessions."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Opening the live feed","On 22 July a direct connection from the browser had worked. It did not hold. The feed's load balancer ties each connection to a cookie that a web page cannot send. I moved the connection to a server relay."],["Locked out during a session","While a session is live, OpenF1 refuses free requests, so the race menus came up empty and you could not switch races. The menus now fall back to Jolpica's calendar."],["The wrong circuit","An outline left over from replaying another Grand Prix stayed in place when live mode began, so every car was drawn on the wrong track. A loose name match could also find any race through the words \"grand\" and \"prix\". Outlines are now tagged with their circuit, and the lookup matches circuit and location exactly."],["Jumpy dots","Every input arrives in steps, so dots jumped by up to half a mini-sector whenever a gap refreshed. They are now steered toward the estimate, always forward, closing the error in about a second."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live and free, with the newest work still on a branch","blocks":[{"p":"DashF1 is live on the web, free and with no login. Replay works for any past session that OpenF1 has."},{"p":"I have no user counts or feedback to report. These are also not there yet:"},{"list":["The safety car, red flag and pit lane work sits on its own branch and is not yet merged into the main version.","On Netlify, a function can only run for a limited time, so the live relay ends and reconnects and timing arrives in bursts. Run locally, the feed does not break.","I have no screen capture of race mode, because it only appears during a real session.","The driver photos and race data come from Formula 1 and the public data services, not from me."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Merge the race work, then test it with people","blocks":[{"list":["Merge the race-condition branch and capture race mode during a real session.","Proposed study: ask someone to follow a battle and say which values are measured, which are history and which are estimated.","Proposed checks: missing telemetry, stale updates, a broken stream, safety-car periods, and the switch back to replay."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A dashboard should say less when it knows less","blocks":[{"p":"The version that filled every panel looked the most finished, but none of its telemetry was measured. Taking those numbers away made the screen more honest, and the one estimate I kept got a label in three places."},{"p":"I also learned to let the data correct the starting assumptions. The first layout of the timing lines was up to 5 seconds of lap time out, and only the crossings of the whole field showed it."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","headline":"One colour. A reason to look closer."}},"hued":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","kind":"product","headline":"One colour a day, found in the real world and scored on your phone.","summary":"Hued is a phone app I designed and built on my own. Everyone gets the same colour each day, goes out to find it, and the phone scores how well the photo caught it.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Mobile beta","context":"Creative habits · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"shared daily colour"},{"value":"48","label":"colours in the documented palette"},{"value":"On-device","label":"colour scoring"}],"problem":"A broad prompt to be creative can be harder to act on than a small constraint. Hued gives everyone the same daily colour and asks them to find and photograph it in the real world. The product connects a simple creative action to a repeatable daily rhythm.","process":["I shaped the concept around one shared colour and a small creative invitation: notice it in the world, photograph it, and see other interpretations. The daily boundary needs a clear local explanation.","A quiet, warm interface keeps the photographs in front. Scores and streaks are supporting feedback; whether they encourage curiosity or pressure is a question for evaluation, not an established habit-formation result."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the prompt shared and specific","choice":"One date-derived colour creates a common constraint for the day.","reason":"A small, concrete invitation can make starting easier and make different responses interesting to compare.","tradeoff":"A UTC boundary may feel surprising across time zones; the deadline needs to be understandable locally."},{"title":"Keep the creative result in the foreground","choice":"The feed emphasises photographs within a quiet, warm visual system.","reason":"The person’s interpretation of the colour should carry more attention than surrounding interface decoration.","tradeoff":"Ranking and streaks can compete with exploration; their value needs to be judged from behaviour and feedback."},{"title":"Treat scoring as guidance with limits","choice":"Colour matching runs locally; suspicious-image heuristics are documented as tentative rather than proof.","reason":"A score can provide feedback without pretending to reliably determine how an image was made.","tradeoff":"Lighting, camera processing, and scene composition affect colour. An unexplained penalty can feel arbitrary."}],"outcome":"I centred the app on a shared daily colour, a photograph and feedback on the attempt. The warm interface gives the photographs most of the attention; scores, the feed and streaks support the daily activity. Participation and the scoring experience still need user evaluation.","reflection":"I want the prompt to encourage noticing things, not just protecting a streak. I would ask people what they chose to photograph and how they interpreted the score before deciding whether to make the competitive features more prominent.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare how people interpret the prompt and score under different lighting conditions, including whether they understand a low score.","Proposed measure: repeat participation with self-reported creative value; evaluate deadline understanding across time zones."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/dailyColor.ts","detail":"Daily prompt, palette and beta scope."},{"label":"src/lib/scoring.ts and README limitations","detail":"On-device colour evaluation and best-effort heuristics. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Hued release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","alt":"Hued native Android sign-in screen with email, password and sign-up controls","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. The daily photography flow is described from the project documentation; no account or photographs were used for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hued.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":21,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"One colour. [[A reason to look closer.]]","scene":"Imagine being asked to make something creative today. A blank prompt is wide open; one findable colour gives your attention somewhere specific to go.","question":"How much structure helps creativity begin?","insight":"[[A small shared constraint]] gives the day a common starting point without prescribing the photograph.","pivot":"I connected the daily colour to finding, photographing, and receiving feedback, while keeping the photographs central.","takeaway":"A good prompt opens possibilities by making the first step small.","journey":[{"title":"Notice","detail":"Find the shared colour in the world."},{"title":"Capture","detail":"Make your own interpretation."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Use the feedback as guidance, with its limits visible."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the invitation specific.","Keep the creative result in the foreground.","Let a score explain rather than overrule."],"annotations":[{"title":"One daily invitation","detail":"The product identity anchors a shared colour challenge."},{"title":"Interpretation stays personal","detail":"Local scoring is feedback, not a verdict on creativity."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · daily prompt and scoring","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person looking for a small creative prompt","context":"Noticing everyday surroundings through one daily colour.","need":"Find a subject and share a personal interpretation.","friction":"Scores and streaks can turn exploration into pressure.","implication":"Let the photograph lead and keep feedback supportive."}},"theme":{"layout":"photojournal","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf6","ink":"#2f2927","dim":"#75615b","line":"#e8d5ca","accent":"#ad4b35","wash":"#f8e2d7","bodyFont":"Montserrat","displayFont":"Montserrat","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A colour in the everyday","process":"A constraint with room for you","decisions":"Designing the daily invitation","outcome":"Notice, photograph, reflect","reflection":"Creativity before streaks"},"decisionHeading":"Make the daily prompt easy to act on.","resultHeading":"A little colour, interpreted differently."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"Hued native Android sign-in screen with email, password and sign-up controls","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. The daily photography flow is described from the project documentation; no account or photographs were used for this capture.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Hued release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026.","width":1080,"height":1920},"v2":{"title":"One colour a day, found in the real world and [[scored on your phone]].","hook":"Hued is a phone app I designed and built on my own. Everyone gets the same colour each day, goes out to find it, and the phone scores how well the photo caught it.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself: a small daily reason to look closely, free to run"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"25 June to 12 July 2026, 35 commits"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android app, with a separate web admin panel"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Expo, React Native, TypeScript, Supabase"},{"label":"Status","value":"Beta, shared as an Android download. No usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Visit the Hued site","url":"https://hued.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"See it for yourself","summary":["The problem: I wanted a small daily reason to look closely at the world. It had to be the same for everyone and cost almost nothing to run.","What I built: an Android app where the daily colour and each photo's score are worked out on the phone. A free-plan server keeps posts, friends, groups and standings.","The key idea: be generous about colour and strict about cheating. I made the colour match more forgiving and left the cheat checks alone.","Where it stands: a beta, built in 18 days. There is no user research behind it and I have no usage numbers to share."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/hued/lead-loop.webp","alt":"Four schematic phone screens in a row: Home with the day's colour, the camera with the colour pinned on top, the result with a score ring, and the standings list","caption":"The daily loop in four screens, redrawn from the app's layout code. These are schematics with sample values, not screenshots.","width":2400,"height":1170},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A small daily prompt that costs almost nothing to run","blocks":[{"p":"Hued started from a brief I set myself. Give everyone one colour a day. They go out, find it in the real world and take a photo. The app scores how well they caught it, and they compare with friends."},{"p":"I added a cost rule. It had to run on free plans. So anything that would cost money for every photo, such as working out a score, happens on the phone. The server only keeps the record."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/same-colour.webp","alt":"Diagram: the date 2026-11-11 feeds three phones, in India, in London and with no signal, which all show the same colour, Soft Seafoam Muted, with a note that no server call is made","caption":"The idea in one picture. The colour comes from the date, so every phone agrees without asking a server, even with no signal.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"There was no user research before I built it. I have no interview notes, survey results or usage numbers, so this page quotes none."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"One colour, one photo, one place in the standings","blocks":[{"p":"The app has four tabs: Home, Standings, Groups and Profile. The daily loop runs through the first two."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/game-loop.webp","alt":"Diagram of six steps in a loop: a new colour, hunt and shoot, scored on the phone, share and lock, streak, standings and feed, with a side note about drafts","caption":"One day in Hued. Look at the red box: the draft step is promised on screen but not built yet.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"p":"A streak counts the days in a row you have posted. The database keeps it, with a rule that runs each time a post is saved."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/streak-rule.webp","alt":"Eight sample days showing the streak count: 1, 2, 3, unchanged on a missed day, back to 1, then 2, 3 and 4","caption":"The streak rule worked through a sample week. A missed day changes nothing until your next post starts the count again.","width":1600,"height":640}]},{"p":"Around the loop sit the social parts: following people, a feed of the day's photos from people you follow, groups with their own chat and standings, a calendar of past finds and an activity inbox."}]},{"id":"colour","label":"Decision 1","nav":"The daily colour","heading":"The colour is made from the date, and leans toward colours you can find","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Home opens on the day's colour, with its name, its hex code and a short prompt about where it might be found."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/annotated-home.webp","alt":"Schematic Home screen with six numbered notes: the streak, the colour name and hex code, the prompt, the capture button, the feed and the tab bar","caption":"Home, as a schematic. Note 2 is the point: name, hex code and prompt all come from the date, on the phone.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"In the build: the daily colour changed four times in eleven days. I started with 48 hand-picked colours, each with a name and a prompt. The next day I grew the list to 137. Ten minutes later I replaced the list with a formula."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/colour-versions.webp","alt":"Four cards in a row: a list of 48 colours on 25 June, a list of 137 on 26 June, a formula made from the date on 26 June, and a findability weighting on 5 July","caption":"The four versions. The swatches in the first two cards are the real lists from the code at the time.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The formula turns the date into a number, and the number into a hue, a saturation and a lightness. Limits keep out grey, neon, black and white. Every phone gets the same answer, offline."},{"p":"But the formula treated every hue the same. About one day in six it picked a colour that is hard to find outside, such as cyan, chartreuse or magenta. On 5 July I gave every colour a findability score."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/findability.webp","alt":"Line chart of findability across the colour wheel: high for earth tones, foliage and sky, low dips at chartreuse, cyan and magenta","caption":"Findability by hue, redrawn from the numbers in the code. Earth, foliage and sky score high. The three dips are the colours nature rarely makes.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Now the code draws six candidates a day and holds a lottery. Each candidate's chance is its findability cubed, so common colours win most days, but not every day."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/daily-pick.webp","alt":"Six colour candidates for 11 November 2026 with their findability and chance, a bar split by those chances with a marker at 38.9%, and the winner, Soft Seafoam Muted","caption":"The draw for 11 November 2026, run on the real code. The most likely candidate lost. The seafoam green had 16.3% odds and won.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"stats":[{"value":"3.3%","label":"of days get a rare colour now, over every date from 2026 to 2035"},{"value":"17.1%","label":"of days would, if the first candidate always won"},{"value":"0.35","label":"findability, the line I used here for \"rare\""}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/month.webp","alt":"Calendar of November 2026 with each day filled in its Hued colour and labelled with its name","caption":"Every colour the current code picks for November 2026. Mostly earth tones, greens and blues, with a rare lime on the 15th.","width":1600,"height":1200}]},{"p":"Rare colours did not go away. When one is the target, scoring needs less of it in the frame, so finding a small patch still counts."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The day changes at midnight UTC for everyone, which keeps the colour shared worldwide. In India that is 5:30 in the morning, and nothing on screen explains it yet."}}]},{"id":"scoring","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Scoring on the phone","heading":"The score is worked out on the phone, so a photo costs the server nothing","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the camera opens with the target colour pinned on top. There is a shutter and nothing else, because Hued takes live photos only."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/annotated-capture.webp","alt":"Schematic camera screen with five numbered notes: the pinned colour chip, the framing guide, the processing message, the zoom badge and the shutter","caption":"The camera, as a schematic. Note 3: the score is ready before anything is uploaded.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"In the build: when you press the shutter, the phone makes two copies of the photo. A tiny one, 64 by 64 pixels, is used for scoring and never leaves the phone. A larger one waits until you share."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/scoring-pipeline.webp","alt":"Flow from the photo to two copies and place data, then three scoring layers, colour match, composition and real-photo guess, multiplied into a score with four verdict bands","caption":"How a photo becomes a score. The three layers multiply, so a weak result in any one pulls the whole score down.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The colour match uses Lab, a way of describing colour where equal distances look about equally different to people. A pixel counts as the colour if it is within 34 Lab units of the target."},{"p":"The upload copy is WebP at quality 0.82, no more than 1,080 pixels on its longest side. That keeps each photo to roughly 100 to 200 KB, so the free 1 GB of storage holds thousands of them."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The server stores the score the phone sends. It only checks that the number is between 0 and 100, so a determined person could send a score they did not earn. Scoring on the server would cost money for every photo, which is what I set out to avoid."}}]},{"id":"fair","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Generous, then strict","heading":"Generous about colour, strict about cheating","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the result screen leads with the score ring, drawn in the day's colour, and a one or two word verdict. Caption, location and sharing come after."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/annotated-result.webp","alt":"Schematic result screen with five numbered notes: the score ring, the verdict, the caption and location card, three small checks and the share button","caption":"The result, as a schematic with a sample score of 72. Note 4 replaced a single \"passed our checks\" label, so you can see which check failed.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"In the build: real colour rarely matches a target exactly. Light, shadow and the camera's white balance all shift it. On 3 July I widened the match, gave more credit for a close but imperfect colour, and lowered the verdict bands."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/relaxed.webp","alt":"Chart of how present a colour counts as against how much of the photo matches, before and after the change, next to a table of four settings before and after","caption":"The 3 July change. At 10% of the frame, a common colour now counts as 71% present instead of 57%. The bottom row is the point: the cheat checks did not move.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The cheat checks run in a fixed order. Some stop a photo outright. Others only lower the score and show a note."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/gate.webp","alt":"Flow of three checks, flat wall, missing camera data and a real-photo guess above 85%, each leading to a blocked message, then a green box for photos that can be shared, beside a note on the limits of the real-photo guess","caption":"The checks before sharing. Read the box on the right: the real-photo guess does much less than its name suggests.","width":1600,"height":960}]},{"aside":{"title":"About the real-photo guess.","detail":"There is no free and reliable way to tell an AI image from a photo, and the code says so in its own notes. The guess is a tentative heuristic, not a reliable detector. On a photo with camera data it can lower the score by about 12% at most."}}]},{"id":"look","label":"Decision 4","nav":"A quiet interface","heading":"A quiet interface, so the day's colour is the loudest thing on screen","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: white, greys, one blue for actions and orange for streaks. The only other colour is the day's, on the Home card, the camera chip, the score ring and the standings tag."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/brand.webp","alt":"The Hued logo, four frames of its loading animation, eight interface colour swatches and the two typefaces, Fraunces and Hanken Grotesk","caption":"The look of the app. The hand-drawn logo carries the personality, so the screens can stay plain.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"In the build: the light and dark palettes use the same name for each role, so every screen switches with one setting. Text on the day's colour turns dark or white depending on how bright the colour is, so the name stays readable whatever the date brings."},{"p":"On 3 July I also gave shared parts and every icon-only button a role and a spoken label, including back, close, flip camera and the shutter. The capture button, for example, is read out as \"Capture today's colour\" followed by the colour's name."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/welcome.webp","alt":"The Hued sign-in screen on Android: the logo, a \"Welcome back\" heading, username and password fields and a light blue sign-in button","caption":"The sign-in screen of the release build, the one real capture I have. A serif heading, one blue action, nothing else.","width":720,"height":1280,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"three"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Letting the colour lead means it can clash with the interface. On 13 November 2026 the colour is a strong blue, #1F7BE1, close to the blue of the buttons."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"The phone does the costly work; the database holds the rules","blocks":[{"p":"The phone works out the colour, the score and the compressed photo. The server keeps posts, people and standings, and the rules about who can see and change what live in the database itself."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/architecture.webp","alt":"Three columns: on the phone, daily colour, scoring and demo mode; on the server, Postgres, database rules and sign-in with storage; around it, the admin panel and releases","caption":"What runs where. With no server keys, the app runs on built-in sample data.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Expo SDK 51, React Native, TypeScript"],["Camera, place and alerts","Expo camera, location and notifications"],["Server","Supabase: Postgres, sign-in, photo storage and two server functions"],["Admin panel","A separate Vite web app with charts"],["Releases","GitHub Actions builds the Android app; Expo updates reach installed apps over the air"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"35","label":"commits in 18 days"},{"value":"20","label":"screens in the app"},{"value":"53","label":"written test cases, 19 of them must-pass"},{"value":"7","label":"admin screens besides sign-in"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hued/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of the build in five groups of days with commit counts: 25 to 26 June 10, 1 to 2 July 2, 3 July 10, 4 to 5 July 11, 12 July 2","caption":"The build by day. Most of the work landed in two bursts: the first two days, and 3 to 5 July.","width":1600,"height":780}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were not on the screens people see."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Photos","On Android the camera reports the photo's size before it is turned upright. Resizing to that width and height squashed portrait photos. Now only one side is set and the other follows."],["Sign-in","People were signed out every hour or two. The phone pauses timers in the background, so the sign-in token missed its refresh and was cancelled. Refreshing now starts when the app comes back to the front."],["Groups","The rules for groups and members referred to each other, and the database stopped with an endless loop. I rewrote them in one file with helper functions and marked nine older scripts as not to be run."],["Security","A pass found tables that anyone could read and a lookup that revealed people's emails. I turned the rules on, removed the lookup and locked an account for 15 minutes after 8 wrong passwords."]]}},{"p":"There are no automated tests on the scoring or the daily colour. The checks that exist are type checking and a written test plan of 53 cases for the feed, group chat, profile grid, capture and admin panel."}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"A beta on Android, with no usage numbers to share","blocks":[{"p":"Hued is a beta. The Android app is shared as a download, and updates reach installed apps over the air. Store listings and privacy answers for the App Store and Google Play are written, and a landing page was built on 12 July."},{"p":"The admin panel has a dashboard and analytics, but I am not sharing numbers from it here. I have no user counts, retention figures or feedback notes to report. These are also not there yet:"},{"list":["Drafts. The app says your best draft is submitted at the end of the day, but drafts are only held in the phone's memory and nothing submits them.","Real screenshots of the main loop. This page uses schematics redrawn from the code.","A run of the written test plan on a real phone. Its own notes say the phone cases still needed an emulator and a device."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Make the promises on screen true, then test with people","blocks":[{"list":["Build the end-of-day draft step, or take the promise off the screen.","Add a sanity check on the server for scores, and automated tests for the scoring and the daily colour.","Fix two quirks in the daily draw. On about nine days in ten the six candidates share one saturation, because of how the number from the date spreads its bits. And names can double up: 5 November 2026 is \"Deep Deep Rose\".","Show when the day changes in each person's own time.","Ask players how they read a low score, especially in poor light, before making the competitive parts more prominent."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Fairness is a design problem as much as a maths one","blocks":[{"p":"The colour list lasted a day. A formula gave endless variety, but it had no sense of what people can actually find. The fix was one number per colour, findability, used twice: to choose the colour and to score it."},{"p":"I also learned to split generous from strict. A score that feels unfair for an honest photo makes the game feel broken, so the colour match got gentler. A cheat that works makes the standings worthless, so those checks stayed fixed."},{"p":"And a check should be described by what it can really do. On a photo with camera data, the real-photo guess can never block anything, so I call it a guess."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","headline":"An event page is only the beginning."}},"yuva-panaji":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","kind":"product","headline":"One sign-up, carried all the way to the registration desk.","summary":"I designed and built the website for Yuva, a youth service group in Goa. The public side lists events and takes sign-ups. The private side helps the committee check payments, print the desk list and run its membership drive.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working system","context":"Community operations · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"connected surfaces"},{"value":"Public","label":"events and registration"},{"value":"Private","label":"committee operations"}],"problem":"A community organisation needs a public account of what it does and a practical way to run the next event. The reviewed Yuva source brings those responsibilities together: public discovery and registration on one side, event administration and committee workflows on the other.","process":["I mapped the service from public event discovery and registration through committee review and the attendance desk. Participants and volunteers need different views of the same event information.","The design priority is continuity: information entered on a registration form should remain useful when a volunteer checks someone in, corrects a record, or prepares a printed list."],"decisions":[{"title":"Connect discovery to the event’s operational record","choice":"The public registration journey and admin roster belong to the same event model.","reason":"A committee should not have to reinterpret a public sign-up into a separate attendance workflow.","tradeoff":"Custom events need flexible fields without making every registration form equally complex."},{"title":"Give public and committee users different surfaces","choice":"Visitors see event and organisation information, while management workflows sit behind admin access.","reason":"The interface should reflect different responsibilities and information needs.","tradeoff":"A clean separation in navigation is only part of the requirement; permissions and data handling must be verified independently."},{"title":"Include the work that happens away from the screen","choice":"The documented system supports desk attendance sheets and spreadsheet exports.","reason":"Community operations often happen at an event desk, where a printable list can be more useful than another dashboard.","tradeoff":"Exported files can become stale. The workflow should explain when a printout was produced and how corrections return to the system."}],"outcome":"I connected public event information and registration with the committee’s follow-up work: rosters, corrections, exports and attendance. The public website and operations portal have different jobs, but the information needs to carry from one to the other.","reflection":"I would review the handoff at an actual event: who checks a registration, who corrects it and what the attendance desk receives. That is where duplicate entry and outdated lists become visible.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: follow one event from discovery through registration, roster correction, and check-in with both participant and committee perspectives.","Proposed measure: duplicate corrections, incomplete registrations, and handoff effort at the event desk, with a documented baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/db.js","detail":"Current public/admin architecture, event operations and Supabase-backed data layer. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://yuva-website.netlify.app/","cover":"assets/products/yuva.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":24,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"An event page [[is only the beginning.]]","scene":"Imagine someone finding an event and registering. Their next step is attendance. For the committee, that same action starts a chain of rosters, corrections, desk sheets, and follow-up.","question":"Can one action stay useful all the way to the event desk?","insight":"[[The public journey and the operational record]] are parts of the same service. The handoff matters as much as the page.","pivot":"I mapped how public discovery and registration continue into committee workflows, including the work that happens away from a screen.","takeaway":"Design the next person’s task into the current person’s action.","journey":[{"title":"Discover","detail":"Find the event and its details."},{"title":"Register","detail":"Submit the information the event actually needs."},{"title":"Prepare","detail":"Carry it into a usable committee roster."},{"title":"Check in","detail":"Support attendance at the event desk."}],"decisionLeads":["Connect the action to the record behind it.","Give each responsibility its own surface.","Design for the handoff beyond the screen."],"annotations":[{"title":"A public front door","detail":"The community website introduces the organisation and its work."},{"title":"An operational continuation","detail":"The case follows the event into registration and committee tasks."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · public and admin workflows","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Event participant & committee volunteer","context":"One person registers; another prepares the roster and attendance desk.","need":"Carry the same event information from discovery into check-in.","friction":"Public sign-ups and operational lists can become disconnected.","implication":"Design the participant and volunteer journeys together."}},"theme":{"layout":"service","mode":"light","paper":"#fbfaf5","ink":"#22301f","dim":"#5c6855","line":"#d8ddce","accent":"#157a45","wash":"#e3eedc","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Two sides of one event","process":"Registration continues at the desk","decisions":"Designing the whole service","outcome":"From public page to roster","reflection":"Following real event work"},"decisionHeading":"Connect public events to the work behind them.","resultHeading":"The public event and the work behind it."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/yuva.webp","alt":"Yuva Panaji — archived product interface","caption":"Original public homepage for Yuva Panaji. Community activity introduces the organisation before visitors enter its event and registration routes.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"One sign-up, carried all the way to the [[registration desk]].","hook":"I designed and built the website for Yuva, a youth service group in Goa. The public side lists events and takes sign-ups. The private side helps the committee check payments, print the desk list and run its membership drive.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"For","value":"Yuva, a youth social service organisation in Panaji, Goa, founded in 2012"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web: a public site and a private admin portal"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July to November 2026"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live, on the organisation's own hosting and database. No usage numbers to share"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Next.js 16, React 19, Supabase, Netlify"}],"links":[{"label":"Visit the live site","url":"https://yuva-website.netlify.app/"}],"linksHeading":"See it live","summary":["The problem: an event sign-up is only the start. The committee still has to check payments, fix mistakes, run a desk on the day, keep the accounts and report afterwards.","What I built: one site with a public front and a private back office. Each event gets its own form, and every sign-up flows into a list, a printed desk sheet and an Excel file.","The key idea: keep it one record from the form to the desk. Nobody types a sign-up out again.","Where it stands: live and run on the organisation's own accounts. I have no counts of events or sign-ups to share, because those sit with members' details."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/lead.webp","alt":"Three schematics side by side: a public event page with a registration form, the same form as a sheet on a phone, and the admin list of registrants with payment and check-in columns","caption":"The public event page, the same form on a phone, and the committee's list it feeds. These are schematics redrawn from the code with sample data, because the real screens hold members' names and faces.","width":2400,"height":930},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A sign-up keeps creating work after the button is pressed","blocks":[{"p":"Yuva is a youth social service group in Panaji, Goa. It runs drives, camps, cultural events and sports, and it recruits new members once a year."},{"p":"From the outside, an event needs a page and a form. Inside the committee, each sign-up starts a chain of jobs. Someone checks the fee was paid. Someone fixes a wrong name. Someone stands at a desk on the day with a list. Someone keeps the accounts and writes up the event afterwards."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/one-event.webp","alt":"Diagram: three steps a participant takes on the left, one registration record in the middle, and six committee jobs on the right, from checking the payment to reporting afterwards","caption":"The problem in one picture. One record in the middle, and six jobs the committee does with it. Sample data.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"So I set myself one rule. A sign-up is entered once, by the person signing up. Every list, sheet and file after that is made from the same record."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I have no interviews or written feedback from the committee, so this page quotes none. The jobs above come from what the admin side was built to do."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"A public front, a private back office and a few pages made for paper","blocks":[{"p":"The site has three parts. Anyone can browse the public pages, register for an event and apply in a membership drive. The committee works in an admin portal behind one shared login. A few plain pages are made to be printed, such as the attendance sheet for the desk."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/site-map.webp","alt":"Map of the site in three columns: public pages, admin portal sections and print pages, with arrows showing how events, sign-ups and applications move between them","caption":"How the site is organised. The middle arrows are the point: a saved event shows on the public page at once, and every sign-up lands in that event's list.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"There are no accounts for the public. A person who registered is known only by the phone number they gave. That one choice shaped a lot of what follows."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/roles.webp","alt":"Table of nine actions against three kinds of user: anyone, someone who registered, and the committee. Deleting an event, closing a drive or starting a new campaign asks the committee for the password again","caption":"Who can do what. Look at the last row: the riskiest actions ask for the password a second time, even when signed in.","width":1600,"height":980}]}]},{"id":"forms","label":"Decision 1","nav":"A form for each event","heading":"Each event asks only what it needs","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a form builder inside each event. Name and phone are always asked. Email, age, gender and address can each be switched on or off and made required. Then the committee adds its own questions, such as a T-shirt size or a school name."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/annotated-form-builder.webp","alt":"The registration form builder with six numbered notes: locked name and phone fields, include and required toggles, a custom dropdown question, reorder arrows, answer types and the apply button","caption":"The form builder. Note 1 matters most: the phone number is how a second entry is caught, and how a person finds their entry later.","width":1600,"height":1070}]},{"p":"The public form then shows exactly those questions, in that order. On a phone the form sits far down the page, so a Register button stays at the bottom of the screen and opens the form as a sheet."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/registration-form.webp","alt":"Three phone schematics: an event page with a Register button pinned to the bottom, the registration form open as a sheet with a UPI payment box, and the success message with a reference number","caption":"Registering on a phone, in three steps. In step 2 the fee adds a UPI box. In step 3 the person gets a reference to quote later.","width":1600,"height":1160}],"layout":"one"},{"p":"In the build: the database is reached through its web interface, which cannot add new columns. So each event's settings live as one hidden row in the same table that holds its questions. One shared function splits that row out before anything shows the form."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Adding a column for each new setting. Each one would have meant a script pasted into the database console by hand. The cost of my choice is that every part of the code that reads questions has to go through that one function, or the settings row would show up as a question."}}]},{"id":"desk","label":"Decision 2","nav":"From form to desk","heading":"The sign-up becomes the list at the desk","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: after a sign-up, the person gets a reference such as YUVA-E12-00047. The committee sees them in that event's list, with a column for every answer. Payment is marked pending, paid or waived. A tick marks the entry verified."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/registration-flow.webp","alt":"Workflow in two rows: the participant's event page, form, server checks and reference number on top; the committee's list, desk sheet, Excel export and after-event work below","caption":"The registration workflow. Step 3 is where the rules live: open, before the deadline, no repeat phone, places left.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/annotated-registrations.webp","alt":"The admin registrations tab with seven numbered notes on the tab, the desk sheet and Excel buttons, the reference, a custom question column, payment status, the verified tick and the edit and remove buttons","caption":"One event's registrations. Note 4: a question added in the builder turns into its own column here, with no extra setup.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: the reference number is made from the database's own row number, which is never reused. So no two people can share one, and nothing extra has to be stored."},{"p":"The desk sheet is a separate page laid out for paper. The list is sorted A to Z and keeps only what the desk needs: name, phone, a Present column and room to sign. Blank numbered rows fill each page for people who walk in."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/desk-sheet.webp","alt":"Schematic of the printed attendance sheet with four numbered notes on the screen-only toolbar, the alphabetical count, the columns kept, and the blank numbered rows","caption":"The desk sheet. Note 4: 54 rows on the first page and 60 on each page after, so every printed page is full.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Paper is a snapshot. A correction made after printing does not reach the sheet, so the desk works from the list as it was when it was printed."}}]},{"id":"edits","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Corrections by phone","heading":"People fix their own mistakes, with the phone number as the key","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: if the committee allows it for an event, an \"Already registered?\" panel appears on the event page. You type the phone number you used, pick your entry, and correct it. The phone number itself cannot be changed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/self-edit.webp","alt":"Three schematic panels: entering a phone number, choosing between two entries that share it, and editing an entry with the phone number locked","caption":"Correcting an entry. Step 2 exists because one number can hold several entries, for example a parent registering two children.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"In the build: before saving, the server checks that the phone given matches the entry. During an audit in October I also changed the lookup to an exact match. Before that, a phone search could match more than one number."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A phone number is not proof that it is yours. Anyone who knows it could open the entry. Doing better needs a one-time code or a signed link sent to the person, which I have not built."}}]},{"id":"drive","label":"Decision 4","nav":"A drive that holds up","heading":"The membership drive is built for a crowd on phones","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: once a year the committee opens a membership drive. A banner appears on the home page and the link is shared. People apply on their phones, get a receipt such as YUVA-M57, and wait for a call. It started as a volunteer drive and was renamed in October; the old links still lead to it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/membership-form.webp","alt":"Two phone schematics of the membership page: the top of the form with eligibility note and fields, and the confirmation with a reference number","caption":"The membership page on a phone. One column, 16px text in the fields, and a receipt at the end. Sample data.","width":1600,"height":1180}]},{"p":"In the build: I planned for many people applying at once on weak signal. The answers are kept in the browser tab for up to 24 hours. Repeat taps are blocked. A retry with unchanged answers carries the same key, so a lost reply cannot make a second application, and the person gets the same receipt back."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/membership-flow.webp","alt":"Workflow in three columns: the applicant's form, draft and single submission; the server's drive check, answer check and receipt; the committee's four statuses, interview notes, batches and exports","caption":"The membership workflow. The drive is checked again at the moment of saving, so a page left open cannot post to a closed drive.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The photo was the hardest part. It is optional, but phone photos are large and the free storage plan is small. So the browser shrinks it before sending, and the server rebuilds it, strips location data and stores a small copy where only the committee can see it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/photo-pipeline.webp","alt":"Diagram of four steps for an applicant photo, from up to 10 MiB on the phone to 300 KiB sent and 160 KiB stored, with bars drawn to scale","caption":"One optional photo, four limits. The bars are to scale: what is stored is about one sixtieth of the largest photo accepted.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/annotated-membership.webp","alt":"The admin membership screen with seven numbered notes on the home-screen shortcut, folded system checks, drive status, counts, search and copy phones, private photos and statuses","caption":"The committee's side of the drive. It works as a calling list: note 5 copies every phone number in view in one tap.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"Isolated tests send 200 different applications at once and 50 retries of the same one, against a stand-in for the database."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"Those tests do not use the real database or hosting, so they say nothing about live capacity. The free plans can pause a project, so the checklist I wrote for the committee asks them to keep another way to collect contacts ready on drive day."}}]},{"id":"admin","label":"Decision 5","nav":"A plainer back office","heading":"The back office got its own plainer design","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the admin portal no longer borrows the public site's look. It has one sans-serif type scale, neutral surfaces, green main buttons and red only for actions that delete."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/admin-dashboard.webp","alt":"Schematic of the admin dashboard: a sidebar with nine sections, four summary cards with numbers left blank, a list of sample events and a ledger summary","caption":"The admin dashboard. The numbers are left blank on purpose, because the real ones belong to the organisation.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"In the build: I listed what made the working screens hard to use, then fixed each one. The public site's editorial type was the first thing to go."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What I changed"],"rows":[["Decorative type, uppercase buttons and small labels made tables hard to scan","An admin-only stylesheet with one type scale and readable tables. Public pages are untouched"],["Mixed emoji icons. The phone menu could not be closed by tapping outside it","One icon set, the current page marked, and a phone menu that closes on a tap outside or Escape and keeps keyboard focus inside"],["A failed load looked like an empty list. No status filter, count or pages","An inline retry, a status filter, result counts, and pages of 25 events or 50 participants"],["A pop-up after every save blocked the next action","Notices that do not block. Delete confirmations stay"],["Login labels were not tied to their fields, and the show-password button could not be reached by keyboard","Both fixed"]]}},{"aside":{"title":"What is still unchecked.","detail":"These changes passed the automated tests and the production build. Real phone checks, keyboard checks and a screen-reader pass have not been done yet."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One Next.js app, and only the server talks to the database","blocks":[{"p":"The whole site is one Next.js app on Netlify. Public pages read from the database on each visit, so an edit in the admin shows at once with no rebuild. The browser never talks to the database directly."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: public pages, admin portal and print pages in the browser; the Next.js server with checks and image handling on Netlify; Supabase, Groq, Netlify Forms and GitHub Actions as services","caption":"What runs where. The middle column is the gatekeeper: every private request checks the sign-in there.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Next.js 16 with the App Router, React 19, Node 22"],["Data and files","Supabase: Postgres and storage, in the Mumbai region"],["Sign-in","One committee login, a signed session cookie that scripts cannot read"],["Images","sharp, to re-encode uploads before they are stored"],["Exports","Excel files for lists and the ledger"],["Drafted reports","A language model on Groq drafts press releases and reports from an event's records"],["Hosting","Netlify, deployed on every push to the main branch"]]}},{"p":"The site started in July as a static site edited through files. Within two weeks I moved it to a live database, because the admin portal needed to save changes as they happened."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/yuva-panaji/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of commits by month: July 79, August 4, September 0, October 7, November 9, with the main milestones under each month","caption":"The build by month. Most of it happened in July; October was an audit and the membership drive.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"stats":[{"value":"99","label":"commits, July to November 2026"},{"value":"3","label":"automated test suites, first added in October 2026"},{"value":"1,205","label":"made-up registrations one test runs against, to prove lists past 1,000 rows"},{"value":"200","label":"applications sent at once in an isolated test"}]},{"p":"In late October I audited the admin side for speed and reliability. Most of what I found was silent: numbers that looked right and were not."},{"table":{"head":["Area","Before","After"],"rows":[["Dashboard","Four requests downloaded every event, team member, registration and ledger row. Counts could stop at 1,000","One request with exact counts from the database"],["Long lists and exports","The database's default limit could hide every row after the first 1,000","Read page by page in a fixed order"],["Money","Totals added up as decimals, which can drift","Totals kept in paise, as whole numbers"],["Registration","Phone lookups matched patterns. Deadlines closed at midnight UTC. Required custom answers were not checked on the server","Exact phone match, deadlines that include the whole day in Goa time, and required answers checked"],["Uploads","A file could be stored before the sign-up was checked, and images had no general size cap","Check first. Images up to 20 MB, PDFs up to 5 MB, and new files removed if the sign-up fails"],["Certificates","A slow reply for the last event could overwrite the attendees of the one just picked","Old requests are cancelled and late replies ignored"]]}},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not claim.","detail":"I did not measure the live site before and after, so I claim no speed-up. The audit also wrote a database script that saves an event in one step and stops two people taking the last place at once. At the time it had not been applied to the live database; it needs a test on a copy first."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live on the organisation's own accounts, with no numbers to share","blocks":[{"p":"The site is live and runs on the organisation's own hosting, database and code accounts. Changes deploy when they are pushed to the main branch."},{"p":"I have no counts of events, registrations or membership applications to report. They live in the organisation's database next to members' names and phone numbers, and I have not taken figures out of it for this page."},{"p":"These are still open:"},{"list":["The committee shares one login, so the site cannot tell who did what.","Self-editing trusts the phone number, as described above.","The audit's database script was not yet applied to the live database at the time of the audit.","Real phone, keyboard and screen-reader checks of the admin are still to do."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Give people their own keys","blocks":[{"list":["Separate logins for committee members, so actions can be traced to a person.","A one-time code or a signed link for self-editing, instead of the phone number alone.","Test the audit's database script on a copy of the database, including two people taking the last place at once, then apply it.","A careful clean-up of stored images that nothing uses any more. Some old events share images, so blind deletion would break them.","A real-device pass on phones, with a keyboard and a screen reader."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"The quiet limits were the dangerous ones","blocks":[{"p":"The worst bugs did not crash anything. A dashboard count that stops at 1,000 looks like a real number. A list that ends at the 1,000th row looks complete. I now check where a number comes from before I trust it on screen."},{"p":"I also learned that free plans are a design brief. The photo limits, the folded system checks and the scheduled ping to keep the database awake all come from working inside them. And a site without accounts still needs a key for each person. The phone number was the simplest key, and the page above is honest about what it costs."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","headline":"A PDF deck you can work with again."}},"deckforge":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","kind":"product","headline":"A flat PDF deck, rebuilt as layers you can edit and animate.","summary":"DeckForge is a web app I designed and built on my own that takes a PDF of a slide deck apart into text, shapes and images you can edit, animate and present, without uploading the file.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working product","context":"Presentation tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Editable","label":"reconstructed page objects"},{"value":"40","label":"documented animation presets"},{"value":"Browser","label":"core document processing"}],"problem":"A PDF preserves how a slide looks but usually loses the convenient objects a presenter wants to edit. DeckForge approaches that gap as a reconstruction problem: recover meaningful text, images, and vector objects, then let a person work with them rather than repainting a screenshot.","process":["I framed the editor around objects a presenter can recognise: a text block, a logo, a shape, or a chart. A technically selectable fragment is not useful if its meaning is unclear.","The flow needs to explain what can be edited, preserve a usable slide when recovery is limited, and distinguish live presentation pacing from an exported recording."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make layers correspond to recognisable objects","choice":"Paths are grouped into likely logos, dividers, shapes, and chart series, while text is reconstructed into blocks.","reason":"An editable layer is useful only if the person recognises what it represents.","tradeoff":"Object recovery is heuristic. Preserving appearance can be more valuable than forcing misleading editability."},{"title":"Let a presenter control the pace","choice":"Animations can wait for an advance action, while timed outputs play sequentially.","reason":"A live talk and an exported recording need different behaviour from the same slide.","tradeoff":"That difference must be visible before export so the presenter knows what an audience will receive."},{"title":"Keep the core document workflow local","choice":"PDF reconstruction and editing run in the browser; phone pairing has a separate connection mechanism.","reason":"Local processing supports a lightweight workflow and avoids requiring a document-upload service.","tradeoff":"Phone pairing still uses signalling, and some remote features exchange preview frames. The privacy explanation should distinguish those paths."}],"outcome":"I organised recovered slide content into recognisable objects that can be edited, sequenced, presented and exported. Some content remains an image, so the interface needs to explain what was recovered and what can actually be changed.","reflection":"I would use a difficult deck to test the explanation, not just a clean example. Someone should be able to tell why one element is editable and another is not, then choose a workable next step.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: import a defined set of native-text, scanned, multi-column, and dense-vector PDFs; record editability and visible fidelity separately.","Proposed study: revise a real slide and present it, measuring manual corrections and surprises between editor, audience view, and exported output."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How extraction works and Known limitations","detail":"Reconstruction pipeline, presentation model, export boundaries and remote-pairing caveat."},{"label":"src/engine and src/store","detail":"Shared extraction, animation, export and document-state behaviour. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://deckforge.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":14,"pdfPage":27,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A PDF deck [[you can work with again.]]","scene":"Imagine needing to revise one text block or animate one diagram, but having only the exported PDF. The visual is preserved; the convenient objects are gone.","question":"What does it take to recover something a presenter can actually work with?","insight":"[[Recognisable objects make editability useful]]. Text, paths, and images need to become meaningful layers rather than a pile of drawing instructions.","pivot":"I framed the journey around recognising an object, revising it, and presenting it, with an explanation when recovered content cannot be edited.","takeaway":"Recover enough structure to make the next action possible.","journey":[{"title":"Recover","detail":"Read text, paths, and images from the PDF."},{"title":"Group","detail":"Reassemble objects the person recognises."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Edit and sequence meaningful layers."},{"title":"Present","detail":"Carry the same slide model into the talk."}],"decisionLeads":["Recover the object a person recognises.","Let the presenter control the pace.","Explain what stays local and what connects."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working reconstruction tool","detail":"The capture introduces the actual editor workflow."},{"title":"An honest fallback","detail":"Scanned or dense pages can remain usable raster layers."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · slide editing journey","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Presenter revising an existing PDF","context":"Making changes shortly before sharing or presenting a deck.","need":"Recognise editable content and control presentation pacing.","friction":"A recovered fragment may be selectable without being meaningful.","implication":"Expose recognisable objects and explain editing limits."}},"theme":{"layout":"slides","mode":"dark","paper":"#17191c","ink":"#f7f8fa","dim":"#b2bac6","line":"#545c67","accent":"#b9aeff","wash":"#302b46","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A deck that needs changing","process":"Objects should be recognisable","decisions":"Designing edit and present","outcome":"The recovered slide","reflection":"When a page stays an image"},"decisionHeading":"Make the PDF’s editable parts understandable.","resultHeading":"A slide that can keep moving."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","alt":"DeckForge — archived product interface","caption":"Original DeckForge starting screen for opening a PDF presentation. Editing and animation begin with an existing deck.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1269,"height":714},"v2":{"title":"A flat PDF deck, rebuilt as [[layers you can edit and animate]].","hook":"DeckForge is a web app I designed and built on my own that takes a PDF of a slide deck apart into text, shapes and images you can edit, animate and present, without uploading the file.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"To work again with slides you only have as a PDF"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, in the browser. A phone page acts as the remote"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React, TypeScript, Vite, Zustand, Tailwind, pdf.js"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"September 2026"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live and free, version 0.1.0. No usage numbers yet"}],"links":[{"label":"Open DeckForge","url":"https://deckforge.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: a deck you only have as a PDF looks right but cannot be changed. Most converters hand back a picture of each page.","What I built: a browser app that rebuilds each page as layers, animates them, and presents or exports the result, with a phone as the remote.","The key idea: be clear about what was recovered. A page that cannot be taken apart stays a picture, and the app says so.","Where it stands: live and free, version 0.1.0. I have no usage numbers or measured success rate to share."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/editor-cards.webp","alt":"The DeckForge editor with a three-card slide open, a list of nine layers on the left, an outline around the selected shape and an animation timeline below","caption":"A slide from a PDF, after import. Each heading and paragraph is its own layer, named by role in the list on the left, and each has its own bar on the timeline.","width":1600,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A PDF keeps the look of a slide and loses its parts","blocks":[{"p":"Slides often reach you only as a PDF, exported from Keynote, PowerPoint, Google Slides, Figma, InDesign or Canva. The PDF looks right. But you cannot fix one line of text, move a logo or animate one chart."},{"p":"Most \"PDF to slides\" tools give you a picture of each page. A PDF is really a list of drawing instructions: put these letters here, fill this shape, paint this image. The objects a person sees, such as a title or a chart, are not stored anywhere."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing a list of PDF drawing instructions on the left with a list of named layers such as Title, Bullet list, Logo and Chart on the right","caption":"The gap in one picture. The PDF stores how to paint the page. A presenter wants to grab a title, a list or a chart.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"p":"DeckForge takes the page apart instead. Text comes back as paragraphs, a logo as one object, and a bar chart as one series. It all happens inside the browser tab. The file is not uploaded and there is no account."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/landing.webp","alt":"The DeckForge home page with the headline \"Turn a flat PDF into an animated deck\", a drop zone for the PDF and four feature labels","caption":"The home page. The illustration on the right lifts a title, an image, a shape and body text off a page, which is the whole idea of the product.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I designed for a presenter who has to revise a deck someone else exported. That is my own assumption. I have no interviews, user feedback or usage data, so this page does not quote any."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Drop a PDF, get layers back, then present or export","blocks":[{"p":"There are three steps. Drop in the PDF. Each page comes back as a slide of layers you can move, restyle and animate. Then present it full screen, with a phone as the remote, or export it."},{"p":"Each import gets its own link and is saved in the browser on that machine. Importing a second PDF never overwrites the first."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/list-slide.webp","alt":"A rebuilt slide with a serif heading and four bullet points, each line set in the original typefaces","caption":"A list slide after import. The four bullets came back as one list layer with real bullet marks, not as a picture.","width":995,"height":590},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/library.webp","alt":"The Your decks area of the home page showing one saved deck with a thumbnail and the label 6 slides","caption":"The library. Decks stay in the browser on this device, each with a thumbnail and slide count.","width":630,"height":354}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"To make the screens on this page I wrote a six-page test deck with sample text and made-up numbers, saved it as a PDF and opened it in DeckForge."}]},{"id":"layers","label":"Layers you recognise","nav":"Layers you recognise","heading":"Layers that match what you see, not loose fragments","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: each layer has a name and a role, such as title, heading, body, label, shape or photo. A photo comes out as a photo. A chart comes out as one chart."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/photo-layers.webp","alt":"Editor showing a slide with a drawn landscape on the left and a heading on the right, and a layer list of four items including one marked Photo","caption":"A picture-and-text slide. The image was lifted out as its own Photo layer, and the heading and caption are separate text layers.","width":1312,"height":640}]},{"p":"In the build: pdf.js opens the file in the browser. My code then reads each page three ways and merges the results. The first reading replays the drawing instructions one by one, keeping track of position, clipping, colour and transparency. That is what tells you where each mark really landed and in what colour."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/pipeline.webp","alt":"Flow diagram: a PDF page is read as drawing instructions, as text and as a rendered picture, then grouped and named, and becomes a slide of layers","caption":"How a page comes apart. The third reading, a rendered picture, is there for colour: it checks what sits under each block of text.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"p":"Grouping turns hundreds of marks into a few objects. Nearby paths join into one object, so a logo made of 40 paths is one layer. Shapes that are alike and evenly spaced join into one series, so a bar chart moves as one chart."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/annotated-chart.webp","alt":"Annotated screenshot of a chart slide in the editor, with five numbered markers pointing at the label, the chart, the layer list and the month captions","caption":"The chart slide from my test file, marked up. Two of the five notes are misses, which is why the roles are shown and not hidden.","width":1600,"height":1260}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Grouping and roles are a best guess from size, position and spacing. On my test file the three cards and their coloured dots came back as one shape layer. I kept the guessing visible, so a wrong name is easy to spot."}}]},{"id":"fallback","label":"The honest fallback","nav":"The honest fallback","heading":"A page that cannot be rebuilt stays a picture, and says so","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: on my test file, five of the six pages came back as layers. The cover did not. Its analysis failed, the import listed that in a note, and the slide kept the original page as one image. The layer panel says so in plain words."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/cover-fallback.webp","alt":"Editor showing the cover slide of the test deck, with an empty layer panel reading \"No editable layers on this slide. The original page is shown as a single image.\"","caption":"The cover of my test file. It still looks right and still presents, but the layer panel is honest that nothing on it can be edited piece by piece.","width":1600,"height":640}]},{"p":"In the build: there are three ways a page ends up as a picture. Each one keeps the page usable, and each one is reported."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/fallbacks.webp","alt":"Decision diagram with four rows: analysis failed, no text to read, more than 6,000 paths, and otherwise rebuilt as layers","caption":"The fallback rules. Every page also keeps its original picture, which can be shown underneath the layers as a safety net.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Forcing every page into shapes. A scanned page has no text to recover, and a dense map can hold thousands of paths. Both would come back mangled. A sharp picture is less useful than layers, but it is never wrong."}}]},{"id":"motion","label":"Auto-animate","nav":"Auto-animate","heading":"One button animates the deck, after reading each slide","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: press Auto-animate and a panel works through the deck slide by slide. It says what kind of slide it found and what it will do. At the end it sums up."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/auto-reading.webp","alt":"Progress panel titled \"Reading your deck\" showing slide 4 labelled Series, described as a series animated along its own row","caption":"Midway through my test deck. Slide 4, the bar chart, was read as a series.","width":598,"height":313},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/auto-result.webp","alt":"Summary panel titled \"Your deck is animated\" with 6 slides staged, 29 layers animated, 1.9s longest build, and the slide types and transitions it chose","caption":"The summary for my test deck. \"Empty\" is the cover, which had no layers to move.","width":598,"height":447}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: each slide is checked against five questions before anything moves. The answers set the order and timing of every layer. The gap between two slides gets a transition chosen from what changes between them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/auto-animate.webp","alt":"Diagram listing five questions auto-animate asks of a slide, leading to a build for each slide, a transition for each seam, and a note that there is no randomness","caption":"How auto-animate reads a slide, redrawn from the product's own description. Question 3 is why three cards arrive card by card.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"p":"Underneath, each of the 40 animation presets is a small formula that turns progress from 0 to 1 into a position, size, blur or opacity. An exit is the same formula played backwards, so every preset works both ways."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"There is no randomness, so the same deck animated twice makes the same choices. That makes the result easy to trust and to undo, but it never offers a second take. For a different feel, six house styles can be applied by hand."}}]},{"id":"pace","label":"The speaker sets the pace","nav":"The speaker sets the pace","heading":"Bullets wait for the speaker, recordings do not","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: any animation can start \"On click\" instead of with the slide. A text layer can go further and spend one press per line, so a list arrives as you talk through it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/annotated-onclick.webp","alt":"Annotated screenshot of the editor with a bullet list set to start on click, six numbered markers on the slide rail, the slide, the inspector and the timeline","caption":"Turning a four-line list into four presses. Marker 4 is the sentence I cared most about: it tells you where the waiting happens and where it does not.","width":1600,"height":1420}]},{"p":"In the build: each animation can carry a build step. Only a live talk waits for a press. Everywhere else nobody is there to press, so the editor, the thumbnails, the video export and the web export play the steps one after another."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/build-stages.webp","alt":"Two timelines of the same slide: in a live talk each bullet starts at a press after a gap; elsewhere the bullets play one after another with no gaps","caption":"The same slide in two places. An illustration of the rule, not a measured timeline.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/present-waiting.webp","alt":"Present mode showing a slide with only its heading, and a toolbar at the bottom with slide count, timer, pointer tools, Record and remote buttons","caption":"Present mode, first press still to come. The toolbar holds the pointer tools, recording and the remote, and fades away on its own.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/present-two-lines.webp","alt":"The same slide in present mode with two of the four bullets showing and the toolbar hidden","caption":"Two presses later. Pressing faster than the animation finishes it at once, so a quick speaker never loses a bullet.","width":1600,"height":1000}],"layout":"two"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The same slide now behaves two ways. If that were hidden, a speaker could export a video expecting pauses that are not there. So the panel states the rule in one sentence, right under the press picker."}}]},{"id":"present","label":"Presenting and the remote","nav":"Presenting","heading":"Two windows for the talk, and a phone as the remote","blocks":[{"p":"Press A while presenting and DeckForge opens an audience window with only the slides in it. You drag it to the projector, or pick it when a video call asks which window to share. The main window becomes the presenter console: the current slide, the next one, your notes and the clock."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/presenter-console.webp","alt":"Presenter console with the current slide on the left, a notes panel and an Up next preview on the right, a large Next button and a slide number strip","caption":"The presenter console. \"Audience window live\" at the top confirms the second window is open. Notes added in the editor appear on the right.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/audience-window.webp","alt":"The audience window showing only the slide, with two bullets visible and no controls","caption":"The audience window, captured at the same moment. No notes, no controls.","width":800,"height":533}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"A phone becomes the remote by scanning a QR code. It shows a live view of the slide, your notes, a laser pointer you steer with a finger, and buttons for effects such as confetti and applause."},{"p":"Pairing is the one part that needs a server, because two devices cannot find each other on their own. The copy I ran for these screens was served as plain files, with nothing to pair through. Both the laptop panel and the phone say exactly that, instead of spinning forever. So I could not capture a paired phone for this page."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/pairing-panel.webp","alt":"Pairing panel with a QR code, a six-digit match code, and two notices saying the build is served as plain static files with nothing to pair through","caption":"The pairing panel on a copy with no pairing service behind it. It names the problem and the two ways to fix it. I blurred the local link.","width":670,"height":624},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/phone-remote.webp","alt":"Phone screen of the DeckForge remote showing \"Not connected\", six effect buttons, Black, Replay, Notes and a large Next button","caption":"The phone remote on the same copy. The layout is all there; the red line at the top explains why it is not connected.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"stats":[{"value":"5–10 ms","label":"from pressing Next to a repainted phone view, down from 228 ms, measured on the local relay"},{"value":"8–13 ms","label":"the same with the deck's tab in the background, down from over a second"}]},{"p":"Those numbers came from one change. The phone's view used to wait for a timer, and browsers slow timers to once a second in a background tab. Now it repaints the moment the slide changes."},{"aside":{"title":"What is not local.","detail":"The deck itself never leaves the browser. On the live site a small function acts as a mailbox: the phone and laptop swap a handshake through it, then talk directly. If a network blocks that, the mailbox carries slide control and small preview pictures, and keeps them for under a minute."}}]},{"id":"export","label":"Getting it out","nav":"Getting it out","heading":"Three ways out: a video, one web page, or the project","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/export-video.webp","alt":"Video export dialog with choices for resolution up to 4K, frame rate 24, 30 or 60, MP4 or WebM, quality, hold time per slide and transitions, and an estimate of 6 slides and 26 seconds","caption":"The video export. Frames are drawn from the layers, not recorded off the screen, so there is no window frame or toolbar in the file.","width":670,"height":598},{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/export-menu.webp","alt":"Export menu with four items: Video (MP4 or WebM), Standalone web presentation, DeckForge project, and Open a project file","caption":"The Export menu. Each choice says in one line what you get.","width":287,"height":262}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"A video can be up to 4K at 24, 30 or 60 frames a second, as MP4 or WebM. You can also record the talk itself in present mode: your voice over the slides, at your own pace, with the pointer and effects."},{"p":"The web export is one HTML file with every animation baked into CSS, the browser's own styling language, and no JavaScript at all. Because each preset is a formula of progress, it can be sampled into CSS steps, so springy and bouncing curves survive."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"An export with no JavaScript cannot do everything. Magic Move, where shared layers glide to their new places, falls back to a crossfade. A video clip on a slide starts when the browser is ready, not at its set point in the build."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One renderer, one store, and the browser does the work","blocks":[{"p":"Everything that touches the deck runs in the browser tab. One renderer draws the canvas, the slide list, the layer thumbnails and present mode, so what you edit is what you present."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/architecture.webp","alt":"Architecture diagram: a large browser box holding extraction, the project store, one renderer, animation, exports and the library, and a smaller box outside the tab used only for pairing the phone remote","caption":"How it is put together. The box on the right is only for the phone remote.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","React 18, TypeScript, Vite"],["State","Zustand, with undo that treats a whole drag as one step"],["Reading the PDF","pdf.js, plus my own code to rebuild pages as layers"],["Look and themes","Tailwind and CSS variables, light, dark and system"],["Phone remote","A WebSocket relay when run locally; Netlify Functions and Blobs as a mailbox, then a direct WebRTC link, on the live site"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"13","label":"commits, on 2 and 3 September 2026. The first already held most of the app"},{"value":"~29,000","label":"lines of TypeScript"},{"value":"7","label":"test files, for extraction, animation, auto-animate, builds, pairing and colour contrast"},{"value":"40","label":"animation presets, and 10 slide transitions"}]},{"p":"There is no automated test run on each push yet. The build does run one extra check: it keeps the site's security policy in step with the one small script allowed to run inline."},{"p":"The hardest problems were small details with large effects."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Colour","pdf.js hands colours over as numbers from 0 to 255, not fractions from 0 to 1. Read the wrong way, a whole document turns white."],["Thin lines","A drawn horizontal line has zero height, so every underline and divider vanished. The stroke width now pads the line's box."],["Fonts","Fonts inside the PDF are lifted out and installed in the browser, then checked against the widths the PDF recorded. A font that fails the check is swapped for a substitute instead of printing blanks."],["Phone lag","The phone waited for a timer that a background tab slows to once a second. It now repaints on the event."],["Recording","The recording clock runs in a separate worker, so it keeps going when the audience window covers the editor."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Contrast and keyboard","nav":"Contrast and keyboard","heading":"Contrast is a test the build must pass, not a promise","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/deckforge/editor-dark.webp","alt":"The DeckForge editor in its dark theme, with graphite panels around a light slide of three cards","caption":"The dark theme is graphite rather than black, so the slide is the only bright thing on screen.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Every colour in the interface comes from one set of named values, so one change themes the whole app in light and dark. Text and controls are held to WCAG 2.1 AA, the common accessibility standard. A contrast ratio says how far apart two colours are in brightness; body text needs at least 4.5 to 1, and the edge of a control needs 3 to 1."},{"p":"A test reads the real stylesheet and fails if any pairing the app uses drops below its floor. The interface also works from the keyboard, with a visible focus ring, dialogs that hold and return focus, targets at least 24 pixels across, and reduced motion respected."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are checks on colour and code. They are not an accessibility audit, and I do not claim one."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live and free, with no usage numbers yet","blocks":[{"p":"DeckForge is live at deckforge.srinidhibhat.com, version 0.1.0, under the MIT licence. It is free and asks for no sign-up."},{"p":"I have no user counts, feedback or measured success rate for extraction, and no results from a set of real-world decks. These limits are known:"},{"list":["Gradients come back as flat colour.","Scanned pages have no text to recover; they stay pictures.","Very dense artwork, such as maps, stays a picture instead of shapes.","Rotated text is handled line by line, not as a paragraph.","Magic Move does not carry into the web export, where it becomes a crossfade.","Direct pairing carries one phone. A second phone falls back to the mailbox.","Video and GIFs travel inside the project file, up to 48 MB each."]},{"p":"The screens on this page come from a built copy on my machine and my own test file, so details may differ slightly from the live site."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Test it on hard decks, then widen what it can export","blocks":[{"list":["Find out why the cover of my test file failed to rebuild.","Proposed: import a fixed set of decks, including clean text, scanned, multi-column and dense vector pages, and record how editable and how faithful each one is, separately.","Planned and not built: Magic Move in the web export, motion paths, a relay server so direct pairing works across networks, trimming a recording inside the app, editing with others at the same time, and ready-animated slide templates."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A guess is fine when it shows that it is a guess","blocks":[{"p":"Taking a PDF apart is guesswork. The PDF never says \"this is a title\" or \"these six bars are a chart\". What made the guesses useful was showing them: a named role on every layer, a note when a page fails, a panel that says nothing can be edited here."},{"p":"The same rule held outside extraction. The pairing panel names the missing service instead of spinning. The animation panel says which places wait for a press. And a clean six-page test file of my own still turned up a failed cover, a lost word gap and a wrong role. Next time I would start with the hardest decks I can find."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","headline":"Family connections with a source to follow."}},"vansh-vriksha":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","kind":"product","headline":"An open family tree where every record says where it came from.","summary":"Vansh Vriksha is a free public family-tree archive that I designed and built on my own, where anyone can add a whole family without an account and every record says what it rests on.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working archive","context":"Community knowledge · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Graph","label":"relationship model"},{"value":"Provenance","label":"per-record intent"},{"value":"Reviewed","label":"contribution workflow"}],"problem":"A family archive is not simply a diagram of names. Relationships can be disputed, spellings vary, and historical records represent some people much better than others. Vansh Vriksha is structured around exploring those connections while preserving the basis for a record.","process":["I treated the archive as a set of connected records with several entry points: a person, a family, a village, or a relationship. The UX should help someone follow a connection without losing its source.","The demonstration content lets the experience be explored; it is not verified family evidence. Missing records, uncertain relationships, and contribution review need visible explanations."],"decisions":[{"title":"Model relationships beyond a simple tree","choice":"The graph includes family unions and parent links rather than forcing every connection into one hierarchy.","reason":"A plain tree can misrepresent remarriage and half-sibling relationships. The data model needs to preserve the actual relationship.","tradeoff":"A more faithful model is harder to lay out and explain, especially on small screens."},{"title":"Keep a record’s basis alongside the record","choice":"The documented model includes provenance and versioned family files.","reason":"Someone reading a family claim should have a way to understand where it came from and how it changed.","tradeoff":"A source field does not guarantee accuracy. Disagreement and corrections still need a human review process."},{"title":"Separate contribution from publication","choice":"Visitors can submit without an account, while a maintainer reviews additions.","reason":"A low-friction contribution path can coexist with an accountable published archive.","tradeoff":"Moderation becomes an operational commitment; the interface should communicate what happens while a submission waits."}],"outcome":"I designed relationship browsing, record context and contribution review as connected parts of the archive. A person can explore a connection and inspect the basis for it. The demonstration records show the interaction; they do not establish verified family histories.","reflection":"I would make gaps in the archive easier to recognise. A missing branch can mean incomplete records, so it should not quietly look like a statement about which families or people matter.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask a family contributor to submit a relationship, inspect its provenance, and correct a disputed connection.","Proposed review: distinguish demonstration records from sourced records in every public count and assess living-person privacy across search and sharing."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/archive.ts","detail":"Versioned record storage, graph exploration, moderation, demonstration data and known biases. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://vanshvriksha.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":14,"pdfPage":30,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Family connections [[with a source to follow.]]","scene":"Imagine recognising a relative in an archive and wanting to know how the relationship was recorded. The diagram can show a connection, but it also needs a basis you can inspect.","question":"How can an archive invite contribution without pretending every claim is settled?","insight":"[[Provenance belongs with the relationship]]. A family graph, versioned records, and review make different parts of that promise concrete.","pivot":"I connected exploration with source-aware records and a contribution path that separates submitting from publishing.","takeaway":"An archive should show what it knows and how it came to know it.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Follow the connections through the archive."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the basis for a record."},{"title":"Contribute","detail":"Submit a correction or a family link."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Publish after a maintainer’s review."}],"decisionLeads":["Model the relationship faithfully.","Keep the basis of a claim close.","Let a contribution wait for review."],"annotations":[{"title":"Multiple ways into a family history","detail":"The public experience offers search and relationship exploration."},{"title":"Coverage stays conditional","detail":"Demonstration records and historical coverage limits need context."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · graph and review model","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Family member exploring a connection","context":"Following names and relationships with incomplete historical information.","need":"Understand a relationship and the evidence behind it.","friction":"A convincing diagram can imply certainty or completeness.","implication":"Keep sources and contribution review near the record."}},"theme":{"layout":"archive","mode":"light","paper":"#fbf6ec","ink":"#37271f","dim":"#78664e","line":"#ddcfb6","accent":"#a8322a","wash":"#f6e2dc","bodyFont":"Archivo","displayFont":"Newsreader","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Following a family connection","process":"A connection needs a source","decisions":"Designing a careful archive","outcome":"The record and its relationships","reflection":"What the archive leaves out"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the evidence behind a relationship visible.","resultHeading":"A family connection with its context intact."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","alt":"Vansh Vriksha — archived product interface","caption":"The original archive interface introduces people, families and the connections between records.","label":"Archive interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"An open family tree where [[every record says where it came from]].","hook":"Vansh Vriksha is a free public family-tree archive that I designed and built on my own, where anyone can add a whole family without an account and every record says what it rests on.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"August to October 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"Families tracing their lineage, free to read and add to"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on desktop and phone"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Next.js 15, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, Zod, Netlify Blobs"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live, running on generated demonstration data. No real-family or usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Open Vansh Vriksha","url":"https://vanshvriksha.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"See it yourself","summary":["The problem: families keep their history in pieces, and an open tree goes wrong in quiet ways. One ancestor gets added twice, four spellings of a name never meet, and a wrong parent link is believed because it is written down.","What I built: a public archive with a family-tree viewer, a search that understands spelling, a relationship finder in English and Konkani, and a way to add a whole family at once that waits for review.","The key idea: a claim is only as good as what it rests on. Every person carries a source mark, every change is kept with an author, a date and a reason, and nothing is published without a person reading it.","Where it stands: live, running on a generated set of made-up people. It does not yet establish any real family counts, and I have no usage numbers to share."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/home.webp","alt":"The Vansh Vriksha home page: a red notice saying the data is a demonstration, the headline 'One tree, with room on it for every family', and one large search box","caption":"The home page, captured from the built site. The red bar at the top says every person shown is fictional. Example searches under the box are blurred because some name real families.","width":2400,"height":1272},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Families remember their history in pieces, and the joins go first","blocks":[{"p":"Most families can name four generations without help. The fifth is often just a first name and a village. After that, the proof that two branches now in different countries were once one household is gone."},{"p":"But families that lose names usually keep four other things: a surname, a gotra (the ancestral sage a family line descends from, passed from father to child), a family deity and a home village. I built the archive around those four, so they work as ways in when the names run out."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/ways-in.webp","alt":"The home page section 'Four things a family tends to remember', with rows for surname, gotra, family deity and home village, each with a short explanation","caption":"The four ways in, from the home page. Each row says why that field survives. The counts on the right are blurred because they come from made-up data.","width":1600,"height":767}]},{"p":"I wanted one public tree that anyone could read and add to, for free and without an account. An open tree like that has three ways to go wrong, and each one shaped a part of the design."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/problems.webp","alt":"Three panels: one ancestor recorded twice with descendants split between the copies; four spellings of one name; a wrong parent link that moves a person into another family","caption":"The three risks, drawn from the product's own documentation. The rest of this page is mostly about how the design answers them.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I have no interview notes or feedback from families to show, so this page quotes none."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Three doors into one archive: read, add and review","blocks":[{"p":"Reading needs no account. Adding needs no account either, but what you add goes to a queue. One maintainer, behind a sign-in, decides what is published."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/site-map.webp","alt":"A map in three columns: nine pages for reading, the contribute form with six kinds of submission, and four maintainer tools","caption":"Every part of the product, grouped by who uses it. Read the middle column as the bridge: anything added waits in the queue.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"p":"The home page asks one question: what is the one name you are sure of?"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/annotated-home.webp","alt":"The home page with five numbered notes: the demonstration notice, the Contribute button, the search box, the blurred example searches and three links","caption":"Start at note 3: one search box takes a name, a surname, a gotra or a village, because different families remember different things.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"A record backed by a register and a record backed by a grandmother's memory are both worth keeping. What would ruin the archive is not being able to tell them apart, so every person carries one of four marks."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/source-marks.webp","alt":"The About page section 'What counts as a source', listing four marks: documented, corroborated, family memory and unverified, each with a short definition","caption":"The four source marks, as the site explains them. Look at the last one: an unverified record is kept, but flagged so nobody builds a branch on it.","width":1600,"height":709}]},{"p":"The relationship finder answers \"how am I related to…?\" in English and in Konkani, the language these families speak at home. English is vague in exactly the places Konkani is precise."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/kin-terms.webp","alt":"Diagram: the English words uncle and aunt split into five Konkani words for father's elder brother, father's younger brother, father's sister, mother's brother and mother's sister","caption":"Where English says uncle or aunt, Konkani has five words, and each carries a different role. The finder works out the path between two people, then names it in both languages.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"A diaspora page shows where families moved, generation by generation."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/annotated-diaspora.webp","alt":"The 'Births by generation' chart: stacked bars for each twenty-year span, coloured by region of birth, with the totals blurred and three numbered notes","caption":"Read the colours left to right as a route: home region, down the coast, the cities, then abroad. The totals are blurred because the people behind them are generated.","width":1600,"height":860}]}]},{"id":"couples","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Couples, not parents","heading":"Children hang from a couple, not from one parent","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a couple sits side by side, a small junction sits below them, and their children hang from the junction. A second marriage gets its own junction and its own set of children."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/union-before-after.webp","alt":"Before and after: a plain tree where four children hang from one man, and a couple layout where two marriages each have their own junction and children","caption":"Why it matters: in a plain tree, half-brothers and half-sisters look like full ones. That is the difference families care about most.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"In the build: genealogy is not really a tree, because a person has two parents and can marry more than once. So the layout works on couples. It measures how wide each branch needs to be, then places it inside that width, so two cards can never overlap. There is no clean-up pass afterwards."},{"p":"The layout code uses nothing from React or the server. The same code runs in the browser, so re-centring the tree on another person happens at once, with no wait for the server."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/tree-annotated.webp","alt":"A drawing of the tree viewer with eight numbered notes: four view modes, a daughters' lines chip, a PDF button, cards, a third line on one card, the marriage junction, tabs that open more layers, and a key","caption":"The viewer opened on one household. Notes 4 and 8 work together: what the whole family shares is said once in the key, so each card can stay short.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"The cards themselves were too small at first. At 190 by 78 pixels a name was cut at about 14 letters, which cuts most names here in half. I made them 244 by 96 and set an order for shortening a name, so the surname is the last thing to go."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/name-fit.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing the old and new card sizes, then four steps for shortening a name: full, father's name as an initial, father's name dropped, and an ellipsis last","caption":"Look at the underline: the surname is the part a reader scans for, so every step protects it.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"In these families a daughter's children are recorded with her husband's family. So by default the tree draws her husband and children, and puts her grandchildren on a tab. I ruled out drawing every line in full, because by the third generation that grafts several other families' trees into this one. It is a reading convention, not a claim about who matters, and one chip turns it off."}}]},{"id":"search","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Spelling-tolerant search","heading":"Four spellings of one name fold into one search key","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: type any spelling of a name and the others turn up too."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/spelling-fold.webp","alt":"Diagram: four spellings of one name fold to the key srinivas, two spellings of another fold to laksmi, beside the seven folding rules in order","caption":"The four spellings are the product's own example. The seven rules on the right only cover the ways these names really vary when written in English letters.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"p":"In the build: each name is stored twice in the search index, once as written and once as its folded key. A search then allows up to two letters of difference on top, and ranks those near misses lower. Some surnames split into two spellings through different colonial spelling systems rather than through speech. Folding cannot join those, so they are linked by hand."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Loose matching for new names people type into the lists. A fuzzy pass once turned one village's name into a city in another state, and nobody was told. New names now match only on an exact key, and a looser guess is shown to the maintainer as a suggestion instead."}}]},{"id":"duplicates","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Duplicates and whole families","heading":"A likely duplicate shows its reasons and offers to link, while you type","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: as you type a name, a box appears under the fields if the person may already be in the archive. It lists the reasons and offers one button: this is the same person."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/match-annotated.webp","alt":"A drawing of the duplicate warning with five numbered notes: it runs while typing, a strong match gets a firmer heading, one click dismisses it, it shows reasons instead of a score, and it offers a link","caption":"Start at note 4. A person can check \"father is Person B\" against what they know. Nobody can check a percentage.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: the first version warned about duplicates on the thank-you screen, after the person had finished. That was the wrong moment. The merge had become a reviewer's problem, when it could have been a two-second choice by the one person who knew."},{"p":"The check compares every name a record carries, including nicknames and the name in Devanagari script, and says which one matched. Family fields count too. A matching gotra is evidence for a match. A different gotra is strong evidence against, because it passes from father to child and never changes."},{"p":"The most common real contribution is not one person. It is a couple, their children, and who each child married, learned in one afternoon with an elderly relative. Sent one at a time, that is many forms, and none can name a parent who has not been published yet. So a whole family can be built on a drawing and sent as one piece."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/builder-annotated.webp","alt":"A drawing of the family builder with six numbered notes: relationship buttons around the selected card, a child button under the couple, a linked existing person, the form beside the drawing, a list that accepts typed answers, and one send button","caption":"The builder. Note 2 is the important one: a child is added from under a couple, so it can never be attached to the wrong marriage.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"In the build: the people in a draft get temporary keys. On sending, every key becomes a real record in one pass and one write. The builder refuses true contradictions, such as a child born before their father. It only warns about unlikely ones, because real families produce records that look wrong and are not."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The builder is a bigger thing to learn than a single form. In return, a relationship is stated by where you add someone, so a link cannot be left half-entered."}}]},{"id":"review","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Review and history","heading":"Records live in version control, and nothing publishes itself","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: after sending, a contribution waits. A maintainer can approve it, decline it, or ask for changes. Asking is the option most review queues lack, and often the only useful reply to a real memory with a date nobody can place."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/review-flow.webp","alt":"Flow of four steps: someone sends a submission, it waits in a queue, a maintainer approves, asks for changes or declines, and approval becomes a commit with an author, date and reason","caption":"From sending to published. Step 4 is the design promise: every change keeps who made it, when, and why.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"In the build: the records are plain JSON files kept in git, not rows in a database. When two cousins disagree about a birth year in twenty years' time, the history will show who claimed what and on what basis."},{"p":"That promise broke when the site moved to a serverless host, where files written on the server vanish on the next restart. So a maintainer's approval is now committed to the repository on GitHub, and the push rebuilds the site. The queue of unreviewed submissions lives in separate storage, because it does not need that history until someone has read it."},{"p":"A check reads the whole family graph before every build. Some mistakes are invisible in a single record and obvious across the graph."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/data-check.webp","alt":"Two lists: errors that stop the build, such as broken links, impossible dates and ancestry loops, and warnings that are only reported, such as very long lives","caption":"Errors stop the site from being built. Warnings do not, because unusual is not the same as wrong.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"With no database, an approved change reaches everyone only after a rebuild, which takes a couple of minutes. The maintainer sees their own change at once."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"No database, one graph in memory, and a check that blocks a broken archive","blocks":[{"p":"When the site starts, the whole archive is read into memory as one graph. Every interesting question, such as six generations of descendants or the path between two people, is a walk through that graph rather than a series of database queries."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: stored family records, reference lists and photo storage; the in-memory graph, search index and data check; and pre-built pages, the tree viewer and open JSON endpoints","caption":"What is stored, what happens when the site starts, and what is served. The dashed box at the bottom is how an approved change gets back in.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Next.js 15 with the App Router, React 19, TypeScript"],["Styling","Tailwind CSS 4. The tree, the charts and the ornaments are hand-written SVG"],["Records","JSON files in git, checked before every build"],["Queue and photos","Netlify Blobs"],["Input checking","Zod, on what contributors send and what the maintainer saves"],["Data libraries","None: no database, no state library, no chart library, no component library"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"13","label":"commits, August to October 2026, one author"},{"value":"27,700","label":"lines of TypeScript, roughly"},{"value":"0","label":"automated tests. The data check is the only gate"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of commits: 2 August 1, 3 August 6, 4 August 3, 5 August 1, 9 August 1, 14 October 1, each with its main changes","caption":"Most of the work happened in the first week of August. October brought one fix for caching and search crawlers.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were not the drawing of the tree."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Saving on a serverless host","Approvals were written to a disk the host wipes on restart, so they were silently lost. They are now committed to GitHub. Raw GitHub errors also reached people's screens; every write path now catches them and shows a plain message."],["The family builder","A \"child\" button under someone's parents added a brother instead of a son: the very confusion the couple layout exists to prevent. Edits made from a wife's side of a marriage were dropped. Both are fixed, and the builder now offers only the marriages the selected person is in."],["Approvals","Approving a story about a person could reset their recorded sex, because a missing field fell back to a default. Now a field a submission does not mention is left alone."],["Speed","The server was fast; the wait was elsewhere. Fonts came from another site and blocked the first paint, and every link in view woke the server. Fonts are now served from the site itself, prefetching is off for on-demand pages, and the diaspora page's photos went from 3.6 MB to 1.2 MB."]]}}]},{"id":"privacy","label":"Privacy and accessibility","nav":"Privacy and accessibility","heading":"Public on purpose, so the risks had to be designed for","blocks":[{"p":"Everything in the archive is public and found by search engines. That is the point, and it is also the risk. The rules for living people are short and stated on the site."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/living-people.webp","alt":"The About page section 'Living people', listing four rules: ask before adding a living person, never submit addresses or phone numbers, anyone can be reduced to a name or removed, children are a name and a parent link only","caption":"The rules as the site states them. They are applied in review by a person, not by code, which is why they are written for people to read.","width":1600,"height":618}]},{"p":"Photos needed one rule enforced by code. A phone photo carries hidden details, including where it was taken."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/photo-privacy.webp","alt":"Flow of four steps: a phone photo with hidden location data, redrawn in the browser at 1600 pixels which drops that data, stored at an address the archive owns, and attached only on approval","caption":"Step 2 is a privacy fix as much as a size one. An archive that quietly published where a photo was taken could reveal a family's home.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"Accessibility: I ran an audit over the whole site and fixed what it found. Dialogs now hold keyboard focus and give it back when they close. The tree's view switch works from the keyboard as a proper set of options. Gender is no longer shown by colour alone. Heading order, table headers and language tags were corrected, and everything pressable is at least 48 pixels."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are fixes from my own audit. I have not tested the archive with people who use screen readers."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, on made-up people, with no real-family numbers","blocks":[{"p":"The site is live. It runs on a generated demonstration dataset: every person in it is fictional. The reference lists around them, such as gotras, temples and villages, are real. A notice on every page says so, and it removes itself when the first real record arrives."},{"p":"So nothing here establishes how many real families the archive holds, and I have no usage numbers to share. The site is also honest about what any family archive misses."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/vansh-vriksha/biases.webp","alt":"The About page section 'What this record is biased towards', with three parts: it follows sons, it follows literacy and money, it follows whoever is online now","caption":"The three biases, stated on the site itself. The demonstration data copies the first one on purpose rather than pretending to a balance real records do not have.","width":1600,"height":909}]},{"list":["There are no automated tests. The data check guards the records, not the interface.","The rate limits are kept in memory per server, so they stop casual misuse, not a determined attacker.","Several family deities still have no freely licensed photograph."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Real records, then real contributors","blocks":[{"list":["Load real records. Importers for family-tree software files and spreadsheets already exist, and they report anything they cannot match instead of guessing.","Proposed test: ask a family contributor to send a relationship, look at what it rests on, and correct a disputed link.","Proposed review: tell made-up and sourced records apart in every public count, and check living people's privacy across search and sharing."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"The family's way of counting is a design input","blocks":[{"p":"The worst bugs I found were the product's own problem, handed back as a button: a child added to the wrong marriage, a wife's side of a marriage that could not be edited. Each time, the fix was to follow how these families actually count relationships, not how a tree diagram usually works."},{"p":"I also learned to put a warning where someone can still act on it. The duplicate check was useless on the thank-you screen and useful under the name field."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","headline":"A bank message is not always a clean transaction."}},"premoney":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","kind":"product","headline":"Bank texts turned into a spending list that says when it is unsure.","summary":"PreMoney is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It reads the bank alerts already on my phone and turns them into a list of payments I can check and correct.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Interactive app","context":"Personal finance · Working product","facts":[{"value":"SMS","label":"transaction input"},{"value":"Confidence","label":"visible in parser model"},{"value":"Review","label":"unresolved merchants"}],"problem":"Manual expense entry asks people to repeat information already present in transaction alerts. PreMoney explores a more useful starting point: extract likely transactions from messages, then make uncertainty and correction part of the workflow.","process":["I framed the core journey as evidence, suggested transaction, review, correction, and summary. A person should be able to understand why an alert became a record and recover when the interpretation is wrong.","Privacy explanations belong alongside optional categorisation and synchronisation. The experience needs to make those choices understandable without relying on a blanket claim that all processing stays on the device."],"decisions":[{"title":"Prefer an uncertain result to a fabricated detail","choice":"The parser lowers confidence when a field is uncertain and records when a merchant is unresolved.","reason":"A plausible but incorrect financial record can be harder to notice than a clear request for review.","tradeoff":"Conservative parsing can miss legitimate alerts. The product needs a recoverable route for false rejections."},{"title":"Make correction useful beyond a single transaction","choice":"Transaction editing and review make suggested categories and corrections inspectable.","reason":"Repeated mistakes should become easier to diagnose than manually editing the same pattern forever.","tradeoff":"A learned rule can also propagate a wrong assumption, so its effect must remain inspectable."},{"title":"Treat enrichment as a separate data decision","choice":"Optional categorisation and synchronisation need a separate explanation from reading a transaction alert.","reason":"People need an understandable account of which data is used by which feature.","tradeoff":"The product documentation describes external processing paths; disclosures and controls need review before making privacy promises."}],"outcome":"I designed a route from the original message to a suggested transaction, correction and summary. The important part is being able to inspect an entry’s origin and fix it when the interpretation is wrong. Accuracy needs to be evaluated against a defined set of messages.","reflection":"I would start with a wrong amount, a duplicate alert and a message that is not a transaction. If correcting those cases is difficult, adding more charts will not make the financial record more dependable.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: use a consented, redacted alert corpus to measure false accepts, false rejects, duplicate errors, and corrections by bank format.","Proposed release review: document the external-processing and sync paths, then test whether a person understands and controls them."],"sources":[{"label":"lib/domain/sms/sms_parser.dart and lib/domain/categorize","detail":"Confidence model, rejection reasons and merchant categorisation."},{"label":"lib/domain/ai/groq_service.dart and lib/ui/settings/sms_sandbox_sheet.dart","detail":"External enrichment payload and diagnostic review surface. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"PreMoney 1.13.0 release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026. No bank messages or personal transactions."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","alt":"PreMoney actual Android dashboard showing its empty state and insufficient-data scores","caption":"Actual Android dashboard with no transactions. Scores stay unavailable until there is enough information; SMS access was skipped for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PreMoney-releases","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":14,"pdfPage":33,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A bank message [[is not always a clean transaction.]]","scene":"Imagine an expense app reading a bank alert. Automation has saved an entry—but if it guessed the amount or merchant, the summary now looks more certain than its input.","question":"How should automatic tracking admit that it is unsure?","insight":"[[A reviewable uncertainty is useful information]]. Confidence, rejection reasons, and unresolved merchants give correction a place in the workflow.","pivot":"I framed the journey around inspecting and correcting a suggested transaction, with separate explanations for optional categorisation and sync.","takeaway":"Automation earns trust by making its uncertainty correctable.","journey":[{"title":"Read","detail":"Distinguish an account movement from other alerts."},{"title":"Flag","detail":"Keep uncertainty in the transaction model."},{"title":"Correct","detail":"Inspect the origin and improve the record."}],"decisionLeads":["Leave uncertainty visible.","Make a correction useful beyond one row.","Explain enrichment as its own data decision."],"annotations":[{"title":"Confidence is a design input","detail":"Ambiguous transaction alerts need an explicit review path before a record feels trustworthy."},{"title":"Correction has a home","detail":"The app includes transaction editing and review."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · transaction confidence and review","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person reviewing everyday spending","context":"Using transaction alerts to understand money movement.","need":"Trust the record enough to review and correct it.","friction":"An ambiguous alert can become a plausible but incorrect transaction.","implication":"Make uncertainty visible and correction recoverable."}},"theme":{"layout":"statement","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f6f3","ink":"#161a18","dim":"#59665e","line":"#d4ded6","accent":"#16745f","wash":"#dcefe8","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A record you need to trust","process":"Uncertainty should stay visible","decisions":"Review before confidence","outcome":"The transaction journey","reflection":"Evaluating the mistakes"},"decisionHeading":"Make uncertain transactions easy to review.","resultHeading":"A suggested transaction, open to review."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"PreMoney actual Android dashboard showing its empty state and insufficient-data scores","caption":"Actual Android dashboard with no transactions. Scores stay unavailable until there is enough information; SMS access was skipped for this capture.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"PreMoney 1.13.0 release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026. No bank messages or personal transactions.","width":1080,"height":1920},"v2":{"title":"Bank texts turned into a spending list that [[says when it is unsure]].","hook":"PreMoney is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It reads the bank alerts already on my phone and turns them into a list of payments I can check and correct.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A spending log without retyping what my bank texts me"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July to August 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android only. iPhones do not let apps read texts"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Flutter, Dart, Riverpod, SQLite"},{"label":"Status","value":"Version 1.17.0, a personal tool. Not on Google Play"}],"links":[{"label":"Get the Android app","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PreMoney-releases"}],"linksHeading":"Release page","summary":["The problem: my bank already texts me about every payment, but those texts also include OTPs, adverts and statements. A wrong number in a money app is worse than no number.","What I built: an Android app that reads bank alerts, asks each one for proof that money moved, and saves it once. Anything it cannot read cleanly goes to a review queue instead of being guessed.","The key idea: when the app is unsure, it says so. One correction from me then fixes every entry of the same kind, past and future.","Where it stands: version 1.17.0, built over six days in July and August 2026. It is a personal tool. I have no accuracy figures from real messages and no other users."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/message-to-entry.webp","alt":"Schematic of a made-up bank alert on the left, three questions the app asks in the middle, and the saved entry on the right showing a cafe payment of 240 rupees","caption":"A schematic drawn from the app's code, with a made-up message. The app reads the alert on the left, asks the three questions in the middle and saves the entry on the right.","width":1600,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"My bank already tells me what I spend, mixed in with everything else","blocks":[{"p":"Every UPI or card payment I make sends me a text. Together those texts are almost a full spending log. Typing the same payments into an expense app felt like doing the work twice."},{"p":"The catch is that most texts from a bank are not payments. OTPs quote amounts. Card adverts use the word \"credit\". Statements quote totals and due dates. A failed payment still says \"debited\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/inbox-mix.webp","alt":"Six made-up bank messages in a grid: one real payment marked Entry, and an OTP, a card advert, a statement, a failed payment and a payment request each marked Skip with the reason","caption":"The problem in one picture. Six made-up messages from one bank. Only the first moved money, and every other one quotes an amount.","width":1600,"height":960}]},{"p":"So the design question was not how to read a payment. It was how to tell a payment from everything that looks like one, and what to do when the app cannot tell."},{"aside":{"title":"Who it is for.","detail":"Me. It is a personal tool for one person on one Android phone. I did no user research, and this page does not claim any."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Four ways in, one path through, three ways out","blocks":[{"p":"A bank alert can reach the app four ways. It can be found in a scan of the inbox, caught live while the app is open, caught in the background while it is closed, or shared in from another app. All four go through the same steps."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/pipeline.webp","alt":"Flow diagram: four ways in (inbox scan, live listener, background capture, share) lead through a sender check, learned rules, the parser and the categoriser, to three outcomes: saved once, review queue, or not saved","caption":"How a message travels. Look at the right-hand column: every message ends in exactly one of three places.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"p":"The app has four tabs: Home, Activity, Insights and Goals. The tools for checking the parser sit under Settings."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/screen-map.webp","alt":"Map of the app: four bottom tabs with their contents, the four first-run screens, and three groups of settings, with Activity and the Tools group highlighted","caption":"The structure. The highlighted parts, the Activity list and the tools in Settings, are where checking and correcting happen.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"On first run the app explains why it wants to read messages before Android asks for permission. It then reads up to a year of bank alerts and says how many it added and how many it skipped. Cash, and anything the messages missed, I add by hand."}]},{"id":"evidence","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Proof, not a block list","heading":"Ask each message for proof that money moved","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the advert does not appear. Nothing in the list asks me to clean up a payment that never happened."},{"p":"In the build: the first parser accepted anything that a list of blocked words did not catch. That was the wrong default. Bank adverts come from the same senders as real alerts, quote rupee amounts and talk about credit cards. The word \"credit\" was read as money coming in."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/phantom-income.webp","alt":"Before and after comparison of the same made-up insurance advert: before, the word credit and the cover amount turned it into fifty lakh rupees of income; after, the card name is blanked, the offer figure is ignored and it is not saved","caption":"The failure that led to the rewrite in version 1.17. My project notes record an insurance advert quoting a cover of ₹50 lakh that was saved as a ₹50 lakh deposit.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Every new advert wording leaked through until I added another blocked word. So in version 1.17 I turned the question around. The parser now asks what a bank leaves behind on a real alert, things an advert has no reason to carry."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/gates.webp","alt":"Five numbered checks in order: never a payment, advert or request, which way money went, how much, and enough proof, each with what it rejects, followed by Accepted","caption":"The five checks. Look at check 1: an OTP is rejected at any level of proof, because it often quotes the amount of the payment it protects.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The last check scores proof. A masked account number, a reference number, the balance afterwards and a \"Not you?\" line each count. The more advert wording a message has, the more of that proof it needs."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/evidence-scale.webp","alt":"Table of points for each mark of a real alert, bars showing that 0 advert tells need 2 marks, 1 or 2 need 4 and 3 or more need 6, and two worked made-up examples","caption":"The proof test. The points and thresholds are the parser's own. The two examples are made up and scored by hand, as an illustration, not a measured result.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"p":"The order of the checks matters, and tests hold it in place. Advert checks run before direction, so the message tester reports an advert as an advert. One test file has a simple rule: every advert in it must be rejected, and every genuine alert must be accepted with the right amount and direction."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Asking for proof can turn away a real payment that arrives with very little detail. For that case a kind of message can be marked \"always import\", which lowers the bar. It never lets an OTP or a failed payment through."}}]},{"id":"unsure","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Unsure, not invented","heading":"When a field cannot be read, lower the confidence and ask","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: an entry the app is unsure about gets a small warning mark, and Home tells me how many are waiting. A merchant it could not read shows as \"UPI Payment\" rather than a made-up name."},{"p":"In the build: each accepted message gets a confidence score. It starts at 100% and drops for each field the parser could not read. The entry goes to the review queue if the merchant has no name or the category is only a guess."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/confidence.webp","alt":"Left: confidence starts at 100 percent and drops 35 for no merchant, 15 for no account digits, 5 for no reference and 15 for advert wording. Right: an entry goes to review if the merchant could not be named or the category is a guess","caption":"How unsure the app is, and what that triggers. The 60% example is made up, worked out from the code's own deductions.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"I did not build a separate review screen. The queue is the Activity list with one filter switched on, so there is nothing new to learn."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/annotated-review.webp","alt":"Schematic of the Home nudge saying 3 transactions need a category, and the Activity list filtered to Needs a category, with six numbered notes","caption":"The review queue, drawn from the code with made-up entries. Note 5 is the point: a placeholder name says what the app does not know.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"Opening an entry shows where it came from. The original message is one tap away, so I can check the app against the bank's own words."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/annotated-detail.webp","alt":"Schematic of an entry's detail sheet for a made-up cafe payment, showing a review banner, the category with a Change button, the source, a transfer switch, the original message and Edit and Delete buttons, with six numbered notes","caption":"An entry in review, drawn from the code with a made-up payment. Start at note 1: the banner says why the entry is here and what fixing it will do.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Filling the gap with a best guess, such as the nearest merchant name in the message. A plausible wrong entry is harder to notice than an honest \"UPI Payment\". The cost is a longer queue on day one."}},{"aside":{"title":"A gap I found while writing this.","detail":"The confidence score is saved with every entry, but only the message tester shows it. The list shows a yes-or-no review flag instead."}}]},{"id":"teach","label":"Decision 3","nav":"One fix, many entries","heading":"One correction should fix every entry of the same kind","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: when I change a category, a switch says \"Remember this for\" the merchant. It is on by default. When I delete an entry made from a message, the app asks whether it was a real transaction at all."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/annotated-teach.webp","alt":"Two schematics side by side: the category picker for a made-up cafe with the Remember switch on, and the delete dialog with This wasn't a real transaction ticked, each with the confirmation message shown below","caption":"The two teaching moments, drawn from the code with made-up entries. The text under each phone is the confirmation the app shows afterwards.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"p":"In the build: a category correction becomes a rule for that merchant. My rules run before the app's 330 or so built-in merchant patterns. The same action refiles every past entry from that merchant."},{"p":"Blocking a message works on its template, not its exact text. The app blanks out the amounts, dates and reference numbers, and what is left is the wording the sender reuses. One tap can clear months of entries that a single advert campaign created."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/template-skeleton.webp","alt":"Two made-up advert messages reduced to one shared skeleton with amounts, references and dates replaced by hash marks, and three rules for deciding whether a new message is the same template","caption":"How \"messages like this\" is decided. The messages are made up. The 0.82 threshold and the merchant rule come from the app's code.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Two messages count as the same template if their skeletons match, or if 82% or more of their words overlap. I set that bar high on purpose. A false match would quietly throw away a real payment, which is worse than having to block a reworded advert twice."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A rule can spread a mistake as fast as it spreads a fix. So every rule, for categories and for messages, is listed in Settings and can be removed."}}]},{"id":"tester","label":"Decision 4","nav":"A tester that says why","heading":"A message tester that explains every decision","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: under Settings there is a page where I can paste any message and see what the parser makes of it. A rejection shows which check stopped it, plus the proof score and the advert score."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/annotated-sandbox.webp","alt":"Schematic of the message tester twice: a made-up card advert rejected as promotional with evidence 1 and marketing signals 5, and a made-up UPI payment parsed at 60 percent confidence with each field listed","caption":"The message tester, drawn from the code. The scores for these made-up messages are worked out by hand, as an illustration.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"p":"In the build: every rejection carries a reason, and every result carries both scores. My rule for a bank that changes its wording is to add the new message to the tests first, then fix the patterns. The tester shows which check a message trips without a debugger."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"This screen is for me, not for a first-time user. It shows the code's own names for reasons, such as \"promotional\". The app has plain-English versions of those reasons, and this screen does not use them yet."}}]},{"id":"scores","label":"Decision 5","nav":"Scores that wait for data","heading":"A score with missing data says so, instead of failing me","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Home has two scores, a Saver Score and a Spender Score. With no income recorded, both say \"Not enough data\", and a hint says what to add."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/annotated-dashboard.webp","alt":"Real screenshot of the PreMoney home screen with no data, showing an empty state and two score gauges reading Not enough data, with six numbered notes","caption":"A real screen from version 1.13.0 on an Android emulator, with message access skipped. Note 4: no zero that would read as a failing grade.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"p":"In the build: each score is made of parts, and each part has a stated target, such as saving 30% of income or keeping six months of costs in reserve. If a part cannot be measured, it is marked unavailable and the total is rescaled from the parts that can."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/scores.webp","alt":"Two cards listing the parts of the Saver Score with targets of 30 percent savings, 15 percent invested and 6 months runway, and the four parts of the Spender Score, above the rule for missing input","caption":"The two scores. The Saver Score looks back six months, so one late salary cannot swing it. The Spender Score looks at about 90 days.","width":1600,"height":780}]},{"p":"The Spender Score describes how I spend, it does not grade me. Investments count as savings, not spending, and moving money between my own accounts counts as neither."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Fitting the scores to a curve. Stated targets are easier to argue with and to act on. The cost is that the targets are mine, and they may not suit someone else."}}]},{"id":"privacy","label":"What leaves the phone","nav":"What leaves the phone","heading":"Reading is local, but three optional features send data out","blocks":[{"p":"The parser, the categoriser and the database all run on the phone. Even the font is bundled, so the app works with no network at all."},{"p":"Three later features change that, and I want to be exact about them. None of them runs until I use it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/data-paths.webp","alt":"Two zones: on the phone always (parsing, the database, fonts, export) and leaves the phone only when used (AI category suggestions, cloud backup, update check), each labelled with when it happens","caption":"What stays and what can leave. The AI step and the backup both include the original message text.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"list":["AI suggestions: a button in the review queue sends each waiting entry's merchant, amount and original message to Groq, an outside AI service, and asks for a category.","Backup: after I sign in, entries, including the original message, and my rules, budgets and goals are copied to Firebase.","Updates: the app reads a public version file and downloads the new app when there is one."]},{"aside":{"title":"What needs fixing.","detail":"The first-run screens still say everything stays on the phone. That was true when I wrote them, before backup and AI suggestions existed. So I make no such promise here, and that copy has to change before anyone else uses the app."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Screens never touch the database, and one number refreshes them all","blocks":[{"p":"Screens talk to Riverpod providers, providers call the domain logic, and only the repositories write SQL. After any write, one \"data version\" number goes up and every view that depends on data reloads. Nearly every write changes several screens, and listing them by hand at each call would have been a steady source of stale screens."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/architecture.webp","alt":"Layer diagram: screens, state, domain logic, repositories and SQLite, with background capture, the Android side and optional services drawn alongside","caption":"How the app is put together. The unique key at the bottom is what lets four routes save messages without creating doubles.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Flutter and Dart, Android only"],["State","Riverpod, with one data-version number"],["Storage","SQLite on the phone, hand-written SQL, no code generation"],["Messages","another_telephony for the inbox and live alerts"],["Charts","fl_chart, with an eight-colour palette checked for colour blindness"],["Optional services","Firebase for sign-in and backup, Groq for AI suggestions"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"22","label":"commits, 28 July to 2 August 2026"},{"value":"76","label":"Dart files, about 19,800 lines"},{"value":"9","label":"test files, for the parser, adverts, duplicates, templates, categories, scores, export and sharing"},{"value":"177 of 184","label":"test messages imported in an emulator run at version 1.6, with all 7 non-payments rejected"}]},{"p":"That last number is a check, not an accuracy figure. The messages were test messages I sent to an emulator, and the run was before the parser was rewritten in version 1.17."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/premoney/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of three days of commits: 28 July with versions 1.0 to 1.5, 29 July with versions 1.6 to 1.16, and 2 August with version 1.17","caption":"The build by day. Most commits raised the version number by one, so the history reads as a list of releases.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were the quiet ones, where nothing showed an error."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Live capture","It never fired, in front or in the background, and logged nothing. The message plugin does not declare its own receiver, so the app has to. I added it to the app's manifest in version 1.9."],["Duplicates","The duplicate key used the inbox row number, which a live message does not have. Roughly half of Indian bank alerts carry no reference number, so most live captures were saved twice by the next scan. The key is now built from the message content, and a migration re-keyed the old rows."],["Updates","The update file sat in a private repository, so every check failed, and the check hid its own errors. The app looked up to date and never updated. The feed now lives in a public repository, the release script writes it last, downloads are checked against their size and hash, and errors show in Settings."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Charts that do not depend on colour alone","blocks":[{"p":"I first picked 14 colours for categories by eye. A palette checker failed them on strength of colour and on how well they separate, both for normal vision and for colour blindness. I cut the set to eight colours that pass, and charts fold anything beyond eight into a grey \"Other\"."},{"p":"Every chart also has a legend with values, so no category is known by colour alone. Each list entry reads out in full to a screen reader, including \"needs category review\" when it is in the queue."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"I have not tested the app with a screen reader user or run a full accessibility audit."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Working on my phone, with no accuracy figures yet","blocks":[{"p":"PreMoney is at version 1.17.0. It installs by hand from a public release page and updates itself from there. It is not on Google Play. Google restricts apps that read text messages, and a listing would need a separate declaration."},{"p":"It is built for one person. These are not there yet:"},{"list":["No measured accuracy on a labelled set of real messages. The tests check known cases, not a rate.","No other users, and no evidence yet that the scores change how I spend.","Some phone makers' battery managers can still stop background capture. A scan of the inbox picks up anything missed.","A salary from an employer the app does not know lands in Other Income until I file it once."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Measure the parser, then fix what the app says about data","blocks":[{"list":["Build a labelled set of messages with personal details removed, and count wrong accepts, wrong rejects and duplicates for each bank's format.","Rewrite the first-run screens so they describe backup and AI suggestions plainly, with a clear way to say no.","Show the confidence score in the list, and use the plain-English reasons in the message tester.","Spot a salary on its own: a large credit that repeats near the start of each month."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"An honest gap beats a confident guess","blocks":[{"p":"The worst bug in this app was not a crash. It was an advert saved as ₹50 lakh of income, which looked exactly like real data. That changed how I designed the rest: ask for proof, mark what is unsure, and keep the original message one tap away."},{"p":"I also learned to distrust silence. Live capture, duplicate keys and updates all failed without an error. Each fix ended with the same rule: if something can fail, it has to say so somewhere I will see it."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","headline":"The song waits until you are ready."}},"soundbox":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","kind":"product","headline":"A piano app that waits until you play it, so you can learn a song without reading music.","summary":"SoundBox is a free piano app I designed and built on my own. It lights up the keys to press, listens through the microphone, and does not move on until it hears the right notes.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working app","context":"Music learning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"arrangement levels"},{"value":"Wait mode","label":"default practice rhythm"},{"value":"On-device","label":"audio processing"}],"problem":"Someone who wants to play a familiar song may not yet read notation or keep up with a timed lesson. SoundBox starts from the song and a picture of the keyboard, then waits for the player to produce the expected note or chord.","process":["I organised the learning experience around a song, an approachable arrangement, and a repeatable passage. The default practice mode waits for the learner instead of making the person chase a clock.","Listening feedback needs a clear ready, heard, and retry state. Recognition reliability has not been established across real instruments and rooms, so recovery deserves as much design attention as successful progression."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make progression depend on readiness","choice":"The default mode advances after the expected musical input rather than at a fixed tempo.","reason":"A beginner can concentrate on finding the right movement without constantly falling behind the interface.","tradeoff":"Recognition errors can still interrupt the experience, so recovery and a clear input state are essential."},{"title":"Offer more than one way into a song","choice":"Chord, simplified, and full-melody arrangements support different levels of challenge.","reason":"A learner can begin with something recognisable and increase complexity when ready.","tradeoff":"An automatically simplified arrangement still needs musical review; lower note density is not always easier to play."},{"title":"Support repetition without punishment","choice":"Section looping and restrained progress mechanics accompany local practice history.","reason":"Practice often means repeating one difficult passage, not completing a song from the beginning.","tradeoff":"Streaks and XP can distract from learning if they reward activity without meaningful progress."}],"outcome":"I designed practice around visible keys, listening-based progression and repeatable passages. Different arrangements give learners more than one way into a song. The app lets them stay with a difficult note; recognition reliability and learning outcomes need separate evaluation.","reflection":"I would watch the full practice loop: listening, finding a key, playing it, interpreting the feedback and trying again. A passage-completion count would miss much of what makes that experience frustrating or useful.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a beginner learning one short passage with the default wait mode, including how they recover from missed detections.","Proposed benchmark: test recognised notes and chords under varied room noise, microphones, and keyboards; report false advances separately from missed inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How the listening works and Practising","detail":"Arrangements, paced learning, recognition approach, local processing and practice loops. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/soundbox","cover":"assets/products/soundbox.svg","coverKind":"title","readingMinutes":18,"pdfPage":36,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"The song waits [[until you are ready.]]","scene":"Imagine knowing the song you want to play, but being unable to read a page of notation or keep up with a timed lesson. Finding the right key already takes attention.","question":"What changes when the lesson follows the learner’s pace?","insight":"[[Removing the clock changes the interaction]]. The default practice loop waits for the expected note or chord, with space to retry.","pivot":"I connected a visible keyboard, familiar songs, and listening-based progression to make the next musical action understandable.","takeaway":"A learning tool should make another attempt feel possible.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Know which key or chord comes next."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Try it without racing a timer."},{"title":"Listen","detail":"Let the app recognise the input."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"Loop the difficult passage when useful."}],"decisionLeads":["Let readiness decide the next step.","Offer more than one way into the song.","Support repetition without punishment."],"annotations":[{"title":"A paced practice loop","detail":"The current chord, visible keyboard and Wait for me control make the next attempt clear."},{"title":"Recognition has limits","detail":"Real room and instrument conditions remain evaluation work."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · wait-mode practice","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Beginner practising a song","context":"Repeating a difficult passage at a comfortable pace.","need":"Find the notes and continue when ready.","friction":"A fixed tempo or unclear listening feedback can interrupt practice.","implication":"Wait for the learner and make retry states clear."}},"theme":{"layout":"practice","mode":"dark","paper":"#0f1017","ink":"#f2f3f8","dim":"#b8bfd1","line":"#42495f","accent":"#b8aaff","wash":"#302b4b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Nunito","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difficult passage","process":"The learner sets the pace","decisions":"Feedback without a race","outcome":"The practice loop","reflection":"Listening in real conditions"},"decisionHeading":"Let the learner set the pace.","resultHeading":"Stay with the passage. Continue when ready."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/soundbox-practice.png","alt":"Original SoundBox practice screen with chord sequence, keyboard and Wait for me mode.","caption":"The practice screen keeps the current chord, keyboard and pace controls together.","label":"Practice interface","source":"SoundBox/docs/play-screen.png","focus":[{"title":"Recognise","detail":"The current chord and keyboard remain visible."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"A passage can be approached at the learner’s pace."},{"title":"Control the pace","detail":"Wait for me is an explicit mode."}],"width":1080,"height":2400,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":25,"y":1470,"width":1030,"height":700,"caption":"The keyboard and Wait for me control sit together in the practice screen.","alt":"Detail of the original SoundBox keyboard, listening button and Wait for me practice mode."}]},"v2":{"title":"A piano app that [[waits until you play it]], so you can learn a song without reading music.","hook":"SoundBox is a free piano app I designed and built on my own. It lights up the keys to press, listens through the microphone, and does not move on until it hears the right notes.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"August 2026, 10 commits over three days"},{"label":"Why","value":"People who cannot read sheet music still want to play the songs they love"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android app and any web browser"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 19, TypeScript, Vite, Capacitor, Web Audio, Basic Pitch"},{"label":"Status","value":"Working app, version 1.1 on Android. Tested by me in a browser and an Android emulator, not yet with learners"}],"links":[{"label":"See the code","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/soundbox"}],"linksHeading":"Look inside","summary":["The problem: to play a song you love, you usually have to decode a page of sheet music, or keep up with a lesson that moves on whether you are ready or not.","What I built: a free app for Android and the browser. Every song comes at three levels, the keys light up on a picture of a piano, and the app listens and waits for you.","The key idea: the hard part was knowing when not to move on. Room noise, a hummed tune and a chord it cannot read must not count, so the app says “listening…” instead of guessing.","Where it stands: a working app, built in three days. There is no independent test of how well it recognises notes on real pianos, and no measured learning results."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/play-desktop.webp","alt":"The SoundBox practice screen on a laptop: Für Elise on the Simple melody level, a row of chord cards along the top, the note E shown large, and the E key lit in violet on a three-octave keyboard","caption":"The practice screen on a laptop, on the Simple melody level of Für Elise. The chord sheet runs along the top, the note to play is large, and its key is lit below.","width":1600,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Most songs are written down for people who can read music","blocks":[{"p":"SoundBox is for someone who wants to play a song they love, well enough to sing along or to record light background music, and who cannot read sheet music."},{"p":"Today that means searching the internet for the notes, finding a page of notation, and trying to work the whole page out onto the keyboard at once. A timed lesson adds a clock on top. I treated time pressure as the most discouraging thing for a beginner, and designed against it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/problem.webp","alt":"Two rows of steps. The usual way: search for notes, find sheet music you cannot read, work the page out at once, a timed lesson moves on. With SoundBox: pick the song, pick a level, see the keys lit, play and it moves on when it hears it","caption":"The problem in one picture. Compare the last step of each row: one clock belongs to the lesson, the other to you.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"Before drawing any screen, I wrote down four rules the app could not break."},{"table":{"head":["Rule","What it meant"],"rows":[["Free forever","No paid services and no licensed content. Everything runs on the device."],["Not scary","No staff notation and no key signatures. Large type and few things on screen."],["Works offline","The songs and the listening all work with no network."],["Accessible","Readable contrast, touch targets at least 48 pixels on a touchscreen, labels for screen readers, and never colour alone."]]}},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did not interview learners or watch a beginner use the app. The audience on this page comes from my own problem statement."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Pick a song, pick a level, play what lights up","blocks":[{"p":"Six songs come with the app: five traditional or classical tunes and one Hindi film song. You can add your own. Each row in the library shows the key, the number of chords and how hard the easiest level is."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/library-phone.webp","alt":"The SoundBox library on a phone: six songs in rows, each with its key, chord count and difficulty dots, and a tab bar at the bottom","caption":"The library on a phone. A row per song, not a card, so the facts you compare when choosing still fit.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/play-full-phone.webp","alt":"The practice screen on a phone for Für Elise on Full melody: chord cards, the note E5 with D sharp next, and one lit key","caption":"The same piece on Full melody. The step counter reads 1 of 182, against 52 steps on Just the chords.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"The idea that holds the product together is the song format. A song stores one set of chords and three ways to play it, from easy to hard. You start where you can and move up when the song stops being hard."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/levels.webp","alt":"Diagram of three level cards, Just the chords, Simple melody and Full melody, each saying what you play and who it is for, above a band saying all three share one set of harmony","caption":"The three levels, in the app's own names. Read the band at the bottom: the levels are three depths of one song, not three songs.","width":1600,"height":730}]},{"p":"Then the loop. The screen shows what to play, you play it on your own piano, and the app listens. In the default mode, called Wait for me, nothing moves until it hears the right thing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/loop.webp","alt":"Flow diagram: See, Play, Listen, Judge, then three outcomes: it matches and the song moves on, a wrong note shown in amber while the song waits, or not sure and it says listening","caption":"The practice loop. The third outcome matters most: when the app is unsure, it says so instead of guessing.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/annotated-play.webp","alt":"The practice screen on a phone with seven numbered notes: named header buttons, the level picker, the chord sheet, the current and next chord, the lit keys, the Listen button and the mode picker","caption":"The practice screen, part by part. Start at note 5: every lit key also carries a ring and a letter.","width":1600,"height":1080}]}]},{"id":"wait","label":"Decision 1","nav":"The song waits","heading":"The song waits for you, and tells you when it is unsure","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Wait for me is the default. There is no clock. A wrong note shows in a neutral amber and the song simply stays where it is. There is no red cross and no buzzer."},{"p":"When the microphone is open, a strip at the bottom says what the app is doing: “warming up…” while the detector loads, “getting used to the room…” for the first second, then “listening…” until it hears something it believes."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/listening-phone.webp","alt":"The lower half of the practice screen after pressing Listen: the C, E and A keys lit, and a strip reading listening beside a level meter and a microphone-off button","caption":"The listening strip after pressing Listen. I captured this in a headless browser with a simulated microphone, so it shows the waiting state, not a note being recognised.","width":1170,"height":1132}]},{"p":"In the build: the three modes, Wait for me, Play along and Hear it, share one position in the song, so switching never loses your place. Pause exists only in the timed modes. In Wait for me the player is the clock, so there is nothing to pause, and the space bar plays the current chord instead."},{"p":"The listening strip names each refusal. “That sounds sung, not played” is something you can act on. Silence looks exactly like a broken microphone."},{"p":"Any mode can loop a section, because nobody practises by playing a song from the top again and again. They play the eight bars they keep fumbling."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/loop-desktop.webp","alt":"The practice screen on a laptop with the Opening section of Für Elise marked Looping, the other section dimmed, and a Looping Opening note under the controls","caption":"Looping a section. The label sits on the sheet where you are reading, as well as under the control that set it.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A song that waits can also get stuck, if the app fails to hear a note you played well. The skip button and the arrow keys let you move on by hand."}}]},{"id":"listening","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Hearing only a piano","heading":"It listens only for a piano, and never sends the sound anywhere","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one Listen button, and in Settings a few plain choices. How noisy is your room? Only listen for a piano? Use a connected digital piano?"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/settings-listening.webp","alt":"The Listening card in Settings: room noise choice, detector choice of Automatic, Accurate or Light, and switches for piano only, right octave and MIDI piano, each with a sentence of explanation","caption":"The Listening settings. Each choice carries a sentence about when to change it, written for someone who is not an engineer.","width":1340,"height":1494,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: my first version had one check. It ignored sounds that were too quiet. Everything after that worked on a pattern with the volume taken out. A fan, a conversation or an empty room all came back looking like music, and the on-screen piano lit up end to end."},{"p":"So I put five gates between the microphone and the song. Each catches a different kind of sound that is not someone playing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/gates.webp","alt":"Diagram of the listening path: microphone, then four gates for room noise, loudness, musical shape and number of notes, then the piano gate, the Basic Pitch detector, a fallback detector and matching the step","caption":"The path a sound takes. Gates 1 to 4 ask whether anything musical is happening. Gate 5 asks whether it came from a piano.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Next, I tested the chord detector against a synthesised grand piano, and it was not good enough. A chromagram, the detector's sum of sound in each of the 12 note names, cannot tell octaves apart."},{"table":{"head":["What was played","What the first detector heard","What the model heard"],"rows":[["C major with two hands","C only","C2 C3 C4 E4 G4, with the octaves right"],["A minor 7 with the sustain pedal down","A only","A2 A3 C4 E4 G4, with the octaves right"],["A melody note over a ringing chord","A C two octaves below anything played","The right note, C5"]]}},{"p":"A small fix solved the first two rows for the old detector. The third could not be fixed that way, so I added Basic Pitch, an open-source note-transcription model from Spotify. It has about 17,000 parameters, and its 725 KB of weights ship inside the app. The old detector stays as the fallback for phones that cannot run the model."},{"p":"Then I found the app was happy to be sung to. Humming the tune moved the song on exactly as playing did. The piano gate closes that gap by looking at how a sound behaves over time: a struck string arrives at once, only gets quieter and holds its pitch. A voice fades in, holds, and wobbles."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/attack.webp","alt":"Bar chart of attack scores from 0 to 1: struck string best case 1.0, struck string worst case 0.5, held voice highest seen 0.07","caption":"The first of the gate's three tests. The gap between 0.5 and 0.07 is the margin it works with. These figures come from synthetic sounds, not a real room.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The model made the live-update bundle grow from 128 KB to 818 KB, after I had cut 310 KB by dropping a component library. I accepted it, because an app whose promise is that it listens gains nothing from being small if it cannot hear a chord. It loads only on the first press of Listen."}}]},{"id":"sideways","label":"Decision 3","nav":"The keyboard gets the width","heading":"The keyboard gets the width, even when the phone refuses to turn","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: held upright, a phone shows two octaves that follow the notes you need. Turned sideways, the chord sheet goes and the keys fill the screen. Zen mode strips the screen down to the keys and the notes falling towards them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/zen-annotated.webp","alt":"Zen mode on a phone turned sideways with five numbered notes: the Play E instruction, the Listen and step controls, notes falling in the lane of their key, the line where notes meet the keys, and the lit E key","caption":"Zen mode. Note 3 is the reason it exists: the instruction arrives directly above the finger that plays it.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"In the build: three octaves on an upright phone made each key barely wider than a fingernail. So upright phones get fewer octaves instead of smaller keys. The two-octave window stays still unless the next notes fall outside it, because a keyboard that moves every bar slides out from under the hand you are matching it to."},{"p":"The Android app first shipped locked to portrait. I unlocked it, then found that many people keep auto-rotate switched off, so turning the phone did nothing. I added a sideways button that tries three routes in turn."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/sideways.webp","alt":"Diagram of three routes in order: ask the Android app, ask the browser, turn the page itself. The first is marked not yet watched on a device, the other two checked in the browser","caption":"Three ways to turn sideways. Route 3 always works, and it is the one most phones will take.","width":1600,"height":600}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/play-sideways.webp","alt":"The practice screen on a phone turned sideways: level picker, the note E, a three-octave keyboard with E lit, and the controls in one row","caption":"A phone on its side. The header no longer wraps onto two lines, so that height goes to the keys.","width":1600,"height":739},{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/zen-phone-turned.webp","alt":"Zen mode captured on an upright phone with the whole page drawn on its side, keys along the left edge","caption":"Route 3 caught on an upright screen: the page has turned itself. Turn the phone and it reads the right way up.","width":780,"height":1688,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"Zen mode only works if a falling note sits exactly over its key at every screen size. Two separate drawings would each scale on their own and drift apart at some sizes, so the keys and the notes are one drawing. Only one thing moves: the group of notes slides down as a single piece. The screen also stays awake on the practice screen, because minutes of looking without touching is exactly what a phone reads as “they have walked away”."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Zen mode hides the chord sheet, so you lose the shape of the song while you play. That is why it is a separate view you step into and out of, not a setting."}}]},{"id":"look","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Every colour has one job","heading":"Round and colourful again, but every colour has one job","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: chunky rounded buttons that sit on a darker lip and press down. Four colours, each with one meaning. Violet is the app and the note you are asked to play. Green is correct and every button that moves you forward. Amber is the streak and anything earned. Coral only ever deletes something."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/look.webp","alt":"Three phone screens side by side: the dark first version of the practice screen from 23 August, the current light version, and the current dark version","caption":"The practice screen on the first day and now. Compare the top right corner: a speaker icon alone at first, two named buttons now.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"In the build: the look went round twice. My first pass had pill-shaped buttons and large flat areas of violet. I rejected it because it read like software for children. So I went square and neutral."},{"p":"That stopped it looking like a toy. It also left six unlabelled icons in the practice screen's header, three filter controls in front of six songs, and option labels cut down to “Se… / Bal… / Str…”. No first-time user could read that screen."},{"p":"So it went round again, on one condition: every shape and colour must carry information. Rounded corners with a lip mean you can press it. Cards have no lip, because you cannot press a card. I kept two things from the square version: a row per song in the library, and fixed-width digits so numbers do not jitter as they change."},{"p":"The default theme flipped the same way. I first chose dark, for a phone on a music stand in a dim room. But most practice happens in daylight next to white keys, where a near-black screen was hard to read. Light is now the default and dark is one tap away."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/options-desktop.webp","alt":"The practice screen on a laptop with the options panel open at the bottom: microphone room setting, piano only or anything musical, and the keyboard shortcuts","caption":"Settings that used to crowd the screen now sit in an options panel, one tap away, each with a name.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A word under every icon takes room. The header keeps only two named buttons, Zen and Options, and everything else costs one extra tap."}}]},{"id":"score","label":"Decision 5","nav":"A streak that cannot nag","heading":"It keeps score, but nothing can be taken away from you","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a daily streak, points for ground you cover, and badges. With nothing played yet, the page says “No streak yet” and how to start one."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/progress.webp","alt":"The Progress screen with an amber No streak yet banner, four counters for XP earned, XP today, finished and last practised, and ten badges with one earned","caption":"The Progress screen as a new player sees it. The empty streak is an invitation, in amber, never a warning in red.","width":1600,"height":839}]},{"p":"In the build: the first version refused to keep score at all, because a streak counter turns a busy fortnight into a punishment. That reason did not go away, so the scoring now follows four rules."},{"list":["A streak survives one missed day. It breaks only after a full day with nothing played.","Points only ever go up. There is no decay, no weekly reset and no league.","Badges are for things you did, such as playing every level of one song, not for days you kept up.","Nothing is ever shown in red."]},{"p":"Points come from new ground: two for each step you had not reached before, and fifty the first time you finish a level. Your furthest point never moves back, because stepping back to drill a hard bar is the right way to practise. Points live apart from your practice history, so clearing the history does not take them away."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Points for time spent. That rewards leaving the app open, and replaying a bar you already know should be worth nothing."}}]},{"id":"songs","label":"Your own songs","nav":"Your own songs","heading":"An upload opens in the editor, never straight into your library","blocks":[{"p":"You can type a song bar by bar, or bring one in: a MusicXML file from MuseScore, a ChordPro file, a plain-text chord sheet, or a SoundBox export. Chords written above a line of words are matched to the syllable under them, so an imported sheet is still singable."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/upload-paste.webp","alt":"The Upload a song dialog with a drop area, a pasted chord sheet with chords above words, and a note that pictures and PDFs of sheet music cannot be read","caption":"A short chord sheet I typed in for this page. The grey note explains why a photo or PDF cannot be read, and what to use instead.","width":1032,"height":1384},{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/editor-pasted.webp","alt":"The song editor after reading the pasted sheet: a note saying 8 bars in 2 sections were read, title, key and tempo fields, and four bars each with a chord and the words under it","caption":"What the app made of it. Each bar kept the words that sat under its chord. Nothing is saved until you press Save.","width":1400,"height":1444}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: importers get things wrong, and a wrong song that saved itself is worse than one you were asked to check. So every upload lands in the editor first."},{"p":"Pictures and PDFs are refused with the reason. Reading notes off an image needs an online service, and nothing in this app leaves the device. A message that only says “unsupported” teaches nobody anything."},{"p":"Most sheet music has no chord names, so in the last change I made the app read them back out of the notes. It weighs each note by how long it sounds, whether it falls on a strong beat and how low it is. Two limits were set by tests failing. The cost of an extra chord note had to double before a quick passing F stopped turning a G bar into G7. The confidence cut-off had to rise to 0.6 before a plain run of C, D, E, F stopped coming back as F major 7."}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One codebase for the phone and the laptop, with the music maths tested on its own","blocks":[{"p":"The same web app runs in a browser and inside a thin Android shell. A laptop propped on the piano is a real way to use it, not an afterthought."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in four columns: pure music maths, sound in and out, screens and storage, and where it runs: Android app, any browser and live updates","caption":"How it is put together. The left column has no screens and no sound, which is what makes it testable.","width":1600,"height":960}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","React 19, TypeScript and Vite"],["Android","Capacitor 8, a thin native shell around the web app"],["Sound","Web Audio for the microphone and the practice synth, Web MIDI for digital pianos"],["Note detection","Basic Pitch on TensorFlow.js, with my own chromagram detector as the fallback"],["Interface","Plain CSS and a small set of controls of my own"],["Updates","Capgo live updates, published by GitHub Actions after the types and tests pass"]]}},{"p":"I chose web technology for one reason: sound. Reading the microphone in real time is well documented in the browser and a chore of native code everywhere else, so I ruled out React Native and Flutter. I also started on Material UI and moved off it. It cost 310 KB in an app meant to work offline on a phone, and the practice screen is not made of Material parts. The rewrite halved the JavaScript."},{"stats":[{"value":"10","label":"commits over three days, 23 to 25 August 2026"},{"value":"12","label":"test files"},{"value":"5","label":"gates between the microphone and the song"},{"value":"6","label":"songs that come with the app"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/soundbox/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of three days with commit counts: 23 August 1, 24 August 7, 25 August 2, and the main changes on each day","caption":"The build by day. Most of the listening work happened on 24 August, the busiest day.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"The tests run the detection maths against synthetic piano sounds, the noise gates against synthetic room noise, and the piano gate against synthetic struck, sung, droning and sharp spoken sounds. They also check the streak across a daylight-saving change."},{"p":"The hardest problems were about sound and time, not screens."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["A doubled root","Playing C in both hands put far more energy in C than in E and G, which then fell under the cut-off (C 1.00, E 0.41, G 0.37 against 0.56). Compressing the loud end brought back all three notes."],["Overtones","One low C also rings at G and E, so a simple detector reports a C major chord for a single key. The detector now damps each note's expected overtones first."],["The same millisecond","Switching level saved two progress records at the same instant, and the library offered to resume the level you had just left. A counter now breaks the tie. A unit test caught it, not my eyes."],["Old app, new code","Live updates can put new web code inside an older Android app. Every call to the native shell reports whether it worked, and each has a browser fallback."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Sized for the device you touch, with colour never working alone","blocks":[{"p":"Sizing follows the kind of pointer, not the width of the window. With a mouse the controls are tight. On a touchscreen every target is at least 48 pixels. A narrow browser window is not a phone."},{"p":"A lit key also carries a ring and its letter. Each level button has a sentence for screen readers, such as “Two chords, one per bar. Hold each one and let it ring.” When the level picker moves to its own row on a small screen, it moves rather than appearing twice, so a screen reader hears one choice and not two."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are rules I built to. I have not run an accessibility audit or a full screen-reader pass, and I do not claim one."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"It works end to end, and has not met a real learner yet","blocks":[{"p":"SoundBox works from start to finish: browse, pick a level, follow the current bar, see the next keys, let the microphone listen, and move through the song. I checked it in a browser and on an Android 14 emulator, in both themes. There is a signed Android build, version 1.1."},{"p":"These things are not established:"},{"list":["There is no independent recognition benchmark. Detection is checked against synthetic piano sounds, not a real piano in a real room.","There are no measured learning results, and no beginner has used it while I watched.","The model's live path, from microphone to screen, has not yet run on real audio, and nobody has timed it on a phone.","The Android shell's own rotation and keep-awake calls have not run on a device yet.","Songs and progress stay on one device. There is no sync."]},{"p":"The screens on this page come from the browser build. The listening features need a microphone and a piano, so none of them shows a note being recognised."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Test it on a real piano, then with a real beginner","blocks":[{"list":["Open “What can it hear?” in Settings on a real piano: check that the model starts, what delay it reports, and whether a two-handed chord lights the right keys.","Try the piano gate in a real room at all three noise settings.","Proposed study: watch a beginner learn one short passage in Wait for me, including how they recover when a note is missed.","Proposed benchmark: test notes and chords across rooms, microphones and pianos, and report false steps forward apart from missed notes.","Smaller features already planned: colour for left and right hand, and looping any range of bars rather than a whole section."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A patient app has to be honest about what it hears","blocks":[{"p":"I set out to remove the clock, and that part was easy. The work was in what waiting asks of the listening. A song that waits is only patient if it moves on when you play and stays when you do not. Each wrong step forward or missed note breaks the promise."},{"p":"I also learned to judge a style by whether it carries information. Rounded and colourful failed once and worked the second time. The shapes stayed similar. What changed was that every corner and colour now meant something."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","headline":"Different starting points. One place to meet."}},"wheelie":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","kind":"product","headline":"Three riders start in three towns. The app works out where they meet.","summary":"Wheelie is a free Android and web app I designed and built on my own for motorcyclists who ride in groups. It finds a meeting point on the way, keeps the group talking, and turns each ride into a card to share.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Beta · accessibility polish in progress","context":"Group mobility · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"documented product pillars"},{"value":"64px+","label":"primary target intent"},{"value":"Beta","label":"not a safety validation"}],"problem":"Group riding begins before everyone reaches the same road. People start in different places, need a meeting point, and can lose contact after setting off. Wheelie brings planning, group location, and helmet voice into one service concept, with large, simple controls as an explicit design constraint.","process":["I framed three moments: meeting before a ride, coordinating with the group, and looking back afterwards. Each moment needs a different level of detail and attention.","The constrained physical context informs large targets, restrained choices, and clear connection states. The next evaluation belongs in stationary or controlled settings; the beta is not evidence of safe in-motion interaction."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a rendezvous, not just a destination","choice":"The service computes a meeting point for people starting from different places.","reason":"The group’s coordination problem exists before shared navigation can begin.","tradeoff":"A fair computed point may not be a suitable physical stopping place; people need to review the suggestion."},{"title":"Reduce reliance on precise gestures","choice":"The design calls for large primary targets and simple tap/drag interactions.","reason":"A constrained physical context should affect the interface model from the start.","tradeoff":"Large buttons alone cannot establish safe in-motion use. Evaluation should begin in stationary or controlled settings."},{"title":"Separate coordination from reflection","choice":"Live map and voice support the current group; ride summaries serve the post-ride story.","reason":"Different moments deserve different information and levels of attention.","tradeoff":"Social summaries can create incentives around speed; the content priorities deserve review before wider use."}],"outcome":"I organised the beta around meeting before a ride, coordinating with the group and reviewing the ride afterwards. Meeting points, group location, helmet voice and summaries serve those different moments. Large controls are a design intention, not evidence that the app is safe to operate while moving.","reflection":"I would move as much setup as possible into the time before the ride. The next checks belong in a stationary or controlled setting, with particular attention to connection status and anything that asks the rider to look away.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: test rendezvous planning and headset controls while stationary, then assess attention demands under an appropriately controlled protocol.","Proposed checks: poor connectivity, location permission changes, unavailable voice, battery use, and clarity about whether rider positions are stale."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and design.md","detail":"Product pillars, beta status, target-size intent and unfinished accessibility/glove review. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Wheelie release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","alt":"Wheelie native Android sign-in screen with blank email and password fields","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. Ride planning and group features are described from the project documentation; no account or rider location was used.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/wheelie","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":39,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Different starting points. [[One place to meet.]]","scene":"Imagine a group starting from different parts of a city. Sharing a destination does not yet answer where to meet or how to stay coordinated once the ride begins.","question":"Which coordination decisions belong before the group is moving?","insight":"[[The ride starts with a rendezvous]]. Planning, rider location, and helmet voice solve different moments of the same service.","pivot":"I connected those moments while treating attention, large controls, and clear connection states as design constraints.","takeaway":"Good coordination should ask for less attention at the busiest moment.","journey":[{"title":"Plan","detail":"Review a suitable meeting point while stopped."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Know who is where and whether the information is current."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Tell the ride’s story after it is over."}],"decisionLeads":["Solve the meeting before the navigation.","Reduce the precision an action requires.","Keep the ride and its later story separate."],"annotations":[{"title":"A connected service","detail":"The three pillars cover meeting, communication, and ride summaries."},{"title":"A beta with evaluation ahead","detail":"Accessibility and glove polish remain in progress."}],"evidenceTag":"Beta artifact · controlled evaluation next","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Ride organiser coordinating a group","context":"Preparing meeting points and keeping a group in contact.","need":"Review a rendezvous and understand group connection status.","friction":"Different starting places and physical interaction constraints complicate coordination.","implication":"Prioritise preparation, large targets, and clear connection feedback."}},"theme":{"layout":"rendezvous","mode":"light","paper":"#f7f9ff","ink":"#1d2a41","dim":"#5b6b84","line":"#d2dcee","accent":"#0b57b4","wash":"#e2ecff","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before the group sets off","process":"Coordination comes first","decisions":"Designing for the context","outcome":"A ride in three moments","reflection":"Controlled evaluation next"},"decisionHeading":"Change the interface around the ride.","resultHeading":"Prepare together. Coordinate clearly. Reflect later."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"Wheelie native Android sign-in screen with blank email and password fields","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. Ride planning and group features are described from the project documentation; no account or rider location was used.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Wheelie release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026.","width":1080,"height":1920},"v2":{"title":"Three riders start in three towns. The app works out [[where they meet]].","hook":"Wheelie is a free Android and web app I designed and built on my own for motorcyclists who ride in groups. It finds a meeting point on the way, keeps the group talking, and turns each ride into a card to share.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"For","value":"Motorcyclists who ride in groups"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android and web, from one Flutter codebase"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Flutter, Riverpod, flutter_map with OpenStreetMap, Valhalla, Photon, Supabase, WebRTC"},{"label":"Status","value":"Beta. Accessibility and glove polish in progress. Not yet tested with riders"}],"links":[{"label":"See the code on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/wheelie"}],"linksHeading":"See the code","summary":["The problem: a group ride starts before anyone is on the same road. Riders leave from different places, need a fair spot to meet, and then need to stay in touch with gloves on.","What I built: a map that works out a meeting point from every rider's own route, group voice through each rider's helmet headset, and a shareable card for every finished ride.","The key idea: first look for a road everyone already takes. Only if there is none, compromise, and never by more than 15% extra riding for anyone.","Where it stands: a beta. All three parts are built and run end to end on the app's stand-in backend. It has not been tested with riders, gloves or real roads, and I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/lead.webp","alt":"Four phone schematics and a ride card: a route preview, turn-by-turn riding with other riders on the map, the meeting point reached card, the voice tab, and a ride summary card","caption":"Four moments of a group ride and the card it leaves behind. These are schematics I drew from the app's layout code at its own sizes, not screenshots. Values and rider names are placeholders.","width":2400,"height":1080},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A group ride starts before anyone is on the same road","blocks":[{"p":"Riding in a group has three parts. First the group has to meet, and the riders often start in different places. Then they ride and need to stay in touch. Afterwards, they want something to show for it."},{"p":"The person using the app is wearing gloves. They glance at the screen for a second at a time, and the phone sits on a handlebar mount that shakes. I designed for that first and for a desk second."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/problem.webp","alt":"Schematic map of Goa: three riders start in Madgaon, Ponda and Old Goa and head for Mapusa. Their routes merge at the Mandovi crossing","caption":"The problem in one picture, using the app's own demo scenario. Look at the gold dot: every rider's fastest route already passes it, so meeting there costs nobody anything.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"I set two rules before writing any code. Every service had to be free, with no billing keys. And every screen had to work with gloves on."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I have no interviews with riders and no tests on a bike. The needs on this page come from a brief I set myself, not from research."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Three moments, three tabs, and the map never closes","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/moments.webp","alt":"Diagram of three columns, before, during and after a ride, mapped to the Map tab, the Map and Voice tabs, and the Rides tab","caption":"The three moments of a ride and where each one lives in the app. The top card in each column is the main job of that moment.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The map is the home screen. Groups, the meeting point and the other riders all sit on top of it. Voice and Rides are separate tabs, but switching to them does not close the map or cut the audio."},{"p":"Here is one ride with a meeting point, from the preview to the stop."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-preview.webp","alt":"Phone schematic of the route preview: 38 min travel time, the destination, a line saying it meets your group on the way, and a Start ride button","caption":"The preview puts travel time first, because that is the decision. The second line says the route goes to the meeting point.","width":470,"height":930,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-nav.webp","alt":"Phone schematic while riding: a blue next-turn banner, the route to a purple meeting point marker, two other riders and a bar with arrival time, distance, speed and End","caption":"Riding. The solid line goes to the meeting point. The dotted line shows the second leg to the destination.","width":470,"height":930,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-meet.webp","alt":"Phone schematic at the meeting point: a card says meeting point reached, 2 of 3 riders here, with Continue and End ride buttons","caption":"At the meeting point the ride stops and counts who has arrived. It waits for a tap to go on.","width":470,"height":930,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"aside":{"title":"Why these are drawings.","detail":"I have no screenshots of these states yet. The schematics follow the layout code, sizes and wording, but they are not captures of the running app."}}]},{"id":"meet","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Where to meet","heading":"Meet where the roads already merge, or not at all","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: in a group, you search for a destination. The app shares it with everyone and, if it finds one, routes you to a meeting point first. The preview says \"Meets your group on the way\"."},{"p":"In the build: the app does not average everyone's positions. It routes every rider to the destination and looks for where those routes join, so riding to the meeting point adds no distance for anyone."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/meeting-logic.webp","alt":"Five numbered steps: route every rider, look for where the routes merge, keep it only if it is far enough from the destination, otherwise try where routes pass closest with a 15% detour cap, otherwise no meeting point","caption":"How the point is chosen. The rules on the right are the actual numbers in the code. Step 5 matters most: no meeting point beats a forced detour.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"I checked it against a fixed scenario: riders in Madgaon, Old Goa and Ponda, heading for Mapusa. Against the live public router, every route merged at the Mandovi crossing, with 0.0% added to anyone's ride."},{"p":"The tests passed. Running the same scenario as a live demo inside the app still found three bugs."},{"table":{"head":["Bug","What went wrong","What I changed"],"rows":[["Old position","The point was worked out from my own position as last sent to the group. That is sent at most every 2 seconds, and a parked phone sends nothing.","It now uses the live location."],["Too close","\"Worth stopping for\" was a fixed 300 m. On a 44 km ride it accepted a point that was really the destination.","Now 5% of the longest route, with 500 m as the floor, and the rule covers fallback points too."],["Drifting","Where two routes share a long stretch, the closest point drifted to a random spot along it, often the destination.","A 5 m tolerance keeps the earliest place the roads meet."]]}},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A merge point is a spot on a road, not a checked place to stop. The app does not look for a lay-by or a fuel station, so riders still need to judge it."}}]},{"id":"wait","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Wait for the group","heading":"The ride waits at the meeting point until someone taps","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/ride-states.webp","alt":"Flow of a ride: idle, preview, riding leg 1, at the meeting point, riding leg 2, arrived, saved. Below, three side cases: off the route, no meeting point, ended early","caption":"One ride as the app sees it. The highlighted step is the one I added for groups.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"What you see: when you reach the meeting point, a card says how many riders are there, such as \"2 of 3 riders here\". The route on to the destination starts only when you tap Continue."},{"p":"In the build: arriving at the meeting point is its own state, not the end of the ride. Continue fetches a fresh route from where you are standing. The ride recording carries on through the stop, so the card covers the whole trip. A test checks that."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Carrying on by itself. It saves a tap, but it would send the first rider off alone while the group is still gathering."}}]},{"id":"gloves","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Built for gloves","heading":"Glove sizes were rules before they were screens","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: large buttons everywhere, and no gesture that needs two fingers or a precise drag. Zoom is two buttons instead of a pinch."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/glove-sizes.webp","alt":"Squares drawn to scale from 24px to a 128px circle, with what each size is used for, and four rule cards: 8px apart, tap or a straight drag, never colour alone, feel every tap","caption":"The sizes side by side. The dashed 24px square is the WCAG minimum. Wheelie's smallest target is twice that.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"p":"In the build: the sizes live in one small set of constants, and the app theme gives every standard button those minimums. A new screen gets them without anyone remembering."},{"p":"Two shared buttons do the rest. Each one gives a vibration on tap. The icon button cannot be created without a spoken name, so no button is a bare icon to a screen reader. Tests check the heights and the labels."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/annotated-signin.webp","alt":"The real Android sign-in screen with five numbered notes on the title, the fields, the show password button, the Sign in button and the create account button","caption":"The one screen I have a real capture of, from the Android release build. Note 4: the main action is the biggest target on the screen.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/annotated-map.webp","alt":"Map screen schematic with seven numbered notes on the search pill, settings, other riders, the group button, zoom buttons, follow button and map credit","caption":"The map at rest. Look at note 3: other riders are told apart by name and arrow as well as colour.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Big targets take space. A column of four 64px buttons uses the right edge of the map, and the turn banner covers the top while you ride."}}]},{"id":"groups","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Sharing positions","heading":"Riders see each other live, and nothing is kept","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-idle.webp","alt":"Phone schematic of the map with two other riders shown as coloured arrows with name labels, and the group button showing a count of 3","caption":"The group on the map. Each rider has a colour, an arrow for their heading and a name.","width":470,"height":930,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-group.webp","alt":"Phone schematic of the group sheet: a large invite code with copy code and copy link buttons, the destination, three riders with online or offline, and a Leave group button","caption":"The group sheet. The code is set in large, spaced letters. Online and offline are words, not just a dot.","width":470,"height":930,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"What you see: create a group and share a 6-letter code or a link. Everyone in it appears on the map with their name."},{"p":"In the build: positions travel as live presence messages and are never written to the database, so no location history is stored. Only members can read a group. Joining goes through one database function that checks the code and adds you in one step, so a stranger cannot list codes and watch a ride."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The live channel is named after the group's id and is public by default. The id is hard to guess and you only learn it by joining, but a stricter channel setting would close the gap. I have not turned it on yet."}}]},{"id":"routing","label":"Decision 5","nav":"Motorcycle routing","heading":"A route for a car can break the rules for a motorcycle","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/routing-ban.webp","alt":"Illustration of Bandra and Worli: the car route crosses the Sea Link over the water, the motorcycle route goes around on land, with four cards on what was wrong, what I ruled out, what I changed and the limit","caption":"The case that changed the router. The red line is legal for a car and banned for a motorcycle.","width":1600,"height":990}]},{"p":"What you see: nothing new on screen. The turn-by-turn guidance looks the same, which was the point."},{"p":"In the build: the first router had no motorcycle profile, so I added a second free router that has a motorcycle setting. I translated its turn instructions into the words the screens already used, and kept the first router as a fallback. Tests check the translation."}]},{"id":"voice","label":"Voice","nav":"Group voice","heading":"Group voice goes phone to phone, through each rider's own headset","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/annotated-voice.webp","alt":"Voice tab schematic with six numbered notes on the mode label, the two mode buttons, the hold to talk button, deafen, the riders list with mute buttons and Leave voice","caption":"The voice tab. Note 3 is why open mic exists: holding is the only gesture here, and design rules say nothing may depend on a hold alone.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"The voice room is the ride group. There is nothing separate to join or pair. If you leave the group, you leave voice too."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/voice-mesh.webp","alt":"Diagram of four phones each linked to the other three, with cards on how audio, connection setup, relays and headsets work","caption":"With no server in the middle, each phone sends audio to every other phone. That is why it is planned for groups of up to 6.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"aside":{"title":"Not tested yet.","detail":"The voice logic and screen are covered by tests against a stand-in. Live audio between two real devices has not been tried, and I have no figures for delay or data use."}}]},{"id":"cards","label":"Ride cards","nav":"Ride cards","heading":"A card for every ride, and the bug it made obvious","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/phone-card.webp","alt":"Schematic of a ride card: the Wheelie name, the destination and date, the route drawn as a line, and four numbers for distance, time, average speed and top speed","caption":"The share card at its fixed size of 360 by 480. The route is drawn from the ride's own points, so it needs no map and works offline. Values are placeholders.","width":440,"height":580,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"Every finished ride is saved on the phone and shown on the Rides tab. A ride ended before arriving is not kept. Share turns the card into an image and opens the phone's share sheet."},{"p":"For saving rides I ruled out a full on-device database. Simple key-value storage had already worked on the web version of this app, and a list of rides did not need more."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/top-speed.webp","alt":"Bar chart: top speed before the fix 4,475 km/h, after the fix 103.3 km/h, with cards on the cause and the new rule","caption":"One jump between two location fixes made the top speed absurd. Both numbers are from simulated rides.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"aside":{"title":"An open question.","detail":"Putting top speed on a share card could reward riding fast. I kept it for this beta, but it deserves a second look before wider use."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Free services, each behind a part I can swap","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in four columns: the Flutter app on the phone, free public map, search and routing services, Supabase for sign-in, groups, live positions and voice setup, and WebRTC between riders","caption":"What runs where. The two highlighted cards are the ones that carry the design: one app on the phone, and live positions that are never stored.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Flutter, for Android and the web"],["State","Riverpod"],["Map","flutter_map with OpenStreetMap tiles"],["Search and routing","Photon, and Valhalla with OSRM as fallback"],["Accounts, groups and live positions","Supabase free tier"],["Voice","WebRTC between phones"]]}},{"p":"Without backend keys, the app swaps in a pretend group with moving riders, pretend voice and simulated GPS. That let me build and demo every feature at a desk. A built-in Goa scenario sets up three riders in their real towns and fast-forwards the ride."},{"stats":[{"value":"18","label":"commits over four days, 16 to 19 July 2026"},{"value":"About 7,300","label":"lines of app code"},{"value":"60","label":"tests passing at the last run I recorded"},{"value":"15","label":"test files, plus one manual check against the live router"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/wheelie/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of four days with commit counts: 16 July 6, 17 July 8, 18 July 2, 19 July 2, and what was built each day","caption":"The build by day. Most of the product went in on 17 July. The last two days redesigned the navigation screens and fixed what the demo and a live route check showed.","width":1600,"height":640}]}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"The rules are written down, and the audit is still to do","blocks":[{"p":"The target is WCAG 2.2 level AA, the common standard for accessible apps. The design rules name the parts that matter most on a bike: target size, a tap-only way to do anything that needs a drag, and consistent help."},{"list":["Targets of 64px for main actions and never under 48px, checked by tests.","State is never shown by colour alone. Online, offline, speaking and errors have an icon or words.","Every icon button has a spoken name.","Body text at 16px or more, and big numbers for distance and speed.","A tap-only way to do anything that otherwise needs a hold or a drag."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"This is a beta, and the accessibility and glove pass is still in progress. The target sizes are a design intent. They are not proof that the app is safe to use on the road, or that it meets WCAG. There has been no glove, sunlight or screen-reader testing yet."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"A working beta that has not been on a ride yet","blocks":[{"p":"There is an Android release build with email sign-in, and the web version can run in demo mode with the stand-in backend. Both are betas."},{"p":"These are not done yet:"},{"list":["No ride with real riders, and no trial with gloves or in sunlight.","Live voice between two real phones has not been tested.","No installs or usage numbers to report.","No screenshots of the main screens. The ones on this page are drawings."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Test standing still before testing on the move","blocks":[{"list":["Finish the accessibility and glove pass, including a screen-reader check.","Test with riders while stopped first: forming a group, checking a meeting point and using the headset controls. Anything used while moving needs a controlled setting after that.","Check what happens with poor signal, a location permission turned off, voice unavailable, and battery use.","Show clearly when a rider's position is old, so nobody trusts a stale dot."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Run the real scenario, not only the tests","blocks":[{"p":"The meeting point passed its tests and still had three bugs. I found them only by running a real place, with real towns, inside the app. The share card did the same for top speed: a number nobody looked at in a test was impossible to miss on a card."},{"p":"The free-only rule also shaped the product more than I expected. It decided the router, the voice design and where positions live. When a free tool was wrong for motorcycles, I had to find another free one rather than pay my way out."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","headline":"Less scrolling. A film worth saving."}},"matinee":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","kind":"product","headline":"A film diary that turns my ratings into what to watch next","summary":"Matinee is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It keeps a record of the films and series I watch, and every suggestion it makes says why it is there.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Interactive app","context":"Entertainment discovery · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"media types: films and series"},{"value":"Local","label":"recommendation scoring"},{"value":"Adaptive","label":"phone and wider layouts"}],"problem":"Finding a title and choosing what to watch are related but different tasks. Matinee connects browsing with a personal record of saved and watched titles, using that history to shape the next set of suggestions.","process":["I connected browsing with the personal record of saved, rated, and watched titles. The design question is how that history can make the next discovery session more relevant without becoming repetitive.","Language preferences, variety, and recognisable film/series labels inform the browsing experience. Navigation and poster density should adapt to the screen while keeping the selected title and next action clear."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep a title’s type visible","choice":"Movie/series identifiers appear in discovery and combined lists.","reason":"A person choosing a two-hour film has a different commitment from someone starting a series.","tradeoff":"A badge helps scanning but does not replace runtime and season information near a decision."},{"title":"Let personal history influence discovery","choice":"Ratings and watch patterns feed a local scoring process, with controls against repetitive franchises or genres.","reason":"Recommendations should reflect preference without becoming a list of near-duplicates.","tradeoff":"A scoring rule is not evidence of relevance. Cold-start and unexpected recommendations need separate evaluation."},{"title":"Adapt the structure to the screen","choice":"Phone navigation becomes a side rail at wider sizes, and poster grids expand.","reason":"The same task can use space differently without turning a larger screen into a stretched phone layout.","tradeoff":"Density should still preserve readable text, meaningful focus order, and a clear selected title."}],"outcome":"I connected discovery, watchlists, history and ratings so previous choices can inform the next one. Film and series context, language preferences and variety all have a place in that flow. I have not established a measured recommendation-accuracy result.","reflection":"I would ask whether people found something they wanted to watch, not just something they clicked. I would also check whether suggestions become too familiar after a few ratings.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare a new profile with a rated-history profile and ask participants why each suggestion feels relevant or repetitive.","Proposed measure: useful selections, unwanted repetition, and confidence choosing across preferred languages."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Current mobile architecture, adaptive layout, watchlist rules and local recommendation factors. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://matinee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/matinee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":42,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Less scrolling. [[A film worth saving.]]","scene":"Imagine having plenty of saved titles and still spending the evening deciding. Discovery only becomes useful when it helps a person make a choice that fits.","question":"How can personal history help without filling the feed with more of the same?","insight":"[[Preference and variety need to work together]]. Ratings can guide the next suggestions, while repetition and language choices deserve their own controls.","pivot":"I connected watching, rating, and discovery into a loop, with a layout that adapts to how much room the person has.","takeaway":"A recommendation earns its place by helping someone choose.","journey":[{"title":"Save","detail":"Keep the titles that interest you."},{"title":"Watch & rate","detail":"Give your history useful preference signals."},{"title":"Discover","detail":"Bring that context back into the next choice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the commitment visible.","Let the history influence the next screen.","Use the screen’s space deliberately."],"annotations":[{"title":"Discovery and tracking connect","detail":"The archived capture shows the browsing surface."},{"title":"Relevance is an evaluation question","detail":"Local scoring makes the approach concrete without establishing accuracy."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · history-to-discovery loop","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Viewer deciding what to watch next","context":"Balancing a personal watch history with a desire for something different.","need":"Choose a relevant title without repeatedly seeing near-duplicates.","friction":"A large catalogue creates more browsing without necessarily creating confidence.","implication":"Connect history with language, variety, and title context."}},"theme":{"layout":"cinema","mode":"dark","paper":"#0a0a0c","ink":"#f3f4f6","dim":"#b1b3c0","line":"#3d3541","accent":"#ff8eb5","wash":"#3e2030","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Outfit","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Something worth watching","process":"History without repetition","decisions":"Designing the next choice","outcome":"A personal discovery loop","reflection":"Finding relevance in practice"},"decisionHeading":"Help someone choose what to watch.","resultHeading":"A viewing history that leads somewhere new."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/matinee.webp","alt":"Matinee — archived product interface","caption":"Original Matinee discovery screen. Posters and titles provide the starting point for browsing films and series.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"v2":{"title":"A film diary that turns my ratings into [[what to watch next]]","hook":"Matinee is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It keeps a record of the films and series I watch, and every suggestion it makes says why it is there.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"My own brief: track what I watch and choose what to watch next"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"June to October 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android first, with a web companion"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Expo, React Native, TypeScript, SQLite, Firebase, TMDB"},{"label":"Status","value":"Working app, version 1.1.0. No usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Matinee on the web","url":"https://matinee.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"See it","summary":["The problem: finding a title and choosing what to watch tonight are different jobs. I wanted my own record of what I had seen and rated to help with the second one.","What I built: an Android app with four places (Discover, Upcoming, Stats and Library), a recommendation engine that runs on the phone, and sync for a second device.","The key idea: variety and reasons are rules in the code. Every pick names its reason, each chosen language gets room in the feed, and no franchise can fill the screen.","Where it stands: version 1.1.0, in three visual looks over five months. I have no usage numbers, and I have not measured how good the suggestions are."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/discover-wide.webp","alt":"Matinee's Discover screen on a wide window: a search bar, All, Movies and Series tabs, a row of genre chips, and a Recommended for You row of eight blurred posters with titles, years and the line Trending now","caption":"Discover on a wide window, in the pink design that came before the teal one. Each poster carries a Movie or Series badge and one short reason. Poster art is blurred on this page because it belongs to the studios.","width":1280,"height":720},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Choosing what to watch is a different job from browsing a catalogue","blocks":[{"p":"Browsing a catalogue is one job. Matinee is for the step after that: keeping track of what I have watched, saving what I want to see, and picking the next thing from what I already liked."},{"p":"It started from a brief I set myself. I built it for one phone first, a Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra, with the web as a companion. One line in my product notes sums it up: Matinee is a tracker, not a streaming browser."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/tracker.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: what stays upfront, what sits behind a menu, and what was left out, with the left-out items struck through","caption":"The rule, turned into three columns. Read the struck-through items: they are what streaming apps usually put first, and I kept them out.","width":1600,"height":770}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"There was no user research. I designed it for my own viewing, so this page shows the design and the build, not findings."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Four places, one button for tonight, and a title page under everything","blocks":[{"p":"The app has four places: Discover, Upcoming, Stats and Library. Any poster opens a title page. A floating button on every tab opens Pick for tonight, which asks for a mood, a type and a length and returns three titles."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/structure.webp","alt":"Map of the app: four cards for Discover, Upcoming, Stats and Library listing what each holds, with the title page and Pick for tonight underneath","caption":"The whole app on one page. The title page and the picker sit under all four places, not inside one of them.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"Discover reads from the top: search, then what kind of title, then genre, then the feed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/discover-annotated.webp","alt":"The Discover screen with six numbered notes: search, the All, Movies and Series tabs, genre chips, the Movie or Series badge, the reason line under each poster, and the four places at the bottom","caption":"Discover with notes. Look at notes 4 and 5: every poster says what it is and why it is there.","width":1600,"height":1270}]},{"p":"Upcoming lists releases in my chosen languages, with new seasons of series alongside films."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/upcoming-annotated.webp","alt":"Two Upcoming cards with four numbered notes on the release date, the type and language labels, the Theatres label and the Remind and Calendar actions","caption":"An Upcoming card from the first version. Note 2 records a later fix: without the language label, the filter looked broken.","width":1600,"height":960}]}]},{"id":"reasons","label":"Decision 1","nav":"A reason on every pick","heading":"Every suggestion says why it is there","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one short line under each pick, such as \"Directed by\" a director I rate well, \"You enjoy Thriller\" or \"Popular in Kannada\". The first version showed it as a banner on the title page."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/reasons.webp","alt":"A reason banner from the first version reading Since you loved, followed by a film title, above six reason phrases numbered in the order the app picks them","caption":"The six kinds of reason, in the order the app picks them. A title gets the first one that applies.","width":1600,"height":770}]},{"p":"In the build: the feed is put together on the phone in four steps. It reads my library, gathers candidate titles, scores each one, then arranges the feed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/pipeline.webp","alt":"Diagram of four steps, profile, candidates, score and arrange, with notes on the optional language model re-ranking and the cache","caption":"From my ratings to a feed of 120 titles. Step 3 is where the reason line comes from.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The biggest change was in how candidates are counted. At first, a title found through a film I rated 4 out of 10 counted as much as one found through a 10. A library I had rated with care still produced something close to a popularity chart."},{"p":"Now each source carries the weight of my rating for it. A 9 or 10 counts fully, a 6 counts about a third, and a 5 or lower hardly counts. A title I logged without rating counts a little more than a 6. This endorsement can add up to 50 points, more than any other term."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/score-terms.webp","alt":"Bar chart of the eleven scoring terms and their ranges, from minus 60 for a disliked director or actor to plus 50 for endorsement by my ratings","caption":"The limits of each term, redrawn from the scoring code. Popularity can add at most 8 points, on purpose.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"If I add a Gemini key, a language model re-ranks the top 60. I kept it as a re-ranker, not a replacement. Its order counts for 65% and the score for 35%, and the language and variety rules still run afterwards."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A reason makes a pick easier to trust, but a scoring rule is not proof that the picks are good. I have not measured that."}}]},{"id":"variety","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Room for every language","heading":"Each chosen language gets room, and no franchise can fill the feed","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: language chips on Upcoming and in settings. The app starts with seven: English, Hindi, Kannada, Tamil, Telugu, Korean and Japanese. Regional titles show up in the feed next to English ones."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/upcoming-phone.webp","alt":"The Upcoming screen at phone width in the dark theme: a search field, five language chips with English, Hindi and Kannada selected, an empty result, and a bottom bar with four icons","caption":"Upcoming at phone width in June. The language chips sit right under the search, so the filter is always in view. This search returned nothing.","width":473,"height":1024,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: the first rule gave every chosen language 15% of the feed. That breaks as soon as you choose many. Seven languages asked for 126 slots in a feed of 120, and nothing was left for what I actually watch."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/language-slots.webp","alt":"Two bars of 120 slots. Before: seven blocks of 18 that run past the end of the feed. Now: seven blocks of 6, and 78 slots shared by viewing habits","caption":"The same seven languages under the old rule and the new one. An illustration worked from the two rules, not a measured result.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"Now 40% of the feed is shared between the chosen languages, and the rest follows how much I watch in each. While the feed fills, two more rules run on every pick. A third title from the same franchise loses 25 points. Each genre already in the feed costs a little more."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Franchises are found by a hand-kept list of 12 names, such as Marvel and Star Wars. A series that is not on the list is never capped."}}]},{"id":"logging","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Three buttons, then More","heading":"Logging stays one tap away, and the rest moves under More","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: in the first version, a title page had six buttons of the same size. Watching a trailer looked as important as logging the film. Now three stay at the top: Watched, Watchlist and Rate. Everything else is under More."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/detail-actions.webp","alt":"Before and after: a June capture of six equal buttons with names blurred, beside a schematic of the October title page with three buttons, a play button on the poster and a More row","caption":"Left, a real capture. Right, a schematic I drew from the October spec, because I have no screenshot of that version yet.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Logging was short from the start. The first version asked when I watched it, with Today and Yesterday as one-tap answers."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/log-date.webp","alt":"Dark logging panel asking When did you watch it?, with Today selected, Yesterday beside it, and day, month and year fields below","caption":"The logging step from the first version. Today and Yesterday are buttons. The date fields are there for anything older.","width":815,"height":342}]},{"p":"In the build: a title can be logged as seen without a rating. Episode progress is stored apart from ratings, so ticking off episodes never asks for a score. A bookmark can sit alongside a Seen entry and its rating, and removing a title can be undone, ratings and episode logs included. The last three each have a check in the test suite."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"A row of buttons under every poster. Posters stay clean: tap opens the title, and a long press opens quick actions. The cost is that a long press is easy to miss."}}]},{"id":"colour","label":"Decision 4","nav":"One colour, two themes","heading":"One source colour, so the rebrand only had to change the hue","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: light and dark themes, both drawn from one brand colour. In August I moved the brand from pink to teal."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/palette.webp","alt":"The pink app icon and brand ramp above the teal app icon and ramp, eleven steps each, with three cards on ramps and contrast ratios","caption":"The two brand ramps, step for step. The darkness of each step is the same. Only the hue moved.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: in August I rebuilt the interface on Material Design 3, Google's design system for Android. Every colour in the app is one step of one of six tonal ramps, a ramp being a set of shades from near black to near white. Light and dark themes pick different steps from the same ramps."},{"p":"So the rebrand was a hue change. Each step kept its lightness and strength of colour. The app icon was recoloured by shifting the hue of its pixels, which kept its shading."},{"p":"One thing slipped. The dark theme uses true black surfaces, and I had tuned those by hand against the pink. After the swap they still had a pinkish-brown cast. I fixed them in the same change."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Values tuned by hand sit outside the system, and that is where the rebrand went wrong. Contrast also shifted a little: the main accent on white went from 6.5:1 to 5.7:1, still above the 4.5:1 that text needs."}}]},{"id":"layout","label":"Decision 5","nav":"Layout by window size","heading":"The window's width decides the layout, not the device","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: on a phone, the four places sit in a bottom bar and posters run three across. On a wider window the bar becomes a side rail and the grid widens, up to seven posters."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/window-sizes.webp","alt":"Five window outlines from compact to extra large, showing a bottom bar on the smallest and a side rail on the rest, with poster grids from 3 to 7 columns","caption":"Five window sizes, drawn from the layout code. dp is the unit Android uses so sizes look the same on any screen.","width":1600,"height":790}]},{"p":"In the build: one hook reads the window width and returns the size class, the margin, the number of poster columns and the number of stats columns. Every responsive screen reads from it, so a phone in landscape, a foldable and a browser window behave the same way at the same width."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A phone turned sideways counts as medium width and gets the side rail. In October I also had to restore the phone layout, keeping three poster columns and two columns of stats cards."}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Every colour pair is checked, after a contrast bug I caught myself","blocks":[{"p":"The first light theme had a contrast bug. I caught it in a capture and fixed it four minutes later."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/contrast-annotated.webp","alt":"A June capture of a title page in the light theme with three numbered notes: a row of streaming logos, six equal buttons, and dark fact cards whose dark text cannot be read","caption":"Note 3 is the bug: the fact cards kept their dark-theme colours in the light theme.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"The Material 3 rebuild made contrast part of the system. These are the accessibility changes in the project history:"},{"list":["The colour pairs in both themes were set to meet the WCAG AA level, the common accessibility standard: 4.5 to 1 for text, 3 to 1 for icons and other meaningful graphics.","Touch targets are at least 48 dp. A later fix padded the last two that were too small.","Charts carry descriptions for screen readers, and rating values are exposed to the browser.","The phone's reduce-motion setting is respected.","In dark mode, a toggled reminder button showed as a near-white pill. It now uses a darker container colour.","On the web, toggle buttons were given a role name that does not exist, which turned them into unlabelled boxes a keyboard could not reach. Fixed in August."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"I have not recorded a screen-reader pass on a real phone, and the October changes have not had a visual check on a device yet."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Everything works on the phone first, and the cloud is optional","blocks":[{"p":"The library lives in a database on the phone. The film data comes from TMDB, a public film and series database, using a key each person adds. Sync and the language model are optional extras."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: screens, what runs on the phone, and services outside the phone, with optional ones drawn dashed","caption":"What runs where. With neither optional service, the app still works fully on one device.","width":1600,"height":940}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Expo, React Native, TypeScript, Expo Router"],["Library","SQLite on the phone, with backups and restores"],["Film data","TMDB, with the person's own key"],["Accounts and sync","Firebase email sign-in and Firestore, optional"],["Suggestions","My own scoring code, plus Gemini if a key is added"],["Builds and updates","GitHub Actions and EAS Build and Update"]]}},{"p":"Sync was the hardest part to get right. A change is saved on the phone first and queued, then sent when the app can reach the server."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/sync.webp","alt":"Diagram of three steps, save, queue and send, above four rules: typed IDs, deletion markers, newer wins, and API keys never synced","caption":"Offline first. The four rules underneath each come from a real collision, listed in the table below.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"Small changes reach installed apps as an over-the-air update, with no reinstall. Anything that touches native code needs a new APK, the Android install file. A check in the build pipeline decides which one a push needs."},{"stats":[{"value":"36","label":"commits, June to October 2026"},{"value":"23","label":"named checks in the regression suite, run against real SQLite in memory"},{"value":"40+","label":"film-database requests behind one feed, now cached until the library changes"},{"value":"6","label":"tonal ramps behind every colour in both themes"}]},{"p":"The October notes record 20 checks passing. The other three were added the next day, for saved keys when secure storage fails."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/matinee/timeline.webp","alt":"Bar chart of commits by period: June 22, July 1, 9 August 8, 24 August 1, October 4, with what changed in each","caption":"The build by month. Most of the work came in June. The two August changes gave it its second and third look.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Speed","One feed costs over 40 requests, and every visit to Discover paid it in full. The feed is now cached for three hours, and any log, rating or dismissal clears it."],["Same number, two titles","A film and a series can share an ID in TMDB, so one could overwrite the other. Every record now carries its type as well as its number."],["Titles coming back","A title removed on one phone could return from the cloud copy. Deletions now leave a marker, and re-adding a title does not bring back its old rating."],["Keys and accounts","Sync used to be tied to the API key. In October it moved to email accounts, and keys stopped leaving the phone."],["Updates that never arrive","An APK built before update channels were set up reports \"up to date\" forever. Settings now shows the channel, so a build that needs replacing is visible."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"A working app, with its newest screens still to be checked on a phone","blocks":[{"p":"Matinee is at version 1.1.0. It runs as an Android app, with a web version as a companion. It needs a TMDB key to show any films, and a Gemini key only for the optional extras."},{"p":"I use Matinee every day myself."},{"p":"I have no usage numbers or feedback to report. These are also not done yet:"},{"list":["I have not measured how good the suggestions are.","The October changes, including the Library tools and the title page, still need a visual check on a real phone. I have no screenshots of the teal design yet.","Account sync is tested against an in-memory database. It still has to be tried on a second phone against the live project."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Check it on the phone, then test the suggestions","blocks":[{"list":["Run the device checks: layout, screen reader, notifications and sign-in.","Capture the teal design on the phone and replace the schematic on this page.","Test the suggestions: compare a new profile with one that has a rated history, and ask people why each pick feels relevant or repetitive."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Weight the evidence, and keep the system whole","blocks":[{"p":"Counting how many sources agreed on a title gave me a popularity chart. Weighting each source by how much I liked it gave me my own feed. The data was the same both times."},{"p":"The colour system turned a rebrand into a single change, except for the one table I had tuned by hand. That table was the only part that went wrong."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","headline":"Turn a time and place into a sky to keep."}},"birthsky":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","kind":"product","headline":"A star map of the night you were born, drawn in the browser and kept as a wallpaper.","summary":"BirthSky is a web page I designed and built on my own. You give it a name, a birth date, a time and a place. It works out the sky over that spot at that moment and lets you save it for a phone or a desktop.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working browser project","context":"Personalised visualisation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Time + place","label":"core input"},{"value":"3","label":"documented visual modes"},{"value":"2","label":"wallpaper formats"}],"problem":"A star map can be a technical chart or a personal object. BirthSky connects a simple biographical prompt to a visual reveal, then gives the person control over labels and the final wallpaper format.","process":["I organised the experience into a familiar prompt, a personal reveal, optional exploration, and a keepsake. Name, date, time, and place give the abstract sky a recognisable starting point.","The reveal needs understandable inputs. Unknown birth times, time zones, and any collection of personal details deserve clear explanations before a precise-looking image encourages confidence."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a recognisable personal question","choice":"A short form collects the time and location before revealing the sky.","reason":"The input makes an otherwise abstract astronomical chart meaningful to the person requesting it.","tradeoff":"Unknown birth times and historical time zones require careful explanation; a precise-looking map can still rest on uncertain inputs."},{"title":"Make annotation a choice","choice":"The result supports alternative modes and toggles for constellation lines and names.","reason":"A person may want to learn from the sky or simply use it as a visual keepsake.","tradeoff":"Switches need clear effects without making the result feel like a complex scientific console."},{"title":"Design the output as part of the journey","choice":"Phone and desktop wallpaper downloads follow the reveal.","reason":"A useful result should fit where the person intends to use it.","tradeoff":"Export must preserve legibility and composition across aspect ratios rather than just scale one screenshot."}],"outcome":"I designed the journey from entering a time and place to adjusting a star map and exporting a wallpaper. Annotation and output choices let the person decide what belongs in the final image. Time uncertainty and data handling need to stay clear throughout.","reflection":"I would make it easier to work with an approximate birth time before adding another visual style. The result is personal, so the person should understand which inputs shaped it and what those inputs can reasonably tell them.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: compare known dates and locations with an authoritative sky reference, including historical daylight-saving and time-zone cases.","Proposed iteration: explain any logging before submission and make uncertain birth-time choices visible in the final result."],"sources":[{"label":"StarMap/index.html and js/app.js","detail":"Input flow, result controls, modes, export entry points and configured logging integration."},{"label":"StarMap/js/astronomy.js and js/exporter.js","detail":"Coordinate calculations and wallpaper generation. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://birthsky.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":10,"pdfPage":45,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Turn a time and place [[into a sky to keep.]]","scene":"Imagine choosing a meaningful date and place, then seeing the sky interpreted as something personal. The experience has to connect a precise-looking visual to understandable inputs.","question":"How can a technical chart become a meaningful personal object?","insight":"[[The reveal and the keepsake are one journey]]. Time and place shape the view; annotation and aspect ratio shape how it is kept.","pivot":"I connected the personal prompt to a customisable sky and wallpaper outputs.","takeaway":"Make the inputs understandable enough to trust the reveal.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Enter the time and location."},{"title":"Reveal","detail":"See the sky rendered from those inputs."},{"title":"Make it yours","detail":"Choose labels and a visual mode."},{"title":"Keep","detail":"Export for a phone or desktop."}],"decisionLeads":["Begin with a personal question.","Let annotation become a choice.","Design the output as part of the experience."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal opening","detail":"The original product connects a biographical prompt to a sky view."},{"title":"A result beyond the page","detail":"Wallpaper exports give the reveal a place to live."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · input, render, export","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person creating a personal sky keepsake","context":"Entering a meaningful date and place, then choosing a wallpaper.","need":"Understand the inputs and keep a result that feels personal.","friction":"Uncertain times can produce a precise-looking picture.","implication":"Explain uncertainty and make visual annotation optional."}},"theme":{"layout":"constellation","mode":"dark","paper":"#030311","ink":"#e8e6f0","dim":"#b5b1ca","line":"#48415d","accent":"#d9bc73","wash":"#31291b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A moment that matters","process":"The reveal starts with the input","decisions":"Designing the keepsake","outcome":"A sky you can keep","reflection":"What an unknown time changes"},"decisionHeading":"Make the date, place and reveal understandable.","resultHeading":"A personal moment, made into a keepsake."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","alt":"BirthSky — archived product interface","caption":"Original BirthSky interface for entering a birth date, time and place to explore the sky.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"A star map of the night you were born, [[drawn in the browser]] and kept as a wallpaper.","hook":"BirthSky is a web page I designed and built on my own. You give it a name, a birth date, a time and a place. It works out the sky over that spot at that moment and lets you save it for a phone or a desktop.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A personal project. No client and no user research"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July 2026. The git history covers one day, from first release to version 1.0.4"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on phones and desktops. No sign-in"},{"label":"Stack","value":"HTML, CSS and plain JavaScript with no libraries. OpenStreetMap place search. Hosted on Netlify"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live, version 1.0.4. No usage numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Open BirthSky","url":"https://birthsky.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: a star map can be a technical chart or a personal object. I wanted one page that gives you both from four answers.","What I built: a static page with its own sky engine in plain JavaScript, three display modes, four switches and two wallpaper sizes.","The key idea: one painter draws everything. A mode only turns layers on or off, and a download is the same painter run again at full size.","Where it stands: live as version 1.0.4. The sky has not been checked against a reference, and the current version logs every visitor's answers to a Google Sheet without saying so. Both are next."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/result-stargazer.webp","alt":"BirthSky's result screen: a dark sky full of stars, constellation lines and labels, with a control bar across the top","caption":"The result screen in Stargazer mode, for the name \"Sample\", 1 January 2000, 9:00 pm, Mumbai. Every capture on this page uses these same test answers.","width":2400,"height":1500},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A date, a time and a place are enough to work out a sky","blocks":[{"p":"There was no client and no research. The brief was the line I put on the landing page: the night sky above you at the moment you were born, calculated, drawn and yours to keep."},{"p":"That line sets two jobs. The sky has to be worked out from the answers, not decorated. And the result has to be something a person keeps, so the page ends in a download, not in a chart."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/landing-form.webp","alt":"The BirthSky landing page on a desktop: a gold title, a short promise and a form with name, birth date, birth time and birth place","caption":"The landing page, filled in with the test answers. Four fields and one button. The faint lines behind it are a slowly turning star chart.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/phone-landing.webp","alt":"The BirthSky landing page on a phone: the title and the four-field form, with no subtitle","caption":"On a phone the subtitle is hidden and the form is compressed so all four fields and the button fit on one screen.","width":900,"height":1151,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"No interviews, no tests with users and no usage numbers. This page describes the product and its code, and nothing more."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Eight steps from four answers to a saved picture","blocks":[{"p":"The page finds the place, turns the local time into world time, and then works out where every star and planet sat in the sky. Two terms come up. Sidereal time is \"star time\": it tells you which part of the sky is overhead at a spot on Earth. A stereographic projection is a way of laying the curved sky flat, the way a world map flattens the globe."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of eight steps in two rows: four answers, find the place, find the moment, set the sky clock, place every object, flatten the dome, paint in layers, keep it. A red note below says the answers are also sent to a Google Sheet","caption":"Read the two rows left to right. The red note at the bottom is part of the flow today and has its own section below.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The result screen keeps every control in one bar and gives the rest of the window to the sky."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/annotated-result.webp","alt":"The result screen with six numbered notes on the back button, the title, the three modes, the four switches, the two downloads and the full-window sky","caption":"Notes 3 and 4 are the whole set of choices. Nothing else on the screen can be changed.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"form","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Four questions","heading":"Four questions, and the time is allowed to be unknown","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: four fields. Birth time starts at midnight and carries a hint, \"Local time at birth place\". Place suggestions appear after three letters."},{"p":"In the build: the typed place goes to OpenStreetMap's place search, which sends back a latitude and longitude. The local time then has to become world time, and that needs the time zone. I did not add a time-zone database. The page guesses the offset from longitude, at one hour for every 15 degrees."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/timezone.webp","alt":"Two BirthSky renders side by side for the same answers: one with the guessed offset of UTC+5, one with India's real offset of UTC+5:30. The second shows Delphinus and Cygnus at its left edge","caption":"Both pictures come from BirthSky's own engine. Only the offset differs. Look at the left edge of the second.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"No database means nothing extra to download or call. But the guess is wrong wherever clocks do not follow longitude. In India it is 30 minutes out, and daylight saving is ignored everywhere. A time left at midnight is also drawn as if it were known."}}]},{"id":"modes","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Three modes","heading":"Three modes for three uses of the same sky","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Stargazer is for reading the sky, with lines, names and planets. Gift adds the person's name, date, time and place to the picture. Pure Sky removes every word and line and leaves only light."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/result-gift.webp","alt":"The result screen in Gift mode: the same labelled sky with the name SAMPLE and a line of birth details in the lower left corner","caption":"Gift. Compare it with the lead image: the sky is the same, with the name and details added in the lower left.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/result-puresky.webp","alt":"The result screen in Pure Sky mode: stars and soft glows only, with no lines or labels","caption":"Pure Sky. The same stars with no lines, names or planets.","width":1600,"height":1000}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: there is one painting function with up to 11 layers. A mode only decides which layers are drawn. The same function paints the screen and both downloads, so a mode never looks different in the saved file."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/layers.webp","alt":"Table of 11 layers painted bottom to top, with filled or open dots showing which appear in Stargazer, Gift and Pure Sky","caption":"The mode is a column in this table. Open dots are the layers the switches can hide.","width":1600,"height":930}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Gift writes personal details into the image itself. Anyone who sees the wallpaper sees the name, birth date, time and place."}}]},{"id":"wallpaper","label":"Decision 3","nav":"A wallpaper, not a chart","heading":"A wallpaper that fills the frame, not a round chart","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the sky runs edge to edge in a rectangle. On a tall phone screen it is zoomed in."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/annotated-gift.webp","alt":"A downloaded Gift wallpaper for a phone with five numbered notes, and an enlarged strip showing the name SAMPLE above a gold rule and a line with the date, time and place","caption":"Note 2 was a fix on launch day. Before it, the portrait view squeezed the shapes.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: the download is not a screenshot. The painter runs again on a hidden canvas at 2160×3840 for a phone or 3840×2160 for a desktop, and the browser saves it as a PNG file. Star sizes, line widths and text are scaled to the size of the picture, so they hold at that resolution."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/export-desktop-stargazer.webp","alt":"A downloaded desktop wallpaper at 3840 by 2160 in Stargazer mode, with constellation lines and names","caption":"The desktop file in Stargazer, shown here reduced. The names stay readable at full size.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/export-phone-puresky.webp","alt":"A downloaded phone wallpaper at 2160 by 3840 in Pure Sky mode, stars only","caption":"The phone file in Pure Sky. No words at all.","width":900,"height":1600,"kind":"tall"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"To fill the corners, the page also draws stars and planets down to about 34 degrees below the horizon, and there is no horizon line or compass. So the picture includes some of the sky nobody at that spot could see. For the test answers, 110 of the 304 catalogue stars the page placed were below the horizon."}}]},{"id":"names","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Indian names","heading":"Indian names are one switch away","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the Indian Names switch changes the labels, not the sky. Sirius becomes Lubdhaka, Betelgeuse becomes Ardra and Aldebaran becomes Rohini. The Sun, the Moon and the five planets change too, so Mars, Jupiter and Saturn read Mangala, Guru and Shani."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/names-english.webp","alt":"Close-up of Orion, Taurus and Canis Major with English star names such as Sirius, Rigel and Betelgeuse","caption":"Switch off. The usual English names.","width":1600,"height":904},{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/names-indian.webp","alt":"The same close-up with Indian names such as Lubdhaka, Rajanya and Ardra, and the planets labelled Mangala, Guru and Shani","caption":"Switch on. Stars without an Indian name in the list, such as Bellatrix and Saiph, keep their English ones.","width":1600,"height":904}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: the swap is a lookup at the moment a label is drawn. Positions are never recalculated, so the switch is instant on screen and in the downloads."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The list is short: 17 stars plus the seven bodies of the solar system. Constellation names stay in Latin."}}]},{"id":"privacy","label":"Your answers","nav":"What happens to your answers","heading":"The current version logs every reveal to a Google Sheet, and does not say so","blocks":[{"p":"The sky is worked out and painted in the browser, and the download goes straight to the device. But each time someone presses Reveal My Sky, the current version also sends a row to a Google Sheet: the time, the name, the birth date and time, the place, and its latitude and longitude."},{"p":"There is no notice on the page about this today, and no consent step. The place text also goes to OpenStreetMap's search while you type."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/data-map.webp","alt":"Diagram of three destinations for the answers: the browser, which uses everything; OpenStreetMap place search, which receives the place text; and a Google Sheet, highlighted in red, which receives the time, name, birth date, birth time, place, latitude and longitude","caption":"The red column is the one to read. It is what the code does today.","width":1600,"height":680}]},{"aside":{"title":"What this means for this page.","detail":"I make no claim that BirthSky keeps your details private. Adding a clear notice, or removing the logging, is first on the list of what is next."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Eight files, no libraries, and a morning of fixes on a phone","blocks":[{"p":"The whole product is a static page: one HTML file, one stylesheet and six scripts, 3,262 lines in all. The sky engine and the painter are written for this page, with no astronomy library. The star positions come from a catalogue of bright stars built into the page."},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Page","HTML and CSS, with a dark theme and three web fonts"],["Sky engine","Plain JavaScript: day count, star time, height and direction of each object, projection"],["Painter","The browser's canvas, one function for screen and file"],["Data","416 bright stars with position, brightness and colour; 21 nebulae, galaxies and clusters"],["Place search","OpenStreetMap"],["Hosting","Netlify, with security headers"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"416","label":"stars in the catalogue, down to magnitude 5.5"},{"value":"69","label":"constellations with lines, 334 line segments"},{"value":"3,000","label":"faint decorative dots in every sky"},{"value":"15","label":"commits, all on 12 July 2026"}]},{"p":"The constellation lines are not typed in by hand. For each constellation, the page joins its catalogue stars with the shortest set of links that connects them all. That always produces clean lines, but not the traditional figures. Orion is a tree of short links, not the hunter."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of 15 commits on 12 July 2026: three around 1:25 am for the release, then a cluster between 8:25 and 10:27 am for phone layout, sheet logging and caching","caption":"The history starts at a finished first version, so the work before it is not in the log. Seven of the 15 commits are phone layout fixes.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/phone-result.webp","alt":"The result screen on a phone, with the control bar stacked in three rows above the sky","caption":"On a phone the bar stacks: title, then modes, then switches and downloads.","width":900,"height":1948,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/birthsky/phone-gift.webp","alt":"The Gift mode result on a phone, with the name and birth details at the bottom of the sky","caption":"Gift on a phone. The overlay is placed by proportion, so it lands in the same spot at any size.","width":900,"height":1948,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Phone layout","The header did not stay pinned, the title was clipped, date and time fields were hard to read in dark mode, and the hidden result layer could sit on top of the form. Seven commits fixed these, ending in a compact layout that fits the form on one screen."],["Portrait sky","On a tall screen the sky looked squeezed. The painter now zooms in when the frame is taller than it is wide."],["Updates not showing","Browsers kept old copies of the scripts. I removed long-term caching and added version numbers to the script links."],["Checks","There are no automated tests and no build step. I have no test results to report."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live as version 1.0.4, with no usage numbers and no accuracy check","blocks":[{"p":"BirthSky is live as version 1.0.4. I have no usage numbers to share. The only record of use is the sheet, and it holds visitors' details, so I do not draw on it here."},{"p":"These limits are in the current version:"},{"list":["The positions of stars and planets have not been checked against a reference sky for any date. The planets come from a simplified orbit model.","The time zone is guessed from longitude, with no daylight saving.","The sky is always drawn as night. For a daytime birth, the Sun shows as a glowing dot among the stars.","The 3,000 faint dots are decoration, in the same pattern for every sky. The Milky Way glow varies a little each time it is drawn, so a download is not an exact copy of the screen.","The sky is a single image with no text description for screen readers."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Fix the logging first, then the inputs","blocks":[{"list":["Add a clear notice beside the Reveal My Sky button that says what is logged and why, or remove the logging. This comes before anything else.","Look up the real time zone for the place and date, including daylight saving.","Check star and planet positions against a reference sky for a set of known dates and places, and correct what is off.","Mark a sky that was drawn from an unknown birth time.","Give the sky a short text description for screen readers."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A sharp picture can hide a rough input","blocks":[{"p":"The picture looks exact whatever goes in. A guessed time zone, a default midnight and stars below the horizon all come out just as crisp as a correct sky. The work that matters next is in the inputs, not in the painting."},{"p":"One painter for the screen and the file was worth it. Every mode and switch worked in the downloads from the first release, because there was no second drawing to keep in step. And a feature added on launch day, like the logging, needs the same care about what it tells people as everything else."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","headline":"How much bigger? Show the difference."}},"scalesee":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","kind":"product","headline":"A billion is easy to write and hard to picture, so I drew it to scale.","summary":"ScaleSee is a web tool I designed and built on my own. Pick two amounts of the same kind, and it draws them side by side at the same scale.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working browser product","context":"Quantitative explanation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"11","label":"categories listed in the source"},{"value":"2","label":"scale modes"},{"value":"Shareable","label":"comparison URLs"}],"problem":"A ratio can be mathematically clear and still be difficult to picture. ScaleSee turns two quantities into a shared visual comparison, choosing a representation that fits the kind of quantity instead of using the same chart for everything.","process":["I selected a visual reference appropriate to each kind of quantity: a ground line for height, nesting for land, or a shared course for speed. The reference is part of the explanation.","An extreme ratio creates a useful design tension: showing true scale can make one quantity nearly disappear. A compressed alternative needs an explicit label that remains with a shared comparison or exported image."],"decisions":[{"title":"Choose the picture to fit the quantity","choice":"Height shares a ground line, land can be nested, and speed uses a common course.","reason":"A meaningful reference can make a ratio easier to interpret than a generic bar.","tradeoff":"Every visual metaphor introduces assumptions; those assumptions need to be stated where they affect the comparison."},{"title":"Keep scale transformations explicit","choice":"True scale and logarithmic scale are separate modes.","reason":"When a small quantity almost disappears, that can be the honest answer; a compressed alternative should declare the change.","tradeoff":"A log view can make a scene more legible while reducing intuitive proportionality."},{"title":"Let the comparison leave the tool","choice":"A URL carries the selected comparison and an image export preserves the current drawing.","reason":"An explanation is more useful when someone can bring it into the conversation that prompted it.","tradeoff":"An exported image needs labels and scale context because it no longer has interactive controls beside it."}],"outcome":"I matched comparison scenes to the quantity being explored and kept the scale mode explicit. Sharing and image export let a comparison leave the tool with its context. Extreme ratios still need testing for correct interpretation.","reflection":"Sometimes a proportional picture is too small to read and an adjusted view is easy to misread. I would check whether someone can identify the scale mode and explain what the picture does to the original numbers.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask readers to estimate the ratio before and after each view, including an extreme comparison and a logarithmic one.","Proposed check: confirm labels, units, assumptions, and scale mode survive in every shared or exported artifact."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s in it and Notes on the numbers","detail":"Category inventory, visual metaphors, scale-mode distinction and sharing/export. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://scalesee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":48,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"How much bigger? [[Show the difference.]]","scene":"Imagine comparing a million with a billion. The arithmetic is simple; making the difference feel concrete is another task.","question":"When does making both quantities visible change the meaning of the picture?","insight":"[[Legibility and proportional truth can pull apart]]. True scale may make one quantity almost disappear; a logarithmic view needs to declare its transformation.","pivot":"I used category-specific scenes and explicit scale modes, with enough context to travel in a shared image.","takeaway":"An explanation should keep its assumptions visible when it leaves the tool.","journey":[{"title":"Compare","detail":"Choose quantities of the same kind."},{"title":"See","detail":"Use a scene that fits the quantity."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Understand the selected scale mode."},{"title":"Share","detail":"Carry the labels and context into the output."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the quantity choose the picture.","Make a transformed scale explicit.","Keep the explanation intact when shared."],"annotations":[{"title":"A scene for the quantity","detail":"The product gives a ratio a concrete visual reference."},{"title":"Context travels with it","detail":"Scale, units, and assumptions matter in an exported comparison."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · comparison and export","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person trying to explain a large difference","context":"Comparing two quantities for a conversation or illustration.","need":"Understand the ratio and share it without losing context.","friction":"Extreme differences make small values disappear or invite misleading compression.","implication":"Declare scale changes and preserve labels in exports."}},"theme":{"layout":"comparison","mode":"light","paper":"#f2f1f0","ink":"#19181c","dim":"#625f68","line":"#d3d0d7","accent":"#8b3fa8","wash":"#eddff2","bodyFont":"Inter Tight","displayFont":"Inter Tight","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difference hard to picture","process":"The reference does the explaining","decisions":"Designing an honest comparison","outcome":"The ratio, made visible","reflection":"Checking what the reader sees"},"decisionHeading":"Choose a reference people can understand.","resultHeading":"Two quantities. One understandable relationship."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","alt":"ScaleSee — archived product interface","caption":"Original ScaleSee comparison surface, with two quantities drawn against the same scale.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"A billion is easy to write and hard to picture, so I [[drew it to scale]].","hook":"ScaleSee is a web tool I designed and built on my own. Pick two amounts of the same kind, and it draws them side by side at the same scale.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"Big numbers are easy to read and hard to picture"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"August 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on desktop and phone. It opens from a file, with no server"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Drawings in SVG. No libraries"},{"label":"Status","value":"Working web tool. Not tested with readers, no usage numbers"}],"links":[{"label":"Open ScaleSee","url":"https://scalesee.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: written down, a million and a billion differ by three zeros. As cash, one is a single brick of notes and the other is a block taller than a person.","What I built: a web tool with 11 kinds of quantity, each drawn its own way, with true and log scale, a link for every comparison and a picture you can save.","The key idea: show the true gap first. When a picture has to be squashed to fit, it says so.","Where it stands: it works on desktop and phone, in light and dark. Nobody has tested it with readers yet, and I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/lead.webp","alt":"ScaleSee comparing $1 million with $1 billion: the sentence \"$1 billion is 1,000× more than $1 million\" above a drawing of one small purple brick of cash and a large green block of cash, with a grey person beside each","caption":"The default comparison, $1 million against $1 billion. The sentence gives the ratio. The drawing shows it as stacked $100 bills, with a person for scale.","width":1600,"height":1183},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Three zeros on paper are a thousand times the cash","blocks":[{"p":"A ratio can be easy to read and still hard to picture. Written down, $1 million and $1 billion look alike: the second just has three more zeros. In $100 bills, the first is one brick about 9 cm thick. The second is a block 2.1 metres tall."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram: on the left, $1,000,000 and $1,000,000,000 written as numbers; on the right, the ScaleSee drawing of one brick of cash beside a block of 1,000 bricks","caption":"The problem in one picture. On the left, the two numbers as we usually see them. On the right, the same pair drawn by ScaleSee at true scale.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"I wanted a tool where you pick any two amounts of the same kind and see them side by side, to the same scale. The same idea works for height, land, weight, time and six other kinds of quantity."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I have no user research or test sessions for ScaleSee, so this page does not quote any. It shows the product and how I made it."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Two amounts in, one sentence and one drawing out","blocks":[{"p":"You choose a category, then type two amounts. Each side has a number and a unit. Money also has a currency, so 1 crore can sit next to $1 million."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/annotated-page.webp","alt":"The top of the ScaleSee page with six numbered notes: the category strip, the two inputs, the ready-made pairs, the sentence, the scale switch and the drawing","caption":"The page from the top. Note 4 is the sentence, and note 6 is the drawing it describes.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"The sentence states the ratio and how many of the smaller one fit inside the bigger one. Then it borrows a pair of real things with about the same gap. A 1,000× gap reads as \"roughly a person next to the Burj Khalifa\"."},{"p":"There are 11 categories, and each one draws its amounts in its own way."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/categories.webp","alt":"Grid of eleven drawings from ScaleSee: money as cash blocks, height as buildings, distance as roads, land as plots, weight as a balance, temperature as thermometers, time as filling bars, data as squares, speed as a race, volume as cubes and energy as a ladder","caption":"All eleven drawings, captured from the working app. Each one is built for one kind of quantity.","width":1600,"height":1170}]},{"p":"Under the drawing, a table gives both amounts in other terms. For money, that means the time it takes to count the cash out at one dollar a second, or what it weighs. At the bottom is a list of real reference points. Pick one and it loads on the right side, so you can walk up the scale."}]},{"id":"pictures","label":"Decision 1","nav":"A picture for each quantity","heading":"Each quantity gets a picture that fits it, not one chart for all","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: money is cash stacked into a block. Land is the small plot laid in the corner of the big one. Weight is a balance. Speed is a race down the same 400 m lap."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/land.webp","alt":"Land comparison of 1 cent and 1 acre: a green square plot with a small purple square in its near corner, a house and a tree, and the text \"1 acre holds 100 of 1 cent\"","caption":"Land. One acre holds 100 cents. The purple square in the near corner is one cent, and a house stands on the plot for scale.","width":1600,"height":1060},{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/weight.webp","alt":"Weight comparison of 70 kg and 6 tonnes: a balance tipped down to the right under a large green block, with a small purple block on the raised pan","caption":"Weight. 70 kg against 6 tonnes. Underneath, the heavier side is also counted in cars.","width":1600,"height":1060},{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/speed.webp","alt":"Speed comparison of 5 km/h and 104 km/h: two cars on two identical road laps, with the times for each written underneath","caption":"Speed. Walking pace against a cheetah on the same lap. The fast one finishes in 13.8 seconds, the slow one in 4.8 minutes.","width":1600,"height":1031}],"layout":"three"},{"p":"In the build: each category has its own drawing function. All eleven take the same input, the two amounts in one base unit and the scale mode. Each returns a picture on a fixed canvas 1,000 units wide and 560 tall, plus a one-line note. A shared kit draws the solids, the silhouettes and the landmarks, so the eleven pictures look like one product."},{"p":"The height scene got the most care. Its landmarks are drawn from their real elevations."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/annotated-height.webp","alt":"The height drawing of 5.9 ft against 828 m with six numbered notes: the ruler, the landmark lines, the ratio bracket, the tiny person, the faded landmarks and the Burj Khalifa model","caption":"The height scene, part by part. Note 4 is the honest answer at this scale: the person is a speck.","width":1600,"height":1250}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Every picture carries an assumption. The cash block assumes $100 bills, about 0.0115 m³ per million. Landmark heights are real but rounded. And eleven drawing functions are eleven things to keep correct, where one shared chart would have been one."}}]},{"id":"scale","label":"Decision 2","nav":"True scale first","heading":"The true gap comes first, and a squashed picture says it is squashed","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: every comparison opens at true scale. If one side is too small to see, a line under the drawing says that is the point, and that log scale will show where it is."},{"p":"A log scale, short for logarithmic scale, gives each tenfold step the same space. Huge gaps then fit on one screen, but the sizes are no longer in proportion."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/height-true.webp","alt":"Height drawing at true scale: a ruler from 100 to 800 metres, the green Burj Khalifa, and a purple label for 5.9 ft above a person too small to see","caption":"True scale. At 828 m against 5.9 ft, the person is a speck, and the note under the drawing says so.","width":1600,"height":1060},{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/height-log.webp","alt":"Height drawing at log scale: a purple person now clearly visible beside the green Burj Khalifa, with no ruler on the left","caption":"Log scale. Both now fit. The ruler is gone, because metre labels on a squashed axis would be false.","width":1600,"height":1060}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: one shared function turns the two amounts into sizes for every category that follows the switch. At true scale it is a straight proportion, with a small minimum so the little side never vanishes. At log scale it squashes. Then each drawing removes whatever would now be false: the height ruler, and the person standing beside the cash and the cubes."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/scale-rules.webp","alt":"Diagram in three groups: seven categories that follow the scale switch, two whose picture is not a size, and two that are a count or a ladder, with notes on what changes at true and at log scale","caption":"Not every category should obey the switch. Four of them ignore it, because their picture is not a size that can be squashed.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Log scale makes a picture easier to read and less true. I kept true scale as the default and let the reader choose, rather than choose the readable view for them."}}]},{"id":"names","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Name the thing","heading":"A number is called after the real thing it is close to","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: type 5.9 ft and the label says \"about a person\", drawn as a person. Type 828 m and it says \"the Burj Khalifa\". Put 5 ft against 7 ft and you get two people at their true heights, not two bars."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/people.webp","alt":"Height drawing of 5 ft against 7 ft: a purple person and a taller green person on a ruler marked in centimetres and metres, both labelled \"about a person\"","caption":"5 ft against 7 ft. Both are named \"about a person\" and drawn as people, at 1.52 m and 2.13 m.","width":1600,"height":1031}]},{"p":"In the build: one small rule looks through that category's list of real things for the closest one, compared by ratio. If it is within 1.35 times, the amount takes its name. That one answer decides both which model is drawn and what the label says."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/naming.webp","alt":"Flow diagram: a value comes in, the closest reference is found, and the result is an exact name, an \"about\" name within 1.35 times, or the plain measurement; below, the three places the name is used","caption":"One rule with two jobs. The sentence above the drawing uses a separate, looser match against 17 known pairs.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"I applied the same thinking to money. A flat white used to read \"$0.005 thousand\". It now falls back to plain numbers and reads \"$5\"."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A name is a rounding. \"About a person\" covers anything from about 1.26 m to 2.3 m. So the measured figure always sits under the name, in a monospaced type that lines numbers up."}}]},{"id":"phone","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Phone layout","heading":"On a phone, the drawing comes before everything else","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: on a phone, the drawing runs edge to edge and pans sideways inside its own frame. A fade on the right edge and a line under it say there is more to see. Everything you can tap is at least 40 px."},{"p":"In the build: the first phone layout put the drawing's card 810 px down an 812 px screen. The main thing sat below the fold, the bottom of the first screen before any scrolling. I cut the space above it, and the card now starts at 666 px."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/phone-fold.webp","alt":"Two phone screens side by side, before and after, with a dashed line where the drawing card starts: at the very bottom of the screen before, and about four fifths down after","caption":"Before and after on the same 812 px screen. Every row saved above the drawing is scrolling the reader no longer has to do.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"One fix needed a second pass. The category strip is three times wider than a phone, so the chosen category is scrolled back into view when it changes. It is checked again once the web font loads, because every label then gets wider. Without that pass, a shared link could open with its category off the screen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/phone-drawing.webp","alt":"ScaleSee on a phone, scrolled to the height drawing, which runs to both screen edges and is cut off on the right, with the line \"drag the picture sideways\" underneath","caption":"The drawing on a phone. It is cut off on the right on purpose, and the line under it ends with \"drag the picture sideways\".","width":1125,"height":2436,"kind":"tall"}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A drawing that pans hides part of itself until you drag it. The other option was to shrink it to the width of the screen, which keeps it whole but makes everything in it smaller."}}]},{"id":"look","label":"The look","nav":"Light and dark","heading":"One drawing in two palettes, with colours chosen by contrast","blocks":[{"p":"The two sides are always violet and green. Violet keeps a strong blue in it where the green has none. The aim is that the pair stays apart for people with colour blindness too. I have not tested that with anyone."},{"p":"Both colours were worked out, not picked by eye. Each is the darkest point on its hue that still carries white label text at a contrast of 4.5 to 1, the usual minimum for small text."},{"p":"In the build: the drawings take their sky, ground and text colours from a palette in the script. Switching the theme swaps that palette and draws the picture again. The violet and green are read back from the page styles each time, which is how they can be lighter in dark mode."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/eiffel-light.webp","alt":"Height drawing in light mode: a purple giraffe label at 5.5 m beside a green Eiffel Tower at 330 m, with a grey pyramid and a tree in the background","caption":"A giraffe against the Eiffel Tower, in light mode. The tower is 60 times taller.","width":1600,"height":1060},{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/eiffel-dark.webp","alt":"The same height drawing in dark mode, with a dark sky and ground and lighter violet and green","caption":"The same comparison in dark mode. Sky, ground and text all change, and the violet and green get lighter.","width":1600,"height":1060}],"layout":"two"}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"The drawing has a text version, and the colours meet AA","blocks":[{"p":"I checked ScaleSee against WCAG 2.1 AA, the common standard for accessible web pages. Every text colour, including the text inside the drawings, clears 4.5 to 1 against the surface it sits on, in both themes."},{"list":["The drawing is marked as an image, with a description written from the comparison: the sentence, the scale used and the note.","A table under the drawing gives the same figures as text, with a caption and proper headers.","Every control has a name. The number boxes have real labels, and the category and scale buttons report which one is on.","Focus shows as a solid 2 px ring. Hit targets are at least 32 px, and 40 px on touch screens.","The moving cars and filling bars stay still when the system asks for reduced motion.","The faded landmarks in the height scene are below 3 to 1 on purpose. They are decoration, and each one is also named on a line."]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/facts-table.webp","alt":"Table titled \"put another way\" comparing $1 million and $1 billion in six rows, such as counting it out one a second: 11.6 days against 31.7 years","caption":"The table under the drawing for $1 million against $1 billion. Counted out at one dollar a second, the billion takes 31.7 years.","width":1600,"height":574},{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/keyboard.webp","alt":"The keyboard shortcuts panel listing ten keys, including S to swap sides, T for true or log scale and P to save the picture","caption":"The keyboard shortcuts. Press the question mark key to open them.","width":800,"height":1094,"kind":"tall"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"This was my own check against the standard. I have no record of anyone using a screen reader on ScaleSee."}}]},{"id":"share","label":"Sharing","nav":"Links and pictures","heading":"Every comparison is a link, and the drawing can be saved","blocks":[{"p":"The comparison is written into the page address after every change: the category, both amounts with their units and currency, and the scale. Copy the link and it opens the same picture."},{"p":"Save the picture writes the drawing out as a PNG image, 2,000 by 1,120 pixels."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/saved-picture.webp","alt":"A saved PNG of the height drawing of 5.9 ft against the Burj Khalifa, with the ruler, landmarks and labels but no sentence or page around it","caption":"A saved picture of 5.9 ft against the Burj Khalifa. It holds the drawing and nothing else.","width":1600,"height":896}]},{"aside":{"title":"What is missing.","detail":"The saved picture leaves the sentence and the note behind. A height picture saved in log scale does not say it was squashed. That is the first thing I would fix."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Eleven drawing functions around one small state","blocks":[{"p":"ScaleSee uses no libraries and needs no server. It opens straight from a file. A short build script can also fold everything into one self-contained file, to send or host."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/scalesee/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: what the tool knows (categories and reference points), what you chose (the state and the link), and what you see (the sentence, the drawing, the table), with a shared drawing kit and delivery underneath","caption":"How it fits together. The state in the middle is small: a category, two sides and a scale mode.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Page","HTML and one stylesheet"],["Logic","Plain JavaScript in seven files, with no framework"],["Drawings","SVG built by my own functions. The animation is SVG too, and is skipped under reduced motion"],["Type","Space Grotesk for the sentence, Inter Tight for the interface, DM Mono for every number, Fraunces for the name"],["Build","A Node.js script that inlines everything into one HTML file"],["Checks","None automated. There are no tests"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"11","label":"categories, each with its own drawing"},{"value":"125","label":"real reference points across them"},{"value":"46","label":"ready-made pairs to try"},{"value":"3,085","label":"lines of HTML, CSS and JavaScript"},{"value":"3","label":"commits, all on 31 August 2026"}]},{"p":"The hardest parts were small and exact."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What was hard, and what I did"],"rows":[["Landmarks","Each one is stored as its setback heights and widths divided by its own height, so the outline stays right at any size. The Burj Khalifa steps left and right on its way up, the way the real tower spirals."],["Tiny amounts","At true scale a small side can round down to nothing. It keeps a minimum size, and the note under the drawing says the speck is the real answer."],["Phone strip","A shared link could open with its category off the screen, because the labels grew wider when the web font arrived. The strip now re-centres after the font loads."],["The name","The wordmark read \"Scale See\", because its two halves were laid out as separate boxes with a gap between them. It is now one word."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"It works, and nobody has tested it with readers","blocks":[{"p":"ScaleSee works on desktop and phone, in light and dark. I built it in August 2026, and its project history has three commits, all on 31 August."},{"p":"I have no usage numbers, feedback or test results. These are also true:"},{"list":["Landmark figures are real but rounded, for example 828 m for the Burj Khalifa and 150 tonnes for a blue whale.","Currency rates are rough and fixed in the code: 83 rupees, 0.92 euros, 0.79 pounds and 150 yen to the dollar.","There are no automated tests. The naming rule and the scale rules have only been checked by eye.","A few labels still slip between singular and plural, for example \"1 litres\"."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Find out whether people read the pictures correctly","blocks":[{"list":["Ask readers to guess a ratio before and after each view, including an extreme gap and a log-scale picture. That would show whether the drawings help, and whether log scale misleads.","Put the sentence and the scale into the saved picture, so it keeps its meaning when it is shared.","Add tests for the naming rule and the scale rules.","Fix the singular and plural labels."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"The speck is the answer, so help people find it","blocks":[{"p":"I learned to treat a tiny side as the answer, not as a bug to hide. The work went into helping people see it: a note under the drawing, a name they know, and a switch that is open about what it changes."},{"p":"I also learned that a number reads better as a thing. \"About a person\" says more than \"1.8 m\", as long as the real number stays right under it."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","headline":"Where would this place be as the plates move?"}},"where-would-i-be":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","kind":"product","headline":"Plate motion is too slow to see, so I built a globe that fast-forwards it.","summary":"Where Would I Be…? is a 3D globe I designed and built on my own. Pick a place, drag the clock, and watch the ground under it ride its tectonic plate for up to 500 million years.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working browser product","context":"Scientific storytelling · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"place-led starting question"},{"value":"Progressive","label":"details on demand"},{"value":"Modelled","label":"extrapolation, not prediction"}],"problem":"Plate motion is slow enough to be invisible in everyday life and large enough to reshape a world over time. This project gives that scale a personal anchor: choose a place, move the clock, and follow the ground beneath it.","process":["I gave the opening one task: choose a place. A time control and a short result then introduce motion, with optional detail kept out of the main globe.","The visual story needs to carry its qualification with it. Constant-motion extrapolation can invite exploration, but the result should not read as a reconstruction or prediction of the continents."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give each stage one main task","choice":"Choose a place first, then explore time, with optional comparison later.","reason":"A clear sequence can make a complex subject approachable without presenting every control at once.","tradeoff":"Progressive disclosure should not hide the model’s most important qualification until after someone has formed a false impression."},{"title":"Protect the space where the answer is drawn","choice":"The design keeps the main globe clear and groups controls in a bottom dock.","reason":"The selected point and its path are the explanation; a floating panel over them would compete with the result.","tradeoff":"A dock must adapt to available height so it does not consume the same space on a small screen."},{"title":"Make the scientific boundary legible","choice":"The source describes plate movement as a constant-motion extrapolation with increasing limits over long horizons.","reason":"Personal storytelling can encourage exploration without claiming to know the future shape of continents.","tradeoff":"A smooth animation can still imply certainty, so the qualification belongs near the time result."}],"outcome":"I used a chosen location as the starting point for exploring time on the globe. Time controls, comparison and optional layers support the exploration, while the model limit stays near the result. The long-term path is an extrapolation, not a geological forecast.","reflection":"I would ask someone to explain why the point moves and why the long-term path is conditional. The interaction should leave them with a useful question about plate motion, not misplaced confidence in a prediction.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor to describe what changed and what assumptions the model makes after a short exploration.","Proposed checks: narrow-screen globe visibility, reduced-motion behaviour, keyboard interaction, and whether uncertainty stays visible at extreme dates."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, The layout rule and How much to trust it","detail":"Interaction sequence, unobstructed visual space and constant-motion model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://wherewouldibe.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":51,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Where would this place be [[as the plates move?]]","scene":"Imagine following the patch of crust beneath a familiar place over a time span you cannot experience. One personal point of reference gives deep time somewhere to begin.","question":"How can a compelling journey keep the model’s limits in view?","insight":"[[A smooth animation is still conditional]]. Holding present plate velocities constant creates an extrapolation whose limits grow with the horizon.","pivot":"I kept the globe clear, began with a place, and let the time control reveal a concise answer before offering deeper detail.","takeaway":"Let the story invite exploration without turning a model into a prediction.","journey":[{"title":"Anchor","detail":"Start from one familiar location."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Move the time control and follow its path."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the result alongside the model’s assumptions."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the first screen one useful question.","Protect the space where the answer is drawn.","Keep the scientific boundary near the result."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal anchor","detail":"The selected point connects a large timescale to a familiar location."},{"title":"Details on demand","detail":"The interface leaves the globe room to explain the path."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · model and interaction sequence","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Curious visitor exploring deep time","context":"Using a familiar location to make plate movement tangible.","need":"Follow a place through time and understand what the model means.","friction":"A smooth moving globe can look more certain than an extrapolation is.","implication":"Keep the model qualification near the time result."}},"theme":{"layout":"deep-time","mode":"dark","paper":"#05050c","ink":"#efedf8","dim":"#b4b0cb","line":"#48435d","accent":"#3ddbff","wash":"#152f3b","bodyFont":"Space Grotesk","displayFont":"Bricolage Grotesque","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar place, another time","process":"A personal anchor for deep time","decisions":"Designing the time journey","outcome":"The moving point","reflection":"Where the model stops"},"decisionHeading":"Keep each prediction’s limits visible.","resultHeading":"A point through time, with the model in view."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","alt":"Where Would I Be…? — archived product interface","caption":"Original location entry screen. Choosing a place starts the exploration of plate motion through time.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"v2":{"title":"Plate motion is too slow to see, so I built a globe that [[fast-forwards it]].","hook":"Where Would I Be…? is a 3D globe I designed and built on my own. Pick a place, drag the clock, and watch the ground under it ride its tectonic plate for up to 500 million years.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"September 2026"},{"label":"Why","value":"A personal project, to make plate motion something you can feel"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on desktop and phone. Runs entirely in the browser"},{"label":"Stack","value":"three.js and WebGL shaders, plain JavaScript modules, open plate data"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live. No usage numbers: the site has no analytics"}],"links":[{"label":"Open Where Would I Be…?","url":"https://wherewouldibe.srinidhibhat.com"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: the ground under you moves about as fast as your fingernails grow. A speed like 56.8 mm a year means nothing to most people.","What I built: a globe that moves all 52 plates at once. It answers in one sentence, and keeps every other number in a sheet you open when you want it.","What changed on the way: the morning after the first version, I rebuilt the interface twice, because its panels covered the globe that gives the answer.","Where it stands: live on the web. It carries today’s plate speeds forward unchanged, so it is an extrapolation, not a reconstruction of the past or a forecast."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/lead-panaji-50.webp","alt":"The globe centred on the Indian Ocean. A track runs from a cyan pin marked today on the coast of Goa to a coral pin marked 50 Myr near the Himalaya. Below, a dock reads: Panjim, Goa, India ends up 2,838 km to the north-east, in the subtropics.","caption":"Panjim, Goa, run 50 million years forward. The cyan pin is where it is today, the coral pin is where its ground ends up, and the dock says it in one sentence.","width":2400,"height":1500},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"The ground moves, but too slowly to feel","blocks":[{"p":"The Earth’s surface is broken into tectonic plates, huge slabs of crust that slide over the hot rock below. They move a few centimetres a year. That is real, and also impossible to picture."},{"p":"I wanted to give that speed a place you know. Pick your town, run the clock, and see where the ground under it ends up."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/scale.webp","alt":"Bar chart on a log scale of how far the ground under Panjim moves: 5.7 cm in 1 year, 5.68 m in 100 years, 57 m in 1,000 years, 56.8 km in 1 million, 568 km in 10 million, 2,838 km in 50 million years","caption":"The same speed at six timescales. Nothing changes that you could notice in a lifetime, and then the town ends up in another climate. Each figure is the app’s own answer.","width":1600,"height":740}]}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Pick a place, run the clock, open the rest only if you want it","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of three steps: pick a place, run the clock, then open the details or compare two places","caption":"The whole product in three steps. The first two share one dock at the bottom of the screen: the place picker turns into the time dial.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"The first screen asks one thing: where are you? Everything else waits until there is a place to talk about."},{"p":"You can use your location, search a city, tap anywhere on the globe or let it pick for you. Your location is used in the browser and never sent anywhere."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/start.webp","alt":"The opening screen: a slowly turning globe over the Atlantic, and a single card at the bottom with Use my location, a search box and a Surprise me button","caption":"The opening screen. One card, one question. The plate seams show only as faint grey lines.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/search.webp","alt":"The place card with Nai typed into the search box, listing Nairobi, Naihati and Naivasha with their region, country and population","caption":"Search, with three letters typed. Each result shows its region, country and population.","width":960,"height":1030}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"Then the clock. The slider runs from 500 million years ago to 500 million years ahead. Five buttons jump to 1,000 years, 1 million, 10 million, 50 million and 250 million. Play runs the whole journey in about seven seconds."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/annotated-explore.webp","alt":"The answer screen for Nairobi at 10 million years, with seven numbered notes on the place chip, the layers button, the centred globe, the two pins, the sentence, the line of plate figures and the time controls","caption":"The answer screen. Start at notes 5 and 6: one sentence, and one quiet line under it. Nairobi rides the Somalia plate at 35.3 mm a year.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"globe","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Nothing covers the globe","heading":"Nothing covers the globe, because the globe is the answer","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a top bar, the globe and one dock at the bottom. Nothing floats over the middle of the screen, where the two pins and the track are doing the explaining."},{"p":"In the build: the first version did the opposite. It opened two panels over the planet. Before you had done anything, they showed six expanded cards and about 25 numbers."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/first-version-desktop.webp","alt":"The first version on a desktop: a picker panel on the left and a readout panel on the right cover most of the globe, which is crowded with coloured boundary lines, a grid and long labels","caption":"The first version, run again from its commit at the same place and time. The panels and labels cover most of the planet, and every boundary is coloured.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The next morning I rebuilt it around one question at a time: pick a place, then explore it. On a phone, though, the answer sheet still took about two thirds of the screen, and very little of the planet was left in view. So I rebuilt it again."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/history.webp","alt":"Four phone screens side by side showing Panjim at 50 million years in each version: two stacked panels; an answer sheet covering the top two thirds; a clear globe with a one-sentence dock; and the same with the logo and a line of plate figures restored","caption":"The same answer in four versions, each captured from its own commit. The third screen fixed the globe and lost the logo and the plate figures. The fourth put them back.","width":1600,"height":1250}]},{"p":"Clearing the screen was not enough on its own. With a dock at the bottom, a centred planet hides its lower third behind it. So the app measures how much room the top bar and the dock take. It moves the globe into the space that is left, and backs the camera off until the whole planet fits."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Every number beyond the sentence is now one tap away instead of on screen. On a phone the details sheet still covers the globe while it is open. It is the one exception, and it closes with Escape or a tap."}}]},{"id":"sentence","label":"Decision 2","nav":"One sentence first","heading":"One sentence first, and every number one tap away","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: “Panjim, Goa, India ends up 2,838 km to the north-east, in the subtropics.” Under it, one line: the plate, its speed, and the climate then and now. The rest is in a details sheet."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/annotated-sheet.webp","alt":"The details sheet for Panjim with five numbered notes: coordinates, three tiles for distance, direction and climate, the compare button, the folds, and the list of places that end up next door","caption":"The details sheet. Note 3 matters most: comparing two places starts from inside an answer. In the first version it was a mode you had to choose before you began.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/phone-answer.webp","alt":"Phone screen of Panjim at 50 million years: the globe fills the top, and the dock holds the sentence, the plate line and the time controls","caption":"On a phone, the globe gets most of the screen.","width":600,"height":1298,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/phone-sheet.webp","alt":"Phone screen with the details sheet pulled up over the page, showing coordinates, three tiles, the compare button and the neighbours list","caption":"Details opens as a sheet from the bottom, with the page behind it dimmed.","width":600,"height":1298,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"In the build: my first one-sentence version went too far. It dropped the plate name and speed into a fold, and never stated the climate change at all. I put them back as that quiet second line."},{"p":"The sentence changes on every frame while you drag. I wanted dragging to move the globe and the numbers and nothing else. The dock keeps one height for its whole life, and each frame only rewrites the words already on the page. Over a 201-step drag, the page made zero changes to its structure, down from 400."},{"p":"Writing the sentence also caught a real bug. It used the plate’s direction of travel to say where the ground went. Run backwards, that is the wrong way round. Panjim “sat 2,838 km to the north-east” 50 million years ago, when it was really to the south-west. The sentence now uses the actual line from start to end."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The sentence is held to two lines, cut short or padded, so the dock never changes height. A long place name gets less room."}}]},{"id":"seams","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Quiet plate seams","heading":"The plate seams are always drawn, but quietly","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a faint grey line where one plate meets the next. One switch turns it into colours: pulling apart, crashing together or sliding past. The key comes on screen with the colours and goes away with them."},{"p":"In the build: this one took four tries over two days."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/seams.webp","alt":"Four cards showing how plate boundaries were shown in each version: coloured from the start, coloured with a permanent key, hidden until asked, and finally a grey hairline always on with colour on request","caption":"Four versions of one decision. I swung from everything on to nothing on, then settled in between.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"Hiding the seams completely was wrong. They are the reason the answer is what it is: you can see that the line runs between your town and the next country. But naming, in three colours, which way every stretch is moving is a lecture nobody asked for."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/layers-edges.webp","alt":"The globe over the Atlantic with boundaries coloured orange, blue and yellow, the layers menu open on the right and the colour key in the bottom-left corner","caption":"Colours switched on from the layers menu. The key appears at the same moment, bottom left.","width":1600,"height":1000},{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/phone-edges.webp","alt":"Phone screen of Panjim at 10 million years with coloured boundaries around India and a key strip above the dock","caption":"On a phone the key sits just above the dock.","width":600,"height":1298,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"wide-narrow"},{"p":"The colours are worked out, not painted on. Nothing in the data says “this is a mid-ocean ridge”. For every short stretch of every plate edge, the build script finds the plate on the other side and compares how the two are moving. It splits that into opening and sliding. The checks compare the results with published rates at twelve well-known boundaries."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A first-time visitor sees grey lines with no key. The About panel explains them, but someone who never opens it may not know what they are."}}]},{"id":"compare","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Comparing two places","heading":"Comparing two places asks when they come closest","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: pick a second place and the app says when the two patches of ground come closest, how close, and how fast they are closing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/compare.webp","alt":"Honolulu and Tokyo compared: a track from Honolulu across the Pacific towards Japan at 65 million years, and a sheet showing closest 1,689 km, when 65 Myr, closing 86 mm a year, and a chart of distance over time with one dip","caption":"Honolulu and Tokyo are 6,206 km apart today. They come closest, 1,689 km, in 65 million years. The chart shows the dip.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: each place turns on its own circle around its plate’s pole. So the distance between two places rises and falls with no simple formula for the lowest point. The solver scans a billion years in coarse steps, keeps every dip, then narrows each one down."},{"p":"I checked it against a brute-force answer from 400,000 evenly spaced samples. Places on the same plate are handled on their own: they never move apart, so the app says so."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Comparing is a button inside an answer, not a mode on the first screen. It is easier to start with one place, and harder to find the comparison if you came for it."}}]},{"id":"trust","label":"How far to trust it","nav":"How far to trust it","heading":"The app says how far to trust its own answer","blocks":[{"p":"The plate speeds come from a published model, NNR-MORVEL56, averaged over roughly the last 780,000 years. The app carries those speeds forward unchanged. Real plates change course, jam and break."},{"p":"So this is an extrapolation. It is not a reconstruction of where places were, and not a prediction of where they will be. Running the clock backwards uses the same assumption, so the past is not reconstructed either."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/trust.webp","alt":"Four columns grading the answer by timescale: 1,000 years solid, 1 million fair, 50 million a decent story, beyond that a well-informed daydream, each with the distance for Panjim","caption":"The app’s own guide to its answers. Look at the last column: past a point the distance even shrinks, because each plate keeps turning on a fixed circle.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/trust-250.webp","alt":"Panjim at 250 million years, with a track running over the top of the globe, and the details sheet open at Should I believe this?, which says that past 50 million years this is a very well-informed daydream, not a prediction","caption":"At 250 million years the sheet says plainly that this is a daydream, not a prediction. The warning only appears past 50 million years.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"aside":{"title":"What is still weak.","detail":"The dock sentence itself carries no warning. At 250 million years it still states 13,471 km with no hedge, and the caveat is one tap away in the sheet."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Every plate moves on the graphics card","blocks":[{"p":"Each plate behaves like a rigid cap turning on a sphere around one fixed point, its pole. Give a plate its pole and its rate, and the future of every point on it is one rotation. That is what lets the app redraw the world for any year instantly."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: open data cleaned by a build script into four JSON files; plate maths and a triangle mesh built in the browser; 52 plate rotations sent to the graphics card each frame, where a shader moves each corner","caption":"What runs where. The heavy work happens once before deploy. In the browser, the graphics card turns every plate on every frame.","width":1600,"height":790}]},{"p":"Every corner of the map is tagged with the plate it belongs to. On each frame, the 52 plate rotations go to the graphics card packed into one tiny 64 by 1 image. A shader, a small program that runs on the graphics card, turns each corner by its own plate. Lines are drawn as thin strips of triangles, so they ride the crust too."},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Globe","three.js, with my own shaders for plate motion"],["Shapes","earcut to cut land and plates into triangles, then my own refinement"],["Code","Plain JavaScript modules. No build step and nothing to install"],["Data","NNR-MORVEL56 plate motions, PB2002 plate outlines, Natural Earth coastlines, GeoNames places"],["Checks","A script of science and geometry checks, run with Node"],["Hosting","Static files, with a strict content security policy"]]}},{"p":"The checks cover which plate a place sits on, motion at twelve famous boundaries, that rotations agree with themselves, that both sides of a shared edge agree, plate areas, place search and the closest-approach solver. The plate outlines must add up to the Earth’s 510.1 million km²."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of six commits: first version on 19 September at 23:54, then five commits on 20 September between 09:17 and 10:17","caption":"The build from the commit history. The first version landed late one night, and every change on this page came the next morning.","width":1600,"height":580}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were in the triangles, not the interface."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/where-would-i-be/mesh.webp","alt":"Two schematic drawings: an edge bowing towards the pole when split in 3D, and a short edge near a pole that sags far from where it should run, with the before and after numbers for each fix","caption":"Two bugs, each fixed in the same commit. Schematic drawings, not to scale.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Seas drawn as land","Long thin triangles were split along a curve the flat map never meant. Splitting in the flat map instead took sea test points drawn as land from 290 to 1 of 22,452."],["Gaps at the poles","Near a pole a long edge measures short. Splitting by sag instead of length closed every gap in 7 passes and 95k triangles, against 12 passes and 147k. The finished mesh got smaller."],["Play froze the app","Play wrote to a label I had deleted in the rebuild. The error stopped the drawing loop, so the whole globe froze. The loop now books its next frame first and survives a bad one."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"A 3D globe that also works from the keyboard","blocks":[{"p":"I built to WCAG 2.2 AA, the common accessibility standard for the web, and measured rather than assumed."},{"list":["Contrast is tested where it is worst: the see-through dock over the brightest land on the globe. Measured that way, the faintest text was 2.88 to 1. I made the dock more solid and lightened that text to reach 4.7 to 1.","The globe works from the keyboard. Arrows turn it, plus and minus zoom, and Enter drops a pin at the centre of the view.","Screen readers hear the answer sentence when it changes. During Play it stays quiet, instead of reading out hundreds of passing sentences.","The slider reads as words, such as “50 Myr from now”, not as a raw number.","The details sheet and the About panel keep keyboard focus inside while open, close with Escape, and return focus where it was.","With reduced motion switched on, the globe stops spinning and Play jumps straight to the end.","Every pointer target is at least 24 by 24 pixels."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are my own checks, not an outside audit."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no numbers to share","blocks":[{"p":"Where Would I Be…? is live on the web and works on desktop and phone. It has no server and no analytics, by design, so I have no visitor counts, and no feedback or user sessions to report."}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Find out what people take away from it","blocks":[{"list":["A small study I would run: let someone explore for a few minutes, then ask what changed and what the model assumes.","Check whether the warning stays visible enough at far dates, since the sentence itself carries none.","Test the globe on narrow screens with the keyboard and with reduced motion, beyond my own checks."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Ask why the panel is there before tidying it","blocks":[{"p":"My first rebuild cleaned up the panel. It did not ask why there was a panel. The globe was the answer the whole time, and anything laid over it was working against the product."},{"p":"I also learned to swing back. I hid the plate seams to quiet the screen, and that hid the reason for the answer. I cut the numbers to one sentence, and that dropped the plate and its speed. Both times the fix sat between my first two tries."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","headline":"A home for my writing, in the form it needs."}},"the-blog":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","kind":"product","headline":"A five-year-old blog, rebuilt so I can publish from any browser.","summary":"The Blog is where I publish as अvinash, in English, Hindi and Konkani. I designed and built it on my own: first on a ready-made theme in 2021, then as my own site with a writing interface in 2026.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working publishing system","context":"Publishing experience · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"reader and author workflows"},{"value":"Drafts","label":"explicit publishing control"},{"value":"Verse","label":"preserved line breaks"}],"problem":"A personal writing practice should not require a technical deployment ritual for every post. The Blog connects a reading experience with a browser editor that handles content, images, drafts, and publishing through a familiar form.","process":["I framed writing, reviewing, and publishing as separate commitments. The editor should support the author’s work without requiring technical knowledge of how a post reaches the website.","The reading experience follows the form of the writing: poetry keeps its line breaks, while topics and series help a reader continue. Publication feedback should make draft and live states easy to distinguish."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give publishing a clear state","choice":"Draft and published are separate states, with a deliberate release action.","reason":"Writing and releasing a piece are different commitments and should remain distinguishable.","tradeoff":"Publishing can take time. The author needs clear confirmation of what is saved and what is live."},{"title":"Let the content form affect the layout","choice":"Poetry preserves line breaks and an appropriate verse treatment, while ordinary articles retain a reading layout.","reason":"The way a piece is written can be part of its meaning.","tradeoff":"Formatting conventions must be discoverable in the editor rather than depend on hidden technical knowledge."},{"title":"Make ongoing writing navigable","choice":"Topics and series connect related pieces within the editorial structure.","reason":"A reader should be able to follow a thread without knowing the order it was published.","tradeoff":"The documented title-based series matching is convenient but implicit; a visible series field would make mistakes easier to avoid."}],"outcome":"I gave poems, essays and ongoing stories a reading structure suited to each form. The publishing flow separates drafts from release, while topics and series help a reader continue. Poetry keeps its line breaks instead of being squeezed into an ordinary article template.","reflection":"I would make draft status and series membership more explicit in the editor. Those small details affect whether I can publish the right version and whether someone can find the next part of a story.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: publish a draft, update an existing post, and add a series instalment without using development tools.","Proposed measure: accidental publication, broken links after slug edits, and clarity about whether the latest save is live."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, Writing a post and Things worth knowing","detail":"Browser editor, draft/build publishing path, verse formatting and title-based series conventions. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/blogs.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":54,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A home for my writing, [[in the form it needs.]]","scene":"Imagine finishing a poem and then having to switch from writing into a development workflow. The mechanics of publishing can become a reason to put the piece aside.","question":"What should the author have to think about after the writing is done?","insight":"[[Publishing state belongs in the editor]]. A draft, a save, and a live page are different moments that need clear feedback.","pivot":"I connected a browser editor with a content-aware reading experience, respecting verse and serial writing along the way.","takeaway":"The tool should respect the shape of the writing.","journey":[{"title":"Compose","detail":"Work through an editorial interface."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Keep draft status and content choices visible."},{"title":"Release","detail":"Carry the saved piece through the build into the reading experience."}],"decisionLeads":["Make publication a visible commitment.","Let the form of the writing matter.","Make the next piece easy to find."],"annotations":[{"title":"An editorial front door","detail":"The site introduces the writing in its own visual voice."},{"title":"A workflow behind it","detail":"The browser editor supports the ongoing publishing practice."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · editorial workflow","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Writer publishing & reader continuing a piece","context":"An author moves from draft to release; a reader moves between related writing.","need":"Preserve the form of the writing and a clear route to the next piece.","friction":"Publication states and implicit series conventions can be difficult to understand.","implication":"Give drafts, verse, and series explicit structure."}},"theme":{"layout":"editorial","mode":"light","paper":"#faf8f3","ink":"#14130f","dim":"#5a564c","line":"#d7d0c0","accent":"#6b3fa0","wash":"#eee5f5","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A draft is not a release","process":"The form is part of the writing","decisions":"Designing reader and author journeys","outcome":"Writing with a place to live","reflection":"Making publication legible"},"decisionHeading":"Make it easy to find and continue reading.","resultHeading":"A home for drafts, poems, and continuing stories."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/blogs.webp","alt":"The Blog — archived product interface","caption":"Original writing homepage under the name अvinash, with poems, essays, fiction and a written podcast.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"A five-year-old blog, rebuilt so I can [[publish from any browser]].","hook":"The Blog is where I publish as अvinash, in English, Hindi and Konkani. I designed and built it on my own: first on a ready-made theme in 2021, then as my own site with a writing interface in 2026.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A home for my own poems, essays, serial fiction and a written podcast"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"May 2021 to July 2026. Rebuilt in July 2026"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on phones and desktops"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Astro 5, Sveltia CMS, Pagefind, Netlify"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live, with 32 posts. No reader numbers to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Read the blog","url":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/"},{"label":"Read Senses, a poem","url":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/p/poem-5/"},{"label":"Read The Murder in Classroom 10","url":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/series/murder-in-classroom-10/"}],"linksHeading":"Read it yourself","summary":["The problem: my blog ran on a borrowed theme. A new post meant a Markdown file, a terminal and a commit, and my poems were stored as code.","What I did: in July 2026 I rebuilt it in Astro with my own design, moved all 32 posts without breaking a link, and added a writing interface I can open from any browser.","The key idea: let the form of the writing shape the page. Poems keep their lines, serials keep their order, and Hindi and Konkani get a proper Devanagari typeface.","Where it stands: live, with 32 posts. I have no reader numbers to share."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/lead.webp","alt":"The blog's home page on a desktop, beside two phone screens: an essay page in the light theme and a poem in the dark theme","caption":"The rebuilt blog. On the left, the home page with the pen name set in Devanagari. On the right, an essay and a poem on a phone. Body text is blurred after the first line.","width":2398,"height":866},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I rebuilt it","nav":"Why I rebuilt it","heading":"The blog had outgrown a theme I borrowed in 2021","blocks":[{"p":"I started the blog in May 2021 on Stack, a free theme for Hugo. Hugo is a tool that turns plain text files into a website. On the first day the site held 22 posts, some dated as far back as 2017."},{"quote":{"text":"My own Blog site (srinidhi-blogs.netlify.app) is built on a HUGO theme!","by":"From my 2021 essay, Have your own internet space!"}},{"p":"It worked, but the theme made most of the choices. It was dark by default and came with its own sidebar and widgets. Publishing a post meant a Markdown file, a terminal and a commit. Markdown is plain text with a few marks for headings and links."},{"p":"Two things fitted my writing badly. Poems sat in code blocks, which kept their line breaks but set them like code. And a serial story was filed under a topic like any other post, with nothing to say which part came first."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/before-after.webp","alt":"Two cards compared: the 2021 blog on a ready-made Hugo theme, and the 2026 blog in Astro with its own design","caption":"The same blog in May 2021 and July 2026. Look at the last line of each card: 22 posts on day one, all 32 moved across with their links intact.","width":1600,"height":790}]}]},{"id":"history","label":"Five years","nav":"Five years in one view","heading":"Most of the work came in three bursts","blocks":[{"p":"The project history has 75 commits. A commit is one saved change to the project. They bunch up around three moments: setting the site up in May 2021, a five-part Formula 1 primer in January 2022, and the rebuild in July 2026."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/timeline.webp","alt":"Bar chart of commits by year: 42 in 2021, 20 in 2022, none in 2023 and 2024, 3 in 2025 and 10 in 2026, with the main events of each year","caption":"Commits by year, redrawn from the project history. The quiet middle is real: no changes at all in 2023 or 2024.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"After January 2022 the blog went quiet. There were two small edits that December, then nothing until a film review in October 2025. The rebuild landed in ten commits over 27 and 28 July 2026."}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"I write in a browser, and readers get plain pages","blocks":[{"p":"The site is static. Every page is built ahead of time as a finished file, so a reader's browser only has to download it. The writing side is a CMS, a content management system: a form in the browser where I write and publish posts."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/how-it-works.webp","alt":"Flow diagram in two rows: browser, sign-in function, editor and save on the writing side; Netlify build, search index, live site and reader on the reading side","caption":"The two halves of the system. The top row is me writing. The bottom row is what happens after I press Save.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"There is no server or database behind the editor. Each save becomes a commit in the project's private repository, and Netlify rebuilds the site from it. A post is live a minute or two later."},{"p":"The home page is the front door, so I built it as a set of shelves, one for each kind of writing."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/annotated-home.webp","alt":"The full home page with six numbered notes: masthead, newest post, series shelf, poems, essays and topics","caption":"The home page from top to bottom. Note 3 is the series shelf, the part the old theme had no place for.","width":1600,"height":1760}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/annotated-post.webp","alt":"A post page with six numbered notes: breadcrumb, topics, title and summary, byline, drop cap and the header controls","caption":"An essay page. Note 5: the body sits in a narrow column made for reading, opened with a drop cap.","width":1600,"height":1420}]}]},{"id":"verse","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Poems as verse","heading":"A poem keeps its lines, and I did not have to learn a new mark","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a poem is set in serif italic beside a thin violet rule. Every line break and every gap between stanzas is kept as written."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/verse-desktop.webp","alt":"The poem Senses on its page, set in italic beside a violet rule, with only the first line readable","caption":"Senses, one of my poems. Only the first line is readable here, but the shape is the point: the breaks and stanza gaps survive.","width":1200,"height":1410,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: I kept the habit my old posts already had. All seven poems were already in code fences, the marks Markdown uses for code. A small plugin now reads any fence with no language named as a poem. So the text of the poems moved across without a single edit."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/verse-rule.webp","alt":"Diagram: on the left, placeholder lines typed between two fence marks; on the right, the same lines shown as verse in italic with a violet rule","caption":"The rule in one picture, with placeholder lines. A fence that names a language still shows as code, so writing about code later still works.","width":1600,"height":660}]},{"p":"The Hindi and Konkani poems needed a real Devanagari typeface, so every font stack includes Noto Serif Devanagari. That had a cost. The pen name in the footer put one Devanagari letter on every English page, which pulled in the whole 124KB Hindi font. Now that letter comes from its own 1.4KB file, and the full font loads only on pages that use it."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The rule is invisible. A writer has to know that a plain fence means a poem. The hint under the editor's Body field says so, but it is a convention, not a button."}}]},{"id":"series","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Serials in order","heading":"A serial is read in order, so its title carries the part number","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: each serial has its own page that lists the parts in order and says they are meant to be read that way. There are three: a murder mystery in three parts, a written podcast in nine episodes and a Formula 1 primer in five parts."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/series-page.webp","alt":"The series page for The Murder in Classroom 10, listing Part 1, Part 2 and Part 3 with dates and reading times, summaries blurred","caption":"The page for my three-part mystery. The large red numbers set the order. The part summaries are blurred here.","width":1600,"height":1167}]},{"p":"In the build: there is no series field on a post. Each series has a rule that looks at the end of a post's title, such as \"(Part 2)\". The rule decides which series a post joins and where it sits."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/series-rule.webp","alt":"Diagram: three real post titles ending in Part 1, 2 and 3 pass through a rule that matches the ending, producing an ordered list under the series name","caption":"Look at the first two titles: they spell the name with a hyphen and the third does not. Matching only the ending keeps all three together.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"I ruled out a series field on every post. It would have meant editing every old part by hand, and the titles already carried the numbers. The rules are edited in the editor's settings. A rule with a typo is skipped with a warning, so it cannot stop the site from building."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Membership is hidden in the title. A new part with a slightly different ending would quietly fall out of its series. A visible series field would make that mistake easier to catch."}}]},{"id":"publishing","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Publishing from a browser","heading":"Publishing takes a password and a Draft switch, not a terminal","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one form per post. Each field has a short hint under it, written for the moment I would need it, such as the warning that changing a published post's address breaks every link to it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/editor-fields.webp","alt":"Drawing of the editor form: title, summary, publish date, URL slug, cover image, topics and tags, draft, feature and fact panel, and body, each with its hint","caption":"A drawing of the editor's fields and hints, made from its settings. I have no screenshot of the editor to show. The shaded fields carry the rules worth knowing: the address, the Draft switch and the poem fence.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"In the build: the editor is Sveltia CMS, an open-source editor that saves straight into the repository. I ruled out its review mode, which sends every post through a separate branch and a pull request. That is the git ceremony I was trying to remove. Instead, Save always commits, and the Draft switch decides whether a post appears on the site."},{"p":"Signing in took three forms in two days. The first version needed a GitHub account on the device. That made writing from a phone, or from someone else's laptop, more trouble than it was worth."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/sign-in.webp","alt":"Three stages of signing in: GitHub sign-in, then a password behind a button and a popup, then a password typed on the page","caption":"From a GitHub account to one password on the page. The middle row is why: a browser will not open a popup without a click, so the button had to go too.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Now a small function on Netlify checks the password and hands the editor a GitHub key that is stored there, never in the code. The key can only change this one repository's files."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Whoever knows the password can publish from anywhere. That is the point of it, and it is also the whole of its security. The password and the key can each be changed without touching the other."}}]},{"id":"light","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Light first, and readable","heading":"Light by default, and every small grey passes contrast","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: warm paper and dark ink, with a violet accent taken from the feather in the logo. Dark mode is there, but only when a reader switches it on, and the site remembers the choice."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/hero-light.webp","alt":"The home page masthead in the light theme: cream paper, black and violet headline","caption":"The default. Every reader starts here, whatever their phone is set to.","width":1600,"height":796},{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/hero-dark.webp","alt":"The same masthead in the dark theme: charcoal paper, cream and lilac headline","caption":"Dark, after the reader presses the moon in the header. The violet lifts to lilac so it stays readable.","width":1600,"height":796}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: the old theme was dark by default, and the first Astro version followed the device's setting. I removed that. The site is a paper-coloured thing, so I made dark a choice the reader makes, not one the phone makes for them."},{"p":"Then I measured contrast. A contrast ratio says how far apart two colours are in brightness, and small text needs at least 4.5 to 1. The faint grey under every caption, byline and date was 3.5 to 1. It is now 5.2 to 1 on the page background. The topic colours were worse."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/contrast-chart.webp","alt":"Paired bar chart of fifteen topic colours, before and after: eleven sat below the 4.5 to 1 line and all fifteen now sit above it","caption":"Each topic's colour before and after. Politics was the faintest. Every new colour clears the line on all three light backgrounds.","width":1600,"height":1010}]},{"p":"The topic colours were picked for cream paper and turn muddy on charcoal. In the dark theme, any colour used as text is mixed part of the way toward the light ink first. Dots and backgrounds keep the colour as picked."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"When I finished, the axe-core checker reported no problems against WCAG 2.2 A and AA on every type of page, in both themes. I did not keep those reports, and no screen-reader user has tried the site."}}]},{"id":"reading","label":"Reading it","nav":"On a phone, and search","heading":"The same pages on a phone, with search that needs no server","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/phone-essay.webp","alt":"An essay page on a phone: breadcrumb, topics, title, summary, byline, cover and the drop cap","caption":"An essay on a phone. The header keeps search and the theme switch in reach.","width":780,"height":1688},{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/phone-menu.webp","alt":"The phone menu open as a sheet over the page, listing Writing, Series, About, Connect and Search","caption":"The menu opens as a sheet over the page with a dimmed backdrop. Escape closes it and returns focus to the button.","width":780,"height":1688},{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/phone-poem-dark.webp","alt":"A poem on a phone in the dark theme, with only its first line readable","caption":"A poem in the dark theme. The violet rule and the line breaks carry over to the small screen.","width":780,"height":1688}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"On a phone the header controls are at least 44 pixels square, the size a thumb can hit reliably. The reading progress bar and the scroll effects were reworked so they do less work on every scroll."},{"p":"Search is built by Pagefind after the pages exist. It writes a small index that the reader's browser searches on its own, so nothing has to run on a server."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/search.webp","alt":"The search page with the word internet typed in and one result: Have your own internet space!","caption":"One word, one result, with the match highlighted. The index covers the full text of every post.","width":1440,"height":780}]}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Five years of Markdown, cleaned up on the way to the page","blocks":[{"p":"The posts were written over five years with no house rules. Rather than edit 32 posts by hand, I added small steps that fix them while the site is built."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/the-blog/pipeline.webp","alt":"Diagram of a post passing through three steps, verse, heading levels and figures, from a Markdown file to a static page","caption":"What happens to every post at build time. Step 2 exists because some posts skipped heading levels, which confuses screen readers.","width":1600,"height":600}]},{"p":"Every old address still works. Posts stay at the same paths they had on Hugo, and the old feed and page addresses are redirected to their new homes."},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Site","Astro 5 with MDX, building static pages"],["Writing interface","Sveltia CMS, saving to a private GitHub repository"],["Sign-in","One Netlify Function that checks the password"],["Search","Pagefind, built after the pages"],["Type","Fraunces, Source Serif 4, Inter and Noto Serif Devanagari, served by the site itself"],["Hosting","Netlify, rebuilt on every save"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"75","label":"commits, May 2021 to July 2026"},{"value":"32","label":"posts moved, every address kept"},{"value":"168","label":"pages in the last build, 107 of them tag pages"},{"value":"67KB","label":"headline font, down from 121KB with no visible change"}]},{"p":"There are no automated tests and no checks that run on each save."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Heading levels","Posts started their headings wherever felt right. The F1 primer opens at level 3 and one mystery part uses a single level 5. Every post skipped a level. A build step now renumbers them in order and keeps the old size for styling."],["Comments","The comment service was set to its dark style on its own dashboard. On cream paper that put pale grey on pale cream, and threads looked empty. I moved comments onto the dark band, where they read in both themes."],["Page load","A timer loaded the comments four seconds into every post, read or not, bringing most of a megabyte of third-party script. It is gone. Comments now load when the reader reaches them, and analytics waits until the page has loaded."],["Hindi font","The Devanagari font was declared with an outdated format name. Outside Chrome and Safari the browser ignored it, and the poems fell back to a system font. One word fixed it."],["Search engines","The sitemap told search engines that every post had changed on the day of the build. Each post now carries its own date."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with 32 posts and no reader numbers","blocks":[{"p":"The blog is live at blogs.srinidhibhat.com. It holds 32 posts: seven poems, essays, a three-part mystery, a nine-episode written podcast, a five-part Formula 1 primer and a film review. The newest is from October 2025."},{"p":"The site has analytics, but I have no reader numbers or feedback to share. Three gaps remain:"},{"list":["I have nothing on record from readers: no test sessions and no feedback.","The accessibility results were not saved, so I cannot show them.","There are no automated tests, so a change could break a page without warning."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Make the hidden rules visible in the editor","blocks":[{"list":["Show in the editor which series a post will join, before it is saved, so a mistyped title cannot drop a part.","Make it clearer whether the latest save is live or still building.","Then test the writing side with three tasks done without any developer tools: publish a draft, update an old post, and add a new part to a series. Count accidental publishing and links broken by a changed address."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"The best rules were habits I already had","blocks":[{"p":"The two rules that made the move easy were not new. My poems were already in fences and my serials already had part numbers in their titles. Reading those habits as rules let the poems and the serials move across without being rewritten."},{"p":"The same choice is the weak spot. A rule nobody can see is easy to break. If I built it again, I would keep the habits and add one line in the editor that says what the habit will do."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","headline":"Get everyone in. Keep the round moving."}},"office-games":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","kind":"product","headline":"Three party games where one player's browser is the game server.","summary":"The Office Games is a small web site of three quick multiplayer games that I designed and built on my own. One player opens a room, shares a six-character code, and their browser runs the game for everyone.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Working project · trivia module","context":"Social play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"4","label":"letters in a room code"},{"value":"Team-based","label":"turn and score model"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"local or network mode"}],"problem":"A short team game loses momentum when people are unsure how to join or whose turn it is. The reviewed Office Games module centres on a simple room flow, a lobby, team roles, and a visible answering/stealing sequence.","process":["I mapped the reviewed trivia journey from joining a room to understanding teams, turns, answering, and stealing. The broader games catalogue is outside the evidence available for this case study.","The UX needs to explain the connection mode before a group commits to a game. Trying a local demonstration and joining colleagues on separate devices are different experiences."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make joining a small action","choice":"A four-letter room code and a player name provide a concise entry path.","reason":"A social break should spend more of its time playing than explaining setup.","tradeoff":"A short code still needs clear invalid-room and full-room handling, and does not establish secure identity."},{"title":"Expose responsibility in the game state","choice":"The interface distinguishes answering and stealing teams, active players, and scores.","reason":"People need to see who can act and why the round is waiting.","tradeoff":"Role-based interaction becomes confusing if waiting states or connection loss are not explained."},{"title":"Keep connection mode understandable","choice":"The setup distinguishes a local demonstration from connected play.","reason":"A demo can help someone try the game, but should not promise multi-device play it cannot provide.","tradeoff":"A connected game needs clear waiting, conflict, reconnection, and host-recovery states."}],"outcome":"For the trivia flow documented here, I focused on room entry, teams, scores and the answering-and-stealing sequence. The interface gives the group a shared view of who can act next. Local demo mode and connected play need different expectations.","reflection":"I would run a short session and pay attention to the pauses: joining, waiting for a team, recovering a connection and changing the host. The answer screen is only one part of whether the break feels easy to join.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: run a short trivia session with several participants and record setup explanations, confused turns, and waiting time.","Proposed checks: separate-device joining, reconnection, duplicate actions, host departure, and a clearly labelled local demo mode."],"sources":[{"label":"src/App.jsx and src/gameService.js","detail":"Reviewed room/lobby/trivia journey, short codes, team state, local-storage mock service and Firebase path. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://officegames.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/officegames.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":57,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Get everyone in. [[Keep the round moving.]]","scene":"Imagine a short team break being spent explaining how to join, who can answer, and why the game is waiting. The social rhythm depends on those transitions being obvious.","question":"What does the group need to understand at every handoff?","insight":"[[Shared state has to become shared understanding]]. Room entry, team roles, and the answering/stealing sequence all contribute to the pace.","pivot":"I focused the reviewed trivia module on a concise joining path and visible responsibility through each round.","takeaway":"A social game moves when everyone knows what happens next.","journey":[{"title":"Join","detail":"Use a short code and a player name."},{"title":"Orient","detail":"See the teams and who can act."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Follow the answering and stealing sequence."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Keep scores and waiting states understandable."}],"decisionLeads":["Make joining a small action.","Show why a round is waiting.","Keep the connection promise clear."],"annotations":[{"title":"A social entry point","detail":"The product offers a quick route into a shared game."},{"title":"Reviewed scope: trivia","detail":"Local demo and network-backed play make different connection promises."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · reviewed trivia module","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Colleague joining a short group game","context":"Entering a room and learning a team role during an office break.","need":"Join quickly and know whose turn it is.","friction":"Waiting and role changes become confusing when the interface is silent.","implication":"Make room, role, and next action visible."}},"theme":{"layout":"playroom","mode":"dark","paper":"#17181c","ink":"#f8f9fa","dim":"#bdc2ce","line":"#495160","accent":"#ff94b7","wash":"#422336","bodyFont":"Outfit","displayFont":"Fredoka","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A short break to play","process":"Someone needs the next turn","decisions":"Designing the social rhythm","outcome":"Inside the trivia round","reflection":"What waiting feels like"},"decisionHeading":"Make the next turn clear to everyone.","resultHeading":"A room with a shared social rhythm."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/officegames.webp","alt":"The Office Games — archived product interface","caption":"Original Office Games entry screen. A group chooses a game before joining a shared room.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"v2":{"title":"Three party games where [[one player's browser]] is the game server.","hook":"The Office Games is a small web site of three quick multiplayer games that I designed and built on my own. One player opens a room, shares a six-character code, and their browser runs the game for everyone.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"July 2026. A separate first attempt, Game of Lies, dates from June 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"Groups of friends or colleagues who want a short game in the browser, with no sign-up"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, played in desktop browsers. Keyboard Scramble needs a physical keyboard"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 19, Vite, PeerJS over WebRTC, one Vercel server function"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live. I have no usage numbers or playtest records to share"}],"links":[{"label":"Play The Office Games","url":"https://officegames.srinidhibhat.com/"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: a quick group game in the browser still needs a place where the game runs and everyone can reach it. My first version needed a Node server and a host for it.","What I built: a hub of three games, Keyboard Scramble, Collision and The Forger. The host player's browser holds the room and checks the rules. Other browsers connect to it directly.","The key idea: keep the server out of the game. The only server code left hands out passwords for a relay that helps browsers behind firewalls connect.","Where it stands: live since July 2026, built in 34 commits over eight days. An earlier one-game prototype with the same name, Game of Lies, is a separate product and is not the live site."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/lead-strip.webp","alt":"Three game screens side by side: a scrambled on-screen keyboard with some keys revealed, a Collision round result reading Collision, and a Forger drawing canvas with a secret word","caption":"One round from each game: Keyboard Scramble, Collision and The Forger. Player names are blurred.","width":2400,"height":551},"sections":[{"id":"what","label":"What it is","nav":"What it is","heading":"Three short games behind one room code","blocks":[{"p":"The Office Games is a home page with three games on it. Each one is short and needs no account. The home page invites you to \"choose a game to play with your friends\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/hub.webp","alt":"The home page with three game cards: Keyboard Scramble in orange, Collision in blue and The Forger in pink, each with a short rule and a Play now button","caption":"The home page. Each card gives the rule in two or three sentences, so nobody has to read a manual before the first round.","width":1600,"height":856}]},{"list":["Keyboard Scramble: type the word on screen while every key on your keyboard types a different letter.","Collision: two teams answer prompts like \"Name a big cat\". If too many people on a team give the same answer, the team takes a strike.","The Forger: everyone draws a secret word one line at a time, except the Forger, who only knows the category."]},{"p":"Every game starts the same way. You type a name and create a private room. You share the room code, and the others type it in to join."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/ks-home.webp","alt":"Keyboard Scramble start screen with a name field, a Create private room button, a room code field and a Join room button","caption":"The start screen. Two paths only: make a room or join one.","width":1290,"height":1155},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/ks-lobby.webp","alt":"The Keyboard Scramble lobby with a room code, three player badges and game settings for mode, difficulty, rounds, time and hints","caption":"The lobby. The host picks the settings. The others mark themselves ready, and their badges turn green.","width":1600,"height":1195}],"layout":"two"},{"aside":{"title":"How these screens were made.","detail":"I captured every game screen on this page from the built site on one computer, with several browser tabs as players. The network link between players was replaced by a local stand-in, so the screens are the real interface but not a real game across devices."}}]},{"id":"two","label":"Two products","nav":"Two products","heading":"The name was used twice, and the live link is the second product","blocks":[{"p":"There are two separate code bases called The Office Games. They share a name and a wordmark. They share no code."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/two-products.webp","alt":"A comparison table of Game of Lies from June 2026 and The Office Games hub from July 2026, covering games, how players connect, room code, history and hosting","caption":"The two products side by side. The pink column is what the live link shows.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"The first, in June 2026, was one game called Game of Lies. Its rooms only work across tabs of one browser, unless each player sets up a database connection by hand. It has no hosting set up."},{"p":"The second started in July 2026 as Keyboard Scramble on its own. The same morning I turned it into a hub for several games and gave it the old name. That hub is the live site, and most of this page is about it. Game of Lies has its own section near the end."}]},{"id":"how","label":"How a room works","nav":"How a room works","heading":"One player's browser runs the game for everyone","blocks":[{"p":"There is no game server. Whoever creates the room becomes the host. Their browser tab holds the room in memory and checks every move against the rules. Then it sends the whole room to every player, and each screen redraws from it."},{"p":"The browsers talk to each other directly through WebRTC, a browser feature for direct connections. To find each other, they use PeerJS's public introduction service. The room code is part of the host's address there, so typing the code is how you find the host."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/room-architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: the host's browser tab holding the room and checking rules, other players' browsers linked to it directly, and the services used only to connect: the PeerJS broker, STUN and TURN servers, and one server function","caption":"Who does what in a room. Everything in the right column helps browsers find each other. None of it holds game data.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"Some networks block direct links. For those, a relay called a TURN server passes the traffic along. The relay needs a password, and a password in the page would be public. So one small server function asks the relay provider for a short-lived password and passes it on."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"With no server, the room lives only in the host's tab. If the host closes it, the room ends, and there is no hand-over to another player. And because the whole room goes to every browser, hidden things like the Forger's secret word can be read by anyone who opens the browser's developer tools. I accepted that for a game among friends. It is not cheat-proof."}}]},{"id":"scramble","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Keyboard Scramble","heading":"Everyone fights the same scrambled keyboard","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a word to type and a picture of a keyboard. Every key types the wrong letter. As the clock runs down, a few keys at a time flip to show what they really type."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/annotated-scramble.webp","alt":"A Keyboard Scramble round with five numbered notes: the leaderboard, the timer, the word with a wrong key shown under it, revealed keys on the keyboard, and the keyboard itself","caption":"Look at note 4: four keys have flipped after the first hint. With the default medium setting, each hint reveals four keys.","width":1600,"height":840}]},{"p":"In the build: at the start of each round the host shuffles 47 keys into a new order and picks the order the hints will appear in. It sends both to every player once. Each browser then translates your key presses itself, so a round needs no messages until you finish or make a mistake."},{"p":"A finished word scores 100 points, plus 10 for each second left, minus 5 for each mistake, and never less than 10. The host can change the mode (words, phrases, sentences, or a chaos mode that reverses the word), the difficulty, rounds, time and hints."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/ks-results.webp","alt":"Results screen ranking three blurred players by points with their mistakes, and the full scrambled keyboard for the round","caption":"After the round the whole scrambled keyboard is shown. The heading says \"Final results!\" even though this was round 1 of 3, a bug I come back to below.","width":1260,"height":1308,"kind":"tall"}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"There is no backspace. A wrong key just counts as a mistake. And the time left is reported by each player's own browser, which the host trusts."}}]},{"id":"collision","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Collision","heading":"In Collision, matching meaning counts, not matching spelling","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: two teams. Each round one team gets a prompt and 30 seconds. Every member answers alone. If half the team or more give the same answer, the team takes a strike. Three strikes and that team loses."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/col-lobby.webp","alt":"Collision lobby with a six-character room code, two team boxes of two blurred players each, Move buttons, and Randomize teams and Start game buttons","caption":"New players are spread across the two teams. The host can move people or shuffle the teams. The game needs at least two on each side.","width":1544,"height":738},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/annotated-collision.webp","alt":"A Collision round with four numbered notes: the strike counts, whose turn it is, a 27-second timer, and the waiting team's view of answers arriving","caption":"The waiting team's screen. Note 4 is what makes the wait fun: you watch the other team's answers arrive.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"In the build: the first rule compared answers letter for letter, ignoring only capitals and outer spaces, and a strike needed more than half the team. So \"NY\" and \"New York\" did not match, and a made-up word was accepted. On 17 July I rebuilt it, and changed the strike to half the team or more. Each question now carries its own list of accepted answers and nicknames, and each answer is matched before it counts."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/answer-matching.webp","alt":"Four steps in a row: clean the answer up, check known nicknames, check the exact answer list, allow close spelling; then two outcomes, matched or rejected","caption":"The four checks, in order. A rejected answer can be fixed straight away.","width":1600,"height":780}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/col-invalid.webp","alt":"A Collision answer box containing qwxz, with a red message saying it does not answer the prompt","caption":"A made-up answer is turned away with a reason, and the clock keeps running.","width":1544,"height":897},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/col-collision.webp","alt":"Round results showing Collision, with both blurred players on the team having answered cake","caption":"Both players said \"cake\", so Team A takes its first strike.","width":1544,"height":897}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"The question bank went the same way. On 10 July it grew past 500 questions. I then cut it back to 44 hand-picked prompts, each with a list of answers that can be checked."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/col-safe.webp","alt":"Round results showing Safe, with two different blurred answers: iron man and captain america","caption":"Two different heroes, so Team B is safe.","width":1544,"height":897},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/col-results.webp","alt":"Game over screen: Team B wins, with Team A on 3 of 3 collisions and Team B on 1 of 3","caption":"The end screen counts strikes, not points.","width":1260,"height":840}],"layout":"two"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A checked list cuts both ways. An answer that is right but missing from my list is rejected too. And six of the 44 questions list fewer than 8 answers, so the question picker never chooses them."}}]},{"id":"forger","label":"Decision 3","nav":"The Forger","heading":"In The Forger, one secret is shown to everyone but one player","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the same screen, with one line different. Artists see the secret word. The Forger sees only the category and is told to blend in. Each player draws one unbroken line per turn, twice around the table."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/forger-artist.webp","alt":"The Forger game for an artist: Category Food, Secret word Watermelon, turn order with blurred names, and a canvas with a half circle and two loose lines","caption":"An artist's turn. The secret word is at the top.","width":1544,"height":1002},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/forger-forger.webp","alt":"The same moment for the Forger: Category Food and the words You are The Forger! Blend in, with the same drawing","caption":"The same moment for the Forger, who has to guess what everyone is drawing.","width":1544,"height":1002}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: a line is a list of points on a 600 by 400 canvas. It is sent when you lift the pen, and the host only accepts it from the player whose turn it is. That rule is what keeps \"one line per turn\" honest."},{"p":"After everyone has drawn twice, everyone votes. A tie, or the wrong person accused, and the Forger wins. If the Forger is caught, they get one guess at the word to steal the win."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/forger-vote.webp","alt":"Voting screen over the faded drawing, asking Who is The Forger? with two vote buttons whose names are blurred","caption":"The vote. You cannot vote for yourself.","width":1544,"height":1002},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/forger-guess.webp","alt":"The Forger was caught, with a One last chance box and a field to guess the word","caption":"Caught, but one guess left.","width":1230,"height":870},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/forger-end.webp","alt":"The artists win screen showing the guess was wrong, the secret word Watermelon and the blurred name of the Forger","caption":"The guess was wrong, so the artists win.","width":1230,"height":848}],"layout":"three"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"It needs at least three players. And as with the other games, the secret word and the Forger's identity travel to every browser. Only the screen hides them."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"The games came quickly, and the connections took the work","blocks":[{"p":"I started with a Node server and prepared it for two hosts, Render and Hugging Face Spaces. Before the morning was out I rewrote it so the host's browser runs the game, and deleted the server. Much of the history after that is about getting browsers to connect."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/hosting-journey.webp","alt":"Timeline of commits on 10, 13 and 17 July 2026, from a Node server to a serverless rewrite, the hub, connection fixes, a TURN password function and the Collision rebuild","caption":"The history in one picture. Hollow dots are the server version that I dropped.","width":1600,"height":980}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Interface","React 19 and Vite, plain CSS"],["Pages","React Router with hash links (#/collision), one page per game"],["Connections","PeerJS over WebRTC, with Google's public STUN servers as a fallback"],["Relay passwords","One Vercel server function that asks the TURN provider, Xirsys, for short-lived credentials"],["Hosting","Vercel, serving the built site"],["Checks","oxlint for code style. No automated test suite"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"34","label":"commits, 10 to 17 July 2026"},{"value":"15","label":"of them about hosting or connections"},{"value":"44","label":"Collision prompts, each with its own answer list"},{"value":"102","label":"Forger words in 10 categories"}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were the ones I could not see on one computer."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Joining","A player could try to connect before their own connection was ready. A short wait before connecting fixed the race. Old connections left open when you went back were also closed."],["Errors","A timeout message was replacing the real reason a join failed. Now the real error is kept."],["Firewalls","The free public relay I used stopped working, so joins timed out behind firewalls. I replaced it with the password function."],["The fix's fix","The function's reply had the wrong shape for the browser, and browsers then kept the bad reply in their cache. Two more commits fixed both."]]}},{"p":"There are no automated tests. One script plays ten made-up players through the Collision rules without a network. It dates from the first day and has not been updated since the answer checks arrived."}]},{"id":"lies","label":"The first attempt","nav":"Game of Lies","heading":"Game of Lies came first, with a demo mode that never leaves one browser","blocks":[{"p":"Game of Lies is the June 2026 product. Two teams, Team Stapler and Team Paperclip, take turns with office trivia. One player answers for the team and may lie. The other team's decider sees only that an answer is locked in, and chooses to steal it or pass."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/gol-home.webp","alt":"Game of Lies home page in a dark theme: Welcome to the Game Room, Host new game and Join game room buttons, and one game card for Game of Lies","caption":"The home page was built for many games but holds one. Top right: \"Local Storage Sync\", the mode it is running in.","width":1600,"height":1147}]},{"table":{"head":["The other team","Answer was right","Answer was a lie"],"rows":[["Passes","Answering team +1","Nobody scores"],["Steals","Stealing team +2","Stealing team −2"]]}},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/annotated-lies.webp","alt":"The answering player's screen with five numbered notes: the mode badge, scores and goal, the answering player prompt, the answer options, and the team chat","caption":"Note 4 is a real flaw. The first option is always the right one, which gives the bluff away once players notice.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/gol-steal.webp","alt":"The stealing team's decider sees that Team A has locked in, with two large cards: Steal, plus or minus 2, and Pass, 0 points","caption":"The decider's choice. The two cards spell out what each one risks.","width":1600,"height":1147},{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/gol-reveal.webp","alt":"The reveal: the correct answer next to Team A's chosen answer, and the message Failed steal, Team B fell for the lie, minus 2 points","caption":"The reveal. Team A lied, Team B stole, and Team B lost 2 points.","width":1600,"height":1213}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"It has two ways to share a room. In \"Local Storage Sync\" the room is saved in the browser, and other tabs of that same browser see each change. To play on separate devices, each player has to paste the settings of a Firebase database into the game first."},{"p":"To try a round alone, the host can add up to nine simulated players. On its turn, a simulated player waits four seconds. Then it answers correctly 70% of the time, or, as the decider, steals half the time."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/office-games/gol-lobby.webp","alt":"The Game of Lies lobby with a four-letter room code, a grid of nine office characters, team buttons, two team lists with blurred names, a lobby chat and host buttons including Add simulated player","caption":"The lobby: pick a character and a team. Two of the four players here are simulated.","width":1600,"height":1573,"kind":"tall"}]},{"aside":{"title":"What the demo mode does not show.","detail":"Local mode is not network play between devices. It only links tabs of one browser on one computer. The screens above were made that way. I have no record that the Firebase mode was ever used for a real game, and the project has no hosting set up."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live, with no usage numbers and a short list of known problems","blocks":[{"p":"The hub is live at the link above. The last commit was on 17 July 2026. I have no player counts or notes from real sessions to report."},{"p":"These problems are known and still open:"},{"list":["When the host leaves, the room ends for everyone. Other players are not told why.","Keyboard Scramble's results heading says \"Final results!\" after every round, because the screen checks a copy of the round number that is not updated during play.","The Forger's header reads \"Round 3 / 2\" while people vote.","The Collision card on the home page still describes the old rule: more than half, and the exact same answer.","Hidden information reaches every browser, and the ten-player script is out of date."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Test the connection before the game","blocks":[{"p":"Collision and The Forger were each added within hours on 10 July. Connections took 15 of the 34 commits, and the worst failures, like a relay that quietly stopped working, could not be seen from one computer. The lesson I take from it: try joining from two different networks early, before adding the next game."},{"p":"Collision taught me that a fair rule has to be one the host can check. \"The same answer\" sounded clear until \"NY\" and \"New York\" disagreed. Writing down the accepted answers made the rule real, and it also showed me where my lists were too short."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","headline":"Health readings with the context attached."}},"forma":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","kind":"product","headline":"A health app where every number says how sure it is.","summary":"Forma is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It reads the activity, sleep and heart records a Galaxy watch shares, and labels each one with when it was measured, where it came from and whether it is only an estimate.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Native Android app · experimental wellness estimates","context":"Personal health","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"main destinations"},{"value":"44","label":"native metric views"},{"value":"Recorded","label":"measurement times retained"}],"problem":"A watch can collect more measurements than a person can comfortably interpret. A number also changes meaning when it is old, incomplete, imported or estimated. I framed Forma around a daily overview that stays useful without making uncertain data look more reliable than it is.","process":["I organised the experience around five destinations: Today for a daily overview, Sleep for the recorded night, Move for activity and workouts, Trends for comparison, and Health for the measurement library. The native app is the product; the React version in the repository is a design preview.","The data constraints shaped the UX. Samsung Health must share records through Health Connect, permissions can be partial, and not every watch measurement is available. Connection setup therefore needs to explain availability rather than treat an empty reading as a failure or a zero.","The later function audit separated Devices from Your data, moved optional personal tools into settings, removed controls without a working purpose, and put formulas behind expandable details. The archived screens illustrate the native visual direction; the audit and current source establish the later navigation and scope. This is an artifact-based retrospective, without a participant study or clinical validation."],"decisions":[{"title":"Distinguish recorded, missing and estimated values","choice":"Keep source labels and measurement times attached to readings. Preserve gaps in histories, identify sample data, and distinguish sleeping heart rate from a recorded resting-heart-rate value.","reason":"A recent refresh does not mean a sensor reading is recent. People need to understand what a value represents before comparing it with yesterday or using it to interpret their day.","tradeoff":"These labels add density. The hierarchy has to keep essential provenance visible while leaving longer explanations available on demand."},{"title":"Let a score open into its explanation","choice":"Show the score and its contributing values first, then offer the calculation and diagnostics in an expandable sheet. Recovery requires recorded sleep and a usable baseline vital; missing inputs can leave the estimate unavailable.","reason":"An unexplained score can look authoritative. Showing its inputs makes the estimate inspectable and lets the interface explain why the same score can accompany different activity records.","tradeoff":"Transparency does not validate the model. Sleep, recovery and body-energy estimates remain experimental wellness interpretations, distinct from Samsung proprietary scores and medical assessment."},{"title":"Separate connecting a device from managing data","choice":"Devices leads with connection state and the next available action. Your data contains coverage, export, optional import and local reset. Targets and personal tools have their own settings paths.","reason":"Someone trying to connect a watch should not have to interpret a coverage report or navigate file-management controls before granting access.","tradeoff":"Separating the tasks adds destinations. Clear names and back paths matter, particularly when permissions are partial or a watch has not synced."}],"outcome":"I organised the native app around a daily overview, dedicated sleep and movement views, histories and a searchable health library. A score opens into its contributors; device setup and data management have separate destinations. The estimates remain experimental, and Samsung sharing depends on the device and permissions.","reflection":"Separating Devices from Your data made the next action clearer. I would now check whether someone can explain an unavailable or older reading as readily as a complete one, and whether they can find the inputs behind a score.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask people to distinguish sample, shared, direct-watch, older and unavailable readings, then explain what an experimental recovery score does and does not mean.","Proposed device checks: follow granted, denied and partial-permission journeys on a Samsung phone and watch, including delayed sync, expired direct readings and corrected history.","Proposed measures: connection-task completion, mistaken interpretations of missing data, and success finding a score explanation or exporting records."],"sources":[{"label":"Forma README and native interface source","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Native Android scope, five destinations, Samsung Health/Health Connect integration, measurement timestamps and experimental score limits. The web project is a design preview."},{"label":"docs/DESIGN.md and docs/UX-AUDIT.md","detail":"The later 1.5 audit supersedes the older 46-measurement design note: 44 native metric views, distinct Devices/Your data flows, optional personal tools and expandable score explanations."},{"label":"Archived native captures · sample data","detail":"Today rings and vitals captures from the project output illustrate the native visual system. The Today capture predates the Health tab rename and personal-tool cleanup; the later Your data capture shows the separated data-management flow. Values are demonstration data."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup.","width":1080,"height":2400},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-data.webp","alt":"Forma Your data screen showing sample coverage, export and separate data controls","caption":"The later Your data panel groups coverage and file controls separately from device setup. All values shown are sample data.","width":1080,"height":2400}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/forma/releases/latest","productActionLabel":"Get Forma for Android","cover":"assets/products/forma.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"productHeaderLabel":"Android release","pdfPage":60,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Health readings [[with the context attached.]]","scene":"Imagine opening a health dashboard after a watch sync. One measurement is recent, one was last recorded days ago, and another has not been shared. Similar-looking numbers can hide very different situations.","question":"How can a daily overview make the meaning of each reading clear?","insight":"[[Data quality is part of the experience]]. Source, measurement time, missing inputs and model coverage change how a reading should be interpreted.","pivot":"I focused the daily overview on readable measurements, with a clear route into history, source context and score explanations.","takeaway":"Clarity includes knowing what the app cannot tell you.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"See Today with explicit sample or source context."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Open sleep, movement or a measurement history."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the contributors behind an experimental score."},{"title":"Manage","detail":"Use Devices to connect and Your data to inspect coverage or export."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep provenance beside the reading.","Show the inputs behind the score.","Give connection and data tasks separate homes."],"annotations":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"evidenceTag":"Native artifacts and documented UX audit · sample data in captures","tone":"lime","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android user reviewing Samsung-shared health records","context":"Moving between a daily summary, a specific measurement and a watch connection that may have partial permissions.","need":"Understand the reading and its source before comparing it with a personal pattern.","friction":"Missing, older and estimated values can look like equally reliable facts.","implication":"Keep time, source and availability visible, with deeper explanation one step away."}},"theme":{"layout":"health-record","mode":"dark","paper":"#101211","ink":"#f4f5ec","dim":"#b4c0b5","line":"#414c43","accent":"#d8fc70","wash":"#303d1e","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A reading without its context","process":"Treat availability as part of the design","decisions":"From summary to explanation","outcome":"A native overview with inspectable detail","reflection":"An honest limit is useful information"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the context close to the reading.","resultHeading":"A daily overview with a route to the evidence."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup.","label":"Archived native interface · sample data","source":"Health++ output/forma-today-rings.png, captured in the project on 13 October 2026. Lossy WebP delivery copy, no UI alteration.","width":1080,"height":2400,"focus":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"details":[{"decision":0,"x":50,"y":1710,"width":980,"height":430,"alt":"Forma sample heart-rate reading with explicit sample and recorded-range labels","caption":"A detail of the archived Today capture. Sample labels and measurement context stay beside the value."}]},"v2":{"title":"A health app where every number says [[how sure it is]].","hook":"Forma is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It reads the activity, sleep and heart records a Galaxy watch shares, and labels each one with when it was measured, where it came from and whether it is only an estimate.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"13 to 14 October 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"Galaxy watch owners on Android. Built around one watch model and one phone"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android phone, Android 9 and up, with an optional Wear OS watch app"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3, Health Connect, WorkManager"},{"label":"Status","value":"Version 1.5.1, installed from GitHub releases. Not on Google Play. The scores are experimental"}],"links":[{"label":"Get the Android app","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/forma/releases/latest"}],"linksHeading":"Try it on an Android phone","summary":["The problem: a number from a watch can be fresh, days old, missing or a formula's guess. Most dashboards show all four the same way.","What I built: a native Android app with five main screens and 44 measurement histories. Every reading keeps its time, its source and its kind beside it.","The key idea: a score should open up to show what fed it, and a missing input should stay missing instead of turning into a zero.","Where it stands: version 1.5.1, installed from GitHub. It is tested on emulators with sample data. The real watch-to-phone sync still needs a check on the device, and I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/forma/lead-screens.webp","alt":"Four Forma screens side by side on a pale green background: Today with activity rings, Sleep with a stage chart, Health with six vital cards, and Move with weekly totals","caption":"Version 3 of Forma: Today, Sleep, Health and Move. Every screen shows the app's built-in sample data, marked \"Demo mode\" in the top corner.","width":2400,"height":1300},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A watch number can be fresh, old, missing or a guess","blocks":[{"p":"A smartwatch measures a lot: steps, heart rate, sleep, blood oxygen and more. On a Galaxy watch, those readings go to Samsung Health on the phone. Samsung Health then decides which ones to share with other apps, and when."},{"p":"So the number an app shows can mean very different things. It might be a reading from two minutes ago. It might be a weight logged last week. It might be a zero that only means nothing was shared. Or it might be a score worked out by a formula. On a typical dashboard these all look alike."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram of four dark tiles showing a heart rate, a weight, zero minutes of exercise and a recovery score, each above a card that says what it really is: fresh, old, missing or a guess","caption":"The problem in one picture. The zero is the dangerous one: no shared workout is not proof of no workout. The values are made up for the diagram.","width":1600,"height":690}]},{"p":"I wanted an app that reads those records and is honest about each one. It should say how old a reading is, where it came from, and when a number is an estimate rather than a measurement."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"No interviews or user testing. I built Forma around one watch model, the Galaxy Watch6 Classic, and a Galaxy phone. Every screen on this page uses the app's built-in sample data, not anyone's health records."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Forma only reads what Samsung Health chooses to share","blocks":[{"p":"Health Connect is Android's shared store for health records. Samsung Health publishes into it, and the person grants Forma each type of record one at a time. Forma works with all of them granted, some of them, or none."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/data-flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of the data path: Galaxy watch to Samsung Health to Health Connect to Forma on the phone, with a separate dashed path from an optional Forma watch app to a short-lived store","caption":"Two paths. The main one feeds history and scores. The optional watch app has its own path, and its readings never mix into history or scores.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"Until records arrive, the app opens on sample data with a \"Demo mode\" badge, so there is something to explore without handing anything over. The app has five places in the bottom bar and four rows in settings."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/destinations.webp","alt":"Diagram of five bottom-bar destinations, Today, Sleep, Move, Trends and Health, above five settings items: Devices, Targets, Your data, App updates and optional Personal tools","caption":"The structure after the version 1.5 clean-up. Today is the overview; the other four go deeper into one subject each.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screens-sleep-move.webp","alt":"Two version 3 screens side by side: Sleep, with 7 hours 48 minutes against an 8 hour goal above a stage chart, and Move, with activity rings and a week of 60,605 steps","caption":"Sleep and Move. Sleep shows recorded time against your own goal, then the night by stage. Move's weekly totals say how many days were shared: \"7/7 days shared\".","width":1600,"height":1420}]}]},{"id":"versions","label":"Three versions","nav":"Three versions","heading":"Three looks in one day, each with fewer slogans","blocks":[{"p":"I redesigned the app twice on the first day, and saved screens of each version. Together they are the clearest record I have of how the design changed."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/generations.webp","alt":"Three versions of the Today screen side by side: a light sage version with three score cards, a dark version with a large body energy gauge, and a dark version with activity rings at the top","caption":"Today in versions 1, 2 and 3. Look at what fills the top half of each: a slogan, then an estimate, then measured activity.","width":1600,"height":1240}]},{"p":"Version 1 was light and calm, with lines like \"A good day to find your flow\". I kept no written notes on why I moved to version 2, so I will only describe it. It went dark, and made body energy, a rough estimate, the biggest thing on the screen."},{"p":"Version 3 turned that around. Measured activity went to the top: steps, calories and exercise against targets. The three estimates became small cards below. My design notes for version 3 say no decorative picture should take space from the measurements."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screen-you-gen1.webp","alt":"Version 1 You screen: a large daily check-in card that says your watch cannot tell the whole story, and a water tracker","caption":"Version 1. The fifth tab, You, led with a mood check-in and a water log.","width":720,"height":1473,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screen-you-gen2.webp","alt":"Version 2 You screen in dark colours: a check-in card titled More than a number, and a water log","caption":"Version 2. Same content, darker, with a bigger check-in button.","width":720,"height":1600,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screen-health.webp","alt":"Version 3 Health screen: six cards of vitals, each with a usual value","caption":"Version 3. The fifth tab became Health, the library of measurements. The check-in and water log moved into optional settings tools.","width":720,"height":1600,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Version 1 was friendlier to look at. Version 3 shows far more numbers at once, so every number needs its label to stay readable."}}]},{"id":"labels","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Time and source on every reading","heading":"Every reading carries its time and its source","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a badge in the header says where the data comes from. Each ring has a target and the amount still to go. The heart rate card says what kind of reading it is."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/annotated-today.webp","alt":"Today screen with five numbered notes: the source badge, ring targets, distance and the link to Move, the three estimate cards, and the heart rate label","caption":"Start at note 5. Here the reading says \"Sample\". With real data the same spot gives the time it was measured.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: Forma keeps two times for every reading. One is when the watch took it. The other is when Forma last checked. A fresh check never makes an old reading look new."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/live-label.webp","alt":"Diagram showing that a watch heart rate is labelled Live for 30 seconds and then shown with its time, and that watch steps lead the step ring for 5 minutes","caption":"\"Live\" runs out. After 30 seconds a heart reading is shown with the time it was taken.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"Totals such as steps use Android's own adding-up and only Samsung Health's records, so the same steps are not counted twice. When a resting heart rate is not shared, Forma can show a heart rate from sleep instead. It needs at least 12 minutes of readings, spread across at least half the night, and it is labelled \"Sleeping heart rate\", never \"resting\"."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Labels take space. \"Sample reading\", \"Recorded\" and \"7/7 days shared\" make each card busier. I kept them short and put longer explanations one tap away."}}]},{"id":"scores","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Scores that open up","heading":"A score opens to show what fed it","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: tap sleep, recovery or energy and a sheet opens. The score comes first, then one row for each input that fed it. The rule and the full working sit underneath, folded away."},{"p":"The idea was there from the start. Version 1 had a \"See the calculation\" link under recovery, and version 2 listed what was \"Behind your readiness\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screens-recovery-early.webp","alt":"Recovery in version 1 and version 2: both show a readiness score of 88, with a See the calculation link in version 1 and See what shaped it plus a list of inputs in version 2","caption":"Recovery in versions 1 and 2. Each already had a link to its working, and version 2 listed the inputs under the score.","width":1600,"height":1280}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/score-sheet.webp","alt":"Schematic of the score sheet: a recovery score, three input rows with bars, and two folded sections, with four numbered notes","caption":"The sheet in version 1.5, redrawn from the app's code because I have no screen capture of it. Note 2 matters most: an input that is missing has no row at all.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"In the build: an input that is not available is left out, not counted as zero. Its weight is shared among the inputs that are there. If there is no recorded sleep, there is no recovery score at all."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/score-weights.webp","alt":"Bar diagram of score weights: recovery as sleep 60, HRV 25 and heart 15 percent, then sleep 80 and heart 20 without HRV, then no score without sleep; sleep score as 50, 30 and 20 percent, then 62.5 and 37.5","caption":"The weights drawn to scale. Samsung never shares schedule consistency, so with its records the sleep score uses the last row.","width":1600,"height":830}]},{"p":"One problem showed me the sheet needed more. Sleep, recovery and energy share inputs and round to whole numbers, so all three could show the same value. That looked like a bug. The sheet now explains why the numbers match, shows the value before rounding, and can copy its working to the clipboard."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"Showing the working does not make the formula right. These are experimental wellness estimates. They are not medically tested and they are not Samsung's own scores. The app says so in the sheet."}}]},{"id":"usual","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Compared with your usual","heading":"Each reading is compared with your own usual, not with a chart of norms","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: each vital sits beside your usual value, with a seven-day line and the change, such as \"−3 vs usual\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/annotated-health.webp","alt":"Health screen with four numbered notes: the count of measurements, separate resting and sleeping heart rate cards, the usual value, and the footnote","caption":"Note 4 is the one I care about most: orange means a bigger change from your own pattern, not a medical finding.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/annotated-vitals.webp","alt":"Lower part of Today with four numbered notes: vitals with seven-day lines, the Recorded VO2 max label, hour-by-hour bars and the Tap an hour hint","caption":"Further down Today. The word \"Recorded\" at note 2 separates a measured value from a worked-out one.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: \"usual\" is the middle value of up to 28 earlier days. It needs at least seven days before it shows. Heart rate variability (HRV, the small changes in time between heartbeats) is compared on a log scale. So that small day-to-day noise does not look like a big change, the expected variation never drops below 1 breath a minute for breathing, 1 point for blood oxygen and 0.15°C for skin temperature."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Health ranges for the general population. Forma describes change against your own past, and does not say whether a value is healthy. The cost is a first week with no comparisons."}}]},{"id":"cleanup","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Devices and data apart","heading":"Connecting a watch and looking after your data are two jobs","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: settings has four rows. Devices shows whether the watch is connected and one next step. Your data holds the counts, export, import and delete."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/devices-before-after.webp","alt":"Before and after diagram: one Devices panel holding connection, sync, import and export and coverage reports, becomes Devices with one main button plus a separate Your data place","caption":"The version 1.5 clean-up. Import, export and coverage reports left the connection flow.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/screen-data.webp","alt":"The Your data screen with sample data: 28 recorded days, 35 measurements with shared values, a date range, an export button, a folded file import section and a clear local data link","caption":"Your data, the only screen captured after the clean-up. \"Sample data\" sits in the card title.","width":720,"height":1600,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: for version 1.5 I audited the app function by function. I checked whether each piece could be reached and whether it did real work, and wrote down how each one was verified."},{"stats":[{"value":"11","label":"unused interface parts deleted. Nothing in the app could reach them"},{"value":"2","label":"unused inputs removed: a name and a bedtime that no feature read"},{"value":"46 → 44","label":"measurements, dropping two that Samsung's connection can never send"},{"value":"4","label":"main settings rows: Devices, Targets, Your data, App updates"}]},{"p":"The audit also found real bugs. Export could report success when nothing had been written. File reading and writing ran on the thread that draws the screen. Trends showed the wrong number of decimals. All three are fixed."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Two places instead of one means one more name to learn. Clear row names and a back arrow to the parent screen have to do that work."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Two days of commits, with checks before every release","blocks":[{"p":"Forma is a native Android app. Every screen, chart and ring is drawn with Jetpack Compose; there is no web page inside it. Updates come from GitHub, not from an app store."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/release-pipeline.webp","alt":"Diagram of the release path: tests and lint, optimised build, signing and publishing on GitHub, then on the phone finding the update, the tap, file checks and Android's install prompt","caption":"How an update reaches the phone. Forma checks the size, checksum, package and signing key before Android is even asked.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3"],["Health records","Health Connect, read only from Samsung Health"],["Background refresh","WorkManager, optional, about every 15 minutes"],["Watch app","Wear OS, Health Services for the 60-second heart check, and the encrypted Data Layer"],["Charts","Drawn by hand on the native canvas"],["Releases","GitHub Actions: tests, lint, signed builds, published manifests"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"12","label":"commits over 13 and 14 October 2026, one author"},{"value":"55","label":"unit checks passing at the last recorded run"},{"value":"39","label":"checks of real screens on an Android 14 emulator"},{"value":"4.2 MB","label":"size of the phone app"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/forma/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of the two days: on 13 October the first two looks, signed updates, activity first, screen capture tools, version 3 and the watch app; on 14 October score explanations, energy fixes, the 1.5 clean-up, setup notes and an energy fallback","caption":"The build day by day, from the project history.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were in reading the data, not in drawing it."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Energy after waking","Energy dropped the whole clock hour in which you woke, so early movement went missing. It now starts at the real wake-up time. If Samsung's hourly summaries are missing, it asks for exact times instead, and six new tests cover those cases."],["Changes during a read","A record could change while Forma was reading. Forma now notes where it started before reading and saves that point only after the import finishes, so nothing is skipped."],["Moving to a new signing key","The first preview was signed with a different key. I checked that installing the new version kept every stored record, setting and the app's identity."]]}},{"p":"There is no advertising and no analytics. Health records stay in the app's private storage and are left out of phone backups. An export leaves out personal notes."}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Installed from GitHub, not yet proven on the real watch","blocks":[{"p":"Forma is at version 1.5.1. It installs from GitHub releases and updates itself after you confirm. It is not on Google Play, and I have no usage numbers to share."},{"p":"I use Forma every day myself."},{"list":["The tests run on emulators, with sample data and made-up Health Connect responses. The full path from the watch through Samsung Health to Forma still needs a check on the real phone and watch.","How the watch installs its own updates still needs a check on a physical Galaxy watch.","The scores are experimental. Nothing about them has been checked against medical measurements.","Screens captured after the 1.5 clean-up are incomplete. Only Your data is on this page in its final form."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Check it on the device, then with people","blocks":[{"list":["Run the device checks: granted, refused and partial permissions on a Samsung phone and watch, a late sync and a corrected record.","Capture the final Today, Health, Devices and score sheet screens.","Ask a few people to sort readings into fresh, old, missing and estimated, and to explain what a recovery score does and does not mean."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A missing number is information too","blocks":[{"p":"The most useful rule was the simplest: missing stays missing. It shaped the rings, the scores, the charts with gaps and the labels. Every place that broke it would have shown a confident number with nothing behind it."},{"p":"The three looks taught me to ask what fills the top of the screen. In two of the three versions it was not a measurement at all. The audit taught me that unused parts pile up fast, even in two days: there were eleven in this app."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","headline":"Sail your ship. Coordinate the fleet."}},"ohcaptain":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","kind":"product","headline":"A naval game for crews of three, where every decision has an owner","summary":"OhCaptain is a three-against-three naval game for the browser that I designed and built on my own. Each crew has one captain who spends and two operators who ask, and the screen is built around the next move each of them can make.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Playable browser prototype · keyboard & mouse","context":"Naval capture-and-hold · Operation Blackwater","facts":[{"value":"3 v 3","label":"captain and two operators per crew"},{"value":"8","label":"capture-and-hold relays"},{"value":"10 min","label":"maximum match window"}],"problem":"In OhCaptain, sailing and firing are only part of the job. Each crew shares supplies, but its captain decides how to spend them. Operators need to request help while both roles keep track of a changing battle. I framed the interface around the next decision a player can actually make.","process":["I separated the crew journey into choosing a station, entering solo play or assembling a room, deploying, and managing the battle. A captain still sails and fights; the extra responsibility is distributing shared supplies. Operators request upgrades or repairs rather than spending the treasury themselves.","The documented Clear Deck revision gives combat a compact score/clock, fleet-alive counts, hull status and a persistent map. Spending moves into the Fleet drawer, while the expanded overview holds rosters, recent events and relay details. Warnings compete for a small number of attention slots instead of becoming a wall of banners.","I reviewed the local playable build, documentation and awareness rules. Solo play with AI fill and peer-to-peer rooms are distinct product paths. Online scope depends on a host keeping the page open; the prototype has no dedicated match service or host migration. Rule checks and seeded scenarios do not establish balance or usability with human crews."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make responsibility visible before deployment","choice":"Offer Captain and Vessel Operator as distinct stations. Captains distribute shared supplies through the Fleet drawer; operators use the same crew context to request an upgrade or repair.","reason":"A shared resource needs a clear decision owner. Role-specific actions let people understand why they can request something but cannot buy it directly.","tradeoff":"The captain can become a bottleneck while also sailing and fighting. Pending requests need to remain discoverable without forcing a panel over combat."},{"title":"Keep essential battle state on the field","choice":"Keep the score, clock, relay balance, fleet-alive counts, hull and corner map available. Put spending in a drawer and detailed rosters, events and defenses in the expanded overview.","reason":"The map helps connect local action to the relay objective. Separating routine awareness from deliberate inspection leaves room to aim and navigate.","tradeoff":"An overview can interrupt action. Solo overview pauses the match; online play continues, so players need to understand that distinction. The prototype targets keyboard and mouse rather than claiming a touch-control experience."},{"title":"Give urgent feedback a limited attention budget","choice":"Show at most one off-screen enemy bearing and reserve one text cue for critical hull, safe healing or an endangered home relay. Limit outgoing damage feedback to two numbers and keep routine radio outside combat.","reason":"An alert is useful only if it helps a player choose an action. A priority order makes a received hit or low hull easier to notice among effects, ships and objectives.","tradeoff":"A quieter interface can hide context that an experienced player wants. The map and overview must provide a dependable route to that detail, and the priority policy needs human playtesting."}],"outcome":"I separated immediate battle awareness, fleet spending and tactical inspection across the combat view, Fleet drawer and overview. The prototype supports solo AI-filled matches and crew rooms in one capture-and-hold arena. The captures here show the actual game; the illustrated harbor is menu artwork.","reflection":"I would put a new crew through requesting a repair, spending supplies and choosing a relay without coaching. Missed requests and time spent searching the overview would help me decide what to simplify next.","nextSteps":["Proposed playtest: form two crews and ask players to explain their role, request or buy a repair, and identify the relay that matters next without coaching.","Proposed checks: invitation and room entry, role reassignment, a guest disconnecting, restrictive peer-to-peer networks, and an honest explanation when a host leaves.","Proposed observations: missed requests, map-to-world confusion, unnoticed critical alerts and time spent in the overview. Compare these before changing the attention policy."],"sources":[{"label":"OhCaptain README · Play, online limits and Clear Deck interface","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Captain/operator responsibilities, AI fill, peer-to-peer rooms, eight-relay objectives, ten-minute match window and host/network limitations."},{"label":"src/awareness.js, src/main.js and src/combat.css","detail":"Battle interpretation, role-specific controls, attention priority, local damage feedback and the single off-screen enemy-bearing policy."},{"label":"Local playable build and docs/BLACKWATER-ART.md","detail":"Actual solo combat and expanded-map captures from 16 October 2026. The harbor capture includes the project’s generated cinematic key art; it is menu presentation, distinct from the procedural playable arena."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull.","width":1280,"height":720},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-map.webp","alt":"OhCaptain expanded tactical map with both fleets, islands and eight labeled relays","caption":"A detail of the actual expanded tactical map. Both fronts, ship positions and relay ownership share the same view; the solo overview pauses play.","width":643,"height":414}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/OhCaptain","productActionLabel":"View the game on GitHub","cover":"assets/products/ohcaptain.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":8,"productHeaderLabel":"GitHub project","pdfPage":63,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Sail your ship. [[Coordinate the fleet.]]","scene":"Imagine taking damage while an operator asks for repairs and an enemy crew captures a relay. The captain must decide where shared supplies go while still steering a ship.","question":"What needs to stay visible when sailing, fighting and command happen together?","insight":"[[Information needs a place and a priority]]. An urgent hull warning, a spending decision and a full fleet overview should not demand attention in the same way.","pivot":"I separated immediate combat awareness, role-specific fleet controls and deliberate tactical inspection.","takeaway":"A crew can coordinate when the next action has an owner.","journey":[{"title":"Choose a station","detail":"Understand captain spending or operator requests."},{"title":"Deploy","detail":"Start with AI fill or assemble a crew through a room."},{"title":"Read the battle","detail":"Keep hull, fleet status and the relay map available."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Open Fleet to spend or request; expand the overview to plan."}],"decisionLeads":["Assign responsibility before the battle.","Give each information layer a place.","Let urgent signals take precedence."],"annotations":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"evidenceTag":"Playable prototype and documented Clear Deck revision · solo captures","tone":"navy","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Player joining a small naval crew","context":"Learning a captain or operator role while coordinating around a finite relay-control match.","need":"Know who can act, what is urgent and where the next objective is.","friction":"Combat, spending requests and tactical detail can overwhelm the same screen.","implication":"Preserve immediate awareness and reveal deliberate command detail when requested."}},"theme":{"layout":"fleet-command","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b141b","ink":"#e9edf0","dim":"#afc1cc","line":"#405766","accent":"#e7c58c","wash":"#313021","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Archivo","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A shared treasury in a live battle","process":"Give each information layer a place","decisions":"From combat awareness to command","outcome":"The sea stays in view","reflection":"The next action needs an owner"},"decisionHeading":"Keep combat clear and command within reach.","resultHeading":"One arena, three layers of information."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull.","label":"Actual solo gameplay","source":"Screenshot of the repository’s local built game on 16 October 2026; WebP delivery copy.","width":1280,"height":720,"focus":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"details":[{"decision":1,"x":14,"y":484,"width":270,"height":222,"alt":"OhCaptain persistent corner map showing relays and fleet positions","caption":"The actual corner map keeps the wider objective available while the player sails."}]},"v2":{"title":"A naval game for crews of three, where [[every decision has an owner]]","hook":"OhCaptain is a three-against-three naval game for the browser that I designed and built on my own. Each crew has one captain who spends and two operators who ask, and the screen is built around the next move each of them can make.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Team","value":"Just me"},{"label":"For","value":"Players joining a small naval crew (my assumption, not research)"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web browser, keyboard and mouse"},{"label":"Stack","value":"JavaScript and CSS. Rooms use direct peer connections"},{"label":"Status","value":"Playable prototype. Not yet tested with human crews"}],"links":[{"label":"View the game on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/OhCaptain"}],"linksHeading":"See the project","summary":["The problem: in a crew game, the captain steers, fights and decides where shared supplies go, often in the same few seconds.","What I built: a playable 3 v 3 browser game with two stations, captain and operator, eight relays to hold and a match of at most 10 minutes.","The key idea: each kind of information gets a place and a priority. Urgent signals share a small budget, spending sits in a drawer, and planning sits in an overview.","Where it stands: a playable prototype for solo play with AI crew and for rooms. No human crew has played a test session yet."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain in play: the player's ship on open water, with scores and a clock at the top, a corner map at bottom left, a hull bar at the bottom and a Fleet button at bottom right","caption":"The real game in solo play. AI crew fill the other seats. Notice how much open sea is left, because what you need sits at the edges.","width":1280,"height":720},"sections":[{"id":"problem","label":"The problem","nav":"The problem","heading":"A captain has to steer, fight and spend in the same few seconds","blocks":[{"p":"OhCaptain is a naval game for the browser. Two crews of three try to capture and hold relays, which are fixed points on the map, before time runs out."},{"p":"Picture one moment. Your ship is taking damage. An operator asks for a repair. The enemy crew is taking a relay. The captain has to decide where the crew's shared supplies go while still steering."},{"p":"So the question I designed around was simple: what has to stay visible when sailing, fighting and command all happen at once?"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram: three demands arriving at the same moment and landing on one captain, beside the three places I gave them: the field, the Fleet drawer and the overview","caption":"The problem in one picture. On the left, three things that can land at once. On the right, the idea the whole game screen follows: each gets its own place.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did no player research for this game. The audience is my own assumption, and no playtest with human crews has been run yet."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Two crews of three, eight relays and a ten-minute clock","blocks":[{"p":"Each crew has one captain and two operators. Everyone sails and fights. The captain has one more job: spending the crew's shared supplies. Operators ask for an upgrade or a repair instead of buying it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/crew.webp","alt":"Diagram of two crews, each with one captain and two operators, between them the numbers 8 relays and 10 minutes, and below a three-step flow of an operator's repair request reaching the captain","caption":"How a match is set up. Look at the bottom row: a repair always passes through the captain, so the supplies have one owner.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"A player chooses a station, then either plays solo, with AI crew in the empty seats, or assembles a crew in a room. Once deployed, the job is to read the battle and coordinate."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/journey.webp","alt":"Four-step flow: choose a station, play solo or assemble a crew, read the battle, coordinate","caption":"The player's path. Step 2 splits: solo play and rooms are separate paths, built differently.","width":1300,"height":700}]}]},{"id":"roles","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Roles before the battle","heading":"Who can spend is settled before the match starts","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: before deploying, you pick a station. Captain and Vessel Operator sit side by side as two cards, each with one line on what it does."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/annotated-stations.webp","alt":"The station picker cut from the game's menu, with three numbered notes: the Captain card, the Vessel Operator card and the Assemble your crew button","caption":"The station picker. Compare notes 1 and 2: only the captain's card mentions the treasury, the shared supplies.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"Why: a shared purse needs one person who decides. If the roles are clear before the battle, an operator understands why they can ask for something but cannot buy it."},{"p":"In the build: controls depend on the station. Both roles open the same Fleet drawer. A captain uses it to spend; an operator uses it to ask."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The captain can become a bottleneck, because they are also sailing and fighting. Requests that are waiting have to stay easy to find, without a panel pushed over the battle."}}]},{"id":"field","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Battle state on the field","heading":"What you need every second stays on the sea","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a revision I called Clear Deck. It keeps a small set of things on screen: score, clock, the balance of the relays, ships still afloat, your hull and a corner map. Spending and detail moved off the field."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/annotated-combat.webp","alt":"The combat screen with eight numbered notes: scores, clock, ships afloat, relay balance line, corner map, hull bar, Fleet button and a bearing marker","caption":"The combat screen, part by part. Start at note 5: the map never leaves, because it ties what happens near your ship to the relays you are fighting over.","width":1600,"height":1500}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/hud-top.webp","alt":"Close-up of the top bar: Tide 599 against Coral 599, a clock at 09:06, three ships afloat on each side and a line reading Signal even, Relays 3 to 5","caption":"The top bar, enlarged. Two scores, one clock and one line on the relays. The scores are tied here at 599.","width":1080,"height":166}],"layout":"one"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/hud-minimap.webp","alt":"Close-up of the corner map: relays A to H, ships as coloured dots, and a key for You, Tide and Coral with M to expand","caption":"The corner map, enlarged. It is small, but it keeps all eight relays in view while you aim.","width":488,"height":424},{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/hud-hull.webp","alt":"Close-up of the hull panel: the ship's name and station, a full bar at 100 of 100, and the Space key for boost and R for repair","caption":"Your hull, enlarged. The two keys you need most sit right under the bar.","width":648,"height":208}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: information lives in three places. The field holds what you need every second. The Fleet drawer, opened with C, holds spending and requests. The expanded overview, opened with M, holds crew rosters, recent events and relay details."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/layers.webp","alt":"Diagram of three columns: the field, always on screen; the Fleet drawer, opened with C; and the expanded overview, opened with M, with what each holds","caption":"Three places for three kinds of attention. Read it left to right, from what you need every second to what you need when you stop to plan.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/annotated-map.webp","alt":"The expanded overview map with five numbered notes: the two fronts, the eight relays coloured by owner, the values under each relay, grey islands and ships as dots","caption":"The expanded overview. Both fronts, every ship and who holds each relay share one view. Ship names are blurred here.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"The overview behaves differently by mode. In solo play it pauses the match. In a room, the match keeps going while it is open."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"An overview can interrupt the action, and players need to know that it pauses solo play but not online play. I also built for keyboard and mouse only. I make no claim about touch controls."}}]},{"id":"attention","label":"Decision 3","nav":"An attention budget","heading":"Warnings share a small budget instead of piling up","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: at most one marker points to an enemy off screen. One line of warning text is kept for three cases: critical hull, safe healing or a home relay in danger. Your own hits show at most two damage numbers. Routine radio stays out of combat."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/attention.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing a ruled-out stack of eight warning banners with the chosen budget: one enemy bearing, one warning line, two damage numbers and no routine radio","caption":"The attention budget. The left side is the option I ruled out, drawn as an illustration, not taken from the game. The right side is the rule the game follows.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"Why: an alert is useful only if it helps a player choose an action. With a fixed order of importance, a hit you take or a low hull is easier to notice among effects, ships and objectives."},{"p":"In the build: the rules that read the battle and decide which signal wins sit together in the game's awareness rules. They cover the single off-screen bearing, the warning priority and the damage feedback."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A quieter screen can hide context an experienced player wants. The map and the overview have to be a dependable route to that detail. The priority order itself still needs testing with people."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Two ways into one arena, with the online limits stated","blocks":[{"p":"Solo play and rooms lead to the same arena, but they are built differently. Solo play fills empty seats with AI crew. A room depends on one player, the host, keeping the page open, with other players joining over direct peer connections."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/paths.webp","alt":"Diagram of the two ways to play: solo with AI fill and a pausing overview, and a room with a host's browser, direct peer connections and no host migration, both leading to the same arena","caption":"How it is put together. The red card is the limit to remember: if the host leaves, the match cannot move to someone else.","width":1600,"height":920}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What it is"],"rows":[["Game","A browser game in JavaScript and CSS, played with keyboard and mouse"],["Solo play","AI crew fill the empty seats. The overview pauses the match"],["Rooms","A host plus direct peer connections. No dedicated match service"],["Battle rules","Awareness rules for role controls, alert priority, damage feedback and the single off-screen bearing"],["Checks","Rule checks and seeded scenarios, which replay set situations"]]}},{"p":"The checks show that the rules do what they say. They do not show that the game is balanced, or that human crews can use it."},{"p":"Online play is where most of the open questions are."},{"table":{"head":["Area","Where it stands"],"rows":[["Host leaves","There is no host migration. The game still needs an honest message for when a host leaves"],["Joining","Invitations, entering a room and changing roles still need to be checked"],["Dropped players","A guest disconnecting has not been checked yet"],["Networks","Restrictive networks that block peer connections have not been checked yet"]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"A playable prototype that no human crew has tested","blocks":[{"p":"OhCaptain is playable in the browser with keyboard and mouse. It supports solo matches with AI crew and crew rooms, in one capture-and-hold arena. The screens on this page are from the actual game, in solo play."},{"p":"There is no hosted version to link to. The project is on GitHub."},{"list":["No playtests with human crews have been run.","I have no player numbers or feedback to share.","Balance has not been tested."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Put two crews in front of it without coaching","blocks":[{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/ohcaptain/playtest.webp","alt":"Plan for a first playtest in four steps: two crews with no coaching, three tasks, four signs of trouble to watch for, then compare before changing anything","caption":"The first playtest I would run. Look at the third column: these are the signs that would tell me what to simplify.","width":1600,"height":780}]},{"list":["Ask each player to explain their role, ask for or buy a repair, and name the relay that matters next.","Watch for missed requests, trouble matching the map to the sea, critical alerts nobody notices, and time spent in the overview. Compare these before changing the attention budget.","Check room invitations and entry, role changes, a guest dropping out, restrictive networks, and a clear message when the host leaves."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A crew can coordinate when the next action has an owner","blocks":[{"p":"Information needs a place and a priority. A hull warning, a spending choice and a full fleet overview should not all ask for attention in the same way."},{"p":"Most of my decisions here were about what to leave off the screen. Whether I left off the right things is something only a real crew can show me, which is why the playtest comes before any more changes."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"halo","name":"Halo","headline":"Keep the action close to the pointer."}},"halo":{"slug":"halo","name":"Halo","kind":"product","headline":"Your most used actions in a ring around the pointer, one shortcut away.","summary":"Halo is a desktop app I designed and built on my own, for macOS and Windows. Press one shortcut, or a mouse button, and a ring of your actions opens wherever the pointer is.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Desktop utility · macOS and Windows","context":"Pointer-based shortcuts","facts":[{"value":"8","label":"slots in a ring"},{"value":"Per app","label":"different action sets"},{"value":"Click or flick","label":"two ways to choose"}],"problem":"A useful shortcut is one I can reach without breaking the task I am doing. Halo puts a ring of actions around the pointer, instead of asking me to hunt through menus or remember another keyboard combination. The design problem was keeping that first choice quick while still allowing the ring to grow.","process":["I organised the experience around opening the ring, choosing a direction and returning to the original task. Clicking supports a deliberate choice; holding the shortcut, moving towards an action and releasing supports a quicker gesture once the layout is familiar.","The editor exposes the same ring spatially. Selecting a slot opens its action, app behaviour, label and appearance settings. App-specific rings and folders hold more choices without putting every action into the first ring.","Halo is an independent project inspired by Logitech’s Actions Ring. I focused on the interaction and visual configuration. Comparing click and flick selection with familiar keyboard shortcuts is still a useful next test."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the first choice spatial","choice":"Keep actions in stable positions around the pointer, with labels available while choosing.","reason":"A direction can become familiar through use, while labels help someone who is still learning the layout.","tradeoff":"Moving actions frequently would work against that familiarity. Customisation needs to feel deliberate."},{"title":"Let the editor mirror the ring","choice":"Select a slot in the ring and edit that action beside it.","reason":"The person can see which position they are changing instead of translating between a list and the final layout.","tradeoff":"A visual editor still needs clear names and keyboard access; position alone is not enough."},{"title":"Keep specialised actions out of the first layer","choice":"Use app-specific rings and folders for actions that belong to a particular context.","reason":"A general ring can stay small while a specialist workflow has its own useful choices.","tradeoff":"Context changes and nested folders must remain predictable. More actions do not automatically make the tool faster."}],"outcome":"I gave the ring and its editor the same spatial model, with click and hold-and-flick selection, per-app configurations and nested action folders. The app supports actions such as app control, media, window management and snippets. Some actions depend on the operating system and the permissions available.","reflection":"I would start a comparison with a small, familiar task set rather than a ring full of possibilities. I want to know which positions people remember, when they hesitate and whether app-specific rings help or surprise them.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: configure three frequently used actions, repeat the task after a break and compare hesitation with an existing shortcut method.","Proposed checks: keyboard-only configuration, ring changes between apps, folder backtracking and permission-denied actions."],"sources":[{"label":"Halo README · using and customising the ring","detail":"Click/flick selection, app-specific rings, folders, platform support and permission boundaries. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Original editor and ring documentation images","detail":"Action-Ring/docs/images/editor.png and classic.png. Existing project graphics, not newly generated interface concepts. The ring example uses a neutral illustrated background."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","alt":"Halo original ring editor with a selected Finder action and its settings","caption":"Original Halo editor capture from the project documentation. The action’s position and settings are visible together.","width":1100,"height":740},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-ring.webp","alt":"Halo ring example with a labelled Play Pause action over a neutral illustrated page","caption":"Original ring example from the project documentation, shown over a neutral illustrated page rather than a live document.","width":720,"height":720}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/Halo","productActionLabel":"View Halo on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":66,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Keep the action [[close to the pointer.]]","scene":"Imagine selecting text, opening an app and returning to the same document several times. A small set of nearby actions could keep that sequence from becoming a tour of menus.","question":"How much can a shortcut tool offer without making its first choice slower?","insight":"[[A small first ring needs a clear way to grow]]. Stable directions, app-specific contexts and folders serve different levels of familiarity.","pivot":"I kept the first ring focused and made the editor use the same positions that appear around the pointer.","takeaway":"A shortcut is useful when returning to the task feels easy.","journey":[{"title":"Open","detail":"Invoke the ring near the current pointer."},{"title":"Choose","detail":"Click a labelled action or use the hold-and-flick gesture."},{"title":"Configure","detail":"Select the same slot in the editor and adjust its action."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the first choice spatial","Let the editor mirror the ring","Keep specialised actions out of the first layer"],"annotations":[{"title":"Visual editor","detail":"The selected slot and its settings share one view."},{"title":"Platform scope","detail":"Some actions depend on OS capabilities or granted permissions."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"halo","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Desktop user with a few repeated actions","context":"Switching between a document, apps and small utility tasks.","need":"Reach a familiar action without a long interruption.","friction":"Too many shortcuts and contexts become another thing to remember.","implication":"Keep the first ring small, stable and easy to edit."}},"theme":{"layout":"halo","mode":"light","paper":"#f8f9fb","ink":"#202630","dim":"#596373","line":"#d4dae3","accent":"#315a9c","wash":"#e6edfa","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Instrument Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How much can a shortcut tool offer without making its first choice slower?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Halo","resultHeading":"A ring that stays connected to its editor."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","alt":"Halo original ring editor with a selected Finder action and its settings","caption":"Original Halo editor capture from the project documentation. The action’s position and settings are visible together.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Action-Ring/docs/images/editor.png and classic.png. Existing project graphics, not newly generated interface concepts. The ring example uses a neutral illustrated background.","width":1100,"height":740},"v2":{"title":"Your most used actions in a ring around the pointer, [[one shortcut away]].","hook":"Halo is a desktop app I designed and built on my own, for macOS and Windows. Press one shortcut, or a mouse button, and a ring of your actions opens wherever the pointer is.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"My take on Logitech's Actions Ring, for any mouse or keyboard, with no account"},{"label":"Platform","value":"macOS 26 or later, and Windows 10 and 11"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Swift, SwiftUI and AppKit on the Mac. C#, .NET 10 and WPF on Windows"},{"label":"Status","value":"Version 1.0.0, free and open source. No usage numbers"}],"links":[{"label":"View Halo on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/Halo"}],"linksHeading":"See the project","summary":["The problem: the controls you use most are spread across menu bars and key combinations, and most only work while their app is in front.","What I built: two native apps, one for the Mac and one for Windows, that open a ring of up to 12 actions around the pointer, with its own ring for each app and folders for the rest.","The key idea: one shortcut does two jobs. Tap it and click, or hold it, flick toward an action and let go. Time and angle tell the two apart.","Where it stands: version 1.0.0 on both systems, free under the GPL. It collects no data, so I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/halo/lead.webp","alt":"Two Halo screens side by side: the Classic ring over a page with Play/Pause highlighted, and the ring editor with the Finder slot selected and its settings beside it","caption":"The ring around the pointer, and the editor that sets it up. The editor draws the same ring, at full size.","width":2400,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A shortcut is only quick if you can reach it from where you are","blocks":[{"p":"Say you are writing in one app and need to mute a call in another. You switch to the meeting, press its shortcut and switch back. The shortcut itself only works while that app is in front."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/problem.webp","alt":"Two columns comparing three steps to mute a Teams call from another app with two steps using Halo","caption":"The problem in one picture. Look at step 2 on the left: the shortcut exists, but only works once you have gone to the app.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"Logitech's Actions Ring answers this with a ring of actions around the pointer. Halo is my own take on that idea, and it is not connected to Logitech. I set three rules for it: it works with any mouse or keyboard, it needs no account, and it sends nothing anywhere."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did not run interviews or user tests for Halo, so this page quotes none. The spec lists binding decisions from technical research, but the notes behind them were not kept."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Press, point, let go, and you are back in your work","blocks":[{"p":"Halo sits in the menu bar on a Mac and next to the clock on Windows. Press its shortcut and a ring of slots opens around the pointer. Each slot holds one action. A new ring has eight, and a ring can have from 4 to 12."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/annotated-ring.webp","alt":"The Classic ring enlarged, with five numbered notes on the first slot, the highlighted Play/Pause slot, the Finder icon, a folder tab and the centre","caption":"The ring as it opens. Start at note 2: the slot under the pointer turns dark and names itself, so you can check before you let go.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"A slot can do far more than open an app. These are the kinds of action it can hold:"},{"table":{"head":["Kind","Examples"],"rows":[["Apps","Open, switch to, show or hide, or quit an app"],["Inside an app","An app's own shortcut, such as Teams: Mute, sent even from another app"],["Websites and files","Open a page, a document or a folder"],["Media","Play or pause, next track, volume, mute, brightness"],["Screenshots","An area, a window or the whole screen, to a file or the clipboard"],["Windows","Snap left or right, fill, centre, move to the next display"],["Text","Snippets with the date, the time or the selected text filled in"],["AI prompts","Send the selected text with a prompt to Claude, ChatGPT, Perplexity or Gemini"],["Smart Actions","Several actions in a row, with pauses between them"]]}},{"p":"I chose the eight starting slots to match Logitech's default Mac ring, with a folder of window layouts where Logitech has a settings shortcut. The most used ones sit straight up, down, left and right."}]},{"id":"choose","label":"Decision 1","nav":"Tap or hold","heading":"One shortcut gives two ways to choose, and time tells them apart","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: tap the shortcut and the ring stays open for you to click. Or hold it, flick toward a slot and let go. Once a layout is familiar, the flick becomes one quick stroke."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/selection-states.webp","alt":"Flow from pressing the shortcut to four outcomes: the ring stays open, the slot runs, the ring closes, or nothing runs, plus the three selection modes","caption":"What each way of letting go does. The two green outcomes are the ones you aim for. The two red ones are how you back out safely.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"p":"In the build: the line between a tap and a hold is 0.28 seconds. Let go sooner with nothing highlighted, and Halo reads it as a tap. These rules live in a small piece of logic with no interface, so I could test every outcome on its own."},{"p":"Picking the slot is pure geometry. Halo measures the angle from the centre to the pointer. While you hold, that is measured from where you pressed, and a flick far past the ring still counts."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/hit-zones.webp","alt":"Two to-scale diagrams of the ring's slices: while holding, slices run to the edge of the screen; after a tap, they stop at a circle 60 points beyond the ring","caption":"How the highlighted slot is found. Note the thin pale band: the highlight holds on for 5 degrees past its edge, so it does not flicker on a boundary.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"Mouse buttons made this harder. Many people will open Halo from a spare mouse button, mapped to the shortcut in their mouse's own software. Some of those programs hold the key down while the button is down. Others send a quick tap, and a hold never happens."},{"p":"So the welcome guide has a Test Your Trigger step. You hold your mouse button for about a second, and Halo tells you which kind of software you have. If it only taps, you can switch to Tap, Then Click."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A time limit is a guess about what you meant. Hold a moment too long before moving and letting go closes the ring. The limit can be changed, and the two fixed modes remove the guess entirely."}}]},{"id":"editor","label":"Decision 2","nav":"An editor shaped like the ring","heading":"You set up the ring on the ring itself, not in a list","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the settings window shows the same ring you use, large, in your chosen look. Click a slot to select it and its settings open beside it. Drag one slot onto another to swap them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/annotated-editor.webp","alt":"The ring editor with eight numbered notes on the ring name, the slot count, the selected Finder slot, a folder tab, an empty slot and three parts of the settings panel","caption":"The editor. Note 3 is the point: the slot you are changing sits in the same spot it has around the pointer, so there is nothing to translate.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"In the build: the editor's ring and the floating ring share one model, one set of measurements and the same two looks. A drop onto the editor is placed by the same angle maths that picks a slot in the live ring. Every change goes through one place that records it for undo and saves it to a readable settings file."},{"p":"The two views do differ in one way, on purpose. The editor keeps every position, so you can see where an empty slot is. The live ring hides empty slots and spreads your actions evenly."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/gap-closing.webp","alt":"Two drawn rings: the editor view with eight positions and one empty, and the live view with seven actions evenly spaced","caption":"The same ring in both views. With seven actions, each one gets a wider slice to aim at in the live ring.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Closing the gaps moves actions when you add or remove one, which works against remembering a direction. People who want fixed positions can turn \"Close up empty slots\" off."}}]},{"id":"apps","label":"Decision 3","nav":"A ring for each app","heading":"Each app can have its own ring, and folders hold the rest","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: a meeting app and a photo editor need different controls. So any app can have its own ring. It opens instead of the main ring, called Everywhere, whenever that app is in front."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/app-rings.webp","alt":"Diagram of the Everywhere ring and an example Microsoft Teams ring, with the steps that decide which one opens; three Teams slots are borrowed from Everywhere","caption":"How Halo picks a ring. The dashed slots on the right are empty in the Teams ring, so they borrow the Everywhere actions in the same positions.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Folders keep the first ring small. Rest the pointer on a folder and its actions fan out on an outer arc. The main ring dims but stays where it was."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/folders.webp","alt":"The Windows folder open: six window actions fanned out on an outer arc beside the dimmed ring, with Next Display highlighted","caption":"A folder of window layouts, open. The ring behind it is dimmed to 55%, so you can see where you came from.","width":720,"height":720},{"src":"assets/cases/halo/teams.webp","alt":"The Add to Ring picker for a Microsoft Teams ring, listing Teams actions such as Mute, Camera and Raise Hand first, then apps","caption":"Adding to a Teams ring. The picker lists that app's own actions first, each with its shortcut.","width":480,"height":560}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: an action inside another app can reach it in three ways. I used the simplest one that works for each action."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/in-app-actions.webp","alt":"Three rows showing how an action reaches an app: by its own shortcut, by a menu command, or by clicking its buttons in order; plus a note on 369 ready-made actions for 30 apps","caption":"The three routes, simplest first. Teams has no shortcut to start recording, so Halo clicks Teams' own buttons by their names.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The 369 ready-made actions for 30 apps are kept as one list. Each entry records where its shortcut is documented. Some come with notes, because an app's settings can change them. Zoom's shortcuts, for example, only work from other apps when they are marked as global in Zoom."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Halo depends on other apps keeping their shortcuts and button names. If an app changes them, the action breaks until the list is updated. This is also why the list has not reached Windows yet."}}]},{"id":"look","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Two looks, quick motion","heading":"A look you can read over any page, and motion that never makes you wait","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: two looks. Classic uses light round bubbles. Glass uses the Liquid Glass material from macOS 26, as one ring of curved segments with the name of the action in the centre."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/classic.webp","alt":"The Classic ring over a sample page, Play/Pause highlighted in near-black with its label beside it","caption":"Classic. The bubbles are almost solid, so the icons stay clear over text.","width":720,"height":720},{"src":"assets/cases/halo/glass.webp","alt":"The Glass ring over a sample page: curved glass segments around a centre that reads Play/Pause, with that segment tinted blue","caption":"Glass. The highlighted segment takes the accent colour and the centre names it.","width":720,"height":720}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"In the build: Logitech's Mac bubbles are only about 38% solid, and reviewers have found them hard to read. I made Classic's bubbles about 90% solid, with a hairline edge and a very soft shadow, so they show up on a white page too."},{"p":"Windows cannot blur the windows behind another window. So for Glass on Windows, Halo takes a small blurred snapshot of the screen just before the ring appears and draws on top of it. With transparency turned off, both systems use solid fills."},{"p":"Every movement has a number in the spec, so the two apps could match."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/motion.webp","alt":"Timing chart: the centre and eight slots opening one after another about 28 ms apart, then bars for the tap or hold threshold, folder opening, highlight, chosen slot flash, close and the empty slot shake","caption":"The motion, redrawn from the spec's timings. The whole ring has opened in under half a second, and clicks and keys never wait for it.","width":1600,"height":1080}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Glass looks right on the Mac, but what shows through it depends on what is behind the ring. Classic is the safer choice on a busy screen, and it is the default."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Two native apps that share one description of a ring","blocks":[{"p":"Halo is two native apps, one for each system. A ring that floats over every app and never takes focus depends on each system's own window rules, so each app handles those its own way. Both apps read and write the same settings file, so a ring exported on a Mac opens on Windows."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/architecture.webp","alt":"Three-column diagram: the Mac app in Swift, the shared settings file, action list and spec in the middle, and the Windows app in C#","caption":"What runs where. The middle column is what keeps the two apps behaving the same.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Mac app","Swift, SwiftUI and AppKit, built with Swift Package Manager. No third-party code"],["Windows app","C#, .NET 10 and WPF with the Fluent theme"],["Shared","One settings file in readable JSON, one list of in-app actions, one spec"],["Install and updates","A disk image on the Mac. Velopack on Windows. Both update from GitHub Releases"],["Checks","Swift tests and xUnit, run by GitHub Actions on every change to the main branch"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"26,300","label":"lines of Swift in the Mac app, tests included, rounded"},{"value":"12,900","label":"lines of C# and XAML in the Windows app, tests included, rounded"},{"value":"144","label":"unit tests: 56 on the Mac side, 88 on Windows"},{"value":"369","label":"ready-made in-app actions for 30 apps"}]},{"p":"The tests cover the parts with no interface: the slot maths, the tap-or-hold rules, settings files and the action list. On Windows, a separate check builds the installer, installs it on a clean machine, runs a first-start self-test with screenshots, and uninstalls it."},{"p":"The hardest problems were where Halo meets the system, not in the ring."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Focus","If the ring took focus, a shortcut it sends would land in Halo instead of your app. The ring window never becomes active, and clicks outside it pass through to the app below."],["Lost key releases","Mouse software, or a Windows security screen, can swallow the release of the shortcut, which would leave the ring stuck. Halo checks the keys itself and treats 0.15 seconds of released keys as a release. A watchdog frees every key it borrowed whenever the ring is hidden."],["Permissions","macOS ties the Accessibility permission to the app's signature, and each new build lost it. I gave the app a fixed identity so permission survives updates, and added a Fix Access button for a stale grant."],["Safe updates","An update replaces the app itself. The Mac updater installs a download only if its checksum, app identity, version and signature all match."],["Windows editor","The ring preview in the editor came up blank, because its width started unset. I fixed it, and the self-test now checks that every preview has a size."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"The ring works from the keyboard and speaks to screen readers","blocks":[{"p":"A ring is a pointer idea, but it should not need a pointer. While it is open, Halo borrows a few keys and hands them back when it closes, so the app you were in keeps its focus."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/halo/keyboard-map.webp","alt":"Diagram of the ring with slots numbered 1 to 8, a number pad mapped by direction, and the keys for moving, running and closing","caption":"The keyboard map. The number pad works by direction: 8 is up, 6 is right, 5 runs the highlighted slot.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"list":["Each change of highlight is announced by VoiceOver on the Mac and Narrator on Windows.","With Reduce Motion on, the ring only fades. Nothing scales or bounces.","With Reduce Transparency on, Glass becomes solid with hairline borders. With Increase Contrast on, the highlight gets an outline as well as a colour.","The ring never closes by itself after a delay, so nobody is rushed."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are features in the code. I have no record of testing them with people who rely on a screen reader or a keyboard."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Version 1.0.0 on both systems, with clear gaps on Windows","blocks":[{"p":"Halo 1.0.0 is free and open source under the GNU GPL. It has no account, no analytics and no tracking. The only request it makes on its own is the daily check for updates. That also means I have no usage numbers to share."},{"p":"I use Halo every day on my own Mac."},{"p":"These are not done yet:"},{"list":["The Mac app is not notarised by Apple, which needs a paid developer account. The first time you open it, macOS asks you to allow it in Privacy and Security.","The Windows installer is not code-signed yet, so Windows may warn before it runs.","The Windows editor is simpler than the Mac one. It has no drag and drop, no icon or tint picker and no editor for Smart Action steps.","Apple Shortcuts, AppleScript and menu commands are Mac only, and the list of ready-made in-app actions is not in the Windows app yet.","This page shows only the Mac app. I have no Windows screens or recordings of the ring in motion to show yet."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Test it with people, then bring Windows level","blocks":[{"list":["A first study: set up three actions people use often, repeat a task after a break, and compare their hesitation with the shortcuts they already use.","Checks I want to run: setting up a ring with the keyboard only, switching rings between apps, backing out of folders, and actions that fail for lack of permission.","Bring the Windows editor and the ready-made in-app actions level with the Mac.","Sign the Windows installer and notarise the Mac app, so neither system warns on first open."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Write the feel down as numbers, and test it like logic","blocks":[{"p":"How a ring feels comes down to a few numbers: 0.28 seconds, 16 points, 5 degrees, 28 milliseconds. Once they were written down and tested without any interface, a second app on another system could behave the same way."},{"p":"The ring was the easy part. Most of my time went to the edges, where Halo depends on the system and on other apps: focus, lost key releases, permissions and other apps' shortcuts. That is where I would look first in my next tool of this kind."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"binnder","name":"Binnder","headline":"Review the file. Keep a way back."}},"binnder":{"slug":"binnder","name":"Binnder","kind":"product","headline":"A phone cleaner where nothing is deleted the moment you swipe.","summary":"Binnder is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It shows your largest files one at a time, and you swipe to keep each one, move it to a bin or delete it for good.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Native Android app · early release","context":"Storage review","facts":[{"value":"5 seconds","label":"undo window"},{"value":"30 days","label":"bin retention"},{"value":"7 days","label":"before kept files return"}],"problem":"Storage cleanup is easy to rush and difficult to undo. I wanted the file itself to be the main thing on screen, with a clear difference between keeping it, moving it to a bin and deleting it permanently. The interaction should make those consequences visible before a gesture becomes an operation.","process":["I put the largest files first and used a large preview with fixed actions. File-type filters help narrow the queue, while matching buttons and accessibility actions make swiping optional.","I separated reversible binning from permanent deletion. An upward swipe asks for confirmation, and every deletion action has a five-second Undo window before the operation begins. A kept file is hidden from review for seven days rather than treated as permanently resolved.","The bin reports its retention period and supports preview and restore. Moving a file there does not free storage, so the interface must not count those bytes as recovered space. The captures use sample portfolio files in a separate emulator, not a scan of someone’s phone."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the file visible while deciding","choice":"Use a large preview, file name, size and fixed Keep, Bin and Delete forever controls.","reason":"The decision should be about a recognisable file, not an unfamiliar path in a long list.","tradeoff":"Some formats need another installed app. The preview must explain that limitation without implying a file is broken."},{"title":"Give permanent deletion its own commitment","choice":"Ask for confirmation and name the file before queuing permanent deletion, then offer the individual Undo window.","reason":"A gesture alone is too easy to misinterpret when the result cannot be recovered through the app.","tradeoff":"Extra confirmation slows cleanup. It belongs to the more consequential action rather than every decision."},{"title":"Describe the bin honestly","choice":"Show retention and restoration separately from bytes actually deleted.","reason":"A recoverable file still occupies storage. The person needs to understand both recovery and capacity.","tradeoff":"Retention depends on the device being able to run cleanup and reach the volume; it is not an exact deletion appointment."}],"outcome":"I designed one-file-at-a-time review with visible alternatives to gestures, a recoverable bin and an explicit permanent-delete path. The native app also accounts for interrupted operations and changed files. I still need to check those recovery paths across Android devices and removable storage.","reflection":"I would test a mistaken swipe, a removed storage volume and a restore destination that already exists. A cleanup tool earns trust when it explains what happened and preserves a way to recover, especially when it cannot finish an operation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: review a small set of test files, undo an unintended action and restore a file without coaching.","Proposed checks: permission loss, app restart during a pending action, missing SD card and a file changed since scanning."],"sources":[{"label":"Binnder README and ReviewPolicy/Storage/SafeDisk source","detail":"Documented five-second Undo, seven-day keep, thirty-day bin, delayed operations, file identity checks and storage-accounting limits. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Native Android captures · isolated test files","detail":"Captured from Binnder 0.1.1 in a separate Android emulator. The displayed design deck is a sample copy of an existing portfolio PDF; no personal phone files were read."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","alt":"Binnder native review screen showing a sample design PDF and keep bin and delete actions","caption":"Native Android capture using a sample portfolio PDF in an isolated emulator. Fixed controls provide the same actions as swiping.","width":1080,"height":1920},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-confirmation.webp","alt":"Binnder native permanent-deletion confirmation naming the sample file and Undo period","caption":"The actual confirmation names the sample file, explains that the bin is skipped and describes the five-second Undo window. No deletion was confirmed for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/Binnder","productActionLabel":"View Binnder on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":69,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Review the file. [[Keep a way back.]]","scene":"Imagine swiping through large downloads and realising that the last gesture targeted the wrong file. The interface needs to answer what happened and whether it can be reversed.","question":"How can a quick review still make destructive actions clear?","insight":"[[Keep, bin and permanent deletion are different decisions]]. Their consequences need different feedback, not just different swipe directions.","pivot":"I gave each action an explicit control and kept the file, consequence and recovery route close together.","takeaway":"The recovery path is part of the cleanup flow.","journey":[{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Preview the largest remaining file and check its details."},{"title":"Decide","detail":"Keep it, bin it or confirm permanent deletion."},{"title":"Recover","detail":"Use Undo while pending or restore from the bin."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the file visible while deciding","Give permanent deletion its own commitment","Describe the bin honestly"],"annotations":[{"title":"Sample files","detail":"The images show an isolated test queue, not personal storage."},{"title":"Space accounting","detail":"Binned files remain on disk until permanently deleted."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"binnder","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android user reviewing shared-storage files","context":"Working through a large-file queue and deciding what to keep.","need":"Understand the file and the consequence before committing.","friction":"Fast gestures can hide the difference between temporary and permanent removal.","implication":"Keep previews and fixed controls visible, with clear recovery states."}},"theme":{"layout":"binnder","mode":"light","paper":"#f9f8ff","ink":"#20212c","dim":"#5b5c6e","line":"#d8d7e6","accent":"#4c5f98","wash":"#e7e9fa","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can a quick review still make destructive actions clear?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Binnder","resultHeading":"A review flow with an explicit recovery path."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","alt":"Binnder native review screen showing a sample design PDF and keep bin and delete actions","caption":"Native Android capture using a sample portfolio PDF in an isolated emulator. Fixed controls provide the same actions as swiping.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from Binnder 0.1.1 in a separate Android emulator. The displayed design deck is a sample copy of an existing portfolio PDF; no personal phone files were read.","width":1080,"height":1920},"v2":{"title":"A phone cleaner where [[nothing is deleted the moment you swipe]].","hook":"Binnder is an Android app I designed and built on my own. It shows your largest files one at a time, and you swipe to keep each one, move it to a bin or delete it for good.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"A brief I set myself: clear space on a phone without losing the wrong file"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Native Android, version 11 and newer"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3, SQLite, WorkManager"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"October 2026, versions 0.1.0 to 0.1.2"},{"label":"Status","value":"Runs in an Android emulator. Not yet tested on a real phone, not published, no users"}],"links":[],"linksHeading":"There is no public download yet","summary":["The problem: clearing storage is easy to rush, and one fast gesture on a file you did not really look at can lose it for good.","What I built: a native Android app that shows one large file at a time, biggest first, with three swipes that have three different consequences.","The key idea: no swipe touches a file straight away. Every deletion waits five seconds with its own Undo, binned files stay for 30 days, and only real deletions count as freed space.","Where it stands: version 0.1.1 runs in an emulator and its rules have 23 automated tests. It has not been tried on a real phone or published, and it has no users."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/lead.webp","alt":"Two Binnder screens side by side: the review screen showing one large PDF preview with Keep, Delete forever and Bin buttons, and the Delete permanently dialog naming the same file","caption":"Version 0.1.1 in an Android emulator, with sample files. Left, the review screen. Right, the one question the app asks before a permanent delete. The name on the sample deck is blurred.","width":1600,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Clearing space is quick until the wrong file goes","blocks":[{"p":"Phones fill up with big files: videos, downloads, old documents. Clearing them is a chore, and a chore is easy to rush."},{"p":"I wanted a tool that keeps the fast part fast and makes the dangerous part slow. You should see the file itself, know what each choice will do, and have a way back when you get it wrong."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram in two rows: a fast gesture, plus a file you cannot see, plus one kind of delete, leads to the wrong file being lost; below it, the four things Binnder puts in the way","caption":"The risk on the top row, and what the app puts in its way on the bottom. This is the thinking behind the app, not a research finding.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"I set the brief myself and wrote four rules before drawing a screen:"},{"list":["Show the file before any delete control.","Keep routine swipes fast. Interrupt only for a choice that cannot be undone.","If a move, a restore or a check fails, keep the data.","No accounts, no analytics, no file uploads."]},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did not interview anyone or test with users for this. Who it is for is my own assumption: anyone with an Android phone who wants space back without losing something they needed."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"One file at a time, biggest first, three ways to decide","blocks":[{"p":"Binnder scans the phone's shared storage and any SD card, then shows files one at a time, largest first, because the biggest files free the most space. You can narrow the queue to images, videos, audio, PDFs or other files."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/annotated-review.webp","alt":"The review screen with seven numbered notes: the filters, the largest-first count, the full-screen button, the preview, the file details, the three action buttons and the swipe hint","caption":"The review screen. Start at note 6: the three buttons sit outside the card, so they are always in reach.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"The preview is the point. Images and PDFs zoom with a pinch, every page of a PDF can be read, and video, audio and text play or show inside the app. Formats Android cannot draw open in another installed app."},{"p":"Then you decide, with a swipe or a button."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/gestures.webp","alt":"Schematic of the review screen with three arrows: left to Keep for seven days, right to Move to bin for 30 days, up to Delete forever after a question","caption":"The three swipes. Left and right happen when you let go. Up asks first. A schematic drawn from the code, not a screenshot.","width":1600,"height":1300}]},{"p":"The three choices differ in what they cost you if you are wrong. Putting them side by side is how I worked out which one needed a question and which did not."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/consequences.webp","alt":"Table comparing Keep, Move to bin and Delete forever on four rows: whether the app asks first, the Undo strip, the way back afterwards and the space freed","caption":"Read the Delete forever column: it is the only one that asks first, and the only one with no way back after five seconds.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"file-first","label":"Decision 1","nav":"The file comes first","heading":"The file gets the screen, and everything else moves out of its way","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the preview takes all the height left after the filters and the file name. Bin and Settings are icons in the top bar. There is no bottom navigation. The three buttons sit below the card and never scroll away."},{"p":"In the build: version 0.1.0 had a bottom navigation bar and smaller previews. In 0.1.1 I removed the bar, made the previews bigger, and moved the full path and date into a Details sheet that opens on request. A sheet is a panel that slides up from the bottom of the screen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/layout-change.webp","alt":"Two schematic phones: version 0.1.0 with a bottom navigation bar, stacked Undo rows and a small preview, and version 0.1.1 with top-bar icons, one Undo strip and a large preview","caption":"Before and after. There is no screenshot of 0.1.0, so the left phone is drawn from the change notes and is only approximate.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"Speed matters when you look at file after file. The app draws the current file and the next two ahead of time, into a memory cache capped at 24 MB, and fades from one file to the next. A full-screen preview opens as its own screen, so a pinch or a scroll there is never read as a swipe."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A bigger preview leaves less room for detail. The card still shows the name, location and size, but the date and a path you can select and copy wait behind Details."}}]},{"id":"ask-once","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Only one swipe asks","heading":"Routine swipes stay fast. Only the one you cannot take back asks first","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: as you drag, the card follows your finger, tilts a little and shows a label with the action. Let go too early and it springs back."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/drag-sequence.webp","alt":"Four schematic frames of a swipe: the card at rest, dragged right with a Move to bin label, sprung back after an early release, and replaced by the next file with the Undo strip showing","caption":"A swipe right, frame by frame. Frame 2 is the one that matters: you see the action named before it happens. A schematic, not a screenshot.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"p":"Keep and Bin happen as soon as you let go, because both can be undone. Delete forever opens a dialog that names the file and says what is different."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/annotated-confirm.webp","alt":"The Delete permanently dialog with six numbered notes: the icon, the question, the file name, its size and location, the consequence and the two buttons","caption":"The dialog. Note 5 carries the weight: it says this skips the bin and gives five seconds before the file is gone for good.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"In the build: one distance decides every swipe, 96 dp. A dp is Android's unit for size on screen, the same physical size on every phone; 96 dp is about 15 mm. The label appears at a third of that distance."},{"p":"An upward swipe only counts when the drag is at least 1.2 times more up than sideways, so a sloppy swipe to the side cannot turn into a permanent delete. A short vibration marks the point where the swipe counts, but it is never the only signal."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The question slows down anyone who wants to delete many files for good. I kept it for that one action and left the bin as the fast route."}}]},{"id":"undo","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Five seconds to undo","heading":"No deletion starts until five seconds have passed","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: after a swipe right or up, a dark strip appears at the bottom with the file's name, a countdown and Undo. Several actions can wait at once, each with its own countdown. The strip stays on screen in the bin, in Settings and in a full-screen preview."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/undo-strip.webp","alt":"Schematic of the Undo strip below the action buttons, with two waiting actions, each showing a countdown label, a file name, an Undo button and a draining progress line","caption":"The Undo strip with two actions waiting. Note 3: undoing one leaves the other alone. Drawn from the code, with example file names.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: a swipe does not move or delete anything. It writes a record marked as pending in a small database on the phone. Undo removes the record, and the file was never touched. Only when the five seconds are up does the app check the file again and act."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/undo-timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline from the swipe to five seconds and after: the choice is saved but not carried out, then checks run and either the action happens or it is cancelled and the file kept","caption":"The five seconds and what follows. If the file changed or is already in another bin, the action is cancelled and the file stays.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"Two details took the most care. The countdown runs on the phone's uptime clock, together with a saved count of restarts, so changing the time of day cannot shorten it. And the pending records survive the app being closed, so closing the app neither skips the wait nor loses the choice."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Leaving the app does not cancel a waiting action. It finishes when Android next lets the app work in the background. After the five seconds, the bin is the way back for a binned file, and there is none for a file deleted forever."}}]},{"id":"bin","label":"Decision 4","nav":"An honest bin","heading":"The bin says plainly that binned files still take up space","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the bin lists each file with the days left before it is deleted, and buttons to preview it, restore it or delete it for good. Its total says how much is in the bin and that this is still using storage. Settings counts only the bytes Binnder has really deleted."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/bin-screen.webp","alt":"Two schematic screens: the recycle bin with a total, a warning that it still uses storage and two file rows with days left, and Settings with the space permanently deleted by Binnder","caption":"The bin and Settings, in the app's own words. Note 5: moving a file to the bin is never counted as space freed. The files and sizes are examples.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"p":"In the build: a binned file moves into a hidden folder on the same storage volume. The move is instant and never makes a second copy. It also means the space is not free yet, and counting it as freed would make the app look better while telling you something false."},{"p":"Restore will not overwrite. If a new file now sits where the old one was, the restore stops and both files are kept. Thirty days after a file enters the bin, an hourly background job deletes it, and the app checks again whenever you open it."},{"p":"One gap is spelled out in Settings. If you uninstall Binnder, the binned files stay on the phone, but the app's list of where each one came from is lost. So the app asks you to restore your bin first."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Thirty days is not an exact appointment. Battery limits, a force-stop or a removed SD card can delay cleanup, and a phone that is switched off cannot run it. The bin screen says so instead of promising a date."}}]},{"id":"exclusions","label":"Decision 5","nav":"Bins stay out of review","heading":"Files that are already in a bin never come back for review","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: nothing, which is the point. A photo you already deleted in your gallery app, or a file sitting in Samsung's My Files trash, should not appear again as something to decide about."},{"p":"In the build: I added this filter in version 0.1.1. Android marks some deleted files as trashed while they keep a normal-looking name, and other apps keep their own recycle folders, so the app checks for both."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/exclusions.webp","alt":"Funnel diagram from everything on shared storage down to the review queue, removing protected app data, files Android has trashed, other apps' recycle folders and Binnder's own bin","caption":"What is filtered out before you see anything. The band widths show the order of the filters, not measured file counts.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The check runs again when you come back to the app and right before any action is carried out. If the check itself fails, waiting deletions are held. The app does not act on a file it is unsure about."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Recycle folders are matched by name, and a list of names can miss one phone maker's own folder. Checking this on real Samsung and other phones is on the list of device checks still to run."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"A small app, with most of its care spent on not losing files","blocks":[{"p":"Binnder is a native Android app in Kotlin, ten source files in all. Everything runs on the phone. The only use of the network is checking for updates."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/architecture.webp","alt":"Architecture diagram in four columns: the screens, the app's logic, the phone's storage with the SQLite record and safe disk operations, and background work for cleanup and update checks","caption":"What runs where. The storage column is where the safety lives: every move and delete passes through it.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Language","Kotlin"],["Interface","Jetpack Compose and Material 3. It follows the phone's light or dark theme and wallpaper colours, with a teal fallback"],["Records","SQLite on the phone, for every waiting action, every bin entry and the history of kept files"],["Background work","WorkManager: cleanup every hour, update checks every 15 minutes from version 0.1.2"],["Updates","Public GitHub releases, checked before Android is asked to install them"],["Oldest Android","Android 11"]]}},{"p":"Every file action is a record that steps through a few named states. That is what lets the app recover if it is stopped halfway through a move."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/journal.webp","alt":"State diagram: pending actions become moving, deleting or purging, then rest in the bin or finish as restored or deleted, with notes on what happens when the app is killed or a step fails","caption":"The life of one action. The two boxes at the bottom are the rules I care most about: a half-finished step is checked, never guessed.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"The other guard is what the app means by \"the same file\"."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/identity.webp","alt":"Diagram of the five facts that identify a file, path, size, modified time, device and inode, and an example where a new file at the same path cancels a waiting move","caption":"Why a stale decision cannot hit a replacement file. The example values are made up; the five facts and the message come from the code.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The rules are covered by tests that run on a computer. The checks that need a real phone are written down and not yet done."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/binnder/checks.webp","alt":"Bar chart of 23 automated tests in four groups, timing rules 7, safe moves 6, bin detection 6 and update rules 4, beside a list of device checks still to run","caption":"What is checked in code, and what still needs a phone. The phone column is the bigger risk.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"stats":[{"value":"10","label":"source files, about 1,550 lines with the tests"},{"value":"23","label":"automated tests for the rules, plus 6 for the release script"},{"value":"3","label":"versions, 0.1.0 to 0.1.2, the last prepared but not released"},{"value":"14","label":"checks on a real phone, written and not yet run"}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were about what happens when things go wrong."},{"table":{"head":["Problem","What I did"],"rows":[["The app is killed mid-action","On the next run, each record is checked against the disk. A file found in the bin is marked as binned, a file still in its old place is left alone, and anything that does not add up is kept and reported."],["A file changes after the scan","Every move and delete first confirms the file's five facts. A new file saved at the same path is never touched."],["A move fails","There is no fallback that copies and then deletes. The original stays where it was, and folders are never deleted, only single files."],["Updates without a server","The app reads public GitHub releases and installs only when the file's SHA-256 checksum, the package name, a higher version number and the exact signing certificate all match."],["Building without the usual tools","The machine I built on had no network access for Gradle, Android's standard build tool. I built the installable app straight from the Android SDK tools instead. A full Gradle build and lint pass are still to run."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Every swipe also has a button and a named action","blocks":[{"p":"Swiping is hard for some people and impossible for others. Every gesture has a labelled button below the card. Screen readers get three named actions on the card itself: \"Keep for seven days\", \"Move to recycle bin\" and \"Request permanent deletion\". All of them call the same code as the swipe."},{"p":"Vibration supports the visible label and never replaces it. The screens scroll when the text is set large, and stay clear of the phone's notches and rounded corners."},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"None of this has been tried with TalkBack, Android's screen reader, or with large text on a real phone. Both are in the device checks."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Built and tested in code, not yet on a real phone","blocks":[{"p":"Version 0.1.1 builds into an installable app and runs in an Android emulator, where the two screenshots on this page were taken with sample files. Version 0.1.2, which adds automatic update checks, is prepared but not released."},{"list":["It has not been tested on a real phone. I wrote 14 device checks, from refusing file access to pulling out the SD card mid-action, and none is recorded as done.","It is not published, so there is no public download link.","It has no users, and no numbers on files reviewed or space freed."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Run the phone checks, then put it in front of people","blocks":[{"list":["Run the 14 device checks on throwaway files, starting with the ones that could lose data: a killed app, a removed SD card and a replaced file.","Capture real screens of the bin, the Undo strip, a swipe in progress and the Details sheet, to replace the drawings on this page.","Publish the signed release, so updates can reach installed copies.","Then a small test with people: review a set of sample files, undo a mistaken swipe and restore a file, without help."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Safety is mostly what happens after the tap","blocks":[{"p":"The swipe and the dialog were a few decisions. Most of the design sat behind them: the record for each action, the five facts that pin down a file, and the rule that a failed step keeps the data. A cleanup tool earns trust when it says what happened and keeps a way back, most of all when it could not finish."},{"p":"I also learned to be exact about words. \"Freed\" has to mean freed. \"Delete\" had to split into two actions with different costs, because one word was hiding two decisions."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"pureeq","name":"PureEQ","headline":"Find a sound you can adjust and keep."}},"pureeq":{"slug":"pureeq","name":"PureEQ","kind":"product","headline":"An equaliser you set by listening, not by guessing at sliders.","summary":"PureEQ is an Android app I designed and built on my own. A two-minute listening check and a few blind A/B rounds on music become one EQ curve for each ear, played on everything the phone plays.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Native Android app","context":"Personal audio tuning","facts":[{"value":"Per ear","label":"separate listening input"},{"value":"A/B","label":"music preference choices"},{"value":"Export","label":"take the profile elsewhere"}],"problem":"A row of equaliser sliders assumes that someone already knows which frequencies they want to change. I approached PureEQ as a guided starting point: explain the setup, gather a few listening responses, compare music and leave the result open to adjustment.","process":["I separated the listening check from the taste test. The first asks whether a short tone is audible through the current headphones; the second asks which of two music treatments the person prefers. Neither needs to be presented as an expert diagnosis.","Preparation is part of the flow: a quiet environment, suitable headphones, stable volume and a left/right check. The result can then be adjusted with named controls, compared with EQ off and saved as a profile.","Export gives the profile a useful life beyond the setup. The documented formats have different capabilities, so a combined curve or per-ear adjustment needs explanation. The captures show the actual native starting flow, not a completed hearing test or a clinical result."],"decisions":[{"title":"Explain the two kinds of input","choice":"Describe listening responses and music preferences as separate steps before asking for either.","reason":"A person should know whether they are reporting audibility or choosing a sound they like.","tradeoff":"A short explanation must not make the listening check sound like a calibrated audiogram."},{"title":"Leave the suggestion editable","choice":"Offer named fine-tuning controls, an EQ on/off comparison and a way back to suggested settings.","reason":"The suggested curve is a starting point. People need to hear and revise it rather than accept it as an unquestionable result.","tradeoff":"Several controls can become confusing together. Reset and suggested-value feedback need to make changes traceable."},{"title":"Carry context into export","choice":"Describe the target format and explain when an app combines left and right curves.","reason":"A technically valid export can still behave differently from the per-ear result shown in PureEQ.","tradeoff":"Compatibility depends on the receiving app and device. Exporting a file does not guarantee the same playback everywhere."}],"outcome":"I connected preparation, listening responses, A/B choices and an editable result into a profile flow. The app can apply the EQ or export it for another player, with limitations explained for each format. This is personal sound tuning, not a clinical hearing assessment.","reflection":"I would test whether people can tell what came from their listening responses, what came from preference choices and what they changed afterwards. If those differences are unclear, the final curve may look more certain than it should.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: complete setup with headphones, explain each stage and compare a saved result with EQ off at matched loudness.","Proposed checks: interrupted setup, a volume change, incorrect channel identification, resetting one adjustment and exporting a combined curve."],"sources":[{"label":"PureEQ README and native UI/theme source","detail":"Listening and preference stages, preparation, editable profiles, reset behaviour and export limitations. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Native Android captures · no listening result","detail":"Captured from PureEQ 1.5.2 in a separate Android emulator. The welcome and preparation screens are genuine app UI; no human listening responses or clinical measurements are represented."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","alt":"PureEQ native welcome screen explaining its listening and music preference flow","caption":"Actual native welcome screen from an isolated Android emulator. It explains the two inputs before a profile is created.","width":1080,"height":1920},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-setup.webp","alt":"PureEQ native new-profile preparation screen explaining listening taste and export","caption":"The actual preparation screen separates listening, taste and the resulting EQ. No hearing-test result is shown.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PureEQ","productActionLabel":"View PureEQ on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":15,"pdfPage":72,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Find a sound [[you can adjust and keep.]]","scene":"Imagine wanting clearer music but not knowing whether to change bass, treble or one particular band. A guided starting point needs to be understandable before the sliders become useful.","question":"How can sound tuning be guided without making its result feel final?","insight":"[[Audibility and preference answer different questions]]. The flow should explain that difference and leave the suggestion open to revision.","pivot":"I separated preparation, listening and taste, then kept comparison, adjustment and reset available around the result.","takeaway":"The person should understand the suggestion well enough to change it.","journey":[{"title":"Prepare","detail":"Check headphones, environment, volume and channels."},{"title":"Listen and compare","detail":"Report tones, then choose between music treatments."},{"title":"Adjust and keep","detail":"Compare, fine-tune, save or export the profile."}],"decisionLeads":["Explain the two kinds of input","Leave the suggestion editable","Carry context into export"],"annotations":[{"title":"Actual setup","detail":"The screenshots show preparation, not fabricated test results."},{"title":"Non-clinical scope","detail":"The personal tuning process is not a medical hearing assessment."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"pureeq","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android listener tuning a pair of headphones","context":"Looking for a guided starting point rather than editing frequency bands unaided.","need":"Understand the inputs and retain control over the sound.","friction":"A generated curve can look like an objective verdict.","implication":"Explain the stages and keep comparison and reset close to adjustments."}},"theme":{"layout":"pureeq","mode":"light","paper":"#f7f7fd","ink":"#181b28","dim":"#575d70","line":"#d9dcea","accent":"#5545c5","wash":"#e9e6fb","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can sound tuning be guided without making its result feel final?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind PureEQ","resultHeading":"A guided suggestion that stays open to adjustment."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","alt":"PureEQ native welcome screen explaining its listening and music preference flow","caption":"Actual native welcome screen from an isolated Android emulator. It explains the two inputs before a profile is created.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from PureEQ 1.5.2 in a separate Android emulator. The welcome and preparation screens are genuine app UI; no human listening responses or clinical measurements are represented.","width":1080,"height":1920},"v2":{"title":"An equaliser you set by [[listening]], not by guessing at sliders.","hook":"PureEQ is an Android app I designed and built on my own. A two-minute listening check and a few blind A/B rounds on music become one EQ curve for each ear, played on everything the phone plays.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Why","value":"My own idea: sound tuned to the listener and the headphones, on any Android phone"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Android 9 and later, native app"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3, Android's DynamicsProcessing audio effect"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"29 October to 3 November 2026"},{"label":"Status","value":"Version 1.7.3, a download that updates itself. Accuracy shown only in simulation. Not a medical device"}],"links":[{"label":"PureEQ download page","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/pureeq-updates"}],"linksHeading":"Try it on an Android phone","summary":["The problem: an equaliser gives you ten sliders and asks which to move. Most people cannot answer that, and the right answer depends on their ears and their headphones.","What I built: an Android app that asks two easier questions, \"did you hear that beep?\" and \"which of these do you prefer?\", and turns the answers into one EQ curve per ear for all audio on the phone.","The key idea: keep the answers, not the curve. Every curve is worked out again from stored answers, so when I improved the maths, saved profiles got better with no new test.","Where it stands: version 1.7.3, built in six days, with 141 automated tests. Its accuracy figures come from simulated listeners. I have no listening tests with other people and no install numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/lead.webp","alt":"Four PureEQ screens side by side: the home screen with a curve for each ear, the listening check, a taste-test round with A and B, and Fine-tune with the curve and an EQ on and off switch","caption":"From left: home, the listening check, one round of the taste test, and Fine-tune. The blue and red lines are the left and right ears.","width":2400,"height":1240},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Ten sliders ask a question most people cannot answer","blocks":[{"p":"An equaliser, or EQ, changes how loud each part of the sound is, from deep bass to high treble. The usual EQ app on a phone is a row of sliders, each for one range of pitches."},{"p":"It assumes you already know which pitches to change. The right setting depends on two things the sliders cannot see. One is your ears, which may hear some pitches less well than others, and differently on the left and right. The other is your headphones, which all colour the sound in their own way."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/problem.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing a generic ten-band equaliser, with the question of which slider to move, against PureEQ's two questions: did you hear that beep, and which do you enjoy more, A or B","caption":"The problem in one picture. On the left, the person has to know the answer. On the right, they only have to report what they hear and what they like.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"Some phone makers build a feature like this into their own phones and earbuds. I wanted it on any Android phone from Android 9 on, with any headphones."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"This started from my own wish, not from user research. I have no interviews, surveys or listening sessions with other people, so this page quotes none."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Two questions, about five minutes, one curve per ear","blocks":[{"p":"A profile is made for one pair of headphones. Five setup steps make the answers trustworthy. Then come the two questions, and then the result."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of the eight steps of a new profile: headphones, room check, mode, volume, left and right, then the listening check and taste test, then your sound","caption":"The whole flow. Steps 6 and 7 are the two questions; everything before them is there so the answers mean something.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"p":"The setup steps each guard against a known way for the result to go wrong. A noisy room hides quiet beeps. A volume change halfway shifts every answer. Swapped earbuds flip the two ears."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/screen-start.webp","alt":"PureEQ welcome screen titled Find your sound, explaining the listening check and the A/B rounds, with a Start button","caption":"The first screen says what will happen in three sentences, before anything plays.","width":1080,"height":1920,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/screen-setup.webp","alt":"New profile screen titled Let's find your sound, listing three steps: listening check, taste and your EQ, then Before you start","caption":"The new-profile screen splits the two kinds of input: what you hear, then what you like.","width":1080,"height":1920,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"These two captures come from version 1.5.2 in an Android emulator. The rest of the screens on this page are the Play Store screenshots I made for version 1.6."}]},{"id":"check","label":"Decision 1","nav":"A check that picks each beep","heading":"A check that picks each beep finishes in about two minutes","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one beep at a time, in one ear, and a big Yes button. You never see how loud the beep is."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/annotated-check.webp","alt":"The listening check screen with six numbered notes: progress, pause, the pulsing rings, the Yes button, the dimmed No button and Play that beep again","caption":"The check screen. Look at notes 5 and 6: both keep timing and replays from skewing a band.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"p":"In the build: version 1.1 used a staircase, the classic method. It turns the beep down after a Yes and up after a No, over and over. It needed about 150 answers and over six minutes."},{"p":"In 1.2 I replaced it with a Bayesian test. That means it keeps a running best guess of your ears, with how sure it is, and updates the guess after every answer. Each next beep is the one expected to teach it the most. All seven pitches and both ears share one guess, so an answer at 2 kHz also says something about 3 kHz."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/check-loop.webp","alt":"Loop diagram of the listening check: best guess so far, pick the next beep, play it, answer, update the guess, with the rule for when a pitch is finished","caption":"One turn of the check. The silent turns in step 4 measure how often a person says Yes to nothing.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"Small details carry a lot of the accuracy. The beeps waver slightly in pitch, so a narrow peak in an earbud cannot pass for a weak band. The No button waits for Bluetooth delay, which can be a quarter of a second or more."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/check-speed.webp","alt":"Bar chart: answers needed fell from about 150 to 64, check time from 6.3 to about 1.8 minutes, and a whole new profile from 13 to 15 minutes to 5 to 6","caption":"Version 1.1 against the current check. These numbers come from simulated listeners in the app's own tests, not from people.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The result mixes your ears with your headphones' own sound. That is on purpose, because the EQ corrects both. It also means the result is relative and cannot be read as a measure of anyone's hearing. The check starts at 500 Hz because deep-bass answers depend too much on how well earbuds seal."}}]},{"id":"fit","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Lift big gaps only partly","heading":"The curve follows the trend and lifts big gaps only partly","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: at the end of the check, a chart of the seven pitches for each ear. Lower on the chart means that band gets more help. Then the profile shows the curve made from it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/screen-profile.webp","alt":"Listening profile screen titled Done. Here's your listening profile, with a chart of seven points per ear from 500 Hz to 8 kHz","caption":"The listening profile. The text says what the zero line is, and that the EQ follows the smooth trend, not every point.","width":1080,"height":1500,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/screen-your-eq.webp","alt":"Fine-tune screen with a profile name, a chart of two EQ curves, blue and red, a preamp of minus 4.1 dB and Play, EQ on and Off buttons","caption":"A saved profile's curve, one line per ear. The two lines part only where the ears differ.","width":1080,"height":2160,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"In the build: the profile never stores a curve. It stores answers and choices. Every curve, for the phone, for exports and for previews, is worked out from them each time."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/profile-to-curve.webp","alt":"Diagram with three columns: what a profile stores, five steps that work out the curve for each ear, and where it goes: all audio, exports and previews","caption":"How a profile becomes a curve. Step 2 is the listening check; steps 1 and 3 can exist without it.","width":1600,"height":1120}]},{"p":"That choice paid off after version 1.4. I put the design through an evidence review in four parts: how the check measures, how results turn into sound, what headphone sound people prefer, and what similar products and regulators do."},{"p":"The review found real faults. The fit took 500 Hz to 4 kHz as its zero line, so a result that slopes towards the treble was split around its own middle. That roughly doubled the treble tilt compared with NAL-R, a published fitting rule. A real difference between the two ears came through at only about half its size."},{"p":"Version 1.5 changed the rule and refit every saved profile from its stored answers."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/gain-rule.webp","alt":"Line chart of the ear-tuning rule: boost rises 0.75 dB per dB up to a 6 dB gap, then about 0.35 dB per dB, capped at plus 8 dB, beside a dashed line for full correction","caption":"The rule since 1.5, drawn from its formula. It shows the rule, not a measured result.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Simulated case","Version 1.4","Now"],"rows":[["Normal listener: colour the EQ added by mistake","0.42 dB","0.34 dB"],["Normal listener: false difference between the ears","0.39 dB","0.14 dB"],["Listener hearing less treble (10 dB at 1 kHz to 35 dB at 4 kHz): treble tilt added","11.3 dB","8.0 dB"],["A real 15 dB difference between the ears: how far short it was read","6.0 dB","3.3 dB"]]}},{"p":"The table comes from the app's whole-pipeline tests, which run the full check with simulated listeners and compare the curve that comes out."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A listener with a large real gap gets less than a full lift. I chose that because a full lift sounds harsh, and in one study people tuning for themselves chose about 0.29 dB per dB (Sabin et al. 2020)."}}]},{"id":"taste","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Taste by blind pairs","heading":"Taste is asked with blind pairs, starting from what most people like","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: A and B, the same music with two settings. You tap between them as often as you like, then pick A, B or Same. You never see what either setting is."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/taste-start.webp","alt":"Bar chart of three listener groups, 64 percent who like the target, 15 percent who want more bass and 21 percent who want less bass and a little more treble, with the four steps of each round","caption":"Where the test begins. The groups come from published research, not from my own testing.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/annotated-taste.webp","alt":"The taste test screen with five numbered notes: the expected number of rounds, the A and B cards, the Heard label, the song choice and the answer buttons","caption":"One round. Note 5 is the fix described below: the answers wait until both sides have been heard.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"p":"In the build: both settings in a pair are matched in loudness, measured on the song that is playing. Then you compare balance, not volume. The test learns two things only, bass and treble, and picks each next pair to leave the least doubt."},{"p":"Version 1.1 halved its way through 12 rounds. Since 1.2 it usually needs 5 to 7. In 1.5 the treble control moved from a narrow shelf at 8 kHz to a broad one from 2.5 kHz, the shape real listeners adjusted in the research. The old one had no population data behind it."},{"p":"I also fixed a problem in the flow. Before 1.5, rounds could get mixed up. Now each new pair is clearly marked, and the answer buttons wait until both sides have played."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Two controls mean few rounds. Warmth, vocals and air are left at zero for the person to set by hand in Fine-tune."}}]},{"id":"edit","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Hear it, change it, put it back","heading":"Every suggestion can be heard, changed and put back","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the result opens in Fine-tune, with EQ on and off, a song of your choice and named sliders. A slider you move shows the suggested value under it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/annotated-fine-tune.webp","alt":"The Fine-tune screen with six numbered notes: EQ on and off, the song choice, Reset, the measured correction button, Ear tuning at 75 percent and the Bass slider","caption":"Fine-tune. Start at note 1: the comparison is fair because both sides play at the same loudness.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"p":"In the build: Reset needs something to return to. Version 1.0 did not keep the taste result, so its profiles had nothing to reset to. From 1.1 the profile keeps what the tests suggested, Reset returns there with Undo, and Redo taste test runs only the A/B part again while keeping the listening result."},{"p":"Matching loudness took real work. An EQ that boosts makes music louder, so the app has to turn the whole sound down to keep the comparison fair and avoid clipping. PureEQ sets its overall level for real music, where one band at a time is loud, instead of for the curve's single highest point. A +6 dB treble lift then costs about 2 dB of level instead of 6."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Many controls are a lot to read. In 1.7.1 I cut the text on every screen to a line or two. The screenshots here are from 1.6, before that cut."}}]},{"id":"outputs","label":"Decision 5","nav":"Profiles follow headphones","heading":"Profiles follow the headphones, and the phone speaker stays untouched","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one profile per pair of headphones, and a switch for each output: the phone speaker, each pair by name, a car."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/annotated-home.webp","alt":"The home screen with six numbered notes: the output playing now, the active profile, its two curves, Apply to all audio, Fine-tune and Export, and other profiles","caption":"Home. Note 1 matters most: everything on the screen is about the output playing right now.","width":1600,"height":1140}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/where-on-card.webp","alt":"Settings section Where it's on, with switches for wireless earbuds (on, playing now), the phone speaker (off) and wired headphones (on), above the Quick Settings tile section","caption":"Where it is on, in Settings. The phone speaker starts off, because profiles are tuned for headphones.","width":1080,"height":1175,"kind":"plain"}]},{"p":"In the build: until 1.5.2 there was one switch. Unplugging your headphones left the phone speaker playing a headphone curve, quieter and duller. In 1.5.3 PureEQ started keeping on or off for each output, with the profile last used there. It re-reads the output when a device connects or playback starts."},{"p":"A Quick Settings tile, in the swipe-down panel, turns it on or off. A long press opens a small menu to switch profile or open Fine-tune. In 1.7 profiles gained a mode for what the headphones are mostly used for."},{"table":{"head":["Mode","What it changes"],"rows":[["Music","Nothing. The profile exactly as tuned."],["General","A small lift for voices and a slow leveller that eases only very loud sound."],["Gaming","Bass held down so explosions do not bury footsteps, +2 dB around footsteps, treble kept for direction, and half the delay, about 21 ms."],["Voice","Less boom, clearer voices, Ear tuning at full strength and a leveller for loud and quiet talkers."],["Auto","Follows what is playing: music as Music, speech as Voice, films as General, games as Gaming. It waits 3 seconds before switching."]]}},{"p":"Every mode plays as loud as Music, so switching changes the balance and not the volume."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"A Calls mode. Android does not let any app's equaliser reach phone or app calls, so the mode is named Voice and covers podcasts, audiobooks and talk videos."}}]},{"id":"words","label":"Decision 6","nav":"Words that fit","heading":"It is called a listening check, because it is not a hearing test","blocks":[{"p":"Until 1.5 the screens said \"Hearing check\" and \"Hearing correction\". The evidence review showed that those words make an app sound like a medical device, which PureEQ is not. Its results are relative: they shape the sound and do not measure hearing."},{"p":"So in 1.5 the check became the Listening check and the correction became Ear tuning. The store listing, screenshots and release notes leave out words such as \"hearing test\" and \"audiogram\". The app says plainly that it is not a medical device, and no translation calls the check a hearing test."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/feature-graphic.webp","alt":"PureEQ store graphic: the app icon, headphones over five bars, beside the name PureEQ and the line Your headphones, tuned to your ears and your taste","caption":"The store graphic I made for the Play listing. The line promises a sound, not a diagnosis.","width":1024,"height":500}]}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"Store the answers, work out everything else","blocks":[{"p":"Everything runs on the phone. There is no account, no analytics and no server of my own."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: screens, test sessions and audio engine inside the app; the profile, the curve maker and exporters in the core; the background service, Quick Settings tile and two trips online on the Android side","caption":"What runs where. The profile in the middle holds only answers and choices.","width":1600,"height":800}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Kotlin and Jetpack Compose with Material 3. No libraries beyond AndroidX at run time"],["Sound for the tests","Android's low-level audio player, with its own thread for beeps and A/B music"],["Sound for all apps","Android's DynamicsProcessing effect, a separate curve per ear, held by a background service"],["Headphone data","AutoEq, an open database of over 6,000 measured headphones, fetched on the phone, never bundled"],["Updates","A GitHub build that checks size, checksum, package, version and signing key before it installs"],["Checks","141 automated tests in 19 files, including whole-pipeline simulations"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"67","label":"commits, 29 October to 3 November 2026"},{"value":"15","label":"versions in that history, 1.1 to 1.7.3"},{"value":"141","label":"automated tests"},{"value":"8","label":"kinds of export, from slider values to impulse-response files"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/pureeq/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of six days with commit counts: 29 October 5, 30 October 27, 31 October 26, 2 November 4, 3 November 5, with the versions and main changes each day","caption":"The build day by day. Most of the work, including the evidence review and the 1.5 rebuild, landed on 30 and 31 October.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were in the sound path, not on the screens."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Muffled music","I noticed lossless music sounded muffled with PureEQ on. An audit found three causes. The tuning cut treble when room noise hid the low beeps, or when someone's treble hearing was keener than average: in 30 simulated checks of each case, the cut passed 2 dB in 13 and in 22. General's leveller turned a loud modern track down about 7 dB. The deepest band dropped in a steep step. Version 1.7.3 halved cuts the two ears share and fixed the other two."],["Android's equaliser","It applies one gain per narrow slice of frequencies, with no smoothing between them. I gave every slice its own band below about 530 Hz and used 107 bands in all. Gaming's shorter frames have wider slices, so it needed its own layout, or each bass slice took its neighbour's gain."],["Auto mode","Android tells an app only what kind of sound each player says it plays, nothing about which app it is. Most games say \"music\", so most games play as Music. The release notes tell people to pick Gaming for those."],["Two ways to ship","The GitHub build updates itself, which Google Play forbids. The same app now builds twice, with and without the updater."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Charts that speak, and buttons that stack at large text","blocks":[{"p":"A chart of curves means nothing to someone who cannot see it. In 1.6 each chart started describing itself to TalkBack, Android's screen reader, as the level for each ear at 100 Hz, 1 kHz, 4 kHz and 10 kHz."},{"list":["A row with a switch is one control that reads its own label, not a label and a separate switch.","Sliders announce their name and value. Screen titles are headings, so a screen reader can jump between them.","At large text, side-by-side buttons stack whenever a label would not fit, instead of breaking words. I checked the screens at 200% text.","The app ships in English, Spanish, German, French, Portuguese (Brazil) and Hindi."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"I have no captures of these states yet, and no one who uses TalkBack daily has tried the app."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Version 1.7.3, with its accuracy shown only in simulation","blocks":[{"p":"PureEQ is at version 1.7.3. The first install is a download from its public page, and after that it updates itself. I wrote a Google Play launch checklist and store listing for a November 2026 release."},{"p":"I use PureEQ every day myself."},{"p":"These are not there yet:"},{"list":["Every accuracy figure on this page comes from simulated listeners. The model's settings were tuned in simulation, not fitted to real people.","No listening tests with other people, no measurements of the sound that reaches the ear, and no install or rating numbers.","The system-wide EQ depends on the phone maker, and Auto mode has not been checked against what real apps report."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Test with real listeners and real phones","blocks":[{"list":["Run the check twice on the same people to see how far results move, and compare the curve's shape with a calibrated booth test, ideally with an audiology research group.","Watch room noise during the check, not only before it, and drop a beep that noise may have hidden.","Play 50 or more modern tracks through typical profiles to confirm the level never clips.","Ship the Play build and capture the 1.7 screens, TalkBack and large-text states for this page."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Keep the answers, not the result","blocks":[{"p":"Storing every answer instead of a finished curve was the decision that kept paying. The 1.5 refit and the 1.7.3 treble fix both reached every saved profile without anyone redoing a test."},{"p":"Simulation let me compare versions in numbers before I listened. It still missed the muffled sound until I heard it on a real phone, and then simulation showed why. I needed both."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"hidden-identity-chess","name":"Hidden Identity Chess","headline":"Familiar chess. Very different information."}},"hidden-identity-chess":{"slug":"hidden-identity-chess","name":"Hidden Identity Chess","kind":"product","headline":"Chess where you can bluff, and your opponent can call it.","summary":"Hidden Identity Chess is a web game I designed and built on my own. It turns a published board game, where you cannot see what the enemy pieces are, into something you can play on a phone, offline or with a friend online.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Playable web game","context":"Independent adaptation · Triple S Games rules","facts":[{"value":"Private","label":"opponent identities stay hidden"},{"value":"3 modes","label":"computer, shared device and online"},{"value":"Move / bluff / challenge","label":"explicit turn actions"}],"problem":"A familiar chessboard is useful orientation, but it can also create the wrong expectations when piece identities are hidden and bluffing is allowed. I focused the adaptation on explaining what is private, what a player can do now and how a challenge changes the game.","process":["I separated arranging an army from playing it. In same-device mode, a covered handoff protects the next player’s view. Online play needs the same distinction in what each player receives, not just a visual cover over shared data.","Zen mode keeps the board, players, turn and Move, Bluff and Challenge controls in view. History, hints and secondary tools move into sheets so they can be inspected without permanently shrinking the board.","The rules and original game concept are credited to Triple S Games. This is an independent digital adaptation. I treated computer play, shared-device play and online rooms as separate flows because they have different privacy and connection requirements."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make hidden information explicit","choice":"Show the player’s own identities and keep the opponent’s pieces visually hidden.","reason":"Someone should understand the information available to each side before making a move.","tradeoff":"Shared-device privacy depends on a clear handoff; it cannot stop someone looking at the screen at the wrong time."},{"title":"Name the unusual actions","choice":"Keep Move, Bluff and Challenge as separate controls with rules available nearby.","reason":"The familiar board should not make a new player assume ordinary check-and-checkmate behaviour.","tradeoff":"A challenge can refer to earlier play, so its target and consequence need careful explanation."},{"title":"Give the board the space","choice":"Use the focused play view and move history and secondary tools into deliberate inspection surfaces.","reason":"The board is easier to read when optional tools do not compete for its space.","tradeoff":"Hiding history can make challenges harder to investigate. The route back to a past move must remain obvious."}],"outcome":"I designed setup, private handoff and the focused game view around the information each player can use. The game supports computer, same-device and online play, with explicit challenge controls and local saving. I still want to test whether a new player understands the handoff and can investigate a challenge.","reflection":"I would watch where an experienced chess player applies an ordinary rule that does not belong here. Those moments would tell me where the adaptation needs a clearer explanation rather than another hint.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: arrange an army, make a bluff and investigate a past move with a first-time player.","Proposed checks: shared-device handoff, keyboard square navigation, a disconnected online player and restoring a local game."],"sources":[{"label":"Project README and original rules credit","detail":"Private setup, computer/shared-device/online modes, focused play, challenge handling and credit to Triple S Games. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Actual local game captures","detail":"Captured from the local built game under /game/ in computer mode. Positions illustrate interaction states; no human playtest result is represented."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess actual board with visible own pieces and hidden opponent identities","caption":"Actual local computer-mode interface. The opponent’s identities remain hidden while the player’s own army is visible.","width":1275,"height":766},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-setup.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess army arrangement screen and mode choices","caption":"Actual setup screen with army arrangement and the separate computer, online and shared-device modes.","width":1275,"height":1102}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hidden-identity-chess-club.soundmite3.chatgpt.site/game/","productActionLabel":"Play Hidden Identity Chess","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":13,"pdfPage":75,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Familiar chess. [[Very different information.]]","scene":"Imagine knowing how a knight moves but not knowing which enemy piece is a knight. The board is familiar; the information behind the decision is not.","question":"How do familiar controls explain a game with unfamiliar information?","insight":"[[The board alone cannot explain the rules]]. Setup, hidden identities and the three turn actions need to be visible in the interaction.","pivot":"I kept the board central and made the difference between moving, bluffing and challenging explicit.","takeaway":"Familiarity should help someone learn, not conceal a different rule.","journey":[{"title":"Arrange","detail":"Place an army privately before the game."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Choose a move or a bluff with the visible information."},{"title":"Investigate","detail":"Use history and a challenge when a move needs scrutiny."}],"decisionLeads":["Make hidden information explicit","Name the unusual actions","Give the board the space"],"annotations":[{"title":"Independent adaptation","detail":"The original concept and rules are credited to Triple S Games."},{"title":"Actual computer mode","detail":"The captures show the app, not a fabricated multiplayer session."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"hidden-identity-chess","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Chess player learning a hidden-information variant","context":"Recognising the board while learning different movement and challenge expectations.","need":"Understand which information is private and which action is available.","friction":"Ordinary chess habits can conceal the rules that changed.","implication":"Name the unusual actions and keep their consequences inspectable."}},"theme":{"layout":"hidden-identity-chess","mode":"light","paper":"#f6f7f2","ink":"#193b35","dim":"#586e66","line":"#dce2d8","accent":"#265b4d","wash":"#e8ead3","bodyFont":"Manrope","displayFont":"Manrope","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How do familiar controls explain a game with unfamiliar information?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Hidden Identity Chess","resultHeading":"A familiar board with clear private information."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess actual board with visible own pieces and hidden opponent identities","caption":"Actual local computer-mode interface. The opponent’s identities remain hidden while the player’s own army is visible.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from the local built game under /game/ in computer mode. Positions illustrate interaction states; no human playtest result is represented.","width":1275,"height":766},"v2":{"title":"Chess where you can [[bluff]], and your opponent can call it.","hook":"Hidden Identity Chess is a web game I designed and built on my own. It turns a published board game, where you cannot see what the enemy pieces are, into something you can play on a phone, offline or with a friend online.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"October 2026"},{"label":"Why","value":"A digital version of a board game I wanted to play, Hidden Identity Chess by Triple S Games. Independent, not an official release"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web game for phone and desktop. It installs to the home screen and plays offline"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Plain JavaScript, a service worker, Next.js 16 on Cloudflare Workers, Cloudflare D1"},{"label":"Status","value":"Live and playable. Not yet tested with other players. No usage numbers"}],"links":[{"label":"Play the game","url":"https://hidden-identity-chess-club.soundmite3.chatgpt.site/game/"},{"label":"The original board game","url":"https://www.thegamecrafter.com/games/hidden-identity-chess-pieces-only-"}],"linksHeading":"Try it yourself","summary":["The problem: in this game you see where the enemy pieces stand but not what they are, and you may make illegal moves on purpose. A normal chess screen shows neither of those things.","What I built: a game you can install, with secret setup, a Move and Bluff switch, challenges and penalties, a computer opponent, pass-and-play on one phone and online rooms.","The key idea: a secret has to be kept in the data, not just covered on the screen. The server blanks out the enemy pieces before it sends a player the board.","Where it stands: live and playable, made in four commits between 17 and 19 October 2026. I have not tested it with other players yet."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/lead.webp","alt":"Five phone screens of the game in order: arranging an army, bluff mode with every square open, a captured piece revealed as a knight, choosing a penalty piece, and the win screen with every piece revealed","caption":"One game, left to right. Every enemy piece is a shield with a question mark until it is captured, or until the game ends.","width":2400,"height":1000},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"Ordinary chess pieces, with the enemy's identities hidden","blocks":[{"p":"Hidden Identity Chess is a board game published by Triple S Games. The pieces move like chess pieces. The difference is what you know. You see your own army, but each enemy piece is hidden until you capture it."},{"p":"You also set up your own army in secret, anywhere in your two nearest rows. There is no check or checkmate. You win by taking the king. And you may move a piece in a way it is not allowed to move, as long as you accept that your opponent can call it out."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/turn-flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of one turn: from Your turn, three choices. Move leads to capture and reveal. Bluff leads to a private record of legality. Challenge leads to two results, legal with no cost, or illegal with the piece removed and a penalty","caption":"The rules in one picture. Look at the Challenge row: a wrong accusation costs nothing, but a caught bluff costs two pieces.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"I wanted to play it on a phone, against the computer or a friend. I set the brief myself: familiar, not cartoonish and not formal, with as little text as possible during play. I gave the look a working name, a modern board-game lounge: a teal table, ivory and sage squares, and warm yellow for whatever needs your attention."},{"aside":{"title":"What I started from.","detail":"The written rules on the publisher's page. There was no research with players. The people I designed for, two friends playing together or far apart and solo players against the computer, are my own assumption."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How a game goes","heading":"Arrange in secret, play, challenge, and take the king","blocks":[{"p":"There are three ways to play. Computer and Pass & play work offline once the game has loaded. Online friend makes a room with a seven-character code that a friend types in on their own device."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-setup.webp","alt":"Setup screen on a phone: three mode tabs, Arrange your army with Shuffle and Classic buttons, a Play against computer button, Game options, and the board with hidden enemy pieces","caption":"Setup. Shuffle mixes your army, Classic gives the usual order, or you tap two pieces to swap them.","width":824,"height":1520},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-lobby.webp","alt":"Online friend tab: Play a friend, a Create a room button, and a room code field with a Join button","caption":"Online friend. One button to make a room, one field to join one. No account is needed.","width":824,"height":1260},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-rules.webp","alt":"The rules sheet titled Chess, with a secret, with sections on arranging your army, capturing the king and promotion","caption":"The rules, one tap away at all times. The unusual ones are written out in full.","width":824,"height":1520}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"On your turn you move, bluff or challenge. Capturing a piece reveals it. Every move goes into a list, and any enemy move that has not been checked yet can be challenged from that list."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-reveal.webp","alt":"A pawn has just captured on d5 and a banner reads A knight. Identity revealed. The captured knight appears beside You","caption":"A capture. The banner names what you took, and the piece joins your captures beside your name.","width":824,"height":1520},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-moves.webp","alt":"The Moves sheet listing e2 to e4 and b8 to e5, with a Challenge button under the enemy move and offence counts of 0 of 2","caption":"The move list. The hidden piece jumped from b8 to e5, so the list offers a Challenge button under it.","width":824,"height":1520},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-challenge.webp","alt":"A dialog titled Challenge b8 to e5? explaining that a legal move stays hidden and an illegal move loses the piece and an extra penalty","caption":"One confirm step, with the stakes in one sentence.","width":824,"height":1520}],"layout":"phones"},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/annotated-penalty.webp","alt":"The board after a caught bluff, with six numbered notes on the notice, the yellow rings, the emptied square, the captured knight, the instruction line and the reduced dock","caption":"The same game a moment later: the bluff was illegal. Start at note 2. Only the pieces you are allowed to remove can be tapped.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-penalty-log.webp","alt":"The Moves sheet after the penalty: b8 to e5 marked Caught: illegal, Forest offences 1 of 2, and Forest pawn removed as the penalty","caption":"The record afterwards. The move is marked as caught and the offence count reads 1 of 2. A second proven offence loses the game.","width":824,"height":1520},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-gameover.webp","alt":"The final board with every enemy piece revealed, a notice that the king capture was checked and legal, and Ivory wins with a Play again button","caption":"A win. The computer challenged the king capture, found it legal, and every piece was shown.","width":824,"height":1480}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"I set up these positions myself in a local copy of the game to show each state. They are not recorded games."}]},{"id":"shields","label":"Decision 1","nav":"One shield for every piece","heading":"Every enemy piece is the same shield until it is captured","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: your own pieces are drawn as chess pieces. Every enemy piece is the same dark shield with a question mark, a little smaller than a real piece. When it is captured, the shield turns into the piece it really was."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/pieces.webp","alt":"The game's piece set: king, queen, rook, bishop, knight and pawn in light and dark, beside two hidden-piece shields on light and dark squares","caption":"The piece set I drew for the game, and the shield on the right. Each piece has a different top, so it still reads at phone size.","width":1600,"height":504}]},{"p":"I drew the pieces myself as simple outlines on one 45-unit grid, with the same line weight on every piece. My rule for the shield was that nothing about it may give the piece away: not its size, its shape, or the label a screen reader announces."},{"p":"In the build: hiding a piece on screen is not enough, because the data behind the screen could still say what it is. So one function makes each player's copy of the board, and it swaps every enemy piece's type for a question mark before anything is drawn or sent. A test checks that both players' copies hide all 16 enemy pieces, and only show the king when the game is set to revealed kings."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Sixteen identical shields put the memory work on the player. To help, the game keeps the full move list one tap away and lets you mark a private guess on any enemy piece. The guess is only a note to yourself, never a reveal."}}]},{"id":"bluff","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Bluffing on purpose","heading":"A bluff is a switch you choose, never an accident","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: Move and Bluff share one switch under the board. In Move, only legal squares light up. Turn on Bluff and every empty or enemy square becomes a target, the switch turns yellow, and a short notice warns that a challenge can expose you."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-bluff.webp","alt":"Bluff mode on a phone: a knight is selected, dots fill every empty square, rings surround every enemy piece, and the Bluff half of the switch is yellow","caption":"Bluff on, with the knight on g1 picked. Compare the dots with a knight's usual two or three squares.","width":824,"height":1520,"kind":"tall"}]},{"p":"In the build: the rules allow any move, so I could have let every move through. I kept legal moves as the default instead. An illegal move in Move mode is refused with a message that points to Bluff, so a slip of the finger never turns into a risk of losing two pieces."},{"p":"When you do bluff, the move is played and the game writes down in a private record whether it was legal. A challenge reads that record. A test checks that a player's copy of the board never contains it, and another checks that the computer opponent makes the same choices whatever that record says."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A bluff costs one extra tap. In a game about reading your opponent, I chose to make lying a deliberate act rather than a hidden side effect."}}]},{"id":"board","label":"Decision 3","nav":"The board gets the screen","heading":"The board gets the screen, and everything else waits in a sheet","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: during a game the phone shows the board, one row for each player, one line of status and a small dock. Moves, hints, sound, rules, install and resign all open as sheets that slide up from the bottom. I call this zen mode, and it is on by default."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/annotated-play.webp","alt":"The phone play screen with six numbered notes on the player row, the shields, the yellow turn text, the status line, the Move and Bluff switch and the dock","caption":"The play screen as it shipped. Note 5 is the one I care most about: the two actions that change the game sit under your thumb.","width":1600,"height":1040}]},{"p":"The first version was not like this. It tried to explain the game while you played it."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/before-after-play.webp","alt":"Two phone screens at the same scale: the first version with many panels below the board, and the final version with only a status line and dock","caption":"Same game, same scale. Everything the first version showed is still there, one tap away.","width":1600,"height":1240}]},{"p":"Setup had the opposite problem. The board came first, so on a phone the Play button sat below the first screen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/before-after-setup.webp","alt":"Two setup screens at the same scale with the Play button outlined: far below an 844px line in the first version, and near the top in the final one","caption":"The outlined Play button moved from below the dashed line to near the top. The board still follows, ready for swapping pieces.","width":1600,"height":1400}]},{"p":"In the build: the board's size is the smallest of 600px, the screen height less 280px, and the screen width less 36px, so it fills whatever the device allows. On touch screens every button outside the board is at least 48px tall, and main actions are 56px. In a short landscape window the squares never drop below 44px; the page scrolls instead."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/polish-desktop.webp","alt":"Desktop in zen mode: the board centred with the dock below and nothing beside it","caption":"Desktop with zen mode on, the default.","width":1120,"height":930},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/desktop-standard.webp","alt":"Desktop with zen mode off: the board on the left and a Moves panel on the right listing three moves, each with a Challenge button","caption":"Zen off. A wide screen has room to keep the move list beside the board.","width":1120,"height":1060},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/s24-landscape.webp","alt":"A phone turned sideways: the board on the left and the Move and Bluff switch with the dock on the right","caption":"A phone on its side. The controls move beside the board instead of shrinking it.","width":910,"height":589}],"layout":"three"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Hiding the history makes a challenge one tap further away, and a challenge often depends on remembering an earlier move. That is why Moves stays in the dock in every phase of the game, including while you choose a penalty."}}]},{"id":"handoff","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Passing the phone","heading":"Passing the phone covers the whole board","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: in Pass & play, two people share one phone. After each move the board is covered and the next player taps Show my pieces when only they can see the screen."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/first-handoff.webp","alt":"First version of the handoff: a covered board reading Over to Forest, with a status card, move list, buttons and tips still shown below","caption":"The first version. The board is covered, but the move list and buttons below stay on show.","width":384,"height":1162},{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/screen-handoff.webp","alt":"Final handoff: the whole table covered in teal with a lock, Pass to Ivory, Keep your army to yourself, and a yellow Show my pieces button. Move and Bluff are greyed out","caption":"The final version. One instruction and one button. Move, Bluff and Challenge are switched off until the next player is ready.","width":824,"height":1520}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"In the build: while the cover is up, the squares are switched off and the game ignores taps on them. A pass-and-play game reopened later starts covered, so whoever picks up the phone does not see the last player's army."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A cover cannot stop someone looking at the screen at the wrong moment. It only makes the safe moment obvious. Online play needs no cover, because each device only ever receives its own player's view."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One rules file, trusted on the server and offline on the phone","blocks":[{"p":"The rules live in one file: setup, moves, bluffs, challenges, penalties and the computer player. The game screen uses it directly. The online rooms import the same file on the server, so both sides apply exactly the same rules."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: the game screen, rules engine, local save and service worker on the device; the rooms API, room code and revision number online; and the filtered view, the private record that is never sent, and the D1 database","caption":"What runs where. The right-hand column is the point: a player only ever receives the board with the enemy pieces blanked out.","width":1600,"height":1100}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Game","Plain JavaScript modules and CSS, no framework"],["Pieces and icons","My own SVG drawings"],["Type","Manrope, stored with the game so it works offline"],["Offline","A service worker and the browser's local storage"],["Online rooms","Next.js 16 with vinext on Cloudflare Workers, a D1 database through Drizzle"],["Checks","43 tests on Node's built-in test runner"]]}},{"p":"The computer opponent is a simple set of rules, not a chess engine that searches ahead. It plays from the same view you have. It never reads your hidden pieces or the private record of bluffs."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/computer.webp","alt":"Table of the three computer styles, Casual, Club and Challenger, with bluff and challenge chances, beside four steps showing how a piece's moves narrow down what it can be","caption":"The three styles differ by a few numbers. On the right, the one case where the computer challenges for certain.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"stats":[{"value":"4","label":"commits, 17 to 19 October 2026"},{"value":"22","label":"review screenshots across three rounds"},{"value":"43","label":"tests in five files: rules, bluffs and challenges, online rooms, offline loading, installing"},{"value":"48h","label":"an online room lasts without a move"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of three rounds: the whole game working on 17 October, less on screen while playing the same evening, and the Samsung phone fix on 19 October, each with a thumbnail","caption":"The three rounds. I checked each one by taking screenshots at several screen sizes before moving on.","width":1600,"height":660}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were about trust and loading, not drawing the board."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Installed app","Installed to the home screen of a Samsung phone, the game failed to open. The host redirected the page, and the saved offline copy kept a trace of that redirect, which the browser rejects when it opens an installed app. The offline copy is now rebuilt without it, every file is checked before an update takes over, and a repair page lets an old install refresh without losing saved games."],["Install timing","Install now appears only after all 11 game files are saved. If a download fails, the earlier version stays in place and the game offers Try again."],["Secrets online","Each seat gets a long random token, and the server keeps only a hash of it. Every move carries a revision number, so if two moves arrive at once only one counts. Tests run two separate clients and check that neither ever sees the other's pieces."],["Old saved games","Games saved before I added challenges have no private record of their moves. The game says those moves cannot be checked, instead of guessing."]]}},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/hidden-identity-chess/s24-install.webp","alt":"A sheet titled Getting your game ready, saying the board, pieces and game are being saved for offline play","caption":"What you see before install is offered: the game saving itself for offline play.","width":407,"height":1159,"kind":"tall"}]}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Big targets, a keyboard path, and no secret in the labels","blocks":[{"list":["On touch screens, buttons outside the board are at least 48px tall and the main actions 56px. Phone labels are 14 to 16px.","The board works with a keyboard. The arrow keys follow the board as you see it, even when it is turned for the other player, and focus moves to a sensible next control after each move.","Focus shows as a thick amber outline. Notices are read out by screen readers as they appear.","If your device asks for less motion, the game turns off its animations.","A screen reader hears a hidden piece as \"hidden enemy piece\", never its name."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"These are things the game was built to do. I have not run a screen-reader pass or a formal accessibility audit."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Live and playable, but not yet played by anyone else","blocks":[{"p":"The game is live at a public link. Anyone can open it, play the computer, pass a phone or start an online room. The game itself needs no account, and it has no ratings, chat or tracking."},{"p":"It is an independent adaptation. The rules and the original idea belong to Triple S Games, and the game says so. It is not an official release."},{"p":"I have no usage numbers to share. These are also still open:"},{"list":["No games between two people are recorded, online or on one phone.","I checked phone layouts at 360, 384 and 412px wide in a browser. A check of the install on a real Samsung phone is not recorded.","There is no way to agree a draw. If a player has no legal move against the computer, the game ends in a draw, a rule I added that is not in the original."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Watch a chess player learn it","blocks":[{"list":["Watch first-time players arrange an army, make a bluff and challenge a past move. Experienced chess players are the most interesting, because they will apply habits that no longer hold.","Test the pass-and-play cover, moving across the board by keyboard, a player who drops out of an online game, and reopening a saved game.","Install it on a real Samsung phone and record the result.","Add a way for both players to agree a draw."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"A secret on the screen is not a secret","blocks":[{"p":"Covering a piece is the easy part. The real work was making sure the hidden facts never reach the wrong place: not the other player's device, not a screen reader label, and not the computer opponent's choices."},{"p":"The first version also taught me something about help text. Each card under the board was useful on its own. Together they pushed the controls off the screen. Moving them into sheets kept every one of them and gave the board its space back."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"five-dead","name":"5 Dead","headline":"Keep the table moving. Keep the turn clear."}},"five-dead":{"slug":"five-dead","name":"5 Dead","kind":"product","headline":"A party card game where one number runs every turn","summary":"5 Dead is a web card game I designed and built on my own. You can play against bots, pass one phone round a table of up to 24 people, or open an online room. Every turn comes down to keeping a shared total at 21 or under.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Playable card game","context":"Independent game inspired by 5 Alive","facts":[{"value":"21","label":"safe running-total limit"},{"value":"3 modes","label":"bots, pass-and-play and online"},{"value":"2–24","label":"extended party-table scope"}],"problem":"A card table has several things to explain at once: the current total, whose turn it is, what a card will do and who is still in the hand. I wanted the presentation to give each moment a clear focus without making players work through a rules panel on every turn.","process":["I kept the running total and current discard near the centre, opponents around the table and the local hand close to the player. Selection and a Play action help separate considering a card from committing it.","The three entry modes make different promises. Bot play and local pass-and-play can work offline after the app is ready; online rooms depend on a connection and wait for a disconnected player. Shared-device turns use a privacy screen between hands.","This is an independent game inspired by Hasbro’s 5 Alive. Larger tables use a disclosed house rule. The generated arena and card artwork are assets used in the game itself; I kept the running total, hand and turn feedback distinct from that background."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the running total central","choice":"Place the total, discard and turn state where the next decision is made.","reason":"A player needs to connect the chosen card to the current state of the hand.","tradeoff":"Effects and animations can obscure that relationship if they linger or hide the next turn."},{"title":"Separate choosing from playing","choice":"Show a selected card and an explicit Play action, alongside the supported drag interaction.","reason":"Considering a card should not accidentally commit it, especially on a crowded phone hand.","tradeoff":"An extra action can slow experienced play. Both methods need clear feedback and usable touch targets."},{"title":"Explain the mode before the table starts","choice":"Distinguish bot, pass-and-play and connected rooms, including their waiting and privacy states.","reason":"A group should know whether it needs one device, several devices or a network before starting.","tradeoff":"Local privacy covers are a social convention; online connection loss can pause the table."}],"outcome":"I brought the hand, table, turn feedback and joining flow into one playable presentation. Bot and local play work separately from connected rooms. Larger tables follow the documented house rules, with waiting and privacy states suited to each mode.","reflection":"I would test the smallest phone hand and the largest table before adding more effects. The useful questions are whether the next player notices their turn and whether someone can tell what a special card just changed.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: start a bot hand, play a special card and explain the next action without opening the full rules.","Proposed checks: overlapping cards, shared-device handoff, a disconnected seat and the clarity of the extended-table rule."],"sources":[{"label":"5 Dead README · modes, rules and extended tables","detail":"Mode boundaries, the 21 limit, hand privacy, online waiting and the disclosed larger-table variant. Reviewed 31 October 2026. Exact deck counts for a particular physical edition have not been verified."},{"label":"Original game captures and DESIGN.md","detail":"Existing outputs/5dead-arena-v2.png and 5dead-card-faces-phone.png. These show the actual game presentation, including its generated arena, avatar and card art."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","alt":"5 Dead actual game table with running total opponents and a selected card","caption":"Original game capture. The arena, avatars and skull card art are generated assets used by the playable game; labels and controls are part of the actual interface.","width":844,"height":390},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-hand.webp","alt":"5 Dead original phone layout showing special cards and the Play control","caption":"Original game capture showing the phone hand, special cards and the Play control.","width":915,"height":412}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/5Dead","productActionLabel":"View 5 Dead on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":14,"pdfPage":78,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Keep the table moving. [[Keep the turn clear.]]","scene":"Imagine a special card changing the direction of play just as an animation finishes. Everyone needs to know what changed and who acts next.","question":"What does the next player need to see before choosing a card?","insight":"[[The state change matters more than the effect]]. Total, turn and the selected card need to remain understandable through the animation.","pivot":"I gave the running total a stable place and kept card selection, commitment and turn feedback distinct.","takeaway":"An effect has done its job when the table knows what happens next.","journey":[{"title":"Choose a table","detail":"Select bots, shared-device play or an online room."},{"title":"Read the turn","detail":"Check the total, discard and available hand."},{"title":"Play and continue","detail":"Commit a card and follow its effect into the next turn."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the running total central","Separate choosing from playing","Explain the mode before the table starts"],"annotations":[{"title":"Actual game artwork","detail":"Generated arena and card art appear in the game itself and are identified as such."},{"title":"Disclosed variant","detail":"More-than-six-player tables use documented house rules."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"five-dead","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Player joining a short card game","context":"Learning the digital table and special-card feedback, possibly on a phone.","need":"Know the current total, available action and next turn.","friction":"Crowded hands and effects can hide a simple decision.","implication":"Keep core state stable and use clear commitment feedback."}},"theme":{"layout":"five-dead","mode":"dark","paper":"#101d31","ink":"#f6f4ec","dim":"#b9c6dc","line":"#435675","accent":"#ffd084","wash":"#3b3021","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Barlow Condensed","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"What does the next player need to see before choosing a card?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind 5 Dead","resultHeading":"A table with a clear total and a clear next turn."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","alt":"5 Dead actual game table with running total opponents and a selected card","caption":"Original game capture. The arena, avatars and skull card art are generated assets used by the playable game; labels and controls are part of the actual interface.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Existing outputs/5dead-arena-v2.png and 5dead-card-faces-phone.png. These show the actual game presentation, including its generated arena, avatar and card art.","width":844,"height":390},"v2":{"title":"A party card game where [[one number]] runs every turn","hook":"5 Dead is a web card game I designed and built on my own. You can play against bots, pass one phone round a table of up to 24 people, or open an online room. Every turn comes down to keeping a shared total at 21 or under.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"October 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"Friends and families playing together, 2 to 24 players"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web app you can install. Plays offline. Phones play held sideways"},{"label":"Stack","value":"Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Cloudflare Workers, D1 with Drizzle, Tailwind CSS 4"},{"label":"Status","value":"Playable. Not yet tested with groups of players"}],"links":[{"label":"View the code on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/5Dead"}],"linksHeading":"See the code","summary":["The problem: a card table has to show several things at once, the total, whose turn it is, what a card does and who is still alive, on screens as small as a phone held sideways.","What I built: an installable web game with bots, pass and play on one device, and online rooms, all running the same rules engine.","The key idea: put the running total in the middle and keep choosing a card separate from playing it. The throw gesture took three rounds to get right while the rules stayed strict.","Where it stands: playable, with 29 automated tests. Friends played it at a game night. Beyond that I have no usage numbers."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/table-desktop.webp","alt":"The 5 Dead game table on a computer: an oval table with the running total 5 in the middle, three opponents around it and a fanned hand of ten cards along the bottom","caption":"A pass and play game on a computer. The total is 5, it is your turn, and your hand is sorted from high to low. The backdrop, the character portraits and the card back are painted out in flat colour: they are generated pictures, not my design.","width":1280,"height":800},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"A card game the whole table can follow, on whatever devices are in the room","blocks":[{"p":"5 Alive is a printed card game. Players take turns adding cards to one shared total. Go past 21 and you lose a life. Action cards reset the total, skip players or make everyone else draw."},{"p":"I wanted a version friends and families could play together on phones, tablets and computers, with or without a network. 5 Dead is my own independent game built on those rules. It is not made with or approved by Hasbro, which publishes 5 Alive. There was no client: the brief was my own."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/turn.webp","alt":"Diagram of a number line from 0 to 21 with 16 highlighted, above four groups of cards: add to the total, set the total, hit everyone else and change the table","caption":"The whole game in one picture. At 16, a 5 is the highest number card that keeps you safe. The pink boxes are the two ways lives are lost.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"The hard part is the screen. At any moment a player needs the total, whose turn it is, what the card they are holding will do and who is still in. With up to 24 players, that has to fit on a phone held sideways."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"Apart from that game night with friends I have not run play sessions, and I have no usage numbers. Nothing on this page reports what players said or did."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"Pick who is at the table, then play","blocks":[{"p":"The setup asks one question first: who is at the table? The answer decides how many devices you need and whether you need a network."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/modes.webp","alt":"Diagram comparing three modes, Bots, Pass and play, and Online room, by players, devices, network, hidden hands and what happens if you leave","caption":"The three modes side by side. Read the Network row: only the online room needs a connection after the first load.","width":1600,"height":740}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/phone-setup.webp","alt":"The phone setup screen with tabs for Bots, Pass and play, and Online, a name field, a player counter set to 4 and a Let's play button","caption":"Setup on a phone. One short form. The line under the button says what this mode needs: no Wi-Fi.","width":397,"height":790,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/rules-guide.webp","alt":"The How to play guide on a phone listing special cards such as Skip, Set to 21, Reset, Reshuffle and Bomb, each with a one-line explanation","caption":"The rules guide. It uses the same card faces as the game, so a card looks the same here and in your hand.","width":412,"height":915,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"p":"In pass and play, one device goes round the table. Between turns a cover screen hides the last hand and names the next player. Nobody sees cards until that player taps to reveal them."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/privacy-screen.webp","alt":"The table with a cover panel across the bottom reading Pass to Player 3 and a Show my cards button","caption":"The cover screen between turns. The hand area is replaced by one instruction and one button. The backdrop, the character portraits and the card back are painted out in flat colour: they are generated pictures, not my design.","width":1280,"height":800}]}]},{"id":"table","label":"Decision 1","nav":"The total in the middle","heading":"The total sits in the middle, and everything else sits around it","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: the running total is the largest thing on the screen. The real last card sits beside it. Opponents sit around the table and your hand runs along the bottom."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/annotated-table.webp","alt":"The computer table with eight numbered notes: the total, the play pile, the direction of play, the turn cue, the mode label, a seat's lives and card count, the sorted hand and the Play button","caption":"Start with notes 1 and 3: the total and the direction of play sit together, because a Reverse changes both what happens next and who acts.","width":1600,"height":1440}]},{"p":"In the build: my first release was a quiet, flat table on a charcoal page, with soft yellow, teal and lilac cards. Twenty minutes later I replaced it. The new table is laid out like mobile card games such as UNO Mobile: a landscape table, big overlapping cards and clear feedback when an action lands. The rules did not change, and the identity and card faces stay my own."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/landing-first.webp","alt":"The first release's home page: a large headline reading Five lives, zero mercy, a fan of pastel cards and the start of the setup panel","caption":"The first release's home page, from the same morning. The pastel card faces were later replaced.","width":978,"height":712}]},{"p":"Your hand is sorted from 7 down to 0, then action cards in a fixed order. Sorting only changes what you see. A test checks that it never changes the actual deal. At exactly 21 the total changes colour and reads \"On the edge\"."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Every action now plays an effect across the table: a bomb sends out a shockwave, a reverse sweeps round. Effects can hide whose turn it is. So effects play one at a time from a queue, bots wait for the current one to finish, and the reduced-motion setting drops the movement but keeps the text and colour cues."}},{"aside":{"title":"About the screens on this page.","detail":"The game has a painted night-sky backdrop, six character portraits and an illustrated card back. They are generated pictures, not my design work, so I have painted them out of every screen here. Everything else is the real interface."}}]},{"id":"choose","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Choosing is not playing","heading":"Choosing a card is not the same as playing it","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: one tap lifts a card and ticks it. Nothing is played yet. The Play button then commits it, and the line under the button says what the card will do."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/selected-card.webp","alt":"A hand of ten cards with the yellow 7 lifted and ticked, and a Play button on the right with the line Add this number to the total","caption":"A selected card. Look under Play: \"Add this number to the total.\" The hint changes with the card you hold.","width":1104,"height":214}]},{"p":"For people who know the game there are faster ways: double-tap a card, toss it upward, or drop it anywhere on the table."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/gesture.webp","alt":"Diagram of how far a finger must move upward before a card lifts or plays, with a list of gestures and what each one does","caption":"The gesture rules. Small movements never play a card. Each pink row on the right is a way to back out.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"In the build: getting the toss right took three rounds on the same morning. Each round added tests that describe the gesture in plain words."},{"steps":[{"title":"Round 1","detail":"Drag a card onto the table, or throw it there. Sideways swipes, slow releases, short twitches and throws away from the table never play a card."},{"title":"Round 2","detail":"A slow drop on the table's rim also plays. So does a card held by its corner, once your grip reaches the table."},{"title":"Round 3","detail":"A small upward toss plays before the card reaches the table, with no speed or aim needed. Taps, flat swipes on a long hand and downward drags stay in the hand."}]},{"p":"A shared guard stops a card from being played twice by a double click straight after a drag. The pointer maths also accounts for the rotated phone layout described below."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Select then Play costs an extra tap for experienced players. I kept it as the main path because it is the one that works for everyone, including keyboard users, and made the shortcuts forgiving instead."}}]},{"id":"faces","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Faces that explain","heading":"The card face tells you what the card does","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: number cards and action cards look different at a glance. Each kind of action has its own colour, symbol and short label."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/faces.webp","alt":"Before and after strips of the same phone hand: pastel cards with a ring on every face, then bold cards with a coloured corner, a black field and a label strip on action cards","caption":"Two hands from the same phone layout, 17 minutes apart. In the after strip, find the Reshuffle card on the right: magenta, its own symbol, its own label.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"p":"In the build: every face is one component drawn in code, not an image. Each face measures its own width, so the corner number, the centre mark and the label scale with the card, not with the size of the hand. Number cards repeat their number in the opposite corner. Action cards drop that second number so it can never collide with the label."},{"p":"The colours follow the cues of the printed game: blue and cyan for draw cards, lime for reset, magenta for reshuffle and pass, orange for reverse. The Bomb has a charcoal and red face inside a gold frame, with a fuse blast. The Reshuffle shows cards inside circular arrows."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/phone-table.webp","alt":"The phone table with ten bold cards in a fan, the Reshuffle card lifted and ticked, and the Play button explaining Shuffle and re-deal all active hands, reset to zero","caption":"The new faces on a phone. The selected Reshuffle card is explained under Play before you commit it. The backdrop, the character portraits and the card back are painted out in flat colour: they are generated pictures, not my design.","width":915,"height":412}]},{"p":"Two tests guard this. One checks that every special card uses its colour and a short label that no other card shares. The other checks that the Bomb and the Reshuffle appear exactly once in every deck, at every table size."},{"aside":{"title":"What I ruled out.","detail":"Colour alone. Borrowing the printed game's colours makes the cards familiar to people who know it, but colour cannot carry the meaning by itself, so every action card also has its own symbol and a word."}}]},{"id":"phones","label":"Decision 4","nav":"Phones, sideways","heading":"Phones play sideways, and any hand size fits","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: on a phone the table always runs sideways. Far seats shrink to a name, hearts and a card count. When a hand gets too long for one fan, it becomes two full rows."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/annotated-phone.webp","alt":"A phone table with a 24-card hand in two rows and six numbered notes on the header, the direction label, the player list, the compact far seats, the two-row hand and the Play button","caption":"Note 5 is the point: 24 cards, every one whole and tappable, without shrinking the controls. The backdrop, the character portraits and the card back are painted out in flat colour: they are generated pictures, not my design.","width":1600,"height":1180}]},{"p":"In the build: when a game starts on a touch device, it asks for full screen and a sideways lock. Support varies, especially on iPhones. Then the page itself rotates the live table, and the same measurements drive both versions."},{"list":["Touch targets: 48px icon buttons, 52 to 56px main buttons and fields, and 16px text in forms.","Cards in a sideways hand stay 80 to 98px wide, with at least 52px of each card showing.","As the hand grows, the fan tightens, then splits into two rows of cards at least 44px wide. Very large hands scroll sideways, with a hint.","The space above and below the cards is reserved, so a lifted card, its outline and its tick never get cut off."]},{"p":"Two tests check that every card stays selectable inside the hand area as the count changes, on phone and computer sizes, and that the hand grows back after cards are played. I checked layouts in the browser at 320, 384 and 412px wide upright, and at 915 by 412 and 915 by 320 sideways."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"All of this was checked in a desktop browser imitating phones. Real orientation locking, notches and the Samsung browser still need testing on real devices."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One rules engine, used by every mode","blocks":[{"p":"The same rules engine deals, checks and applies every move. Bots and pass and play run it on the device. Online rooms run it on the server, which is the only judge of a move."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: on each device the screens, rules engine and offline store; online the room service and a database row per room; and what each player receives: their own hand, the public table and recent receipts","caption":"How it fits together. The receipts on the right let every phone play the same animation without learning anyone's private cards.","width":1600,"height":960}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["App","Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript"],["Hosting","Cloudflare Workers"],["Online rooms","D1 (SQLite) through Drizzle, one row per room with a revision number"],["Offline play","A service worker, with saved games in browser storage"],["Styling","Tailwind CSS 4, plus my own styles for the table and the cards"],["Sound","Made in the browser with the Web Audio API, after the first tap, with a remembered mute"],["Checks","29 tests run with Node's own test runner, plus a room test against a local server"]]}},{"p":"Fairness was a rule from the start. The same deal rules and the same luck apply to every seat, bots included."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/fairness.webp","alt":"Diagram of four steps from secure random numbers to dealing ten cards each, with three notes on what bots, hand sorting and the online server can and cannot see","caption":"How a deal is made. Step 2 is the easy one to miss: without it, some cards would turn up slightly more often.","width":1600,"height":700}]},{"p":"The printed game is for 2 to 6 players. For bigger groups I add a whole deck for every six players, so the mix of cards stays the same."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/deck.webp","alt":"Bar chart of one 77-card deck: 47 number cards from 0 to 7 and 30 action cards from Reverse down to Bomb","caption":"One deck. Low numbers are common and the 7 is rare. The counts are my own: the published leaflet does not list them.","width":1600,"height":820},{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/scaling.webp","alt":"Bar chart of four table sizes, 2 to 6, 7 to 12, 13 to 18 and 19 to 24 players, each showing cards dealt and cards left to draw","caption":"Bigger tables. At the top of every band, 22% of the cards are left to draw from. An illustration worked out from the counts, not a measured result.","width":1600,"height":740}],"layout":"one"},{"stats":[{"value":"9","label":"commits, all on 19 October 2026"},{"value":"29","label":"automated tests: 16 for the rules, 13 for the interface"},{"value":"77","label":"cards in one deck, up to four decks at once"},{"value":"2–24","label":"players at one table"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/five-dead/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of 19 October 2026 from 10:00 to midnight with nine commits, from the first release at 10:38 to the hand layout at 23:30","caption":"The build in one day. Most of the morning went on the throw gesture, the evening on phones and card faces.","width":1600,"height":820}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were about trust, not looks."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What was hard, and what I did"],"rows":[["Hidden hands online","Every phone has to animate the same draw or bomb without learning anyone else's cards. The server keeps receipts of the last 12 public actions, such as who played what and how many cards were drawn. Tests check that a receipt never reveals a private draw."],["Two moves at once","Two players can press Play at the same moment. Each room has a revision number, and a save only lands if the number has not changed since the move was checked. The losing move is rejected, not merged."],["Throwing a card","It took three rounds to make a toss feel natural without letting a twitch play a card. The tests now describe the gesture in plain sentences."],["Whole games","Engine tests play complete simulated games for every table size and rule variant, and check that no card is lost or created."]]}}]},{"id":"access","label":"Accessibility","nav":"Accessibility","heading":"Motion, sound and touch all have a quieter path","blocks":[{"list":["With reduced motion turned on, the flying cards, shockwaves and sweeps are removed. Short text and colour cues stay, so nothing important depends on movement.","Sound starts only after you tap something, the mute setting is remembered, and the game stays quiet when its tab is hidden.","Keyboard users can select a card and press Play. The drag and toss gestures are extras, never the only way.","The direction of play is shown as a word, such as Clockwise, and \"Your turn\" is spelled out."]},{"aside":{"title":"What this does not prove.","detail":"I have not tested the game with a screen reader, and these choices have not been checked by a disabled player."}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Playable, and not yet played by a real group","blocks":[{"p":"5 Dead works with bots, pass and play and online rooms. Bots and pass and play keep working offline once the app has fully loaded. The code is on GitHub."},{"p":"These things are not done or not known yet:"},{"list":["No group has played it. I have no player numbers, quotes or results.","Tables of seven or more follow my house rule. Tests simulate them, but no real group has tried one.","The deck counts are my own. The published leaflet explains each card but does not list how many there are.","Installing on real iPhones and Android phones has not been tested.","Old rooms stop being playable after 24 hours, but nothing yet deletes them from the database."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Put it in front of a real table","blocks":[{"list":["Run a first session: start a bot game, play a special card, and see whether players can say what happens next without opening the rules.","Test the smallest phone hand and the biggest table with real people before adding more effects.","Try pass and play hand-offs and a dropped connection in an online room, and check that the bigger-table rule is clear before the game starts.","Install it on real phones, and add a clean-up job for expired rooms."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"Be forgiving with input and strict with rules","blocks":[{"p":"The gesture got looser twice in one morning while the rules engine did not change at all. That split made each round cheap: the input could forgive a sloppy toss because the rules engine, not the gesture, decides what is legal."},{"p":"Keeping the view apart from the deal paid off the same way. I replaced the whole table and every card face in one day, and the tests for dealing and fairness never had to move. Next time I would put the game in front of a real group before the second redesign, not after."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"alibi","name":"Alibi","headline":"Watch privately. Give just enough away."}},"alibi":{"slug":"alibi","name":"Alibi","kind":"product","headline":"A video party game that keeps one player's secret while the whole room plays together.","summary":"Alibi is a browser game I designed and built on my own. Everyone watches the same short clip except one player, who gets a different one without being told, and the group has to work out who.","role":"Design and build, solo","status":"Playable browser game","context":"Social deduction · 3–8 players","facts":[{"value":"3–8","label":"players in a room"},{"value":"One word","label":"clue from each player"},{"value":"Private","label":"clips and votes before reveal"}],"problem":"A social-deduction game loses its rhythm when someone misses a step or sees information too soon. Alibi gives most players the same clip and one player a related alternative, then moves the group through clues, discussion and voting. The flow needs to protect the mystery without making the group wait without an explanation.","process":["I structured each round as private viewing, one-word submission, shared clues, discussion, a hidden vote and the reveal. Players need a shared phase and a private view at the same time.","I used a solo practice path with clearly labelled simulated opponents so the whole sequence can be tried before inviting friends. A practice lobby should not look like proof that other people are connected.","The project includes room chat and built-in calling, with external calls as another option. Playback depends on YouTube availability and embedding, and the server keeps room state in memory. Those limits make readiness, replacement clips and host recovery controls part of the design."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep private viewing separate from the group phase","choice":"Show each assigned clip privately and release clues together after submission or timeout.","reason":"Early clues or role information could change the game before everyone has had a chance to act.","tradeoff":"A shared phase still needs a clear explanation when a player is loading or has not submitted."},{"title":"Make practice an honest invitation","choice":"Label simulated players throughout solo practice and let the person try the real sequence.","reason":"Someone can learn the rhythm without assembling a group or mistaking demo activity for real participants.","tradeoff":"Practice can explain controls but cannot reproduce the conversation and hesitation of a group."},{"title":"Explain waits and recovery","choice":"Keep the current phase, readiness and host controls visible when playback or connection needs attention.","reason":"The group needs a next action rather than an unexplained delay.","tradeoff":"A replacement clip or returning player can affect the round. The interface needs to say when play restarts instead of hiding that change."}],"outcome":"I connected the lobby, private watching, clues, discussion, votes and reveal into one group flow. Solo practice explains the steps with simulated opponents; connected rooms support real players. A study with a real group is the next step for evaluating the social experience.","reflection":"I would watch a real group through a failed clip and a late player, not just a successful reveal. Those pauses would show whether the instructions preserve the social rhythm or turn the host into technical support.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: run a short group session and observe phase explanations, accidental information sharing and waiting time.","Proposed checks: a blocked YouTube embed, a reconnecting player, a departing host and switching between built-in and external calling."],"sources":[{"label":"Alibi README and round-state source","detail":"Private assignments, simultaneous clues, secret votes, practice labelling, playback and connection limits. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Actual browser captures · solo practice","detail":"Captured from the local VideoImposter application. The practice capture uses its clearly labelled simulated opponents, with no camera, microphone or personal-call recording."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","alt":"Alibi actual landing interface explaining its video-imposter game","caption":"Actual local browser interface. The five-player diagram explains the rules before a room starts.","width":1275,"height":717},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-practice.webp","alt":"Alibi actual solo-practice lobby with clearly labelled simulated players","caption":"Actual solo-practice lobby with clearly labelled simulated opponents.","width":1275,"height":717}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/VideoImposter","productActionLabel":"View Alibi on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":12,"pdfPage":81,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Watch privately. [[Give just enough away.]]","scene":"Imagine one person still watching while everyone else begins discussing their clue. The game needs to coordinate the phase without revealing which clip anyone received.","question":"How can a shared round protect information that belongs to one player?","insight":"[[Shared timing and private information need separate treatment]]. A visible phase can coordinate the room without exposing a clip or a vote.","pivot":"I made the round sequence explicit and kept solo practice visibly different from a room of real players.","takeaway":"A clear next step leaves more room for the conversation.","journey":[{"title":"Enter","detail":"Create or join a room, or choose labelled solo practice."},{"title":"Watch and clue","detail":"See a private clip and submit one word."},{"title":"Discuss and reveal","detail":"Compare shared clues, vote privately and see the result."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep private viewing separate from the group phase","Make practice an honest invitation","Explain waits and recovery"],"annotations":[{"title":"Labelled practice","detail":"The capture includes simulated players rather than fabricated participation."},{"title":"Playback dependency","detail":"Clips use the official YouTube player and can be unavailable."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"alibi","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Friend joining a social video game","context":"Entering a room and learning the round while keeping a clip private.","need":"Understand the phase and what can safely be shared.","friction":"Playback delays and unclear waits interrupt the group.","implication":"Show readiness, private boundaries and the host’s next available action."}},"theme":{"layout":"alibi","mode":"dark","paper":"#12141b","ink":"#f4f5fa","dim":"#b4c4da","line":"#3b4557","accent":"#b9f64a","wash":"#2b381e","bodyFont":"Manrope","displayFont":"Barlow Condensed","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can a shared round protect information that belongs to one player?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Alibi","resultHeading":"A private clip inside a shared round."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","alt":"Alibi actual landing interface explaining its video-imposter game","caption":"Actual local browser interface. The five-player diagram explains the rules before a room starts.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from the local VideoImposter application. The practice capture uses its clearly labelled simulated opponents, with no camera, microphone or personal-call recording.","width":1275,"height":717},"v2":{"title":"A video party game that keeps [[one player's secret]] while the whole room plays together.","hook":"Alibi is a browser game I designed and built on my own. Everyone watches the same short clip except one player, who gets a different one without being told, and the group has to work out who.","facts":[{"label":"Role","value":"Design and build, solo"},{"label":"Timeline","value":"October 2026"},{"label":"For","value":"3 to 8 friends playing together over a call"},{"label":"Platform","value":"Web, on computers and phones. Needs a running Node server"},{"label":"Stack","value":"React 19, Vite, Express 5, Socket.IO, WebRTC, the YouTube embedded player"},{"label":"Status","value":"Playable. No permanent public link yet, and no player feedback to share"}],"links":[{"label":"View the code on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/VideoImposter"}],"linksHeading":"See the project","summary":["The problem: an imposter game spoils itself if a clip, a clue or a vote shows up too early, or if the group sits waiting for one player's video.","What I built: a browser game for 3 to 8 friends. The server sends each player only their own share of the round, through six timed phases, with a clearly labelled solo practice mode.","The key idea: privacy is decided on the server. A screen cannot leak what it was never sent.","Where it stands: playable, with 32 automated tests. I prepared it for a seven-player game night, and friends played full rounds on the night. It still has no permanent link."],"lead":{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/home.webp","alt":"The Alibi home screen in dark charcoal and acid green, with the headline Trust no one, a fan of five player cards, a panel to create or join a lobby, and five clip packs below","caption":"The home screen. You can create a lobby, join one with a code, or play solo practice. The five cards show how a round works: four players get the same clip, one gets a different one.","width":1441,"height":1150},"sections":[{"id":"why","label":"Why I built it","nav":"Why I built it","heading":"An imposter game falls apart if anyone sees too much, too soon","blocks":[{"p":"The idea comes from an imposter game played on a popular YouTube channel. Everyone watches a clip. One person, the imposter, has seen a different but related clip and does not know it. Each player gives one word about what they saw, the group talks, and then everyone votes on who the imposter is."},{"p":"I wanted to play it with friends on their own devices, over the internet. That means each person needs a private screen, while the group shares one clock. Alibi is my own version. It is not connected to that channel or to YouTube."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/round-secrets.webp","alt":"Diagram of five players, four sent Clip A and one sent Clip B, above two lists: what the room has to share and what each player has to keep private","caption":"The problem in one picture. The left list must be shared on time. The right list must stay hidden until the right moment, or the round is spoiled.","width":1600,"height":760}]},{"p":"Two things can spoil a round. Something private shows up early, such as one clue before the others are in. Or the game stalls while one person's video loads, and the group waits with no idea why."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not have.","detail":"I did no research with players. I built the game for my own group of friends, and this page does not quote any of them."}}]},{"id":"how","label":"How it works","nav":"How it works","heading":"A round runs in six phases, and each one has its own clock","blocks":[{"p":"A host creates a room and shares the link. Friends join with just a name. There is no sign-up. The host picks a clip pack, the number of rounds (3, 5, 8 or 10), the clip length and the time to talk."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/round-flow.webp","alt":"Diagram of six phase cards in a row, Lobby, Watch, One word, Discuss, Vote and Reveal, each with its time limit and what moves the round on","caption":"One round. Look at the bottom of each card: every phase moves on when everyone is done, or when its timer runs out, so one slow player cannot stall the group.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"In the watch phase you see a private player with one button. The clip starts at a set point and stops by itself. Then you get one box for one word."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/watch.webp","alt":"The watch phase: a panel headed For your eyes only with a Play my clip button, a timer reading 0:00 of 0:15, and the room chat on the right","caption":"Watching. The panel is marked as your private video, and the call is muted while clips play. This host had set 15-second clips.","width":1310,"height":740},{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/clue.webp","alt":"The clue phase: a panel headed A word to the wise with a text box, a Lock my clue button and four of five players marked as locked in","caption":"The one-word clue. The line under the box says nobody sees your word until everyone has submitted. Others only see that you are done.","width":1320,"height":518}],"layout":"one"},{"p":"The reveal is where everything held back comes out at once."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/reveal-annotated.webp","alt":"The reveal screen with four numbered notes: the result heading, both clips side by side, the vote count per player, and the Next round button","caption":"The reveal in a practice round. Note 2 is the payoff: both clips side by side, so the group sees what the imposter was looking at.","width":1600,"height":900}]}]},{"id":"server","label":"Decision 1","nav":"The server keeps secrets","heading":"Each screen only receives what that player is allowed to know","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: your own clip, your own clue, and a tick for everyone else. Nothing on your screen says whether you are the imposter."},{"p":"In the build: the whole room lives on the server. Before every update, the server builds a separate copy for each player and leaves out anything that player should not have yet. The list of clip pairs never reaches a browser at all."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/per-player.webp","alt":"Diagram with the full room on the server on the left, and on the right what an ordinary player and the imposter each receive, which look the same apart from the clip","caption":"The two right-hand copies have the same shape. Only the clip differs, so neither player can tell their role from the data.","width":1600,"height":720}]},{"p":"Tests check this directly. One confirms that exactly one player gets the other clip without seeing any roles. Others check that clues appear together, and that a vote cannot be changed or cast for yourself."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"A host can add up to 20 pairs of their own YouTube links. That host knows what both clips are, though not who got which."}}]},{"id":"practice","label":"Decision 2","nav":"Honest practice","heading":"Practice players are labelled as practice everywhere they appear","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: solo practice puts you in a room with four simulated players. They are ready at once, lock in clues and vote, so you can see a whole round alone before inviting anyone."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/lobby-annotated.webp","alt":"The practice lobby with six numbered notes pointing at the solo practice label, the SOLO code, the phase list, the Practice tags on seats, the chat welcome message and the ready count","caption":"The practice lobby. Notes 1, 2, 4 and 5 each say in a different place that these players are simulated.","width":1600,"height":1000}]},{"p":"In the build: simulated players are flagged in the room data. Three of them give fixed words and all of them vote at random, which is honest about what they are. Nobody else can join a practice room. If a host leaves a real room, control passes to another real player, never to a simulated one."},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"Practice teaches the steps. It cannot copy the talk, the bluffing and the hesitation of a real group."}}]},{"id":"clips","label":"Decision 3","nav":"Short clips, no repeats","heading":"Clips are short, start at a set point and never repeat back to back","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: my first version let a host pick 30, 45 or 60-second clips, with 45 as the default. Each player pressed \"I've seen my clip\" when they were done."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/phone-watch-45.webp","alt":"Phone screen of the first watch phase, saying Watch 45 seconds, with a timer of 0:00 of 0:45 and an I've seen my clip button","caption":"Before: 45 seconds, and a button to say you had finished.","width":385,"height":815,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/settings.webp","alt":"The room settings dialog with rounds, time to watch set to 30 seconds and time to point fingers set to 90 seconds, and a button to add a custom YouTube pair","caption":"After: 30 seconds is the longest choice. The clip stops by itself and marks you done.","width":502,"height":572}],"layout":"two"},{"p":"A few hours later I capped clips at 30 seconds, with 15 and 20 as shorter choices. The round now moves on by itself once everyone has watched. One of the rules I wrote for the game is that a round should be quick to understand and replay."},{"p":"In the build: curated clips start 15 seconds into the source video. YouTube can resume a video where it was last left, so every round loads a fresh player and sets its own start and end. A late signal from the old clip cannot end the new one. Eight tests cover playback alone."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/clip-choice.webp","alt":"Diagram of five numbered steps the server follows to pick the next pair of clips, from the chosen pack down to a random pick, with the reason for each on the right","caption":"How the next pair is chosen. Step 3 was added the day after the first build. Before it, a pack that started over could serve the pair just played.","width":1600,"height":860}]},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The library is small, eight pairs in all, so packs run out. The throwbacks pack has one pair and must repeat. The room is told when a pack starts over. Playback also depends on YouTube, where a video can be blocked in some countries or removed."}}]},{"id":"look","label":"Decision 4","nav":"A game, not a booking page","heading":"A dark game lobby instead of a polite booking page","blocks":[{"p":"What you see: my first version used ivory and sage, soft headings and food photos as the hero. It looked like a hotel booking site. I dropped it the same evening, before the first commit, and started again as a game."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/look-before-after.webp","alt":"Two home screens side by side: the first in ivory with a pizza and burger photo, the second in charcoal and acid green with a fan of player cards","caption":"The same screen before and after. The content barely changed. Colour, type and the player cards changed what kind of evening it promises.","width":1600,"height":880}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/design-sheet.webp","alt":"Sheet of the game's colours, its heading and body fonts, a green Create lobby button, an amber warning strip and three corner sizes","caption":"The rules behind the look. Note the amber strip: a clip problem gets its own colour, so a warning never looks like a hint about someone's role.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"p":"In the build: the player faces are drawn in code, a coloured tile with a dark visor, two eyes and a mouth. They blink every six seconds, and the blink stops for anyone whose device asks for less motion. The card marked with a different clip appears only in the labelled example on the home screen, never on a real player's seat."},{"p":"On phones the order changes. Create and join come first. Inside a room, the ready and start control sits right under the heading. Text boxes use 16px type so phones do not zoom in when you type."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/phone-entry.webp","alt":"Phone home screen with the create lobby panel at the top, above the introduction","caption":"Create or join comes before the introduction.","width":385,"height":700,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/phone-room.webp","alt":"Phone lobby with the room code, an invite button and a 7 of 7 ready bar with a Start game button directly under the heading","caption":"Start game sits right under the room heading.","width":385,"height":545,"kind":"plain"},{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/phone-vote.webp","alt":"Phone vote screen headed Who's got a bad alibi, with four player cards showing each player's one-word clue and a Lock my vote button","caption":"Voting on a phone. Each card shows that player's clue. Names are blurred.","width":385,"height":1010,"kind":"plain"}],"layout":"phones"},{"aside":{"title":"The trade-off.","detail":"The arcade look leans on small labels, down to 11px for some details. They are harder to read than the rest of the screen."}}]},{"id":"night","label":"Game night","nav":"Ready for seven players","heading":"Getting it ready for seven friends on a Teams call","blocks":[{"p":"Two weeks later I prepared Alibi for a game night with seven players. The group would talk on Microsoft Teams, not the in-app call. The game cannot mute Teams, so the instructions tell everyone to mute while watching and unmute when the clues appear."},{"p":"I added a three-step guide to the lobby and clearer headings for each phase."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/night-annotated.webp","alt":"The game-night lobby with seven ready players and six numbered notes pointing at the room code, the three-step guide, the phase list, the seats, the Start game button and the voice controls","caption":"The lobby with seven players ready. Note 2 is the new three-step guide. Player names are blurred.","width":1600,"height":1060}]},{"p":"I also listed what could go wrong in the middle of a game, and gave each case an answer inside the game."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/recovery.webp","alt":"Diagram of six problems, such as a clip that will not play or a dropped connection, each with what the game does about it","caption":"The first row mattered most. Replacing clips mid-round keeps the round number and the scores, so a broken video does not cost anyone points.","width":1600,"height":790}]},{"p":"Before the night I checked it end to end. Seven separate clients played a full round through the public link: watching, private clues, discussion, private votes and the reveal. All 16 clip videos were still listed as available that day. For the in-app call, seven test clients with generated camera and microphone feeds connected all 21 pairs between them."},{"aside":{"title":"What I do not know.","detail":"I have no notes, photos or feedback from the night itself, so this page does not claim it went well."}}]},{"id":"build","label":"The build","nav":"The build","heading":"One server decides the round, and tests guard the secrets","blocks":[{"p":"The server is the only place a round is decided. Browsers send actions, such as \"lock my clue\", and get back their own copy of the room."},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/architecture.webp","alt":"Diagram in three columns: the game screen, YouTube player and recent clip list in each browser; rooms, game rules and Express on one Node server; and the optional in-app call, relay server and Teams","caption":"What runs where. The shaded boxes hold the secrets, and both are on the server.","width":1600,"height":900}]},{"table":{"head":["Part","What I used"],"rows":[["Game screen","React 19, built with Vite"],["Server","Node with Express 5. Rooms are kept in memory"],["Live updates","Socket.IO, with a separate copy of the room for each player"],["Video","YouTube's official embedded player. Nothing is downloaded or hosted again"],["Optional call","WebRTC between browsers. Voice by default, camera optional"],["Hosting","A Docker file and a Render setup, plus a script for a temporary public link"]]}},{"stats":[{"value":"5","label":"commits, 16 to 30 October 2026"},{"value":"32","label":"automated tests, all passing at the last recorded run"},{"value":"7","label":"separate clients in the largest full-round test"},{"value":"21","label":"call connections made between seven test clients"}]},{"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/alibi/timeline.webp","alt":"Timeline of three dates: 16 October with the first build, 17 October with the clip changes, and 30 October with the game-night preparation","caption":"The build by date. Most of the game arrived on the first day. The later work went into clips and recovery.","width":1600,"height":620}]},{"p":"The hardest problems were about time and order, not about the screens."},{"table":{"head":["Area","What went wrong, and what I changed"],"rows":[["Old video positions","YouTube could carry over where a video was last stopped, and a late signal from the old clip could end the new one. Each round now gets a fresh player and reloads its own segment. Tests cover seeking, buffering and clips that end early."],["Repeats","In the first version, when a pack ran out and started over, the pair just played could come straight back. The server now skips it whenever another pair is left."],["Seven-way calls","Seven cameras need 21 connections, and the setup messages arrive in bursts. Call setup now has its own allowance, so a burst cannot block clues and votes. Camera video is capped at 15 frames a second where the browser allows it."],["Drop-outs","A player who drops keeps their seat for 90 seconds. One test plays a full round with three clients, reconnects one of them and hands over the host."]]}}]},{"id":"status","label":"Where it stands","nav":"Where it stands","heading":"Playable from start to finish, with no permanent home yet","blocks":[{"p":"Alibi works end to end, in solo practice and in real rooms. It has no permanent public address. For a game night it runs on my computer behind a temporary link, which lasts only while the computer stays on and connected."},{"p":"These limits are still there:"},{"list":["Rooms live in the server's memory, so a restart ends every game.","It runs as one server. More than one would need shared storage for rooms.","In-app calls across different networks need a relay server, which is not set up.","Beyond that game night I have no usage numbers."]}]},{"id":"next","label":"What is next","nav":"What is next","heading":"Watch a real group hit a problem, then give it a permanent home","blocks":[{"list":["Watch a real group through a round that goes wrong, not only one that works: a blocked video, a player who reconnects, a host who leaves. That would show whether the instructions keep the game moving or turn the host into tech support.","Compare a round on the in-app call with a round on Teams.","Move it to a permanent host with a relay server, so friends on any network can join."]}]},{"id":"learned","label":"What I learned","nav":"What I learned","heading":"In a party game, privacy belongs on the server","blocks":[{"p":"The most useful decision was to keep secrets on the server and send each player only their share. After that, no screen design could leak a role by mistake, and the tests could check it."},{"p":"Most of my later work was not new features. It was making the game fail politely: a clip that will not load, a player who drops, a host who leaves. I also learned to drop a look early. The first one was tidy and wrong for the mood, and seeing that cost one evening instead of a rebuild."}]}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","headline":"Finding a class file should be straightforward."}}};
